hadiths[count]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1748","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun, who said: Al-Walid narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abu Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad ibn Miskin al-Yamami, who said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abu Kathir, who said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, who said: Abu Hurayrah (RA) reported:\n\nWhile 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) was delivering the sermon to the people on a Friday, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (RA) entered. He (Uthman) made a subtle remark to him (Umar), saying: \"What is the matter with some men who delay after the call to prayer?\"\n\n'Uthman (RA) said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, I did not do more than perform ablution when I heard the call, then I came.\"\n\n'Umar (RA) said: \"Ablution as well? Have you not heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'When one of you comes for Friday prayer, let him perform ghusl (ritual bath)?'\"\n\nIn the narration of al-Walid, it is stated: \"was delivering the sermon to the people,\" without mentioning \"on a Friday.\"\n\n---"
"1749","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, who said: I heard al-Zuhri say: I heard Salim inform, on the authority of his father, who said: I heard that the Prophet (ﷺ) said — and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, who said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from his father — that he heard the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was on the pulpit say:\n\n\"Whoever among you comes to Friday prayer should perform ghusl (ritual bath).\"\n\n---"
"1750","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm: Narrated to us Abū Bakr: Narrated to us Ṣakhr ibn Juwayriyah: On the authority of Nāfiʿ: On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA) who said:\nI heard the Prophet (ﷺ) delivering a sermon and saying, “When one of you comes for the Friday prayer, let him perform ghusl (a full bath).”"
"1751","**\nNarrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Qaz'ah: Narrated to us Al-Fudayl — meaning ibn Sulayman —: Narrated to us Musa ibn 'Uqbah, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), while delivering a sermon to the people, said:\n\n*\"When one of you comes to the mosque, let him perform ghusl (ritual bath).\"*"
"1752","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', who said: Zaid bin Hubab narrated to us. [And in another chain:] Narrated to us Abduh bin Abdullah Al-Khuza'i, who said: Zaid informed us, who said: Uthman bin Waqid Al-Umari narrated to me, who said: Nafi' narrated to me, from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever among the men and women attends the Jumu'ah ("
"1753","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walid, on the authority of Quraysh ibn Anas, on the authority of Hisham ibn Urwah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\"The people used to be their own masters, so they would go out to Friday (prayer) in their usual state. It was said to them: 'If only you were to perform ghusl (ritual bath).'\""
"1754","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb, he said: My paternal uncle narrated to me, he said: ‘Amr—who is Ibn al-Harith— informed me, on the authority of ‘Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja‘far, that Muhammad ibn Ja‘far narrated to him, on the authority of ‘Urwah, on the authority of ‘A’ishah (RA), that she said:\n\nThe people would come to Friday prayer from their homes in the outskirts, wearing coarse woolen cloaks (*‘aba’*), and dust and sweat would affect them, so their garments would emit an odor. One of them came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was with me, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“If only you were to purify yourselves for this day of yours.”\n\n---"
"1755","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman Al-Muradi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: He informed us that Sulayman (who is Ibn Bilal) said: On the authority of 'Amr (who is the son of Abu 'Amr), the freed slave of Al-Muttalib, who said: On the authority of Ikrimah, who said: On the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), that two men from the people of Iraq came to him and asked him"
"1756","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawwaqi and Salm ibn Junadah, who said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us. Ya'qub reported: Al-A'mash narrated to us, and Salm ibn Junadah reported on the authority of Al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever performs wudu on the day of Jumu'ah and perfects his ablution, then attends the Jumu'ah prayer, draws near (to the imam and the prayer area) and listens attentively, will be forgiven for all his sins between him and the next Jumu'ah, along with an additional three days. And whoever touches the pebbles (i.e., fidgets with small stones during the khutbah) has engaged in vain, idle action (laghw).\""
"1757","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli, narrated to us Yazid (meaning Ibn Zuray'), narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of al-Hasan, on the authority of Samurah ibn Jundub (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n*\"Whoever performs wudu' (ablution), then he has done well, and whoever bathes (ghusl), then that is better.\"*"
"1758","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn al-Durris, and 'Abdah ibn 'Abdullah al-Khuza'i. Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' and Ibn al-Durris said: Narrated to us Husayn, while 'Abdah said: Narrated to us Husayn ibn 'Ali, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Aws ibn Aws, who said that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the day of Friday and said:\n\n*\"Whoever washes (the deceased), then bathes himself, and comes early, proceeds quickly, draws near (the imam), listens attentively, and does not indulge in idle talk, for every step he takes, he will have the reward of a year's fasting and standing in prayer.\"*\n\nMuhammad ibn al-'Ala' did not mention *\"and mentioned the day of Friday.\"* He also said: *\"Whoever washes\"* with the lightened form (i.e., without the doubling of the letter).\n\nIbn al-Durris said: *\"It will be recorded for him for every step.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (a narrator) said: Whoever says in the narration *\"whoever washes (the deceased) then bathes himself\"* (مَنْ غَسَّلَ وَاغْتَسَلَ), its meaning is: whoever has intercourse (with his wife or slave), thus obligating the bath upon his wife or slave, and then bathes himself. And whoever says *\"whoever washes and bathes himself\"* (مَنْ غَسَلَ"
"1759","###\n Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak Al-Mukharimi narrated to us, saying: Ya'qub bin Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, who said: Muhammad bin Muslim bin Abdullah bin Shihab Al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Tawus Al-Yamani, who said: I said to Ibn Abbas: \"Some people claim that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Perform Ghusl (ritual bath) on the day of J"
"1760","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la al-San‘ani. He said: Harun ibn Muslim, the companion of al-Hinna’ Abu al-Hasan, narrated to us. He said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from ‘Abdullah ibn Abi Qatada, who said:\nAbu Qatada came to me on the day of Friday while I was performing ghusl (ritual bath). He asked, “Is this ghusl of yours from janabah (major ritual impurity)?”\nI replied, “Yes.”\nHe said, “Then perform another ghusl, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n‘Whoever performs ghusl on the day of Friday, he will remain pure until the next Friday.’”\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: This is a ghareeb (uncommon) hadith; none narrated it except Harun.\n\n---"
"1761","Yahya ibn Habib al-Harithi narrated to us, saying: Rauh narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, saying: I heard 'Amr ibn Dinar narrating, from Tawus, from Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"It is obligatory upon every Muslim to perform a full bath (ghusl) once every seven days and to apply perfume if he can find it.\""
"1762","###\n Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: It was narrated to me by Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abdur-Rahman and from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Abu Hurairah (RA) and Abu Sa'id (RA), who said: We heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Whoever performs Ghusl (rit"
"1763","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulaymaan: Shu’ayb narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us, from Ibn ‘Ajlaan, from Sa’eed al-Maqburee, from his father, from ‘Abdullah ibn Wadee’ah, from Abu Dharr (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n“Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, and does so thoroughly, then wears his best clothes, then applies some of the oil from his household—what Allah has decreed for him—or some of his perfume, and then does not separate between two people (i.e., does not step over others), Allah will expiate for him the sins between that Friday and the previous Friday.”\n\nSa’eed said: So I mentioned this to ‘Umarah ibn ‘Amr ibn Hazm, who said: “He spoke the truth, and it also adds three extra days.”"
"1764","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, who said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn ʿAjlān, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from his father, with this chain of narration.\n\nAbū Bakr said: Bundār said to us, \"I have preserved it from his mouth.\" He also said, \"From his father,\" and this, in my view, is a mistake. The correct narration is: \"From Saʿīd, from his father.\"\n\n---"
"1765","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yaḥyā, who narrated to us ʿAmr ibn Abī Salāmah, on the authority of Zuhayr, on the authority of Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father (ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr), from ʿĀʾishah (RA); and (also narrated to us) from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUrwah, on the authority of his father, from ʿĀʾishah (RA); and from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from a man from among them (the group of these narrators). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered the Friday khutbah, and he saw the people wearing striped garments (al-nimār). So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no blame on any of you, if you have the financial means, to acquire two garments for the Friday prayer in addition to the two garments of your work.\""
"1766","**\nNarrated to us Al-Hasan ibn As-Sabbah Al-Bazzar, narrated to us Hafs—meaning Ibn Ghiyath—on the authority of Hajjaj, on the authority of Abu Ja'far, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) had a cloak which he would wear on the two 'Eids and on Friday.\""
"1767","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us Abu Ahmad. And narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Yazid, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yusuf, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Isa, from Yahya ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Aus ibn Aus, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever performs ghusl (ritual bath) and then goes early in the morning to the mosque, sits close to the imam, listens attentively, and remains silent, will have the reward of a year's fasting and standing in prayer.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Abu Musa.\n\nIn the narration of Muhammad ibn Yusuf, it is added: \"For every step he takes, he will have the reward of a year's fasting and standing in prayer.\""
"1768","**\nNarrated to us Ziyad ibn Ayyub Abu Hashim, who said: Narrated to us Mubashshir — meaning ibn Isma'il — on the authority of al-Awza'i, who said: Narrated to me Yahya ibn Abu Kathir, on the authority of Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\"The one who hurries to the prayer is like one who brings a camel as a sacrifice, and the one who follows him is like one who brings a cow as a sacrifice, and the one who follows him is like one who brings a sheep as a sacrifice, and the one who follows him is like one who brings a bird as a sacrifice.\""
"1769","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar, narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of al-Zuhri, and narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"On the day of Friday, there are angels stationed at every gate of the mosque, recording the people in order of their arrival, first to first. Then, when the imam comes out, the scrolls are folded.\"\n\n'Abd al-Jabbar added: \"When the imam sits down, the scrolls are folded.\"\n\nBoth of them said: \"And they listen to the sermon. The one who comes early to the prayer is like one who brings a camel as a sacrifice, then the one who follows him is like one who brings a cow, then the one who follows him is like one who brings a ram,\" until he mentioned the chicken and the egg.\n\nAl-Makhzumi said: \"Like one who brings a cow,\" and he said: \"Like one who brings a ram.\""
"1770","Narrated to us Ali ibn Hujr, who narrated to us Isma'il (referring to Ibn Ja'far), who narrated to us Al-Ala'. [Sahih] And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, who narrated to us Shu'bah, from Al-Ala'. [Sahih] And narrated to us Abu Musa, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far; he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he said: I heard Al-Ala'. [Sahih] And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Bazee', who narrated to us Yazid (referring to Ibn Zuraiq), who narrated to us Rawh ibn al-Qasim, from Al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\"On every door of the mosque on the day of Jumu'ah, two angels are stationed who record the arrivals in order of precedence: the first [arriver] first, [each receiving reward equivalent to] a man who offers a sacrificial camel, a man who offers a cow, a man who offers a sheep, a man who offers a bird, and a man who offers an egg. When the Imam sits [to deliver the Jumu'ah khutbah], the scrolls are rolled up.\"\nBundar reported: [The wording of the narration is] \"When he [the Imam] sits, the scrolls are rolled up.\"\nAli ibn Hujr reported: [The wording is] \"he brought a bird\" [instead of the phrase \"and a man who brings an egg\" found in other narrations].\nIbn Bazee' reported: [The wording is] \"When the Imam exits [after completing the Jumu'ah prayer], the scrolls are rolled up.\""
"1771","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Quta'i: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; Hammam narrated to us; Matar narrated to us.\n\nAnd it was also narrated to us by Abu Hatim Sahl ibn Muhammad: al-Muqri' narrated to us; Hammam informed me; from Matar; from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb; from his father; from his grandfather; from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said:\n\n\"On the Day of Friday, angels are stationed at the doors of the mosque, recording the arrival of the people. When the imam comes out, the scrolls are folded and the pens are lifted. Then the angels say to one another, 'What is keeping so-and-so?' The angels say, 'O Allah, if he is astray, guide him; if he is sick, grant him healing; if he is in need, enrich him.'\"\n\nThis is the narration of al-Muqri'.\n\nAl-Quta'i said: \"The angels sit at the doors of the mosque.\"\n\nHe also said: \"They say to one another, 'O Allah, if he is astray, guide him; if he is...' and so on until the end.\""
"1772","**\nNarrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā al-Fazārī, he said: Ibrāhīm—meaning Ibn Saʿd—informed us, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abū Salamah and al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"When the prayer is established, do not come to it while you are running. Rather, come to it while you are walking, with tranquility and dignity upon you. Whatever you catch up with, pray it, and whatever you miss, make it up.\"*"
"1773","Abu Musa narrated to us, Abu 'Amir narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dh'ib narrated to us, from Az-Zuhri, from As-Sa'ib ibn Yazid, who said: \"The call (Adhan) that Allah mentioned in the Quran for the Day of Jumu'ah was made when the Imam came out, and when the prayer was established, during the time of the Prophet (ﷺ), Abu Bakr, and 'Umar. Then, when 'Uthman came, the people increased in"
"1774","**\nIt was narrated to us from Salam bin Junadah, who said: Waki' narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn Abi Dhi'b, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of al-Sa'ib bin Yazid, who said:\n\"The call to prayer (Adhan) during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), Abu Bakr, and Umar (RA) consisted of two Adhans on the day of Friday. This continued until the time of Uthman (RA), when the people increased in number. So he commanded that the first Adhan be called at al-Zawra'.\""
"1775","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Shawkar bin Rafi' al-Baghdadi, who narrated to us Ya'qub bin Ibrahim, who said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, who narrated to me Muhammad bin Ibrahim al-Taymi, from 'Imran bin Abi Yahya, from 'Abdullah bin Ka'b bin Malik, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Whoever performs Ghus"
"1776","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Khushram, who informed us that Isa (meaning the son of Yunus) narrated from Al-Mubarak (who is the son of Fadalah), who narrated from Al-Hasan, who narrated from Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to stand on the Day of Friday, leaning his back against a wooden pillar, or a tree trunk, or a palm tree—Al-Mubarak expressed uncertainty regarding which one it was. When the number"
"1777","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar: Narrated to us 'Umar ibn Yunus: On the authority of 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar: On the authority of Ishaq ibn Abi Talhah: Narrated to us Anas ibn Malik (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to stand on the day of Friday, leaning his back against a palm trunk set up in the mosque, and deliver the sermon.\n\nThen a Roman came and said, \"Shall we not make something for you to sit on, as if you were standing?\" So they made for him a pulpit with two steps, and he would sit on the third. When the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) sat on the pulpit, the trunk groaned like a bull, so much so that the mosque trembled with its groaning out of grief for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) descended to it from the pulpit and embraced it while it was groaning. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) embraced it, it became silent. Then he said, \"By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if I had not embraced it, it would have continued groaning like this until the Hour is established out of grief for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\"\n\nSo the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered that it be buried, meaning the trunk.\n\nIn the narration of Jabir (RA), the Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"This trunk wept for what it missed of the remembrance (of Allah).\"\n\n---"
"1778","**\nNarrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Tamām al-Miṣrī: Narrated to us Yūsuf ibn ʿAdiyy: Narrated to us Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ṭāʾifī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid—who is al-ʿAdwānī—from his father, who said:\n\n*\"I once saw the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) standing with the support of a bow or a staff when he came to them. I heard him say: 'By the heaven and the comer by night' (Sūrah al-Ṭāriq 86:1). I memorized it during the Jāhiliyyah while I was still a polytheist. Then I recited it after accepting Islām. The tribe of Thaqīf asked me about it, saying: 'What did you hear from this man?' So I recited it to them. Those among the Quraysh who were with them said: 'We know our companion better. Had we known that what he says is true, we would have followed him.'\"*"
"1779","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, who said: They differed concerning the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and what it was made from. So they sent to Sahl ibn Sa'd, who said: \"There is no one left among the people more knowledgeable about it than me. It was made from the wood of the *athl* tree of al-Ghabah.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"*Athl* is *tarafāʾ*.\"\n\n---"
"1780","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Hisham ibn Ammar, narrated to us al-Walid, narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"When the Prophet (ﷺ) ascended the pulpit, he said to the people, *'Sit down.'* Ibn Mas'ud (RA), who was at the door of the mosque, heard him and sat down. The Prophet (ﷺ) then said, *'Come here, O Ibn Mas'ud.'*\""
"1781","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm: Narrated to us Abū Baḥr Abd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUthmān al-Bakrawī: Narrated to us ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: Narrated to us Nāfiʿ, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) would deliver two sermons on the day of Friday, and he would sit between them.\"\n\nAbū Bakr said: I heard Bundār say: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd would hold this shaykh—meaning al-Bakrawī—in high esteem."
"1782","Muhammad bin Umar bin Hayyaj Abu Abdullah Al-Hamdani narrated to us, saying: Yahya bin Abdur-Rahman bin Malik bin Al-Harith Al-Arhabi narrated to us, saying: Abdur-Rahman bin Abd al-Malik bin Abjar narrated to me from his father, from Wasil bin Hayyan, who said: Abu Wa'il said: \"Ammar bin Yasir delivered a sermon to us; he was eloquent and concise. When he descended, we said to him: 'O Abu Al-Yaqzan"
"1783","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) narrated in the report of Jabir ibn Samurah (RA):\n\"The sermon of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was concise.\""
"1784","Narrated on the authority of Al-Hakam ibn Hazn (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ): He (ﷺ) praised Allah, and extolled Him with good, light, and blessed words."
"1785","**\nNarrated to us Al-Husayn ibn 'Isa Al-Bistami, narrated to us Anas meaning Ibn 'Iyad, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad.\n\nAnd it was also narrated to us 'Utbah ibn 'Abdillah, who informed us 'Abdullah ibn Al-Mubarak, who said: Sufyan informed us, from Ja'far, from his father, from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would say in his sermon:\n\n*\"He would praise Allah, extol Him with what is due to Him, then say: 'Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide him, and whomever He leaves astray, none can guide him. Verily, the most truthful speech is the Book of Allah. The best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad (ﷺ). The worst of affairs are the newly invented ones, and every innovation is a misguidance, and every misguidance is in the Fire.'*\n\nThen he (ﷺ) would say:\n\n*'I have been sent and the Hour like these two.'* (He would gesture with his two fingers, the index and the middle finger.)\n\nAnd when he (ﷺ) mentioned the Hour, his cheeks would turn red, his voice would rise, and his anger would intensify, as if he were a warner of an approaching army saying: *'The Hour has come upon you, or has touched you.'*\n\nThen he (ﷺ) would say:\n\n*'Whomever leaves behind wealth, it is for his family. Whomever leaves behind a debt or dependents, then it is to me (to settle) or upon me, for I am the guardian of the believers.'\"*\n\nThis is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Al-Mubarak. The wording of Anas ibn 'Iyad differs from this wording."
"1786","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ma'n, on the authority of the daughter of al-Harithah ibn al-Nu'man, who said:\n\n\"I did not memorize Surah Qaf except from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), which was recited in every Friday sermon. Our oven and the oven of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) were one and the same.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"This daughter of al-Harithah is Umm Hisham bint Harithah.\""
"1787","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of Yahya ibn ‘Abdullah, on the authority of Umm Hisham bint Harithah ibn an-Nu‘man (RA), who said:\n\n*\"I recited 'Qaf and the Glorious Qur’an' from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He would recite it every Friday on the pulpit when he addressed the people.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (the transmitter) said: *\"This Yahya ibn ‘Abdullah is the son of ‘Abd ar-Rahman ibn Sa‘d ibn Zurarah. Ibrahim ibn Sa‘d attributed his lineage.\"*"
"1788","###\n Narrated to us by Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'idi, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il (meaning Ibn Ja'far), who said: Narrated to us by Shurayk (who is Ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir), from Anas (RA), that a man entered the mosque on a Friday through a gate near the house of the judge, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was standing on the pulpit delivering the sermon. The man stood facing the Messenger of Allah ("
"1789","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr, narrated to us Isma'il — meaning Ibn Ja'far — narrated to us Humaid, from Anas (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and 'Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Dirhami, who both said: narrated to us Khalid — and he is Ibn al-Harith — narrated to us Humaid, who said: Anas (RA) was asked, \"Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raise his hands?\"\n\nHe replied: It was said to him on the day of Friday, \"O Messenger of Allah, the rain has ceased, the land has become barren, and wealth has perished.\" So he raised his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits, and he supplicated for rain while we did not see a single cloud in the sky.\n\nHe (Anas RA) continued: Before we finished the prayer, a young man who lived nearby was already hurrying back to his family due to the intensity of the rain. The Friday prayer continued for a whole week.\n\nThey said, \"O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the travelers have been delayed.\"\n\nSo the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) smiled and gestured with his hand, saying, **\"O Allah, send rain around us, not upon us.\"**\n\nThus, the rain was lifted away from al-Madinah.\n\nThis is the wording of the hadith of Khalid ibn al-Harith. However, Abu Musa added in his narration, \"The rain has ceased.\"\n\n---"
"1790","**\nNarrated to us Humaid reported from Anas (RA) that Abu Bakr (RA) said in a narration: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) smiled.\""
"1791","Narrated to us by Bashr bin Mu'adh, who said: Narrated to us by Yazid, meaning Ibn Zura'ah, who said: Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) did not raise his hands in any of his supplications, or at any time of supplication, except when praying for rain (Istisqa'); in that case, he would raise his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was visible.\""
"1792","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said in the narration of Shurayk ibn ‘Abdullah, from Anas (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raised his hands. I have already dictated this in the narration of Qatadah, from Anas (RA): \"He does not raise his hands in any of his supplications except in seeking rain (al-istisqa’), meaning except when he asks Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, to send rain upon them, and when he asks Him to withhold the rain from them.\"\n\nHe (Abu Bakr RA) said: And he applied the term *al-istisqa’* to both meanings:\n1. His asking Allah to send rain upon them.\n2. His asking Allah to withhold the rain from them.\n\nThe proof for the correctness of this interpretation is that Anas ibn Malik (RA) has narrated in the narration of Shurayk ibn ‘Abdullah from him (Anas RA) that he raised his hands during the sermon on the pulpit on Friday when he asked Allah to send relief upon them. Similarly, he raised his hands when he said, *\"O Allah, around us and not upon us!\"* This statement is also a seeking of rain (al-istisqa’), except that he asked Allah to withhold the rain from the dwellings and houses, while the rain would fall upon the mountains, hills, and valleys.\n\n---"
"1793","###\n Narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa al-Qattan: Jarir narrated to us, on the authority of Husayn. And narrated to us by Ali bin Muslim: Hushaym narrated to us, informing us that Husayn said: I heard Umarah bin Ruwaybah al-Thaqafi say: Bishr bin Marwan delivered a sermon while raising both his hands in supplication. Umarah said: \"May Allah disgrace these two hands! I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the pulpit, and"
"1794","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, who said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, who said: Shuʿbah narrated to us, who said: Muslim ibn Junādah narrated to us, who said: Wakīʿ narrated to us, from Sufyān, all of them from Ḥuṣayn, who said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr say: Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) did not raise his hands except in seven cases: when seeking rain, when supplicating against a group, when seeking Allāh’s protection from a group, when seeking Allāh’s protection from evil, when seeking Allāh’s protection from the punishment of the grave, when seeking Allāh’s protection from the trials of life and death, and when standing on the pulpit on Friday, he would point with his finger and supplicate."
"1795","###\n Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Abdul-Hakam narrated to us, saying: My father and Shu'ayb informed us, saying: Al-Layth informed us, narrating from Khalid bin Yazid, from Ibn Abi Hilal, from Iyad bin Abdullah, from Abu Sa'id (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivered a sermon to us one day, and he recited Surah Sad. When he reached the verse of Sajdah (prostration), he descended from the pulpit, prostrated"
"1796","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us by Shurayk... [The narration continues as follows:] A man stood up on the pulpit on the day of Jumu'ah (Friday) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when will the Hour (the Day of Judgment) come?\" The people gestured to him, saying, \"Be silent!\" He asked the question three times, and each time the"
"1797","###\n Narrated to us Abdullah bin Muhammad al-Zuhri, who said: Narrated to us Salim bin Qutaiba, on the authority of Yunus bin Ishaq, on the authority of Al-Mughira bin Shibl, on the authority of Jarir bin Abdullah (RA), who said: \"When I arrived in Medina, the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering a sermon. He said: 'From this door or from this passageway, the best of the people of Yemen will enter upon you. Behold, there is a trace"
"1798","**\nNarrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth: narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Musa, from Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq, from al-Mughirah ibn Shibl, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:\n\"When I approached the city of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I dismounted my riding animal, unpacked my luggage, put on my best clothes, and entered while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering a sermon. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) greeted me with salam, and the people stared at me. I said to a man sitting next to me, 'O Abdullah, did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mention anything about me?' He replied, 'Yes, he mentioned you in the best terms. While he was delivering the sermon, he suddenly said: \"A man will enter upon you from this door or this opening from the best of Yemen. Indeed, there is upon his face the touch of an angel.\"'\nJarir (RA) said: 'I praised Allah for what He had made known of me.'\""
"1799","**\nNarrated to us Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Makhzūmī: narrated to us Sufyān: on the authority of Ibn ʿAjlān: from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ: that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (RA) entered on the day of Friday while Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam was delivering the sermon. He stood up to pray, so the guards came to make him sit down, but he refused until he had prayed. When Marwān finished, we came to him and said, “May Allāh have mercy on you! They nearly did something to you.”\n\nHe replied, “I would not leave them after something I had seen the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) do.”\n\nHe then mentioned that a man came on the day of Friday while the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon in a state of shabbiness. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) commanded the people to give charity, but they could not find any garments, so he ordered two garments for him. He then prayed two rakʿahs while the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon.\n\nThe following Friday, the man came again while the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) again commanded the people to give charity, so a man threw one of the two garments. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) shouted or rebuked him and said, “Take your garment.”\n\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) then said, “This man came in a state of shabbiness, so I ordered the people to give charity. They could not find any garments, so I ordered two garments for him. Then he came today, and I ordered the people to give charity, so this man threw one of his two garments.” He then ordered him to pray two rakʿahs."
"1800","Abu Zuhayr Abd al-Majid bin Ibrahim narrated to us, al-Muqri' narrated to us, Sulayman bin al-Mughira narrated to us on the authority of Humayd bin Hilal, on the authority of Abu Rafa'ah al-Adawi (RA), who said: \"I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was delivering the sermon, so I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a foreign stranger has come asking about his religion, and he does not know what his religion is?' So the Prophet (ﷺ) turned towards me and paused his sermon. A chair was brought for him, whose legs were made of iron. Humayd said: 'I believe he saw black wood and mistook it for iron.' Then the Prophet (ﷺ) began teaching me what Allah had taught him regarding this matter, then he returned to his sermon and completed its conclusion.\""
"1801","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdah ibn 'Abdillah al-Khuzā'ī, narrated to us Zayd—meaning Ibn al-Hubāb—on the authority of Husayn—who is Ibn Wāqid—who narrated to me 'Abdullāh ibn Buraydah, on the authority of his father, who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering a sermon when al-Hasan and al-Husayn (RA) came forward wearing two red shirts, stumbling and standing. He descended, took them, and placed them before him. Then he said:\n*\"Allāh and His Messenger have spoken the truth: 'Your wealth and your children are but a trial.' (64:15) I saw these two and could not restrain myself.\"*\n\nThen he resumed his sermon."
"1802","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abdullah ibn Sa‘īd al-Ashajj and Ziyād ibn Ayyūb, both said:\nNarrated to us Abū Tumaylah, who said:\nNarrated to us Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid, who said:\nNarrated to us ‘Abdullah ibn Buraydah, on the authority of his father, who said:\nWhile the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering a sermon on the pulpit, he mentioned something similar to this and then said:\n“I could not restrain myself, so I descended, took them both, and carried them.”\nHe did not say: “Then he resumed his sermon.”"
"1803","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Nasr, who said: narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah, who said: narrated to me Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, that his father narrated to him, that Abu Hurayrah (RA) said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"If a man performs ghusl (ritual bath) on Friday, washes his head, then applies the best of his perfume, wears his best clothes, then proceeds to the prayer without separating between two people, and listens attentively to the imam, his sins between this Friday and the next will be forgiven, with an additional three days.\""
"1804","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Ma'mar Al-Qaisi, he said: Narrated to us Habban, he said: Narrated to us Wuhayb, he said: Narrated to us Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"If you speak on the Day of Jumu'ah while the Imam is delivering the sermon, you have engaged in idle talk, and your prayer is rendered void.\" This refers to the time when the Imam is delivering the"
"1805","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la: Ibn Wahb informed us; Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab; Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed him; Abu Hurayrah (RA) said.\n\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Aziz al-Aylisi: Salamah informed them, from 'Uqayl; Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib; Abu Hurayrah (RA) said.\n\nAnd narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from the hadith of 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Ibrahim ibn Qariz, from Abu Hurayrah (RA).\n\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi': 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Qariz, from Abu Hurayrah (RA).\n\nAnd from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n*\"If you say to your companion, 'Be quiet,' while the imam is delivering the Friday sermon, you have engaged in idle talk.\"*\n\nThis is the wording of the report of 'Abd al-Razzaq.\n\nAnd al-Bursani narrated it, but the others did not mention the hearing. Some of them said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said. And some of them said: From the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"1806","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Khashram: Ibn ‘Uyaynah informed us.\nAnd it was also narrated to us Sa‘id ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu’l-Zinad, from al-A‘raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n“If a man says to another man—while the imam is delivering the khutbah—‘Listen!’ then he has committed a lapse.”\n\nSufyan said: “Abu Hurayrah’s wording *laghayta* is his own dialect; its correct form is *laghawta*.”\n\nAl-Makhzumi said: “If you say to your companion on Friday, while the imam is delivering the khutbah, ‘Listen!’ then you have committed a lapse.”\n\n---"
"1807","Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban, who narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, who informed us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, who narrated to us Sharik ibn Abdullah, on the authority of Atta ibn Yasar, on the authority of Abu Dharr (RA), that he said: \"I entered the mosque on Friday, and the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the khutbah. I sat close to Ubayy ibn Ka'b. When the Prophet (ﷺ) recited Surah Bara'ah (At-Tawbah), I said to Ubayy: 'When was this Surah revealed?' He frowned at me and did not speak to me. Then I waited for an hour, then asked him again, he frowned at me and did not speak to me. Then I waited another hour, asked him again, he frowned at me and did not speak to me. When the Prophet (ﷺ) finished his prayer, I said to Ubayy: 'I asked you, so you frowned at me and did not speak to me.' Ubayy said: 'You have no reward from your prayer except for what you wasted [by speaking during the khutbah, which invalidated the reward of the portion of your prayer spent talking].' So I went to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, I was beside Ubayy while you were reciting Bara'ah, so I asked him when this Surah was revealed. He frowned at me and did not speak to me, then he said: \"You have no reward from your prayer except for what you wasted.\"' The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Ubayy spoke the truth.'\""
"1808","He said: Muhammad bin Abu Zakariya bin Haywayh al-Isfara'ini narrated to us, and he informed me that Ibn Abu Maryam narrated to us the same (hadith)."
"1809","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj, narrated to us Husayn ibn 'Isa al-Hanafi, narrated to us al-Hakam ibn Aban, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the Friday sermon when he recited an ayah (Qur'anic verse). A man sitting next to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (RA) said, 'When was this ayah revealed? Indeed, I have not heard it until now.' 'Abdullah (RA) said, 'Subhanallah (Glory be to Allah).' The man fell silent.\n\nThen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) recited another ayah, and the man said to 'Abdullah (RA) the same thing. 'Abdullah (RA) again said, 'Subhanallah.' When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) finished the prayer, Ibn Mas'ud (RA) said to the man, 'You did not observe the proper etiquette with us.'\n\nThe man said, 'Subhanallah.'\nIbn Mas'ud (RA) then went to the Prophet (ﷺ) and mentioned this to him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, 'Ibn Umm 'Abd has spoken the truth. Ibn Umm 'Abd has spoken the truth.'\""
"1810","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: Usāmah informed me, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), that he said:\n\n“Whoever performs ghusl (ritual bath) on Friday, then applies some of his wife’s perfume if she has any, and wears his best garments, then does not step over people’s necks (i.e., does not push through the crowd) and does not speak idly during the sermon, his (minor) sins between that Friday and the next will be forgiven. But whoever speaks idly or steps over people’s necks, there is no expiation for him.”"
"1811","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Hashim: Narrated to us 'Abdur-Rahman (meaning Ibn Mahdi), from Mu'awiyah (who is Ibn Salih), from Abu Az-Zahiri, who said:\nI was sitting with 'Abdullah ibn Busr on the day of Friday, and he continued to narrate to us until the imam came out. Then a man came stepping over the necks of the people. So he ('Abdullah ibn Busr) said to me: \"A man came stepping over the necks of the people while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the khutbah, and he (the Prophet ﷺ) said to him: 'Sit down, for you have caused annoyance and come late.'\" Abu Bakr (the narrator) said: \"In the khutbah also, there are chapters which I have already mentioned in the Book of the Two Eids.\""
"1812","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya—meaning Ibn Sa‘id—who said: Narrated to us Ibn ‘Ajlan, from Sa‘id ibn Abi Sa‘id, from his father, from ‘Abdullah ibn Wadi‘ah, from Abu Dharr (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n“Whoever performs ghusl on Friday—performing the ghusl well, or purifies himself well—and then wears his best clothes, applies whatever perfume Allah has written for him or the oil of his family, and does not separate between two people, will have his sins forgiven until the next Friday.”\n\nBundar said: “I memorized it from his mouth, from his father.”\n\nAbu Bakr said: “I do not know anyone who followed Bundar in this narration. Al-Jawad’s narration may weaken at times.”"
"1813","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Zuraiq, who reported: Habib al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Three categories of people attend the Friday prayer (Jumu'ah): The first is a man who attends it while engaging in idle, vain talk; his only share from the prayer is the mere act of attendance. The second is a man who attends it with supplication (du'a); he is one who calls upon Allah, so if Allah wills He will grant him his request, and if He wills He will withhold it from him. The third is a man who attends it with dignity, attentive listening, and calmness, who does not step over the neck of a Muslim (i.e., does not push past, trample, or harm a fellow Muslim to secure a spot), and does not harm anyone at all. This attendance serves as an expiation for him until the Jumu'ah that follows it, plus an additional three days; this is because Allah says: 'Whoever comes with a good deed will have ten times the like thereof' [Surah Al-An'am 6:160].\""
"1814","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Hujr, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ja'far, narrated to us al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\"The five daily prayers, and from one Friday prayer to the next, are expiations for what is between them, so long as one does not commit major sins.\""
"1815","**\nNarrated to us Abu Ja'far As-Simnaniy, narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, from Abu Marhum — and he is 'Abd ar-Rahman ibn Maymun — from Sahl ibn Mu'adh ibn Anas al-Juhani, from his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade the act of **al-Hibwah** on the day of Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon."
"1816","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id: on the authority of Ibn 'Ajlan: on the authority of 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb: on the authority of his father: on the authority of his grandfather, who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade buying and selling in the mosques, and that poetry be recited in them, and that lost property be announced in them, and (he forbade) forming circles (of people sitting) on Friday before the prayer.\""
"1817","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy, who said: Mu'awiyah—meaning ibn Hisham—narrated to us, who said: Shiban narrated to us, from Firas, from 'Atiyyah, from Abu Sa'id, who said that the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\"If a man performs wudu' and perfects his purification, then comes to Friday prayer, refraining from idle talk and foolishness until the imam finishes the prayer, it will be an expiation for what was between that Friday and the one before it.\""
"1818","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us al-A'mash, on the authority of Abu Salih, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever performs wudu' on the Day of Jumu'ah and does so well, then comes to Jumu'ah, draws near, remains silent, and listens attentively, his sins between that Jumu'ah and the next will be forgiven, with an additional three days. And whoever plays with pebbles has committed a frivolous act.\""
"1819","Narrated to us by Abdullah bin Sa'id Al-Ashajj, who said: Abu Khalid and Abdah bin Sulaiman, both, narrated to us from Ibn Ishaq. And it was also narrated to us by Harun bin Ishaq, who said: Abu Khalid narrated to us from Muhammad bin Ishaq. And it was also narrated to us by Al-Hasan bin Muhammad, who said: Muhammad bin Ubaid narrated to us, who said: Muhammad bin Ishaq narrated to us. And"
"1820","Muhammad bin Rafi narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, stating: I heard Nafi' saying that Ibn Umar (RA) said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"None of you should make his brother rise from his seat and then sit in it himself.\" I (Nafi') asked Ibn Umar (RA): \"Is this [prohibition] specific to the day of Jumu'ah (Friday)?\" He replied: \"It applies to the day of Jumu"
"1821","There are multiple connected chains of narration for this hadith:\n1. Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who reported: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to him. And (Ya'qub) also narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdah, who informed us: Abd al-Aziz (i.e., al-Darawi), and narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who reported: Khalid (i.e., ibn Abd Allah) narrated to him. All of these narrators reported from Suhail [ibn Abi Salih].\n2. And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, who reported: Jarir narrated to him. And narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu'adh, who reported: Yazid ibn Zura'ih narrated to him, who reported: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to him. They both (Yazid and Rawh) reported: Suhail narrated to us, from his father (Abu Salih), from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"If any one of you gets up from his gathering place, then returns to it, he has a greater right to it than anyone else who sat in it in his absence.\"\nYusuf (ibn Musa) added an extra incident detail: Then a man got up from his gathering, so I sat in his place, then he returned and Abu Salih (RA) made me get up from it.\n\n---"
"1822","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-‘Ala’, narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of ‘Ubayd Allah, on the authority of Nafi‘, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade a man to make his brother stand up from his place and then sit in it himself. Instead, he said: “Make room, expand, and be spacious.”\n\n---"
"1823","###\n Narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Musa, who said: Jarir narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon while standing. Then, a caravan from Sham arrived, and the people dispersed towards it until only twelve men remained with him. Then this verse from Surah Al-Jumu'ah was revealed: \"And when they see a transaction or amusement, they dis"
"1824","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj, narrated to us Abu Khalid, who said Ibn Ishaq informed us: Abu Bakr ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Give the mosques their due right.\"\n\nIt was said: \"What is their due right?\"\nHe (ﷺ) said: \"Two rak'ahs before you sit down.\""
"1825","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-‘Ala’, who said: Sufyan narrated to us, who said: Ibn ‘Ajlan and ‘Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from ‘Amir ibn ‘Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from ‘Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n“When one of you enters the mosque, let him pray two rak‘ahs before sitting.”"
"1826","Abdullah bin Hashim narrated to us, saying: Abdur-Rahman (meaning Ibn Mahdi) narrated to us, from Malik, from Amir bin Abdullah bin Az-Zubayr, with this same chain of narration as the previous one. He added: \"(performing two units of prayer) before sitting down.\""
"1827","**\nNarrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yaḥyā, narrated to us Ibn ʿAjlān.\nAnd narrated to us Abū ʿAmmār, narrated to us al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd (who is the son of Abū Hind).\nAnd narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Abū ʿĀṣim, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ziyād ibn Saʿd.\nAnd narrated to us al-Ṣanʿāniyy, narrated to us al-Muʿtamir, who said: I heard ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah narrating, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd.\nAnd narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Dirhamiyy, narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAdiyy, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq—all of them—from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqiyy, from Abū Qatādah ibn Ribʿiyy (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said,\n“When one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit until he has prayed two rakʿahs.”\n\nThis is the narration of Ibn ʿAjlān.\nIn the narration of Ibn Abī ʿAdiyy, it is: “Whoever enters this mosque…”\nAnd he said: I heard ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqiyy say, and Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq added:\nMuḥammad ibn Isḥāq said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me from ʿĀmir ibn ʿAbd Allāh from ʿAmr ibn Sulaym from Abū Qatādah (RA) from the Prophet (ﷺ) with the same wording."
"1828","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: Narrated to me Usamah, on the authority of Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb al-Juhani, who said:\nI heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah say: We were once with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when he said,\n\"Have you entered the mosque?\"\nI said, \"Yes.\"\nHe said, \"Have you prayed in it?\"\nI said, \"No.\"\nHe said, \"Then go and pray two rak'ahs.\""
"1829","**\nNarrated to us Mūsā ibn ‘Abd al-Raḥmān al-Masrūqī: Narrated to us Ḥusayn—meaning ibn ‘Alī al-Ju‘fī: On the authority of Zā’ida: Narrated to us ‘Amr ibn Yaḥyā al-Anṣārī: Narrated to me Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān: On the authority of ‘Amr ibn Sulaym al-Anṣārī: On the authority of Abū Qatāda (RA), the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\nI entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sitting among the people. I sat down, whereupon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “What prevented you from praying two rak‘ahs before you sat down?” I replied, “O Messenger of Allah, I saw you sitting and the people sitting.” The Prophet (ﷺ) said, “When any one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit down until he has prayed two rak‘ahs.”\n\n---"
"1830","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who narrated to us Sufyan, who said: We memorized this narration from Ibn Ajlan, on the authority of Iyad, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"Marwan was delivering the Friday khutbah, so I stood to offer prayer. The guards came to me to make me sit down, but I refused until I finished my prayer. When I completed the prayer, they came to me and said: 'They almost took action against you, may Allah forgive you.' I replied: 'I will never abandon these two [voluntary rak'ahs] ever again after I heard them from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'\""
"1831","Narrated to us Hatim bin Bakr bin Ghailan al-Dabbi, who said: Isa bin Waqid informed us, who said: Shu'bah informed us, on the authority of Muhammad bin al-Munkadir, on the authority of Jabir bin Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you comes to the mosque while the Imam is delivering the sermon, let him pray two rak'ahs before sitting down.\""
"1832","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibn Al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr and Abu Az-Zubayr, from Jabir (RA), who said:\n\n'Amr said: A man entered the Masjid while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the Khutbah on Friday. Abu Az-Zubayr said: Sulayk Al-Ghatafani entered the Masjid while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the Khutbah on Friday. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: \"Have you prayed?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then pray two rak'ahs.\"\n\nNarrated to us Al-Makhzumi separately with this wording, and he said: \"Then stand up and pray two rak'ahs.\"\n\nHe (Sufyan) also said once after the narration of Abu Az-Zubayr: \"And the name of the man was Sulayk ibn 'Amr Al-Ghatafani.\""
"1833","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, Bishr ibn Mu'adh, and Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam—they all said: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd.\n\nBishr said: Narrated to us 'Amr, while the other two said: On the authority of 'Amr ibn Dinar, on the authority of Jabir (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who narrated: Ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us from Ayyub.\n\nAlso narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu'adh, who narrated: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, who narrated: Ruwh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us.\n\nAlso narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ishaq al-Jawhari, who informed us: Abu 'Asim narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj—all of them—on the authority of 'Amr ibn Dinar, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:\n\n\"A man entered while the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. He (the Prophet) asked, 'Have you prayed?' The man replied, 'No.' The Prophet (ﷺ) said, 'Stand up and pray two rak'ahs.'\"\n\nIn the narration of Ahmad ibn 'Abdah and Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, it is stated: \"Have you prayed, O so-and-so?\"\n\nIn the narration of Abu 'Asim, it is stated: \"Have you prayed two rak'ahs?\" The man replied, \"No.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Pray two rak'ahs.\""
"1834","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi‘, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Razzaq, who said: Ibn Juraij informed us, who said: ‘Amr ibn Dinar informed me, that he heard Jabir ibn ‘Abdullah (RA) say:\n\nA man came while the Prophet (ﷺ) was on the pulpit delivering the Friday sermon. He asked him, “Have you prayed two rak‘ahs?” He said, “No.” He (ﷺ) said, “Then pray two rak‘ahs.”\n\n---"
"1835","**\nNarrated to us ʿAlī ibn Khashram: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus informed us, on the authority of al-Aʿmash, from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir (RA), who said:\nSulayk al-Ghaṭafānī came on the day of Friday while the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. He sat down, so he (ﷺ) said to him:\n“O Sulayk, stand up and pray two rakʿahs, and make them short.”\nThen he (ﷺ) said:\n“If any of you comes on the day of Friday while the Imām is delivering the sermon, let him pray two rakʿahs and make them short.”\n\nAbū Bakr (RA) said:\nThe Prophet (ﷺ), after Sulayk had finished the two rakʿahs, commanded whoever came to Friday while the Imām was delivering the sermon with this commandment—every Muslim who enters the mosque while the Imām is delivering the sermon until the Day of Resurrection.\nHow can any scholar claim that the Prophet (ﷺ) restricted this command to Sulayk al-Ghaṭafānī merely because he entered the mosque in a dishevelled state at the time of the Prophet’s (ﷺ) sermon? The Prophet (ﷺ) issued the command in general terms to whoever enters the mosque while the Imām is delivering the sermon to pray two rakʿahs, after Sulayk had finished his two rakʿahs.\n\nAbū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (RA), the narrator of this report, swore by Allāh that he would never abandon these two rakʿahs after the Prophet’s (ﷺ) command to pray them. Whoever claims that this was specific to Sulayk, or to the one who enters in a dishevelled state at the time of the Prophet’s (ﷺ) sermon, has contradicted the explicit reports of the Prophet (ﷺ). For his (ﷺ) statement:\n“If any of you comes on the day of Friday while the Imām is delivering the sermon, let him pray two rakʿahs,”\nmakes it impossible that he (ﷺ) intended only one person and not others, because the wording “if any of you comes” in Arabic usage cannot apply to a single individual to the exclusion of the collective.\n\nI have recorded the various chains of this report in the chapter on Friday (prayer)."
"1836","Ahmad bin Mani‘, Ziyad bin Ayyub, and Mu’ammal bin Hisham narrated to us, saying: “Isma‘il narrated to us.” Ziyad stated: “Ayyub informed us,” while the other two narrators said: “from Ayyub.” [Ayyub] then said: “I said to Nafi‘: ‘Did Ibn Umar (RA) pray before the Friday (Jumu‘ah) prayer?’ He replied: ‘He certainly used to prolong the voluntary prayer before it, and he would pray two rak‘ahs after it in his home. He also reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform this same practice.’”"
"1837","###\n Narrated to us, narrated to us Abdullah bin Saeed Al-Ashajj, he said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Az-Zuhri, from As-Sa'ib bin Yazid, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had only one Mu'adhdhin. When he went out, the Mu'adhdhin would pronounce the Adhan, and when he sat down, he would pronounce the Iqamah. Abu Bakr and Umar (RA) did"
"1838","Narrated to us by Salim ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us by Waki', on the authority of Jarir ibn Hazim, on the authority of Thabit al-Bunani, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) would descend from the minbar on the day of Jumu'ah, speak to a man and be spoken to by him, and then proceed to his place of prayer (musalla) and perform the prayer.\""
"1839","**\nNarrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, on the authority of Wakee‘ (who said): Narrated to us Ya‘la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi, on the authority of Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa‘, on the authority of his father (Salamah ibn al-Akwa‘, may Allah be pleased with him), who said:\n“We used to pray the Friday (Jumu‘ah) prayer with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when the sun had passed its zenith (zaalat al-shams), then we would return (home) following the shadow (al-fay’).”"
"1840","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah: Narrated to us Abu Dawud: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dhi'b: From Muslim ibn Jundub: From al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam (RA), who said:\n\"We used to pray Jumu'ah (Friday prayer) with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), seeking the early time. It would not be except for the time it takes for one or two footsteps.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \"I do not know whether this Muslim heard (the hadith) from al-Zubayr or not.\""
"1841","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashajj: narrated to us Abu Khalid, on the authority of Humaid, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said:\n\"We used to go early for Jumu'ah (Friday prayer), then take a siesta (qailulah).\""
"1842","Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Mansur, who narrated to us Harmiyy ibn Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah, who narrated to me Abu Khaldah, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik (RA), and Yazid al-Dabbi called out to him on a Friday during the reign of al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, saying: \"O Abu Hamzah! You witnessed the prayers of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and you pray alongside us, so how did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to offer prayer?\" Anas (RA) replied: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would hasten the Friday prayer when severe cold was present, and delay it to a cooler time when severe heat was present.\""
"1843","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm: narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, the scribe of ʿAlī (RA), who said:\nMarwān used to appoint Abū Hurayrah (RA) as his deputy over Madīnah. So he led them in the Friday prayer one day and recited in it: *\"Al-Jumuʿah\"* and *\"If the hypocrites come to you\"* (62:1–7). I said: \"O Abū Hurayrah! You have recited with us a recitation that ʿAlī (RA) recited with us in al-Kūfah.\" He said: \"I heard my beloved Abū al-Qāsim, the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), recite with them.\""
"1844","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm: Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī, from Jaʿfar, in the second narration:\n*\"When the hypocrites come to you...\"* (Qurʾān, Sūrat al-Munāfiqūn, 63:1)"
"1845","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi (may Allah have mercy on them), they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Dhamrah ibn Sa'id, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah ibn Mas'ud (RA), who said:\n\nAl-Dahhak ibn Qays wrote to al-Nu'man ibn Bashir (RA), asking him: \"What did the Prophet (ﷺ) recite on Friday, along with Surah al-Jumu'ah?\"\n\nSo he wrote back to him: \"He would recite *Hal ataka hadith al-ghashiyah* (Surah al-Ghashiyah).\"\n\nAnd al-Makhzumi (RA) said in his narration: \"He asked him what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would recite in the Friday prayer?\"\n\nSo he wrote back to him: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would recite Surah al-Jumu'ah and *Hal ataka hadith al-ghashiyah* (Surah al-Ghashiyah).\""
"1846","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Yusuf, narrated to us Isma'il ibn Abi Uways, narrated to me my father, from Dhamrah ibn Sa'id, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from adh-Dhahhak ibn Qays al-Fihri, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir al-Ansari (RA), who said:\n\nWe asked him, \"What did the Prophet (ﷺ) recite on the day of Friday along with the surah in which Friday is mentioned?\"\n\nHe replied, \"He would recite along with it *Hal ataaka hadeethul ghaashiyah* (Surah al-Ghashiyah).\"\n\n---"
"1847","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us ‘Abdur-Rahman, who said: Narrated to us Shu‘bah, and narrated to us Ya‘qub ibn Ibrahim, who said: Narrated to us ‘Uthman ibn ‘Umar, who informed us Shu‘bah. Also narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan ath-Thaqafi, who said: Narrated to us Sa‘id (meaning Ibn ‘Amir), who said: Narrated to us Shu‘bah, from Ma‘bad ibn Khalid, from Zayd ibn ‘Uqbah, from Samurah ibn Jundub (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to recite in the Friday prayer *Sabbihisma Rabbika al-A‘la* (Surah 87) and *Hal ataka hadithul ghashiyah* (Surah 88).\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: I have written about the combination of ‘Eid and Friday occurring on the same day, and the recitation in them in the Book of the Two ‘Eids."
"1848","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibnul-'Alaa', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I memorized it from Az-Zuhri.\n\nAlso narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad Az-Zuhri and Sa'eed ibn 'Abdur-Rahman Al-Makhzoomi, who both said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I heard Az-Zuhri narrate, on the authority of Abu Salamah ibn 'Abdur-Rahman, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Whoever catches one rak'ah of the prayer has indeed caught the entire prayer.\"*"
"1849","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, on the authority of al-Awza‘i, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Whoever catches one rak‘ah of the prayer has indeed caught the entire prayer.”\n\nAl-Zuhri (RA) said: “We hold that this applies to the Friday prayer as well. So if one catches one rak‘ah of it, let him pray another [rak‘ah] along with it.”"
"1850","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun in Alexandria, on the authority of Al-Walid ibn Muslim, who said: Al-Walid narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Awza'i, who said: Az-Zuhri narrated to me, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever catches one Rak'ah of the Jumu'ah prayer, he has caught the prayer"
"1851","###\n Ahmad bin Abdullah bin Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; he said: Yahya bin Ayub informed us; from Usama bin Zayd al-Laythi; from Ibn Shihab; from Abu Salamah; from Abu Hurairah (RA); who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever catches one Rak'ah of the Jumu'ah prayer, let him pray another one.\" Usama said: And I heard"
"1852","###\n Ahmad bin Mani' narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Husayn narrated to us, on the authority of Abu Sufyan and Salim bin Abi Al-Ja'd, from Jabir (RA), who said: \"While the Prophet (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon on Friday while standing, a caravan from Medina arrived. The companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) rushed towards it, leaving only twelve men with him, among whom were Abu Bakr (RA) and Umar (RA"
"1853","Narrated to us by Abu Khaythamah, Ali ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Harrani; he said: My father narrated to us; he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, who heard it from him, from Abdullah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to a group of people who were neglecting the Jumu'ah (Friday) prayer: \"I have indeed thought of ordering a man to lead the people in prayer, and"
"1854","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm and Muḥammad ibn Maʿmar: They said, \"Narrated to us Abū Dāwūd: Narrated to us Zuhayr, on the authority of Abū Isḥāq, on the authority of Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n'I was about to command something like this,' but Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm said: 'Stay behind.'"
"1855","**\nNarrated to us Mūsā ibn Sahl al-Ramlī: narrated to us al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ Abū Tawbah: narrated to us Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām, from his brother Zayd ibn Salām, who heard Abū Salām al-Ḥabashī say: al-Ḥakam ibn Mināʾ informed me, from Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Let certain people desist from abandoning the Friday prayers, or their hearts will be sealed, and then they will be among the heedless.”"
"1856","###\n Narrated to us by Yunus bin Abd al-A'la as-Sadafi, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that Ibn Abi Dh'ib informed us. In another narration: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Rafi' and Ibn Abd al-Hakam. Ibn Rafi' said: Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Fudayk, who informed us that Ibn Abi Dh'ib informed us. Ibn Abd al-Hakam said: Ibn Abi Fudayk informed us, saying: Narrated to us"
"1857","**\nNarrated to us Salam ibn Junadah: Ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Amr. Also, Salam ibn Junadah narrated to us as well, he said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah al-Laithi, from Ubaydah ibn Sufyan al-Hadrami, from Abu al-Ja'd ad-Damri (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Whoever leaves off the Friday prayer three times without a valid excuse\" — in the narration of Ibn Idris: \"his heart will be sealed\" — and in the narration of Waki': \"he is a hypocrite.\""
"1858","###\n Muhammad ibn al-A'la al-San'ani narrated to us, saying: Al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, saying: I heard Muhammad (ibn 'Amr) say; and Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us, saying: Isma'il narrated to us, saying: Muhammad narrated to us. And Bundar narrated to us, saying: Al-Wahhab (meaning Al-Thaqafi) narrated to us. And Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying:"
"1859","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Ma'di ibn Sulayman, narrated to us Ibn 'Ajlan, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Take heed! Is it possible that one of you takes his flock of sheep to a distance of one or two miles, and then finds no pasture for them at that distance, so he ascends higher until Friday comes, but he does not attend it? Then Friday comes again, but he does not attend it? Then Friday comes again, but he does not attend it until a seal is set upon his heart.\""
"1860","**\nNarrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: On the authority of ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Umar, on the authority of Nāfi‘, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) who said:\n\"The people of Qubā’ used to perform the Friday prayer along with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\"\n\n‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Umar (RA) said:\n\"The Anṣār used to attend the Friday prayer with ‘Umar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (RA), then they would return and take a siesta at his residence due to the heat and the early performance of the prayer. The people used to do that.\""
"1861","**\nNarrated to us Bundaar, narrated to us Abu Daawood and Yazeed ibn Haaroon, both of them said; and narrated to us Abu Moosa, narrated to us Yazeed ibn Haaroon — Hammaam —.\n\nAlso narrated to us Abu Moosa, narrated to us Abu Daawood, narrated to us Hammaam.\n\nAlso narrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani’, narrated to us Abu ‘Ubaydah — meaning al-Haddaad — and narrated to us Hammaam, and narrated to us Salam ibn Junaadah, narrated to us Wakee’ — from Hammaam ibn Yahya — from Qataadah — from Qudaamah ibn Wabarah al-‘Ujaylee — from Samurah ibn Jundub (RA) — that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Whoever leaves the Friday prayer without a valid excuse, let him give in charity a dinar. If he does not find a dinar, then half a dinar.”\n\nIbn Mani’ did not say: “al-‘Ujaylee.”\n\nIn the narration of Wakee’: “Whoever misses the Friday prayer, let him give in charity a dinar or half a dinar.”\n\nNarrated to us Moosa, narrated to us Abu Daawood, narrated to us Hammaam with this chain in a similar manner, and he did not say: “al-‘Ujaylee.”\n\nNarrated to us Moosa, narrated to us Abu Daawood, narrated to us Hammaam, informed us Hammaam ibn Yahya — from Qataadah — with a similar narration.\n\n**COMMENTARY:**\nThis hadith is graded as **Sahih** by al-Albaani in *Saheeh Abi Daawood* (1052). However, the commentator notes that he does not know of Qataadah having heard from Qudaamah ibn Wabarah, nor does he have knowledge of the grading of Qudaamah. Despite this, the hadith is supported by other chains and is generally accepted as authentic.\n\n---\n\n**\nNarrated to us Moosa, narrated to us Abu Daawood, narrated to us Hammaam — from Hammaam ibn Yahya — from Qataadah — from Qudaamah ibn Wabarah al-‘Ujaylee — from Samurah ibn Jundub (RA) — that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Whoever leaves the Friday prayer without a valid excuse, let him give in charity a dinar. If he does not find a dinar, then half a dinar.”"
"1862","###\n Narrated to us by Bishr bin Mu'adh al-'Aqadi, who said: Narrated to us by Nasih bin al-'Ala', who said: Ibn Abi 'Ammar, the freed slave of Banu Hashim, narrated to me, saying: I passed by Abdur-Rahman bin Samurah on a Friday while he was at the river of Umm 'Abdullah, and the water was flowing over his children and freed slaves. I said to him: \"O Abu Sa'id, (is this"
"1863","**\nNarrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, narrated to us Sufyān ibn Ḥabīb, from Khālid al-Ḥaḏḏāʾ, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū al-Malīḥ, from his father Muḥammad, who said: I witnessed the Prophet (ﷺ) at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, and they were afflicted by rain on a Friday such that the lower parts of their sandals were not wetted. So the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered them to pray in their places (i.e., not to come to the mosque for Friday prayer).\n\nAbū Bakr said: No one mentioned on the day of Friday other than Sufyān ibn Ḥabīb."
"1864","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: 'Abbad — meaning ibn 'Abbad — informed us, on the authority of Yusuf ibn Musa, on the authority of Jarir, all of them narrating from 'Asim, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith, that Ibn 'Abbas commanded the mu'adhdhin to give the call to prayer on a Friday when it was a rainy day, saying:\n\n\"Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\"\n\nThen he said to him: \"Proclaim to the people that they should pray in their homes.\"\n\nThe people said to him: \"What is this that you have done?\"\n\nHe replied: \"One better than I has done this. Do you order me to bring the people out, or to burden them with carrying filth from their paths to your mosque?\"\n\nThis is the narration of Ahmad ibn 'Abdah.\n\nYusuf said: \"From 'Abdullah ibn al-Harith — a man from the people of Basrah, a relative of Ibn Sirin — and he said: 'To bring the people out and burden them with carrying filth from their paths to your mosque.'\""
"1865","**\nNarrated to us Mu'ammal ibn Hisham, who said: Narrated to us Isma'il, on the authority of 'Abd al-Hamid, the retainer of al-Ziyadi, on the authority of 'Abd Allah ibn al-Harith, that Ibn 'Abbas (RA) said to his caller to prayer (mu'adhdhin) on a rainy day:\n\n\"If you say: 'I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah,' then do not say: 'Come to the prayer,' but say: 'Pray in your houses.'\"\n\nThe people seemed to find this strange, so he said:\n\n\"Why do you find this strange? Indeed, one better than me has done this. Verily, the Friday prayer is obligatory (ʿazmah), but I disliked to send you out so that you would walk in the mud and slippery places.\""
"1866","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, Abu 'Asim narrated to us, 'Abbad (who is Ibn Mansur) narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on a rainy Friday: \"Pray in your dwellings.\""
"1867","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed us, on the authority of 'Umar ibn 'Ata', who said: Nafi' ibn Jubayr sent me to al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid to ask him. So I asked him, and he said: \"Yes, I prayed Jumu'ah in the al-Maqsura with Mu'awiyah. When he finished the prayer, I stood up to pray. He sent for me and I came to him. He said to me: 'When you pray Jumu'ah, do not connect it with another prayer unless you leave the mosque or speak. For indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that.'\"\""
"1868","###\n Yusuf bin Musa narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: Umar bin Atiyyah bin Abi Al-Khuwar informed me that Nafi' bin Jubayr sent him to Sa'id bin Yazid to ask him about something he had witnessed from Mu'awiyah (RA). Sa'id said: \"I prayed with him (Mu'awiyah) in the enclosed area (Al-Maqsurah). When I stood up to pray in my place, he said to me:"
"1869","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya; he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; he said: Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, and Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to, when he prayed the Friday prayer, enter his house and pray two rak'ahs."
"1870","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli: al-Walid narrated; Malik said: Nafi‘ narrated to me, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) that he saw the Prophet (ﷺ) praying two rak‘ahs in his house after the Friday prayer and after the Maghrib prayer."
"1871","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi, they said: Narrated to us Sufyan.\n\nAlso narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, he said: On the authority of Ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father (Abdullah ibn Umar), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pray two rak'ahs after al-Jumu'ah."
"1872","Ali ibn Hajar narrated to us, Asim ibn Suwayd ibn Amir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Musa ibn al-Harith al-Ta'i, from his father [Musa ibn al-Harith], from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the Banu Amr ibn Awf on a Wednesday, and he observed things there that he had not seen before in their date palm groves. He (ﷺ) said: \"If only you, when you come for your Eid on this day, would remain until you hear my words.\" They replied: \"Yes, by our fathers and mothers, O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\" Then when Friday arrived, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) led them in the Jumu'ah prayer, after which he prayed two voluntary rak'ahs in the mosque. He (ﷺ) had not previously been seen praying two rak'ahs after the Jumu'ah prayer in the mosque; he would return to his home before that day. Thus, he mentioned this hadith."
"1873","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: 'Abd al-'Aziz — meaning Ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi — informed us; and 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' narrated to us, who said: Sufyan narrated to us, both of them from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, *\"Pray four rak'ahs after the Friday prayer.\"*\n\n'Abd al-Jabbar added: *\"The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded them to pray four rak'ahs after the Friday prayer.\"*"
"1874","**\nNarrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi (RA), both said:\nNarrated to us Sufyan.\n\nAlso narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, who said:\nNarrated to us Jarir.\n\nAlso narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, who said:\nNarrated to us Waki' from Sufyan, all of them from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: *\"Whoever among you prays after the Friday prayer, let him pray four rak'ahs after it.\"*\n\n---"
"1875","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah and al-Hasan ibn Qaz'ah, they said: Narrated to us al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman, who narrated to us Abu Hazim, from Suhayl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi (RA), who said:\n\"We used to gather with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then return home to have our lunch and take a midday nap.\""
"1876","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqī: Narrated to us 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Sahl ibn Sa'd (RA), who said:\n\"We would not have our midday meal (*ghadā*) nor take a siesta (*qīlūl*) except after the Friday (*Jumu'ah*) prayer.\""
"1877","**\nNarrated to us Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah: Narrated to us al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān: Narrated to us Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, on the authority of Anas ibn Mālik (RA), who said:\n*\"We would gather with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then return home to take a siesta (qaylūl).\"*"
"1878","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya bin Fayyad al-Basri, who said: Narrated to us Sa'id bin Anbasah, known as al-Qattan, who said: Narrated to us Abdullah bin Busr, who said: I saw Abdullah bin Busr, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he would finish the Friday prayer, he would leave the mosque for a considerable distance, then return to the mosque and pray whatever Allah willed him to pray. I said to him, \"May Allah have mercy"
"1879","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, Qurrah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah Ad-Duba'iyy, who said:\nI said to Ibn 'Abbas: \"I have a vessel in which Nabidh (a drink made from dates or raisins) is prepared for me, and I drink from it. But if I sit long with people, I fear being exposed because of its sweetness.\"\n\nHe said: \"A delegation of 'Abdul-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: 'Welcome to the delegation, not as those who are disgraced or regretful.'\n\nThey said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Between us and you are the idolaters of Mudar, and we do not reach you except in the sacred months. So instruct us with an action that, if we hold to it, we will enter Paradise by it, and we will call to it those behind us.'\n\nHe said: 'I command you with four things and forbid you from four: Faith in Allah — and do you know what is faith in Allah?'\n\nThey said: 'Allah and His Messenger know best.'\n\nHe said: 'To testify that none has the right to be worshipped except Allah, to establish the prayer, to give the zakah, and to fast Ramadhan, and to give the fifth of the spoils of war.\n\nAnd I forbid you from Nabidh in the Duba', Naqir, Hantam, and Muzaffat.'\""
"1880","**\nNarrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Wakīʿ, on the authority of Ḥanẓalah al-Jumaḥī, on the authority of ʿIkrimah ibn Khālid al-Makhzūmī, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Islam has been built upon five [pillars]: testifying that there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakāh, performing the pilgrimage to the House, and fasting the month of Ramaḍān.\""
"1881","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli: Narrated to us Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal: Narrated to us 'Asim—meaning ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab—who said: I heard my father narrate, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said something to the same effect."
"1882","**\nNarrated to me ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr al-Saʿdī who said: Narrated to us Ismāʿīl—meaning Ibn Jaʿfar—who said: Narrated to us Abū Suhayl from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n*\"When the month of Ramaḍān comes, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of the Fire are closed, and the devils are chained.\"*\n\nAbū Bakr (the narrator) said: Abū Suhayl is the uncle of Mālik ibn Anas."
"1883","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Ala' bin Kurayb, who said: Abu Bakr bin Ayyash narrated to us, on the authority of Al-A'mash, on the authority of Abu Salih, on the authority of Abu Huraira (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When the first night of Ramadan comes, the devils and the rebellious jinn are chained up, the doors of the Fire are locked so that none of them is opened, and the doors of Paradise are opened"
"1884","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Yahya ibn Hakim, both said:\nNarrated to us Abu 'Amir who said:\nNarrated to us Kathir ibn Zayd who narrated to me 'Amr ibn Tamim who narrated to me his father that he heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) saying:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"This month of yours has been made to overshadow (i.e., precede) you by the oath of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). There has not passed by the Muslims a month better for them than it, nor has there passed by the hypocrites a month worse for them than it, by the oath of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\n\nRewards and voluntary acts of worship are recorded for a believer before he enters it, and his burdens and hardships are recorded before he enters it. That is because the believer prepares in it the strength for spending on acts of worship, while the hypocrite prepares in it following the heedlessness of the believers and pursuing their faults. So it is a gain which the believer acquires.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Yahya.\nBundar said, \"So it is a gain for the believers, which the transgressor takes advantage of.\"\n\n'Amr ibn Tamim—he is called: the freed slave of Banu Rummanah, a Medinan."
"1885","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', who narrated to us Zaid bin Hubab, who narrated to me 'Amr bin Hamzah Al-Qaysi, who narrated to us Khalaf Abu Al-Rabi', the Imam of the mosque of Ibn Abi 'Arubah, who narrated to us Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"You will be greeted, and you will greet,\" repeating this three times. Then 'Umar bin Al-Khattab (RA) said"
"1886","**\nNarrated to us Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani, narrated to us Sahl ibn Hammad Abu 'Attab, and it was conveyed to us Sa'id ibn Abi Yazid, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yusuf, they both said: Narrated to us Jarir ibn Ayyub al-Bajali, from al-Sha'bi, from Nafi' ibn Burdah, from Abu Mas'ud (RA), who said: Abu al-Khattab al-Ghifari said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and Sa'id ibn Abi Yazid said: From Abu Mas'ud (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), and this is the narration of Abu al-Khattab.\n\nHe said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say one day when Ramadan had begun:\n\n*\"If the servants knew what Ramadan was, my nation would wish that the entire year were Ramadan.\"*\n\nA man from Khuza'ah said: \"O Prophet of Allah, tell us more.\" He said:\n\n*\"Verily, Paradise is adorned for Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the year's end. When the first day of Ramadan arrives, a wind blows from beneath the Throne, rustling the leaves of Paradise. The wide-eyed maidens (Hur al-'Ayn) look at that and say: 'O Lord, grant us spouses from Your servants in this month who will delight our eyes with them and they will delight their eyes with us.'*\n\n*There is no servant who fasts a single day of Ramadan except that he is granted a spouse from the wide-eyed maidens in a pavilion of pearl, as Allah has described: 'Wide-eyed maidens, sheltered in their tents' (Quran 55:72). For each woman, there are seventy garments, none of which is the same color as another. She is given seventy kinds of perfume, none of whose fragrance resembles another. For each of these women, there are seventy thousand servants to attend to her needs, and seventy thousand male servants. With each servant is a golden platter containing food whose taste becomes more delightful with each bite, unlike the first. For each woman, there are seventy beds of red ruby, each bed having seventy mattresses with brocade linings. Above each mattress are seventy thrones. Her husband is granted the like of that on a bed of red ruby inlaid with pearls, upon which are two golden bracelets. This is for every day he fasts in Ramadan, besides the reward for his good deeds.\"*\n\nJarir ibn Ayyub al-Bajali sometimes differed from al-Firyabi (in the wording of the hadith).\n\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, from Qutaybah, narrated to us Jarir ibn Ayyub, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from Rafi' ibn Burdah al-Hamdani, from a man from Ghifar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said something similar up to his words: *\"Wide-eyed maidens, sheltered in their tents.\"*"
"1887","**\nNarrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr al-Saʿdī, narrated to us Yūsuf ibn Ziyād, narrated to us Hammām ibn Yaḥyā, from ʿAlī ibn Zayd ibn Judʿān, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Salmān (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) delivered a sermon to us on the last day of Shaʿbān. He said:\n“O people! A great and blessed month has drawn near you. In it is a night that is better than a thousand months. Allāh has made its fasting obligatory and its night’s standing optional. Whoever draws near to Allāh in it with a virtuous deed is like one who fulfills an obligatory duty in another time. Whoever fulfills an obligatory duty in it is like one who fulfills seventy obligatory duties in another time.\nIt is the month of patience, and the reward of patience is Paradise. It is the month of mutual compassion. In it the sustenance of the believer increases.\nWhoever gives ifṭār to a fasting person in it, his sins are forgiven, his neck is freed from the Fire, and he receives a reward like his without any reduction in the reward of the fasting person.”\n\nThey said: “Not all of us can find what to give as ifṭār to the fasting person.”\nHe said: “Allāh grants this reward to whoever gives ifṭār to a fasting person with a single date, a sip of water, or a sip of milk.\nIt is a month whose beginning is mercy, its middle is forgiveness, and its end is freedom from the Fire.\nWhoever lightens the burden of his slave in it, Allāh will forgive him and free him from the Fire.\nMake abundant in it four matters:\nTwo matters with which you will please your Lord, and two matters without which you cannot do without.\nAs for the two matters with which you will please your Lord: testifying that there is no god but Allāh and seeking His forgiveness.\nAs for the two matters without which you cannot do without: asking Allāh for Paradise and seeking refuge with Him from the Fire.\nWhoever gives food to a fasting person in it, Allāh will give him to drink from my Cistern such that he will never thirst until he enters Paradise.”"
"1888","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who said: I was informed by Ibn Wahb, who said: Sulayman (i.e., Ibn Bilal) informed me, from Kathir ibn Zayd, from Al-Walid ibn Rabah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ascended the pulpit and said: \"Ameen, Ameen, Ameen.\" It was said to him: \"O Messenger of Allah, why are you saying this?\""
"1889","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Imran al-‘Abidiyy: narrated to us Ibraheem ibn Sa‘d, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Abdullah, from Ibn ‘Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was the most generous of people in doing good, and he was most generous during the month of Ramadan. He would continue to increase in generosity until it ended. Jibril (AS) would come to him and review the Qur’an with him. So when Jibril (AS) met him, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was more generous in doing good than the blowing wind.\""
"1890","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar; Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; Ata informed me, from Abu Salih al-Zayyat, from Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Fasting is a shield [junnah].\""
"1891","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, he said: Ibn Abi 'Adiyy narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, Sa'eed — and he is the son of Abu Hind — narrated to us, from Muttarif, who said:\n\nI entered upon 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As, so he called for milk to drink. I said: \"Indeed, I am fasting.\" So he said: \"Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n'Fasting is a shield from the Fire, like the shield of one of you from battle.'\n\nAnd he said: 'And a good fast is fasting three days of every month.'\""
"1892","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī: narrated to us Ibn Wahb: informed me Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim: from Sayf ibn Abī Sayf: from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān: from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf: from Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (RA), who said:\nI heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say,\n“Fasting is a shield as long as it is not breached.”"
"1893","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bishar, who narrated to us Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith, who narrated to us Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub, who said: I heard Abu Nasr al-Hilali, on the authority of Raja' ibn Haywah, on the authority of Abu Umamah (RA), who narrated: I said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), guide me to a righteous deed.\" He (ﷺ) replied: \"Adhere to fasting, for there is no deed comparable to it.\" Abu Bakr stated: \"This Muhammad ibn Abi Ya'qub is the one whom Shu'bah described as the chief (sayyid) of the Banu Tamim.\""
"1894","**\nNarrated to us ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, on the authority of Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Abū Salamah, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Whoever fasts the month of Ramaḍān out of faith and seeking reward, his past sins will be forgiven. And whoever stands in prayer on the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr) out of faith and seeking reward, his past sins will be forgiven.\"*"
"1895","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Abu Dawud Sulayman ibn Dawud, narrated to us Aban—meaning ibn Yazid al-‘Attar—on the authority of Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, on the authority of Zayd ibn Abi Salam, on the authority of Abu Salam, on the authority of al-Harith al-Ash‘ari (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Indeed Allah revealed to Yahya ibn Zakariyya five words, commanding him to act upon them and to command the Children of Israel to act upon them. It was as if he hesitated in conveying them, so ‘Isa (AS) came to him and said, ‘Indeed Allah has commanded you with five words that you should act upon them and command the Children of Israel to act upon them. So either you convey them to them, or I will convey them.’ Yahya said, ‘O my brother, do not do so, for I fear that you will precede me with them, and I will be struck down or punished.’\n\nSo he gathered the Children of Israel in Bayt al-Maqdis until the mosque was filled, and they sat on the balconies. Then he addressed them, saying, ‘Indeed Allah has revealed to me five words that I should act upon them and command the Children of Israel to act upon them:\n\nFirst: Do not associate anything with Allah. For the example of one who associates partners with Allah is like a man who buys a slave with his own gold or silver, then gives him a house to live in and says, ‘Work and give your earnings to me.’ But the slave works and gives his earnings to another besides his master. Which of you would be pleased if his slave did that? Indeed, Allah created you and provided for you, so do not associate anything with Him.\n\nAnd when you stand for prayer, do not turn your necks. For Allah turns His Face to the face of His slave as long as he does not turn away.\n\nAnd I command you with fasting. Its example is like a man among a group of people with a pouch of musk; all of them love to find its fragrance. Indeed, fasting is more beloved to Allah than the fragrance of musk.\n\nAnd I command you with charity. Its example is like a man who was captured by the enemy, who tied his hands to his neck and brought him close to strike his neck. So he said, ‘Will you accept that I ransom myself from you?’ And he kept giving a little and a lot until he ransomed himself.\n\nAnd I command you with much remembrance of Allah. The example of remembrance of Allah is like a man being pursued by the enemy quickly on his trail until he reaches a fortified fortress and secures himself within it. Likewise, the slave does not escape from Shaytan except by the remembrance of Allah.’\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said, ‘And I command you with five things that Allah has commanded me with: [They are] the Jama‘ah (congregation), listening, obedience, migration, and jihad in the path of Allah. Whoever separates from the Jama‘ah by even a handspan, he has indeed cast off the bonds of Islam and faith from his neck, unless he repents. And whoever claims the claim of Jahiliyyah, he is from the fuel of Hellfire.’\n\nIt was said, ‘O Messenger of Allah, even if he fasts and prays?’ He said, ‘Even if he fasts and prays. Call yourselves by the claim of Allah, Who named you the believers, the Muslims, the slaves of Allah.’”"
"1896","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Tasneem, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad — meaning Ibn Bakr al-Bursaani — who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who said: ‘Ataa’ informed me, from Abu Saalih al-Zayyaat, that he heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) say that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Allah (SWT) says: Every deed of the son of Adam is for him, except fasting. It is for Me, and I shall reward for it. Fasting is a shield. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad (ﷺ), the breath of the fasting person on the Day of Resurrection is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk. The fasting person has two moments of joy: when he breaks his fast, he rejoices in his breaking, and when he meets his Lord, he rejoices in his fast.\"*"
"1897","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi, on the authority of Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Every deed of the son of Adam is for him, the good deed receives tenfold to seven hundredfold reward. Allah says: 'Except fasting, for it is for Me, and I will reward it.' He leaves his food and drink for My sake, and he leaves his pleasure for My sake, and he leaves his wife for My sake. The odor of the mouth of the fasting person is more pleasant to Allah than the fragrance of musk. And the fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord.\""
"1898","**\nNarrated to us Bishr ibn Hilāl: narrated to us ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī: he said, I heard Maʿn ibn Muḥammad narrating, on the authority of Saʿīd al-Maqburī, who said:\nI was with Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī and Abū Hurayrah in al-Baqīʿ, when Abū Hurayrah narrated to us from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said:\n“The one who eats and is grateful is like the fasting, patient person.”\nHe (Abū Hurayrah) said: the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n“Allāh, Mighty and Majestic, said: ‘Every deed of the son of Ādam is for him, except fasting; it is for Me, and I shall reward it. He leaves his food, drink, and desires for My sake.’”"
"1899","**\nNarrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Bashshār ibn Manṣūr al-Sulamī (RA), who said: Narrated to us ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī, on the authority of Maʿn ibn Muḥammad, who said: I heard Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī (RA) say: I heard Abū Hurayrah (RA) in this Baqīʿ saying: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said something to the same effect.\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) (RA) said: The two chains of narration are both *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Saʿīd al-Maqburī and from Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī, both from Abū Hurayrah (RA). Do you not hear al-Maqburī say: “I was with Ḥanẓalah ibn ʿAlī and Abū Hurayrah in the Baqīʿ”?\n\n---"
"1900","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibraheem ad-Dawraqi, who said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us.\n\nAlso narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mundhir, who said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, who said: Dirar ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) and Abu Sa'eed (RA), who both said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Allah (SWT) says: 'Fasting is for Me, and I will reward it.' The fasting person has two joys: when he breaks his fast, he rejoices, and when he meets his Lord, he rejoices in his reward. By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad (ﷺ), the odor of the mouth of the fasting person is more pleasant to Allah than the fragrance of musk.'\"\n\n(Ya'qub ad-Dawraqi did not say: \"when he meets his Lord, he rejoices in his reward.\")"
"1901","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us 'Abdur-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi, who informed us 'Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i, on the authority of Abu Mujahid, from Abu Midlah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"There are three whose supplication is not rejected:\n1. The fasting person until he breaks his fast,\n2. A just ruler,\n3. And the supplication of the oppressed, which Allah raises above the clouds and opens for it the gates of the heavens. The Lord, Mighty and Sublime, says: 'By My might and majesty, I will surely aid you, even if it be after some time.'\"\n\nAbu Mujahid is this man whose name is Sa'd al-Ta'i, and Abu Midlah is the freed slave of Abu Hurayrah (RA). 'Amr ibn Qays is one of the worshippers of this world."
"1902","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'di, narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi and others, on the authority of Abu Hazim, on the authority of Sahl ibn Sa'd (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"There is a gate in Paradise called al-Rayyan, which the fasting will enter, and none will enter it besides them. When the last of them has entered, it will be closed, and whoever enters will drink, and whoever drinks will never again be thirsty.\""
"1903","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb, narrated to us my uncle, who informed me on the authority of 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr—he is Ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj—from Yazid, the freed slave of Salamah—he is Ibn Abi 'Ubayd—from Salamah ibn al-Akwa' (RA), who said:\n\n\"We were in Ramadan during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Whoever wished would fast, and whoever wished would break the fast and make expiation by feeding a needy person. This continued until the verse was revealed: *'So whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it.'* [Quran 2:185]\""
"1904","**\nNarrated to us Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā al-Qurashī, (who said): My paternal uncle ʿUbayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to me; (he said): Ismāʿīl narrated to us; on the authority of Abū Isḥāq; on the authority of al-Barāʾ (RA), who said:\nWhen one of the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ) was fasting and the time for breaking the fast arrived, but he went to sleep before breaking his fast, he would neither eat anything that night nor the following day until sunset. Indeed, Qays ibn Ṣirma had been fasting. When the time for breaking the fast arrived, he came to his wife and said, “Do you have any food?” She replied, “No, but I will go and look.” So she went out to seek food for him. Meanwhile, he continued working that day until sleep overcame him. His wife then came and said, “May you be deprived!” He then spent the night in that state. When midday came the next day, he fainted. When this was mentioned to the Prophet (ﷺ), this verse was revealed:\n“It has been made permissible for you, on the night of fasting, to go to your wives [for intimacy].” (Al-Baqarah 2:187)\nThey rejoiced greatly at this. Then he (ﷺ) said: “Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct from the black thread of dawn.” (Al-Baqarah 2:187)"
"1905","Narrated to us Al-Rabi' bin Sulayman Al-Muradi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Yونس informed me from Ibn Shihab, who said: Salim informed me that Abdullah bin Umar (RA) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"When you see the crescent (of the month of Ramadan), fast, and when you see it (the crescent of Shawwal), break your fast. If it is hidden from you, then estimate it.\""
"1906","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri, who said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, who said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, who said: Nafi' narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Indeed, Allah has made the crescent moons as fixed times, so when you see it, fast, and when you see it, break your fast. If it is obscured from you, then estimate it, and know that the month does not exceed thirty days.\"\n\n---"
"1907","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Hujr al-Sa'di, narrated to us Isma'il — meaning ibn Ja'far — on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said,\n\"The month is twenty-nine nights, so do not fast until you sight it, and do not break your fast until you sight it, unless clouds obscure it from you. If it is obscured from you, then estimate it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"Isma'il ibn Ja'far was one of the most reliable preservers of hadith in his time.\"\n\n---"
"1908","**\nNarrated to me Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, and he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), in a similar narration to the hadith of Ibn 'Umar, that he said:\n*\"If it is obscured for you, then count thirty (days).\"*"
"1909","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walīd, narrated to us Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah, narrated to us Ibn Fuḍayl, narrated to us ʿĀṣim ibn Muḥammad al-ʿUmarī, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n*\"The month is like this and this and this\"*—meaning thirty days—*\"and the month is like this and this\"*—and he held up three fingers—*\"and the month is like this\"*—and he held up two fingers. Then in the third instance, he closed his fist, leaving one finger raised. Then he said: *\"If it is obscured from you, then complete thirty [days].\"*\n\nIn the narration of Ibn Fuḍayl: Then he clapped his hands together and held back one of his fingers, saying: *\"If it is obscured from you, then [complete] thirty [days].\"*"
"1910","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Hashim, who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi, on the authority of Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Abu Qays, who said:\nI heard Aisha (RA) say:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would be very cautious about the crescent moon of Sha'ban more than any other month. Then he would fast upon seeing the crescent moon of Ramadan. If it was obscured from him, he would count thirty days (of Sha'ban) and then fast.\""
"1911","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Mansur, on the authority of Rib‘i ibn Hirash, on the authority of Hudhayfah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “Do not advance this month (Ramadan) before sighting the crescent moon, or completing the number (of days).”"
"1912","Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan al-Bazzar narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Kathir narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak, who said: I entered upon Ikrimah on the day of doubt in Ramadan, and he was eating. He said, \"Come near and eat.\" I said, \"I am fasting.\" He said, \"By Allah, you will come near.\" I said, \"Then narrate to me.\" He said: Ibn Abbas narrated to"
"1913","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-Wahhab, on the authority of Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"The month is twenty-nine [days].\" He then held up his thumb.\n\n[He (ﷺ) said:] \"So do not fast until you sight it, and do not break your fast until you sight it. If it is obscured from you, then estimate it.\"\n\n---"
"1914","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'eed al-Ashajj (may Allah have mercy on him) more times than I can count, narrated to us Abu Khalid, on the authority of 'Amr ibn Qays, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Sila ibn Zufar (RA), who said:\n\"We were with 'Ammar (RA) when a roasted sheep was brought to him. He said, 'Eat.' Some of the people turned away and said, 'I am fasting.' 'Ammar (RA) then said, 'Whoever fasts on a day about which there is doubt (whether it is Ramadan or not) has indeed disobeyed Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ).'\""
"1915","**\nNarrated to us Bundar, who said: narrated to us Muhammad (meaning Ibn Ja'far), who said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Abu al-Bakhtari say: We witnessed the crescent of Ramadan while we were at Dhat 'Irq. He said: So we sent a man to Ibn 'Abbas to ask him. Ibn 'Abbas said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed Allah has made it easy for you to sight the crescent. So if it is obscured from you, then complete the number (of days).\"\n\nNarrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, who said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, who said: narrated to us Shu'bah with the same chain."
"1916","**\nNarrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr al-Saʿdī: Na Ismāʿīl—meaning Ibn Jaʿfar—on the authority of Muḥammad—meaning Ibn Abī Ḥarmalah—on the authority of Kurayb, that Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith sent him to Muʿāwiyah in al-Shām.\n\nHe said: So I arrived in al-Shām and completed her errand. The new moon of Ramaḍān appeared while I was in al-Shām, and we sighted the crescent on the night of Friday. The people saw it, fasted, and Muʿāwiyah fasted.\n\nI then came to al-Madīnah at the end of the month. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās asked me about the crescent and said, “When did you see the crescent?”\n\nI replied, “We sighted it on the night of Friday.”\n\nHe asked, “You yourself saw it on the night of Friday?”\n\nI said, “Yes, I saw it on the night of Friday, and the people saw it, fasted, and Muʿāwiyah fasted.”\n\nHe said, “But we sighted it on the night of Saturday, so we will continue fasting until we complete thirty days or we sight it again.”\n\nI asked, “Do you not suffice with the sighting of Muʿāwiyah and his fasting?”\n\nHe replied, “No. This is not how the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) commanded us.”"
"1917","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar Bundār and Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, both said: Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Bundār said: Shuʿbah narrated to us, and Yaḥyā said: From Shuʿbah, from Jabbālah ibn Suḥaym, who said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar narrate from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said, “The month is twenty-nine [days].”"
"1918","**\nNarrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faraniyy, and Ahmad ibn Mani', and Mu'ammal ibn Hisham—they said: Narrated to us Isma'il—he is Ibn 'Ulayyah—he informed us Ayyub, and al-Za'faraniyy and Mu'ammal said: On the authority of Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The month is twenty-nine days.\""
"1919","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from Ḥusayn, from ʿAmr bin Murrah, from Abū Al-Bakhtarī, who said: \"We set out for ʿUmrah. When we descended at the valley of Nakhlah, we saw the crescent moon. Some of the people said, 'It is three nights old,' while others said, 'It is two nights old.' Then we met Ibn"
"1920","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad bin al-Walid (RA), from Marwan — meaning Ibn Mu'awiyah (RA) — from Isma'il (RA).\n\nAnd narrated to us 'Abdah bin 'Abdillah (RA), he informed us Muhammad — meaning Ibn Bishr (RA) — from Isma'il bin Abi Khalid (RA), from Muhammad bin Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas (RA), from his father (Sa'd bin Abi Waqqas (RA)), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: *\"The month is like this, and like this, and like this.\"* — indicating with his fingers three times. Then in the third time, he clenched his fingers.\n\nIn the narration of Muhammad bin Bishr (RA), he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to us while saying: *\"The month is like this, and like this, and like this.\"* — and then he clenched his fingers on the third time."
"1921","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to me Umar ibn Yunus, narrated to us Ikrimah ibn Ammar, narrated to me Simak Abu Zumayl, narrated to me Abdullah ibn Abbas, narrated to me from Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) who said:\n\nWhen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) separated himself from his wives, I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, you were only in seclusion for twenty-nine days.\"\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Indeed, the month can be twenty-nine days.\""
"1922","**\nNarrated to me Ahmad ibn Mani', narrated to us Ibn Abi Zaydah.\nAnd narrated to us Ali ibn Muslim, who said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Zaydah, who informed me: Isa ibn Dinari.\nAnd narrated to us Bundar, who said: narrated to us Ahmad and Uthman ibn Umar, who both said: narrated to us Isa ibn Dinari, from his father, from Amr ibn al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said:\n\n*\"When I fasted with the Prophet (ﷺ) twenty-nine days, it was more frequent than when I fasted with him thirty days.\"*\n\nAli ibn Muslim said: *\"Amr ibn al-Harith ibn al-Mustaliq.\"*\nBundar said: *\"From Ibn al-Harith, and he did not name him.\"*"
"1923","**\nNarrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān al-ʿIjlī, narrated to us Abū Usāmah, who said: Zāʾidah narrated to us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), who said:\nAn Arab from the desert came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"I sighted the crescent moon last night.\"\nHe (ﷺ) asked, \"Do you bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger?\"\nThe man replied, \"Yes.\"\nHe (ﷺ) then said, \"O so-and-so, stand up and call the people to prayer, and let them fast tomorrow.\""
"1924","**\nNarrated to us Mūsā ibn ‘Abd al-Raḥmān al-Masrūqī: On the authority of Ḥusayn ibn ‘Alī al-Ju‘fī, from Zā’ida, with this chain and similar wording. He said: The Prophet (ﷺ) ordered Bilāl to give the call to prayer for the people."
"1925","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani', narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us Husayn, on the authority of al-Sha'bi, who narrated to me Adi ibn Hatim (RA), who said:\n\"When the verse, *'And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread'* [Qur'an 2:187], was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n'It is the whiteness of the day from the blackness of the night.'\""
"1926","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Mutarrif, on the authority of 'Amir, on the authority of 'Adi ibn Hatim (RA) who said:\nI said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what is the meaning of the verse: *'until the white thread becomes distinct from the black thread'*? Are they two actual threads?\"\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"You are indeed one with a wide back (i.e., slow to understand). Have you ever seen two threads?\"\nThen he (ﷺ) said, \"No, rather it refers to the darkness of the night and the brightness of the day.\"\n\n---"
"1927","###\n Muhammad bin Ali bin Muhriz (originally from Baghdad, later moved to Fustat) narrated to us, saying: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There are two Dawns (Fajr): As for the first one, it does not make food unlawful (for the fasting person), nor does it make prayer lawful; but as for the second one"
"1928","###\n Narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Kathir al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us al-Mu'tamir, from his father, from Abu 'Uthman, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Let the Adhan of Bilal not prevent any of you from your Suhoor (pre-dawn meal), for he calls out (or gives the Adhan) so that your sleeper may wake up and your one"
"1929","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqî, narrated to us Ibn 'Ulayyah, on the authority of 'Abdullâh ibn Sawâdah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Samurah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allâh (ﷺ) said, \"Do not be deceived by Bilâl's call to prayer or by the whiteness of this column of dawn until it spreads and flies forth.\""
"1930","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, who said: Narrated to us Mulāzim ibn ʿAmr, who said: Narrated to us ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Suhaymī, who said:\n\nQays ibn Ṭalq came to me during Ramaḍān and said: My father Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī narrated to me that the Prophet of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Eat and drink, and do not be deceived by the rising bright light. Eat and drink until the redness appears before you.\"*\n\nAnd he (the Prophet ﷺ) indicated with his hand.\n\n---"
"1931","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Ubaydullah bin Umar, who said: Nafi' informed me, from Ibn Umar (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, Bilal (RA) gives the Adhan at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum (RA) gives the Adhan.\""
"1932","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Hafs (meaning Ibn Ghiyath).\nAnd narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yahya—both from 'Ubaydullah—who said:\nI heard al-Qasim narrate from 'A'ishah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n*\"Indeed Bilal (RA) gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum (RA) gives the call to prayer.\"*\n\nHe (the narrator) said: There was no interval between their two calls except the time it takes for this one to descend and that one to ascend.\n\nAl-Dawraqi said, narrating from al-Qasim, and he also said:\n*\"When Bilal (RA) gives the call to prayer, eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum (RA) gives the call to prayer.\"*\n\nHe (the narrator) said: There was no interval between their two calls except for this one to descend and that one to ascend.\n\nAbu Bakr (Ibn Abi Shaybah) said:\nThis report is of the type I mentioned from the reports that contain subtle defects (*mu'allalah*), upon which analogy (*qiyas*) is permissible, and it is certain that when the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded eating and drinking after Bilal's (RA) call, he informed them that intimacy and all that is permissible for the one not fasting is also permissible at that time—not that he only permitted eating and drinking and nothing else.\"*"
"1933","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Lahī‘ah informed me, on the authority of ‘Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of Ibn Shihāb, on the authority of Sālim ibn ‘Abd Allah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said:\n\n“Whoever does not resolve and intend the fast before dawn, then there is no fast for him.”\n\nAnd Ibn ‘Abd al-Ḥakam informed me that Ibn Wahb informed them with the same wording, and he added:\n\nHe said: Mālik and al-Layth said likewise.\n\n---"
"1934","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bakr (RA) who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) informed us from the Prophet (ﷺ) who said, \"Actions are but by intentions, and every man shall have but that which he intended.\""
"1935","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bakr (RA) who said: The hadith of Aisha (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) would come to her and say: *\"Do you have any food for breakfast? Otherwise, I am fasting.\"*\n\nWe have transmitted this in the chapter on the voluntary fasting."
"1936","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi, on the authority of Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, on the authority of 'Asim, on the authority of Zirr, on the authority of 'Abdullah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Take the pre-dawn meal (suhur), for indeed there is barakah (blessing) in the suhur.\"*\n\nNarrated to us Abu Yahya Muhammad ibn 'Abdir-Rahim al-Bazzar, narrated to us Ahmad ibn Yunus, on the authority of Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash with this chain to the same effect, elevated (marfu').\n\n---"
"1937","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammād (meaning Ibn Zayd).\nAnd narrated to us Abū 'Ammār, narrated to us Ismā'īl ibn Ibrāhīm.\nAnd narrated to us 'Imrān ibn Mūsā al-Qazzāz, narrated to us 'Abd al-Wārith.\nAnd narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Muḥammad, narrated to us Shu'bah—all of them from 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Ṣuhayb.\nAnd narrated to us Ziyād ibn Ayyūb, narrated to us Hushaym, informed us 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Ṣuhayb, from Anas (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Eat suḥūr (pre-dawn meal), for indeed there is barakah (blessing) in suḥūr.\"*"
"1938","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dawraqī, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim, who said:\nNarrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, who said:\nNarrated to us Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Yūnus ibn Sayf, from al-Ḥārith ibn Ziyād, from Abū Ruhm, from al-ʿIrbaḍ ibn Sāriyah (RA), who said:\nI heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) call a man to eat *suḥūr* and say:\n“Come to the blessed meal.”\nAnd al-Dawraqī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim also said:\nI heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) calling to eat *suḥūr* in the month of Ramaḍān and saying:\n“Come to the blessed meal.”\nAnd they added:\nThen I heard him say:\n“O Allāh, grant Muʿāwiyah knowledge of the Book and the reckoning, and protect him from punishment.”\n\nʿAbd Allāh ibn Hāshim said:\nFrom Muʿāwiyah, and he said:\n“Come to the blessed meal.”"
"1939","**\nNarrated to us Bun-dar, narrated to us Abu 'Asim, narrated to us Zam'ah, from Salamah ibn Wahraam, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbaas (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n*\"Seek help with the pre-dawn meal (suhoor) for fasting during the day, and with the midday nap (qayloolah) for standing in prayer during the night.\"*"
"1940","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, who said: Narrated to us by Abdur-Rahman, who said: Narrated by Musa bin Ali. And also narrated to us by Yunus, who said: Narrated to us by Abdullah bin Wahb. And also informed me by Ibn Abd al-Hakam that Ibn Wahb informed them, saying: He informed me by Musa bin Ali bin Rabbah. And also narrated to us by Muhammad bin Isa, who said: Narrated to us by Abdullah"
"1941","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us Khalid—meaning ibn al-Harith—who said: Narrated to us Hisham, the companion of al-Dastwa'i, who said: Narrated to us Qatadah.\n\nAlso narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, who said: Narrated to us Waki', from Hisham, the companion of al-Dastwa'i, from Qatadah.\n\nAlso narrated to us Bundar—Muhammad ibn Bashshar—who said: Narrated to us Salim ibn Nuh, who said: Narrated to us 'Umar ibn 'Amir, from Qatadah, from Anas, from Zayd ibn Thabit (RA), who said:\n\"We had Suhoor with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then we stood up for prayer.\"\nI said: \"How much time was there between them?\"\nHe said: \"The time it takes to recite fifty verses.\"\n\nThe meanings of their hadiths are the same, and this is the hadith of Waki'."
"1942","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Maskeen al-Yamaami, on the authority of Yahya ibn Hassan, on the authority of Sulaymaan ibn Bilal, from Abu Haazim, who heard Saalim ibn Sa'd (RA) say:\n\n\"I used to take the pre-dawn meal (suhoor) with my family, and then I would hasten so that I could pray the Fajr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\""
"1943","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik informed him.\n\nAlso narrated to us al-Rabi‘ ibn Sulayman, who said: al-Shafi‘i said: Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us ‘Amr ibn ‘Ali, who said: Abu ‘Asim informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: al-Zuhri informed me.\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad ibn Tasnim, who said: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us, who said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, who said: al-Zuhri informed me, from Humayd ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah (RA) informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered a man who broke his fast in Ramadan by having intercourse to either free a slave, or fast for two consecutive months, or feed sixty poor people.\n\nMalik (RA) said in the commentary to his narration: “And his breaking the fast was due to intercourse.”\n\n---"
"1944","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I memorized it from the mouth of al-Zuhri, who heard Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informing, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"I am ruined!\" The Prophet (ﷺ) asked, \"What has ruined you?\" He replied, \"I had intercourse with my wife during the month of Ramadan.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) asked, \"Can you free a slave?\" He said, \"No.\" He asked, \"Can you fast for two consecutive months?\" He said, \"No.\" He asked, \"Can you feed sixty poor people?\" He said, \"No.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Then sit down,\" so he sat down. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought a large basket (ʿaraq) containing dates. The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Take this and give it in charity.\" The man said, \"O Messenger of Allah, is there anyone poorer than us in this household?\" There was no household between its two sides (i.e., in the entire area) poorer than us.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) laughed until his molars were visible and said, \"Go and feed your family with it.\""
"1945","###\n Narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa, on the authority of Jarir, from Mansur, from Muhammad bin Muslim az-Zuhri, from Humaid bin Abdur-Rahman, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, \"I have had sexual intercourse with my wife during Ramadan.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) asked him, \"Do you have the means to free a slave?\" He replied, \"No.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) asked, \""
"1946","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us.\nAnd narrated to me Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam that Ibn Wahb informed them, saying: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to him that Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him that 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to him that he heard 'A'ishah (RA) say:\n\nA man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the mosque during Ramadan and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, I am burning (with desire)!\" So the Prophet (ﷺ) asked him about his situation. He replied, \"I have had relations with my wife.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Give charity.\" The man said, \"By Allah, I have nothing, and I am unable to do so.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Then sit down.\"\n\nWhile he was sitting, a man came leading a donkey laden with food. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) asked, \"Where is the one who is burning?\" The man stood up, and the Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Give this in charity.\" The man said, \"To someone other than us? By Allah, we are indeed hungry, and we have nothing.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Then eat it.\"\n\nIbn 'Abd al-Hakam added: The man said, \"O Messenger of Allah, to someone other than us? By Allah...\""
"1947","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī: narrated to us Muṣʿab ibn ʿAbdillāh: narrated to us ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿUbaydah al-Darāwardī: from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah al-Makhzūmī: from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn al-Zubayr: from ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Zubayr: from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) was in a wide shade when a man from Banū Bayāḍah came to him and said, “O Prophet of Allah, I am burning [with need].” The Prophet (ﷺ) asked him, “What is the matter with you?” He replied, “I had intercourse with my wife while fasting in Ramaḍān.” The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him, “Free a slave.” He said, “I cannot find one.” He said, “Then feed sixty poor people.” He said, “I do not have that.” He said, “Then sit down,” so he sat. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a basket containing twenty ṣāʿ of food, and he asked, “Where is the man who just asked?” He replied, “Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Take this and give it in charity.” The man said, “O Messenger of Allah, to someone more needy than me and my family? By the One Who sent you with the truth, we have no supper tonight.” The Prophet (ﷺ) said, “Then take it for yourself and your family.”\n[The report does not mention fasting.]\n\nAbū Bakr (the narrator) said: If the wording “a basket containing twenty ṣāʿ” is established, then the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered this man who had intercourse to feed each poor person one-third of a ṣāʿ of dates, because twenty ṣāʿ divided among sixty poor people gives each one-third of a ṣāʿ. However, I do not consider this wording established. In the report of al-Zuhrī there is: “a basket containing fifteen or twenty ṣāʿ.” This is in the report of Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir from al-Zuhrī. As for Hiql ibn Ziyād, he narrated from al-Awzāʿī from al-Zuhrī that it was fifteen ṣāʿ. I have recorded both versions later, and I do not know of any scholar from the Ḥijāz or Iraq who said that in the expiation for intercourse one should feed each poor person one-third of a ṣāʿ in Ramaḍān. The people of the Ḥijāz said: feed each poor person one mudd of food, whether dates or otherwise. The Iraqis said: feed each poor person one ṣāʿ of dates. As for one-third of a ṣāʿ, I do not recall anyone among them mentioning it.\n\nAbū Bakr said: It may be that the omission of the command to fast two consecutive months in this report is because the inquiry in this report occurred in Ramaḍān before the month was completed, and fasting two consecutive months for this offense cannot begin until after Ramaḍān is finished and a day of Shawwāl has passed. Therefore the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered the man who had intercourse to feed sixty poor people, since feeding was possible in Ramaḍān if the man possessed the means, so he ordered him to do what was immediately possible rather than what could only be done after days and nights had passed. And Allah knows best.\n\nI do not recall in any report of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the inquiry of the man who had intercourse came before Ramaḍān ended. Hence, if the inquiry occurred after Ramaḍān, it would be permissible to order fasting two months, because fasting at that time for the expiation would be valid.\n\n---"
"1948","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: In the narration of 'Aishah (RA), she said: *\"Indeed, we were hungry; we had nothing.\"* This is found in the narration of 'Amr ibn al-Harith (RA). And in the narration of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith (RA), it is: *\"We had no supper that night.\"* And in the narration of Abu Hurayrah (RA), it is: *\"Between its two sides (i.e., the two lava tracts of Medina), no one was more in need than us.\"*"
"1949","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Aziz al-Aylisi, that Salamah informed them, from 'Uqayl, that he asked Ibn Shihab about a man who had intercourse with his wife during Ramadan. He said: Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me, that Abu Hurayrah (RA) informed him, that he said:\n\nWhile I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), a man came to him and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, I am ruined!\" He said, \"What is the matter with you?\" He said, \"I had intercourse with my wife during Ramadan.\" He said, \"Free a slave.\" He said, \"I do not have the means.\" He said, \"Then fast two consecutive months.\" He said, \"I am not able to do that.\" He said, \"Then feed sixty poor people.\" He said, \"I do not have that.\" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a basket containing dates, and he said, \"Take this and give it in charity.\" He said, \"There is no one more deserving of it than my family, O Messenger of Allah. There is no one between the two ropes of Medina more in need of it than I am.\" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) laughed until his molars were visible, and then said, \"Take it and seek Allah's forgiveness.\""
"1950","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Mu'ammal, narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Mansur, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who mentioned the hadith and said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was brought a basket containing fifteen or twenty *sa’* (a measure) of dates. The Prophet (ﷺ) said, “Take it and feed it to the poor on your behalf.”"
"1951","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Mihran ibn Abi 'Umar al-Razi, from Sufyan al-Thawri, who said: Ibrahim ibn 'Amir and Habib ibn Abi Thabit narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Mansur, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and mentioned the hadith.\n\nHe said: Then a container was brought containing fifteen *sa’* or twenty *sa’* (of dates). However, he made a mistake in the chain, saying: \"From Abu Salamah.\" And in the narration of Hajjaj as well, from al-Zuhri: \"Then a container was brought containing fifteen *sa’* of dates was brought,\" except that Hajjaj did not hear from al-Zuhri.\n\nI heard Muhammad ibn 'Amrah narrate from Ahmad ibn Abi Zhabyah, from Hushaym, who said: Hajjaj said: \"Describe al-Zuhri to me; he was not seeing him.\""
"1952","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said in a narration from al-Zuhri: *\"Feed sixty poor people.\"*"
"1953","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa’id al-Ashajj: narrated to us Abu Khalid: narrated to us al-A‘mash: on the authority of al-Hakam, Salamah ibn Kuhayl, and Muslim al-Batin: from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, ‘Aṭā’, and Mujāhid: from Ibn ‘Abbās (RA) who said:\n\nA woman came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, “My sister died and she had to make up two consecutive months of fasting.”\nHe (ﷺ) asked, “If your sister had a debt, would you pay it off?”\nShe replied, “Yes.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Then the right of Allāh is more deserving [to be fulfilled].”"
"1954","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm: narrated to us Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ al-Aṣbahānī: on the authority of Hishām ibn Saʿd: on the authority of Ibn Shihāb: on the authority of Abū Salamah: on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he had had relations with his wife during Ramaḍān. He mentioned the hadith, and at its end he (ﷺ) said: *\"Fast one day and seek Allah’s forgiveness.\"*\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said: This chain is a mistake."
"1955","###\n The report from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd bin Abdur-Rahman, is the authentic one, not the one from Abu Salamah. Al-Hajjaj bin Artah also narrated similarly to the report of Az-Zuhri, from Amr bin Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather. Regarding the report of Amr bin Shu'ayb, it was stated: Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb and Harun bin Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Abu Khalid narr"
"1956","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qata'i, al-Husayn ibn 'Isa al-Bastami, and a group. This is the narration of Abu Musa.\n\n'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to me, he said: I heard my father narrate to me, he said: al-Husayn — and he is al-Mu'allim — narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, that Ibn 'Amr al-Awza'i narrated to him, that Ya'ish ibn al-Walid narrated to him, that Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah narrated to him, that Abu al-Darda' narrated to him, that the Prophet (ﷺ) \"vomited then broke his fast.\"\n\nSo I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus and mentioned this to him, whereupon he said: \"He spoke the truth. I poured out his ritual ablution for him.\"\n\nHowever, al-Bastami and Muhammad ibn Yahya said: From al-Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Awza'i, from Ya'ish ibn al-Walid, from his father, from Ma'dan, from Abu al-Darda'.\n\nThe correct version is that which Abu Musa narrated — that it is Ya'ish from Ma'dan, from Abu al-Darda'.\n\nHatam ibn Bakr ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr, from Ya'ish, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, from Abu al-Darda', similar to the narration of Abu Musa.\n\nHisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated it from Yahya, he said: A man from our brothers — intending al-Awza'i — narrated to me from Ya'ish ibn Hisham, that Ma'dan informed him, that Abu al-Darda' informed him, similar to the narration of 'Abd al-Samad, except that he did not say: \"in the mosque of Damascus.\"\n\nBundar narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman — meaning ibn 'Uthman al-Bakrawi — narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us. However, Abu Musa said: From Ya'ish ibn al-Walid ibn Hisham. As for Bundar, he attributed him to his grandfather. Both said: Ma'dan informed him.\n\nBy the narration of Hisham and Harb ibn Shaddad, it is known that the correct version is that narrated by Abu Musa, and that Ya'ish ibn al-Walid heard from Ma'dan, and there is no father between them."
"1957","**\nNarrated to us al-Bistami and Muhammad ibn Yahya, they said: On the authority of al-Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from al-Awza'i, from Ya'ish ibn al-Walid, from his father, from Ma'dan, from Abu al-Darda (RA):\n\n*\"The correct statement is what Abu Musa said: Indeed, it is Ya'ish from Ma'dan, from Abu al-Darda (RA).\"*"
"1958","**\nNarrated to us Hatim ibn Bakr ibn Ghaylan: ‘Abdus-Samad narrated to us; Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from ‘Abd ar-Rahman ibn ‘Amr, from Ya‘ish, from Ma‘dan ibn Abi Talhah, from Abu ad-Darda’ (RA), similar to the hadith of Abu Musa (RA).\n\nThe chain of narration is *Sahih* (authentic) according to al-A‘zami."
"1959","**\nNarrated to us Hishām al-Dastuwā’ī from Yaḥyā who said: A man from among our brothers—intending al-Awzāʿī—narrated to me from Yaʿīsh ibn Hishām that Maʿdān informed him that Abū al-Dardā’ informed him of a hadith similar to the narration of ʿAbd al-Ṣamad, except that he did not say: “in the mosque of Damascus.”\n\nNarrated to us Bundār: Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥmān—meaning ibn ʿUthmān al-Bakrawī—Narrated to us Hishām, except that Abū Mūsā said: “from Yaʿīsh ibn al-Walīd ibn Hishām.”\n\nAs for Bundār, he attributed him to his grandfather and both said: “Indeed Maʿdān informed him.”\n\nThus, by the narration of Hishām and Ḥarb ibn Shaddād, it is known that what is correct is what Abū Mūsā narrated, and that Yaʿīsh ibn al-Walīd heard from Maʿdān, and there is no intermediary between them, his father."
"1960","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Ḥujr al-Sa‘dī, narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Yūnus, from Hishām, from Ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “If one vomits intentionally, he must make up the fast; but if vomiting overcomes him, he does not have to make up the fast.”"
"1961","Ali narrated to us once again, saying: \"Whoever is overcome by vomiting is not required to make up the fast, but whoever induces vomiting intentionally must make up the fast.\" Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, Abu Sa'id al-Ju'fi narrated to us, Hafs bin Ghiyath narrated to us, from Hisham, with this chain of narration, and he mentioned the hadith."
"1962","Narrated to us by Ali bin Sahl ar-Ramli, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Walid bin Muslim, who said: Narrated to me by Abu Amr, meaning Al-Awza'i, who said: Narrated to me by Yahya, who said: Narrated to me by Abu Qilabah al-Jarmi, who said: Abu Asma' ar-Rahabi narrated to me from Thawban, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."
"1963","Narrated to us Ziyaad ibn Ayyoob, narrated to us Mubashshir — meaning ibn Ismaa’eel — from al-Awzaa’ee, who narrated to me Yahyaa ibn Abee Katheer, who narrated to me Abu Qilaabah al-Jarmee, from Abu Asmaa’ ar-Rahabee, who narrated to me Thawbaan, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allaah (ﷺ), that he went out with the Messenger of Allaah (ﷺ) on the twelfth night remaining of the month of Ramadhaan to al-Baqee’ (the graveyard). The Messenger of Allaah (ﷺ) saw a man having cupping (hijama) done, so he said: “The cupping practitioner and the one cupped have both broken their fast.”\n\nThis is the narration of al-Waleed.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"1964","###\n Abbas bin Abd al-Azim al-Anbari and Al-Husayn bin Mahdi narrated to us. Abbas said: \"Our narrator was,\" and Al-Husayn said: \"He informed us.\" They both said: \"Abd al-Razzaq informed us, who said: Ma'mar informed us, from Yahya bin Abi Kathir, from Ibrahim bin Abdullah bin Qariz, from Al-Sa'ib bin Yazid, from Rafi' bin Khadij, who said: The Messenger of Allah ("
"1965","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Shaybani in Baghdad, who said: Narrated to me 'Ammar ibn Matar Abu 'Uthman al-Rahawi, who said: Narrated to us Mu'awiyah ibn Salam — I have already narrated this chapter in its entirety in *al-Kabir*.\n\nAbu Bakr said: It has been established by report from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said, \"Both the one who performs cupping and the one for whom it is performed break their fast.\"\n\nSome of those who differed with us on this issue said: \"Cupping does not break the fast of the one who is fasting,\" and they supported this with the report that the Prophet (ﷺ) \"underwent cupping while fasting and in the state of ihram.\"\n\nHowever, this report does not indicate that cupping does not break the fast of the one who is fasting, because the Prophet (ﷺ) only \"underwent cupping while fasting during a journey,\" not while resident. For he was never in the state of ihram while resident in his city; rather, he was in ihram only while traveling.\n\nAnd the traveler, even if he has resolved to fast and some part of the day has passed while he is fasting (abstaining from food and drink), is still permitted to break his fast by eating and drinking. This is unlike what some scholars have mistakenly thought — that if the traveler enters upon the fast, he is not permitted to break it until he completes the fast of that day in which he entered.\n\nThus, if he is permitted to eat and drink after resolving to fast and some part of the day has passed while he is fasting, then it is also permitted for him to undergo cupping while traveling during some part of the fasting day, even if cupping breaks the fast.\n\nThe evidence that the fasting person may break his fast by eating and drinking while traveling on a day in which some part has already passed while he is fasting is as follows..."
"1966","###\n It was narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abdah, who said: It was narrated to us by Yazid ibn Zuray', who said: It was narrated to us by Sa'id al-Jurayri, on the authority of Abu Nadrah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came upon a stream of rainwater on a hot summer day. There were many pedestrians, and the people were fasting. He stopped by it, and there was a large group"
"1967","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi: Narrated to us al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman — from Humaid — from Abu al-Mutawakkil — from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) granted a concession to the fasting person regarding kissing and cupping.\"\n\n**COMMENTARY:**\nThis narration appears in multiple chains with slight variations. Some versions explicitly attribute the concession to the Prophet (ﷺ), while others state it as Abu Sa'id's statement. The wording \"cupping for the fasting person\" is included in the report, though it may have been added by al-Mu'tamir during narration. Some scholars consider this a mursal addition, while others accept it as part of the original narration."
"1968","This report was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sana'ani and Bishr ibn Mu'adh, who stated: Al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: I heard Humaid narrating, on the authority of Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) permitted the fasting person to kiss (his spouse; the term *al-qiblah* here refers specifically to spousal kissing in the context of fasting rulings). Abu Bakr [a subsequent hadith narrator, not the Companion Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (RA)] said: He did not mention anything beyond this content. I said to al-Sana'ani, \"What about cupping (hijamah)?\" He became angry and denied that cupping was mentioned in this report. The proof that there is no mention of cupping in the report from the Prophet (ﷺ) is [this]."
"1969","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn Sa'id, who also said: Narrated to us Abu al-Nadr, who said: Narrated to us al-Ashja'i, from Sufyan, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) granted a concession to the fasting person regarding cupping and kissing.\"\n\nThis narration explicitly states that the concession was granted to the fasting person regarding cupping and kissing, and it does not mention the Prophet (ﷺ) directly in the chain."
"1970","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn Bazī‘, narrated to us Abū Yaḥyā, narrated to us Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl and al-Daḥḥāk ibn ‘Uthmān, on the authority of Abū al-Mutawakkil al-Nājī, on the authority of Abū Sa‘īd al-Khudrī (RA), who said regarding cupping (al-ḥijāmah):\n\n“They used to dislike it.”\nHe (Abū Sa‘īd) said: “Or he said: ‘They used to fear weakness.’”"
"1971","**\nNarrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said:\n\"I only disliked cupping for the fasting person out of fear of weakness.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: The report of Qatadah and the report of Abu Yahya from Humayd and al-Dahhak ibn Uthman indicate that Abu Sa'id did not narrate from the Prophet (ﷺ) the concession for cupping for the fasting person. For it is not permissible for Abu Sa'id to narrate that the Prophet (ﷺ) granted a concession for cupping for the fasting person, and then say: \"They disliked it out of fear of weakness.\" Since he permitted it absolutely without exception or condition, it is permitted for all people. It is not permissible to say: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) permitted cupping for the fasting person while it is disliked out of fear of weakness,\" and that he (ﷺ) did not exclude from this permission those who are not afraid of weakness from those who are.\n\nIf it is authentic from Abu Sa'id that the Prophet (ﷺ) granted a concession for cupping for the fasting person, then the meaning of this statement would be that Abu Sa'id said: \"It was disliked for the fasting person for what the Prophet (ﷺ) permitted him in it.\" And it is not permissible to interpret this as the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) narrating a concession from the Prophet (ﷺ) for a thing while disliking it.\n\n---"
"1972","**\nIt has also been narrated from ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from ‘Ata’ ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa‘id al-Khudri (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “Three things break the fast of the one who is fasting: cupping, vomiting, and a wet dream.”\n\nNarrated to us Yaḥya ibn al-Mughīra Abu Salama al-Makhzūmī: Ismā‘īl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us from ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam.\nAnd narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Yaḥya: Sa‘īd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us from ‘Abd al-Rahman.\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Khaythama) said: This chain is a mistake; it does not contain ‘Aṭā’ ibn Yasār nor Abu Sa‘īd. Moreover, ‘Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd is not one whose hadiths the people of precision accept as evidence because of his poor memory of chains of transmission. He was a man whose trade was worship, asceticism, exhortation, and abstinence; he was not one of the constant hadith-scholars who preserve chains.\n\n---"
"1973","Sufyan ibn Sa'id al-Thawri, who was unmatched in his memorization during his time by many, narrated this report on the authority of Zayd ibn Aslam, who said: \"From a companion of his, from a man from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'He does not break his fast who vomits, nor he who has a wet dream, nor he who undergoes cupping (hijama).'\"\n\nAb"
"1974","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu 'Asim, from Sufyan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"
"1975","Narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yusuf, narrated to us Sufyan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, who narrated to me a man from our companions, from a man from the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The fasting person does not break his fast by vomiting, nor by having a wet dream, nor by cupping.\"\n\nAbd al-Razzaq did not elevate this narration (i.e., he did not narrate it from the Prophet (ﷺ) directly).\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"1976","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who said: Ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ), from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said the same."
"1977","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, (who said) Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd informed us, Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n---\n*\"If one person performs cupping (hijama) on another, and the one who is cupped and the one who does the cupping both break their fast.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"1978","###\n Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, on the authority of Zayd bin Aslam, on the authority of Ata' bin Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Three things do not break the fast of the one who is fasting: wet dreams, vomiting, and cupping.\" I heard Muhammad bin Yahya say: \"This report is not well-preserved (not authentic) from Abu Sa'id nor from Ata' bin Yasar."
"1979","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abdullah Al-Ansari, on the authority of Abu Al-Mutawakkil, on the authority of Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"There is no harm in cupping (Al-Hijamah) for the one who is fasting.\""
"1980","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Minhal, from Hammad, from Humayd, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) who said:\n*\"He (the Prophet ﷺ) did not see any harm in a fasting person having cupping (hijama) done.\"*"
"1981","Narrated to us Muhammad, who said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn al-Mubarak, on the authority of Khalid al-Hadda', on the authority of Abu al-Mutawakkil, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"There is no harm in cupping for the one who is fasting.\""
"1982","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us Musa ibn Harun al-Burdi, narrated to us 'Abdah, from Sulayman al-Naji, from Abu al-Mutawakkil, that Abu Sa'id (RA) said: \"The one who applies cupping (al-hajam) and the one for whom it is applied both break their fast.\"\n\nAnd I do not think that Ma'mar (RA) uttered it in this wording."
"1983","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Nasr, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Kathir, on the authority of al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu Asma' al-Rahabi, from Thawban (RA) who said:\n\"I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the twelfth night of Ramadan. He (ﷺ) passed by a man who was having cupping done. He (ﷺ) said: 'Both the one doing the cupping and the one having it done have broken their fast.'\""
"1984","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Nasr, on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn Salih and Yahya ibn ‘Abdullah ibn Bukayr, on the authority of al-Layth ibn Sa‘d, who narrated to me Qatadah ibn Di‘amah al-Basri, on the authority of al-Hasan, on the authority of Thawban, on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: **\"The one who performs cupping and the one for whom it is performed have both broken their fast.\"**\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: **\"As for everything I have not stated until the end of this chapter, then it is not a condition of ours in this book. And al-Hasan did not hear from Thawban.\"**\n\nAbu Bakr said: **\"This report of Thawban is, in my view, Sahih in this chain.\"**"
"1985","**\nNarrated to us ‘Āsim ibn Laqīt ibn Sabirah, on the authority of his father, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ):\n“When you perform *istinshāq* (snuffing water into the nose), do so thoroughly, except when you are fasting.”"
"1986","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulayman al-Muraadi and Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlaani, they said: Narrated to us Bashar ibn Bakr, on the authority of Ibn Jaabir, on the authority of Sulaym ibn ‘Aamir Abu Yaḥyaa, who said: Abu Umamah al-Baahili narrated to me, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n*\"While I was sleeping, two men came to me and took hold of my upper arms. They brought me to a rugged mountain, and said: ‘Climb up.’ I said: ‘Indeed, I cannot.’ They said: ‘We will make it easy for you.’ So I climbed up until I reached the middle of the mountain, when suddenly I heard loud voices. I said: ‘What are these voices?’ They said: ‘This is the wailing of the people of the Fire.’ Then they took me further, and I saw people hanging by their heels, with their mouths slit open, and blood flowing from their mouths.\"*\n\nHe (the narrator) said: I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"These are the people who break their fast before it is permissible to do so.\"\n\nThen he said: \"May the Jews and Christians be ruined!\" Then Sulayman said: \"I do not know whether Abu Umamah heard this from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) or if it was something from his own opinion.\"\n\nThen he (the angel) took me further, and I saw people who were most swollen, with the foulest of smells, and the most hideous appearance. I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"These are the slain disbelievers.\"\n\nThen he took me further, and I saw people who were most swollen, with the foulest of smells, as if their smell was the smell of menstrual rags. I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"These are the fornicators and adulterers.\"\n\nThen he took me further, and I saw women whose breasts were being bitten by snakes. I said: \"What is the matter with these?\" He said: \"These are the women who prevent their children from suckling (i.e., weaning them prematurely).\"\n\nThen he took me further, and I saw young boys playing between two rivers. I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"These are the offspring of the believers.\"\n\nThen he ascended with me to a higher place, and I saw a group of three people drinking from their own wine. I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"These are Ja’far, Zayd, and Ibn Raawaahah.\"\n\nThen he ascended with me to another high place, and I saw a group of three people. I said: \"Who are these?\" He said: \"This is Ibraaheem, Moosa, and ‘Eesa, and they are looking at me.\""
"1987","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar (also known as Bundar), who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adi. And narrated to us Al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us Khalid ibn Al-Harith. They all said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umair"
"1988","Narrated to us Bundar, on the authority of Abu Dawud, on the authority of Shu'bah with this same isnad, and he added: Shu'bah reported: Habib ibn Abi Thabit (RA) said, \"I met Abu al-Mutawwis and he narrated this hadith to me.\"\nCommentary: This narration forms part of the chain for the hadith addressing the severe warning for abandoning the fast during the blessed month of Ramadan without a legitimate Shariah excuse. Its status as Sahih is conditional, as both Ibn al-Mutawwis and his father are unknown narrators, per the chapter heading."
"1989","**\nNarrated to us Isma'il ibn Bishr ibn Mansur al-Sulami: Narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la: Narrated to us Hisham: On the authority of Muhammad: On the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"If one of you forgets while fasting and eats or drinks, then let him complete his fast, for Allah has only fed him and given him drink.\""
"1990","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad and Ibrahim, the two sons of Muhammad ibn Marzuq al-Bahili al-Basri, who said:\nMuhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us;\nMuhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us;\nfrom Abu Salamah;\nfrom Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n*\"Whoever breaks his fast in Ramadan forgetfully, there is no making up (qadha') for him nor any expiation (kaffarah).\"*\nThis is the narration of Muhammad.\n\nIbrahim (one of the two narrators) said in his narration:\n*\"Whoever eats or drinks in Ramadan forgetfully, there is no making up (qadha') for him nor any expiation (kaffarah).\"*"
"1991","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, who said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, who said: Hisham narrated to us, from Fatimah, from Asma'.\n\nAnd narrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, who said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma', who said:\n\"We broke the fast during Ramadan on a cloudy day during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Then the sun appeared.\"\n\nHisham was asked, \"Were they commanded to make up the fast?\"\nHe replied, \"There is no way around it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \"This report does not state that they were commanded to make up the fast. This is Hisham's own statement: 'There is no way around it.' There is no such statement in the report, nor is it clear to me that they were required to make up the fast. If they broke their fast while the sun was still with them (i.e., before sunset), and then it became clear that the sun had not yet set, like the statement of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA): 'By Allah, we do not abandon what we have erred in regarding sin.'\""
"1992","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Khashram: ‘Isa narrated to us.\nAlso narrated to us ‘Abdullah ibn Sa‘id al-Ashajj: Ibn Numayr narrated to us.\nAll from al-A‘mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “When any one of you is fasting, he should neither indulge in obscene speech nor act in ignorance. If someone insults him or behaves ignorantly toward him, let him say: ‘I am fasting.’”\nAshajj added in his narration: “When it is the day of fasting for one of you …”"
"1993","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz — meaning ibn Muhammad — on the authority of Suhayl, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"If one of you is fasting, then let him not engage in obscene speech. If someone insults him, abuses him, or fights with him, then let him say: 'Indeed, I am fasting.'\""
"1994","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Uthman bin Umar, who said: We were informed by Ibn Abi Dh'i'b, on the authority of Ajlan, the freed slave of Al-Mushma'il, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Do not engage in foul language while you are fasting. If someone reviles you, say: 'I am fasting.' And if you are standing, then sit down.\""
"1995","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, (who said) 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us.\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Isa, 'Abdullah — meaning Ibn al-Mubarak — narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"Whoever does not abandon false speech and acting upon it, Allah has no need for him to abandon his food and drink.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Bundar.\nIn the narration of Ibn al-Mubarak, it is: \"And acting upon it and ignorant behavior.\"\n\n---"
"1996","**\nNarrated to me Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, and Anas ibn 'Iyad informed me, from al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his paternal uncle, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Fasting is not merely from eating and drinking. Rather, fasting is from [idle] speech and obscenity. So if someone insults you or acts ignorantly toward you, say: 'Indeed, I am fasting. Indeed, I am fasting.'\""
"1997","**\nNarrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr: narrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar: narrated to us ʿAmr—he is Ibn Abī ʿAmr—on the authority of Abū Saʿīd al-Maqburī, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said,\n\n“How often does a fasting person gain nothing from his fast except hunger and thirst, and how often does a person standing in night prayer gain nothing from his night vigil except sleeplessness.”\n\n---"
"1998","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘lā al-San‘ānī, narrated to us Bushr—meaning ibn al-Mufaddal—narrated to us Ibn ‘Awn, on the authority of Ibrāhīm, on the authority of al-Aswad who said:\n\nWe set out—Masrūq and I—to Umm al-Mu’minīn (May Allah be pleased with her) to ask her about intimacy (during fasting). We felt shy, so I said, “We have come to ask about a need, but we feel shy.” She said, “What is it? Ask whatever comes to your mind.” We said, “Did the Prophet (ﷺ) used to engage in intimacy while fasting?” She replied, “He used to do so, but he had more control over his desire than you do.”\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said: Allah, exalted is His praise, addressed His Prophet (ﷺ) and his community in the Arabic language, which is the most comprehensive of all languages—no one can grasp all of its nuances except a prophet. The Arabs, in their speech, apply a single word to two things or to multiple things. A single thing may be called by several names, and Allah may forbid one thing while permitting another that is different from the forbidden one. A single word may apply to both the permitted and the forbidden, and similarly, something that is forbidden may be permitted, and a single word may apply to both. Thus, a single word may refer to two different things—one permitted and the other forbidden—yet share the same name.\n\nWhoever is ignorant of this aspect of the Arabic language and assumes that the two matters are contradictory because one act is permitted under a certain name while another act is forbidden under the same name, such a person has not reached the level of understanding required to engage in jurisprudence or issue fatwas. It is obligatory for him to seek knowledge or remain silent until he attains the knowledge that permits him to issue fatwas and engage in jurisprudence.\n\nWhoever understands this linguistic principle knows that what is permitted is not the same as what is forbidden, even if a single word may apply to both the permitted and the forbidden. This is the type of linguistic phenomenon to which Allah, the Exalted, alludes in His Book when He states regarding intimacy with women during the day of fasting: “Now have relations with them and seek what Allah has ordained for you, and eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread. Then complete the fast until nightfall.” (Qur’an 2:187)\n\nAllah permitted intimacy with women, eating, and drinking during the night, then commanded us to complete the fast until nightfall. The permitted intimacy during the night that is coupled with eating and drinking refers to sexual intercourse, which breaks the fast. However, Allah permitted the Prophet (ﷺ) to engage in intimacy that does not involve intercourse while fasting, as he (ﷺ) used to do.\n\nThe intimacy mentioned in the Qur’an that breaks the fast is different from the intimacy the Prophet (ﷺ) engaged in while fasting. The word “intimacy” (mubāsharah) is a single term applied to two different acts: one permitted during the day of fasting, and the other forbidden during the day of fasting, as it breaks the fast.\n\nThis is similar to the statement of Allah, the Exalted: “O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave off trade.” (Qur’an 62:9) Our Lord, exalted and glorified, commands us to hasten to Friday prayer, while the Prophet (ﷺ) said, “When you come to prayer, do not come running; come walking, and upon you is tranquility.”\n\nThe word “hasten” (sa‘y) applies to walking briskly, walking quickly, and going to the place. The “hasten” that Allah commands regarding going to Friday prayer refers to going to it, while the “hasten” that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade regarding coming to prayer refers to running and walking quickly.\n\nThus, the word “hasten” applies to two different acts: one commanded and the other forbidden.\n\nI will clarify this type of linguistic phenomenon, Allah willing, in the book *Ma‘ānī al-Qur’ān*, if Allah grants me the ability to do so."
"1999","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' bin Sulaiman, who said: Narrated to us Shu'ayb bin Al-Layth, who said: Narrated to us Al-Layth, from Bukayr, who is the son of Abdullah bin Al-Ashajj, from Abd al-Malik bin Sa'id Al-Ansari, from Jabir bin Abdullah, from Umar bin Al-Khattab (RA), who said: \"One day, I felt a strong desire and kissed while I was fasting. Then I went to the Messenger"
"2000","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala': Narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I asked 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, \"Did you hear your father narrate from 'A'ishah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to kiss her while he was fasting?\" He remained silent for a while, then said, \"Yes.\""
"2001","**\nNarrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani (RA), narrated to us 'Ubaydah, narrated to us Mutarrif. And narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, from Mutarrif. And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us Mutarrif, from 'Amir, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would fast and not mind kissing from my face until he broke his fast.\"\n\nYusuf (RA) said: \"He would kiss whatever part of my face he wished.\"\n\nAz-Za'farani (RA) said: \"He would kiss any part of my face he wished.\""
"2002","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bakr (RA) in a narration from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqīq (RA), on the authority of Ibn 'Abbās (RA) who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) would kiss [his wives] on the head while fasting.\"\n\n[Commentary]\nAl-A‘zamī (RA) said: \"Its chain is *Ṣaḥīḥ*.\"\n\n---"
"2003","**\nNarrated to us Bishr ibn Mu‘ādh al-‘Aqadī: Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Dinār al-Ṭāḥī: Narrated to us Sa‘d ibn Awz, on the authority of Miṣda‘ Abū Yaḥyā, on the authority of ‘Ā’ishah (RA), who said:\n“The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to kiss her while fasting, and he used to suck her tongue.”"
"2004","Narrated to us Bishr bin Muadh; he narrated to us Abu Awana, on the authority of Sa'd bin Ibrahim. And narrated to us Yahya bin Hakim; he narrated to us Ibn Abi Adiyy, on the authority of Shu'ba, on the authority of Sa'd bin Ibrahim, on the authority of Talha bin Abdullah, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) leaned in towards me to kiss me, so I said, \"Indeed I am fasting.\" He replied, \"And I am fasting as well.\" Then he kissed me. Bishr bin Muadh said: [This report is transmitted] from Talha, a man from his own people [a standard Isnad comment affirming Talha’s reliability as a narrator from his own clan]."
"2005","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘lā al-San‘ānī and Bashar ibn Mu‘ādh who said: Narrated to us al-Mu‘tamir who said: I heard Humayd narrating, on the authority of Abū al-Mutawakkil al-Nājī, on the authority of Abū Sa‘īd al-Khudrī (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) granted a concession regarding kissing for the fasting person."
"2006","Abu Bakr (RA) narrated: The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Were it not that I would burden my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the Siwak (tooth-stick) before every prayer.\" He did not make an exception for the fasting person over the non-fasting one. Thus, there is evidence in this narration that using the Siwak for the fasting person before every prayer is a virtue, just as it is for the non-fasting person."
"2007","###\n It has been narrated to us by Abu Musa, on the authority of Sufyan (meaning Ibn Uyaynah), from Asim ibn Ubaydullah. And it has been narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Abu Musa, who said: It has been narrated to us by Yahya (Bundar said: It has been narrated to us by Sufyan), and Abu Musa said: On the authority of Sufyan. And it has been narrated to us by Abu Musa, on the authority of Abd"
"2008","###\n Narrated to us Ali bin Ma'bad, who said: Narrated to us Ma'mar bin Muhammad bin Ubaydullah bin Abi Rafi', who said: My father narrated to me, from his father Ubaydullah, from Abi Rafi', who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) descended upon Khaybar, and I descended with him. He called for some antimony (ithmid) kohl, and he applied it to his eyes during Ramadan while he was fasting.\" The narrator added: \"It"
"2009","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Jabbar ibn Al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, who said: I heard Sumayy, and I heard him from Sumayy, who heard it from Abu Bakr, that Mu'awiyah sent Abdur-Rahman ibn Al-Harith to Aisha (RA). Abu Bakr said: So I went with my father, and I heard Aisha (RA) saying: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would sometimes be in a"
"2010","**\nNarrated to us Abu 'Ammar, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from Sumayy. And it was also narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, who said: Narrated to us Sumayy, who heard Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi say that he heard 'A'ishah (RA) say:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would say words to this effect:\n*\"Whoever intends to fast should delay the ghusl (ritual bath) for janabah (major ritual impurity) until the break of dawn.\"*\n\n**COMMENTARY:**\nAbu 'Ammar added in all these narrations: *\"From...\"*"
"2011","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab, narrated to us Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, who said: \"I know the people most about this hadith.\"\n\nMarwan heard that Abu Hurayrah (RA) was narrating from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) [a hadith about fasting while junub].\n\nNarrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Yahya, from Ibn Jurayj, narrated to me 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abu Bakr, from his father, that he heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) say: \"Whoever wakes up in a state of janabah should not fast.\"\n\nSo Abu Bakr and his father 'Abd al-Rahman went until they entered upon Umm Salamah (RA) and 'A'ishah (RA), and both of them said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would wake up in a state of janabah and then fast.\"\n\nSo Abu Bakr and his father went until they came to Marwan and informed him. He said: \"I adjure you by Allah to go to Abu Hurayrah (RA) and have him narrate [the hadith] to you.\"\n\nHe said: \"Did both of them tell you that?\"\n\nThey said: \"Yes.\"\n\nHe said: \"They know better. Indeed, al-Fadl informed me of it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: Abu Hurayrah (RA) referred the report to someone more knowledgeable, truthful, and righteous in his narration. However, the report is abrogated, not due to a mistake or error. Rather, when Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, first obligated fasting upon the Ummah of Muhammad (ﷺ), He prohibited them from eating, drinking, and sexual intercourse after sleeping during the night of fasting. Thus, it is likely that the report of al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas—\"Whoever wakes up in a state of janabah should not fast\"—was during that time before Allah permitted sexual intercourse until the break of dawn.\n\nWhen Allah, the Exalted, permitted sexual intercourse until the break of dawn, it became permissible for someone in a state of janabah who wakes up before having bathed to fast that day. For Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, when He permitted sexual intercourse until the break of dawn, it was known that the one who engages in it before dawn would be required to do what Allah had permitted in His revealed text. There is no way for such a person to bathe except after dawn.\n\nIf it were not permissible for him to fast after waking up before bathing, then sexual intercourse before dawn—even if there was the least amount of time to bathe—would have been prohibited and not permitted.\n\nIn Allah's permitting sexual intercourse during the night after it had been prohibited after sleep, it becomes clear and established that janabah remaining after dawn due to sexual intercourse during the night is permissible and does not invalidate fasting.\n\nThus, the hadith of 'A'ishah (RA) and Umm Salamah (RA) about the Prophet (ﷺ) fasting after waking up in a state of janabah abrogates the hadith of al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas (RA), for this action of the Prophet (ﷺ) likely occurred after the permission for sexual intercourse until dawn was revealed.\n\nNow listen to a report from the scribe of the revelation to the Prophet (ﷺ) confirming the correctness of what I have interpreted from the hadith of al-Fadl ibn 'Abbas (RA).\n\n---"
"2012","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli, narrated to us al-Walid — meaning ibn Muslim — who said: I heard ibn Thawban — and he is ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit ibn Thawban — on the authority of his father, on the authority of Makhul, on the authority of Qabisah ibn Dhu’ayb, that he informed Zayd ibn Thabit of the statement of Abu Hurayrah (RA) that he said:\n“Whoever dawn overtakes him in the month of Ramadan while he is junub and has not performed the ghusl, then he has broken his fast and must make up the day.”\n\nSo Zayd ibn Thabit said:\n“Indeed Allah has prescribed fasting upon us just as He has prescribed the prayer upon us. So if a man were to wake up and the sun had already risen upon him while he was asleep, would he leave the prayer?”\nI said to Zayd: “Then does he fast and make up another day?”\nZayd said: “Two days for one day.”\n\n---"
"2013","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, on the authority of 'Irak ibn Malik, on the authority of 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his mother Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would enter upon the dawn in a state of major ritual impurity (janabah) from relations with women, not from a wet dream. Then he would continue fasting.\"\n\n---"
"2014","Narrated to us Ali bin Hajr al-Sa'di, who narrated to us Ismail bin Ja'far, on the authority of Abdullah bin Abd al-Rahman bin Ma'mar, known as Abu Tuwala, that Abu Yunus, the freed slave of Aisha (RA), narrated to him, from Aisha (RA):\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) to seek a legal ruling while Aisha (RA) was listening from behind the door. He said, \"O Messenger of Allah, the time for prayer catches me while I am in a state of major ritual impurity (junub) – should I fast?\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) replied, \"And I am caught by the time of prayer while I am junub, yet I fast.\"\nThe man said, \"You are not like us, O Messenger of Allah! Allah has forgiven for you all your past and future sins.\"\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"By Allah, I hope that I am the most fearful of you of Allah, and the most knowledgeable of you of what to avoid [as sin].\"\nAbu Bakr (RA) then commented: \"This expression of hope is of the type that it is permissible for a person to make regarding matters in which he has no doubt or uncertainty, saying 'I hope that such and such will be the case', for there is no doubt that the Prophet (ﷺ) was absolutely certain, without any doubt or suspicion, that he was the most God-fearing of all people, and the most knowledgeable of them of what to avoid as sin. This falls under the same category as the narration of Alqama bin Qais: when he was asked, 'Are you a believer?', he replied, 'I hope [that I am]', and there is no doubt or uncertainty that he was among the believers upon whom the rulings applicable to believers were enforced, including those related to marriage, commercial transactions, and the laws of Islam. I have already explained this issue in my book on Faith. So listen to the clear evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) intended by his statement 'I hope' exactly what I have informed you of: he had sworn by Allah that he was the most fearful of Allah among all people.\""
"2015","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abd al-Raḥmān, narrated to us Sufyān, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, from ʿĀʾisha (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) granted a concession regarding a certain matter, but some men refrained from it. So he (ﷺ) said:\n\"What is the matter with some men to whom I command a matter but they refrain from it? By Allah, I know them best in their fear of Allah and I know them best in their reverence for Him.\"\n\n---"
"2016","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' al-'Attar who said: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Zuhri say: Safwan informed me.\n\nAnd narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman who both said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri.\n\nAnd narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram who said: Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Safwan ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Safwan, from Umm al-Darda', from Ka'b ibn 'Asim al-Ash'ari that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"It is not from righteousness to fast while traveling.\"\n\n[Al-Hasan did not attribute Ka'b (ibn 'Asim), and al-Makhzumi did not say al-Ash'ari.]\n\nThis wording has been recorded in al-Kabir."
"2017","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Zurarah al-Ansari, on the authority of Muhammad ibn 'Umar ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw a man around whom people had gathered and under whom they had shaded him. They said, \"This is a man who is fasting.\" Thereupon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"It is not righteousness that you fast while traveling.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: This report indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) only said these words when the fasting traveler was not accepting Allah's concession until the fast became severe upon him, and there was a need to shade him."
"2018","**\nNarrated to us Bundār who said: Narrated to us Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah on the authority of Ibn Abī Dhī’b, from Saʿīd ibn Yasār, from Jābir (RA):\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, *\"Righteousness is not in fasting while traveling,\"* meaning: Righteousness is not *only* in fasting while traveling until the fasting person is overcome by weakness, so much so that he requires shade and water to be sprinkled upon him. For indeed, Allah, the Exalted, has granted the traveler a concession to break the fast and has permitted him to make up the days later. In His clear revelation, He has made known that He intends ease for them, not hardship in this matter. Thus, whoever does not accept the ease of Allah, it may be said to him: \"Your taking upon yourself hardship is not righteousness,\" and hardship will only intensify upon you in this matter.\n\nIt is also possible that the meaning of the hadith is: *\"Righteousness is not solely in fasting while traveling,\"* meaning: Not all righteousness lies in fasting while traveling. Indeed, righteousness may also include fasting while traveling, as well as accepting Allah’s concession and breaking the fast while traveling.\n\nI will, if Allah wills, provide evidence later to confirm the correctness of this interpretation."
"2019","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar Bundār, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Wahhāb ibn ‘Abd al-Majīd, narrated to us Ja‘far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jābir (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out in the Year of the Conquest towards Makkah. He fasted until he reached Kurā‘ al-Ghamīm, and the people were fasting with him. Then he asked for a cup of water, raised it until the people could see it, then drank from it.\n\nAfter that, it was said to him, \"Some of the people have been fasting.\"\nHe said, \"They are the disobedient ones, they are the disobedient ones.\""
"2020","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Sinan al-Wasiti, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, narrated to us Hammād, on the authority of Abū al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jābir (RA) that:\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) once traveled during the month of Ramaḍān. Fasting became extremely difficult for one of his companions, to the extent that his camel began to wander aimlessly beneath a tree. When the Prophet (ﷺ) was informed of this, he ordered the man to break his fast. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) called for a vessel, placed it in his hand, and drank while the people were watching."
"2021","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Raḥmān, narrated to us Sufyān, from al-A‘mash, from Abū al-Ḍuḥā, from Masrūq, from ‘Ā’ishah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) granted a dispensation in regard to a certain matter, but some men abstained from it. So he (ﷺ) said:\n“What is the matter with some men to whom I command a matter but they abstain from it? By Allāh, I know them best in regard to Allāh and I know them best in regard to the severity of their fear of Him.”\n\n---"
"2022","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: In the narration of Abu Sa'id (RA), it is reported that the Prophet (ﷺ) came upon a river of water from the sky of this same kind. He (ﷺ) said in the narration: *\"Indeed, I am not like you; I am riding, while you are walking. Indeed, I am the least burdened among you.\"*\n\nThis narration indicates that the Prophet (ﷺ) himself fasted but initially commanded them to break their fast, since fasting was not difficult for him (ﷺ) as he was riding and had a mount to carry him, not needing to walk. He (ﷺ) commanded them to break their fast because they were walking, and fasting would be difficult for them while traveling on foot. Thus, he (ﷺ) called them disobedient when they refused to break their fast after he (ﷺ) had commanded them to do so, knowing that fasting would be difficult for them since they had no mount and needed to walk.\n\n---"
"2023","**\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Hashim, narrated to us Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi, from Mu'awiyah, from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid, who narrated to me Qaz'ah, who said:\nI came to Abu Sa'id (RA) while he was overwhelmed by people asking him questions. When the people dispersed from him, I said, \"I will not ask you about what these people are asking you about.\" Instead, I asked him about fasting while traveling.\n\nHe said, \"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to Makkah while we were fasting. We stopped at a place, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, 'You are about to approach your enemy, and breaking the fast will make you stronger.' So it was a concession, and some of us fasted while others broke the fast.\n\nThen we stopped at another place, and he said, 'You will meet your enemy tomorrow morning, and breaking the fast will make you stronger, so break your fast.' It was then an obligation, so we broke the fast.\n\nThen he said, 'I saw us fasting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) after that while traveling.'\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: This narration clearly explains that the Prophet (ﷺ) called them disobedient when he commanded them to break the fast to be stronger against their enemy when they were near them and needed to fight them. Yet they did not comply with his order. As for the narration of Jabir (RA) regarding the Year of the Conquest, and this narration is also from that journey, when the Prophet (ﷺ) firmly commanded them to break the fast to be stronger, they fasted until some of them were overcome by weakness, needing to be shaded and have water sprinkled on them. They became too weak to fight their enemy. It was then permissible for him to call them disobedient, since he had commanded them to strengthen themselves against their enemy, but they did not obey or strengthen themselves.\""
"2024","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walid, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Husayn, on the authority of Mujahid, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n**\"Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me.\"**"
"2025","Abu Bakr said: \"I have narrated the report of Anas bin Malik al-Qushayri later on in the section regarding the permissibility of breaking the fast in Ramadan for the pregnant woman and the nursing mother.\""
"2026","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Wahb. And it was also narrated to me by al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, Ibn al-Harith informed me, on the authority of Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Murawwih, from Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami, that he said:\n\n\"O Messenger of Allah! I find myself strong enough to fast while traveling, so is there any sin upon me?\"\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"It is a concession from Allah. Whoever takes it, then it is good. And whoever wishes to fast, there is no sin upon him.\"\n\nHe (the narrator) said: And in the narration of Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"So adhere to the concession of Allah which He has granted you, so accept it.\""
"2027","Narrated to us Sa'id bin Abdullah bin Abd al-Hakam, who said: My father narrated to us, who said: Bakr bin Muzar narrated to us, from Umarah bin Ghaziyyah, from Harb bin Qays, and Umarah claimed that he was pleased with Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Indeed, Allah loves that His concessions (rukhas) be accepted, just as He loves that His disobedience be left.\""
"2028","###\n Narrated to us by Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', he said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Hisham ibn Urwah. And narrated to us by Ja'far ibn Muhammad, he said: Narrated to us by Waki', from Hisham. [This is a second chain]. And narrated to us by Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Tasnim, he said: Informed us by Muhammad, meaning Ibn Bakr, he said: Informed us by Shu'"
"2029","**\nNarrated to us Abu `Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, who said: Narrated to us Marwan ibn Mu`awiyah al-Fazari, who said: Narrated to us `Asim al-Ahwal, on the authority of Abu Nadrah, on the authority of Abu Sa`id al-Khudri and Jabir ibn `Abd Allah (RA), that they both traveled with the Prophet (ﷺ), and he would fast while the one fasting fasted and break his fast while the one breaking his fast broke his fast. Neither did the one breaking his fast criticize the one fasting, nor did the one fasting criticize the one breaking his fast.\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: This is a lengthy chapter which I have extracted in my al-Kabir."
"2030","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab (meaning al-Thaqafi). Also narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Salam ibn Nuḥ, they both said: narrated to us al-Jurayri. Also narrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub ibn Isma'il, narrated to us Sa'id (who is al-Jurayri), from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said:\n\n\"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the month of Ramadan. Some of us were fasting, and some of us were not fasting. The one who was not fasting did not criticize the one who was fasting, nor did the one who was fasting criticize the one who was not fasting.\"\n\nThey used to consider that whoever found strength and fasted, that was good and beautiful, and whoever found weakness and broke his fast, that was also good and beautiful.\n\nThis is the narration of al-Thaqafi, except that he did not say \"during the month of Ramadan.\" Salam ibn Nuḥ did not say \"good and beautiful,\" but rather said: \"They used to consider.\" In the narration of Ibn 'Ulayyah: \"We used to go out early with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ),\" and he did not say \"during the month of Ramadan.\""
"2031","**\nNarrated to us Abdah ibn Abdullah and Muhammad ibn Khalaf al-Haddadi (may Allah have mercy on them both), they said: Narrated to us Abu Dawud al-Hafari, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\"We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at Marr adh-Dhahran when food was brought. He (ﷺ) said to Abu Bakr and Umar (RA), 'Come near and eat.' They replied, 'We are fasting.' He (ﷺ) said, 'Eat, for you are traveling companions for yourselves. Depart for your traveling companions. Come near and eat.'\"\n\nMuhammad ibn Khalaf said: Sufyan ibn Sa'id ath-Thawri narrated to me.\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah, the narrator) said:\n\"This report is of the same kind as that which I mentioned earlier—that the fasting person in travel has the choice to break the fast after part of the day has passed, since the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded them to eat after they had informed him that they were fasting.\"\n\n---"
"2032","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Ala' bin Kurayb, on the authority of Hafs bin Ghiyath, on the authority of 'Asim, on the authority of Mawarriq, on the authority of Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was on a journey. Some of the companions fasted, while others broke their fast. Those who broke their fast girded their loins and worked, whereas those who were fasting became weak and could not perform some of the work."
"2033","###\n Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, on the authority of 'Asim, on the authority of Mawarriq, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said: \"We were with the Prophet (ﷺ) on a journey. Some of us were fasting, and some of us were breaking the fast. We stopped at a place on a very hot day, so hot that some of us were shielding themselves from the sun with their hands. Most of us"
"2034","Abu Bakr narrated: The report of Ibn Abbas states: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fasted during Ramadan in the Year of the Conquest until he reached Al-Kadid, and then he broke his fast."
"2035","**\nNarrated to us 'Abdul-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us az-Zuhri. And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, he informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah, from az-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fasted during the Year of the Conquest until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast.\"\n\nAnd it is only taken by the last part of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\n\nThis is the narration of 'Abdul-Jabbar. And Sufyan added: \"I do not know whether this is from the statement of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), or from the statement of 'Ubaydullah, or from the statement of az-Zuhri.\""
"2036","###\n It was narrated to us by Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Al-Sabbah, who said: It was narrated to us by Ubaydah ibn Humayd, who said: It was narrated to us by Mansur. And it was also narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Musa, who said: It was narrated to us by Jarir, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) departed from"
"2037","**\nNarrated to us Qaz'ah ibn Yahya, on the authority of Abu Sa'id (RA) who said: \"Indeed, we would fast after that while traveling with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the journey of the Conquest of Makkah. I have already dictated this before.\""
"2038","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar ibn Rib'iyy al-Qaysiyy, narrated to us Abu 'Asim, from Sa'eed ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Tanukhiyy, narrated to us 'Atiyyah ibn Qays, narrated to us Qaz'ah ibn Yahya, from Abu Sa'eed al-Khudri (RA), who said:\n\"We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the two nights remaining of Ramadan. We set out fasting until we reached al-Kadid. We were commanded to break the fast, so we did so, and we woke up refreshed. Some of us were fasting, and some of us had broken the fast. When we reached Marr al-Zahran, we were informed of the enemy's presence. We were commanded to break the fast, so we broke it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \"This is reported by Ibn 'Abbas and Abu Nadrah from Abu Sa'eed in this chapter.\"\n\n---"
"2039","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam: Yaḥya ibn Ayyub informed us: Ḥumayd narrated to me that Bakr ibn ‘Abdullah al-Muzani narrated to him, saying: I heard Anas ibn Malik (RA) say, “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was on a journey along with his Companions. Fasting had become difficult for them, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called for a vessel containing water. He drank while mounted on his camel, and the people were watching him.”\n\n---"
"2040","**\nNarrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri', narrated to us Sa'id — he is Ibn Abi Ayyub — narrated to me Yazid ibn Abi Habib, that Kulayb ibn Dhuhal al-Hadrami informed him, from Ubayd ibn Jubayr, who said:\n\n\"I traveled with Abu Basrah al-Ghifari, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), on a ship from al-Fustat during the month of Ramadan. He set out (to sea) and then brought his meal, saying, 'Come near.' I said, 'Do you not see the houses (on the shore)?' So Abu Basrah said, 'Do you desire to abandon the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?'\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"I do not know Kulayb ibn Dhuhal nor Ubayd ibn Jubayr, and I do not accept the religion of one whose uprightness I do not know.\""
"2041","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Abd al-Hakam, who said: my father and Shu‘ayb both informed us, saying: al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib.\n\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya: Ibn Abi Maryam informed us, saying: al-Layth informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu’l-Khayr, from Mansur al-Kalbi: that Dihyah ibn Khalifah set out from his village toward the village of ‘Uqbah ibn ‘Amir from al-Fustat during Ramadan. He broke his fast, and the people broke their fast with him, while others disliked breaking their fast. When he returned to his village, he said: “By Allah, I have seen something today that I never thought I would see. A people have turned away from the guidance of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and his Companions.” He then said to those who had fasted: “O Allah, take me to You.” Ibn ‘Abd al-Hakam said: He set out from his village in Damascus al-Mizzah to a village the size of the village of ‘Uqbah ibn ‘Amir, then he broke his fast. The rest of the wording is the same.\n\nMuhammad ibn Yahya said: Ibn Lahī‘ah says in this narration: Mansur ibn Zayd al-Kalbi."
"2042","###\n Narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi and Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayub, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il, who is Ibn 'Ulayyah, who said: Narrated to us by Ayub, who said: Abu Qilabah told me this hadith, then he said to me: \"Would you like to meet the one who narrated it to me?\" So he guided me to him. I met him, and he said: \"A close relative of mine"
"2043","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-'Ijli, narrated to us 'Ubaydullah, on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of Abu Qilabah, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said:\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was having his meal. He (the Prophet (ﷺ)) said, \"Come and eat.\"\nThe man replied, \"I am fasting.\"\nSo he (the Prophet (ﷺ)) said, \"Come and eat. I will tell you about fasting: Indeed, Allah has relieved the traveler of fasting and half of the prayer, and the pregnant woman or the nursing mother.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: Anas ibn Malik al-Ansari (RA) is from the tribe of Bani 'Abdullah ibn Malik.\n\n---"
"2044","**\nNarrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Waki', on the authority of Abu Hilal, from 'Abdullah ibn Sawadah, from Anas ibn Malik (RA) — a man from the tribe of Bani 'Abdullah ibn Malik — who said: Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad narrated to me, who narrated to me 'Affan, who narrated to us Abu Hilal.\n\nAnd also narrated to us al-Hasan, who narrated to us 'Asim ibn 'Ali, who narrated to us Abu Hilal, and he mentioned the hadith.\n\n'Affan said in his narration from Anas ibn Malik (RA): \"And she is not from the Ansar.\" And 'Affan said in his narration: \"And the nursing mother.\""
"2045","###\n Muhammad bin Yahya and Zakariya bin Yahya bin Aban narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Muhammad bin Ja'far informed us, Zayd (who is Ibn Aslam) informed me, from Iyad bin Abdullah, from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"I have not seen anyone more deficient in intellect and religion than you, O women, yet you can overpower the mind of a prudent man.\" The women asked him, \""
"2046","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-‘Ala’, narrated to us Sufyan, from Yahya, who said: I heard Abu Salamah say: I heard ‘A’ishah (RA) say:\n“There would be upon me (the obligation of) fasting from Ramadan that I had not made up until Sha‘ban came.”"
"2047","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from 'Aishah (RA), with similar wording:\n\n*\"The menstruating woman must make up the fasts for the days she missed, but she is permitted to delay making up the obligatory fasts until the following Sha'ban.\"*"
"2048","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Razzaq, who said: We were informed by Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya bin Sa'id narrated to me, saying: I heard Abu Salamah bin Abdur-Rahman say: I heard Aisha (RA) say: \"There used to be some days of Ramadan upon me that I was unable to fast until Sha'ban would come.\" I thought that this was due to her special status with the Prophet (ﷺ). Yahya"
"2049","Narrated to us Ali ibn Shu'ayb, who narrated to us Abu al-Nadr, who narrated to us al-Ashja'i, from Sufyan, from al-Suddi, from al-Bahiyy, from Aisha (RA), who said: \"I never made up the remaining fasts that were due to me from Ramadan during the time of the Prophet (ﷺ) except in the month of Sha'ban.\"\nCommentary: This narration serves as evidence for the ruling detailed in the chapter: Aisha (RA) delayed making up her missed obligatory Ramadan fasts (including those she missed due to menstruation, which must be made up during her days of ritual purity) until Sha'ban, confirming that a menstruating woman is permitted to delay the make-up of her excused missed fasts until the following Sha'ban."
"2050","Narrated to us Ibrahim bin Mas'ud al-Hamdani, who said: Narrated to us Abu Usamah, who said: Narrated to us Zaidah, from Isma'il al-Suddi, from Abdullah al-Bahi, from Aisha (RA), with a similar narration. And he said: \"During the entire life of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\""
"2051","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Uthman al-Ijli, who said: Narrated to us Ubaydullah, from Shaiban, from as-Suddi, from Abdullah al-Bahyi, who said: I heard Aisha (RA) say: \"I did not make up any fasts that were due upon me from Ramadan except during the month of Sha'ban, until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away.\"\n\nAlso, narrated to us Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Abdul-Hakam, who said: Narrated to"
"2052","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb; he narrated to us his uncle, who narrated to me Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ja'far. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, who informed us Yahya ibn Ayyub, who narrated to me Ibn Abi Ja'far. And narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban, who narrated to us Amr ibn Dhafir, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ja'far, from Muhammad ibn Ja'far (who is Ibn al-Zubayr), from Urwah, from Aishah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever dies while he has missed fasts to make up, his guardian should fast on his behalf.\""
"2053","###\n Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani narrated to us, saying: Al-Mu'tamar narrated to us, stating: I read to Al-Fudayl bin Mayssara, from Abu Hariz, regarding a woman who died while she owed fasting days. He said: Ikrima narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: A woman came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, my mother passed away while she owed fasting for fifteen days.\" The Prophet ("
"2054","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Ibn Abi ‘Adiyy, on the authority of Shu‘bah, on the authority of Sulayman, on the authority of Muslim al-Batīn, on the authority of Sa‘īd ibn Jubayr, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbās (RA) that a woman once set out to sea and vowed to fast for a month. She then died, so her brother came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and asked him about it. The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded him to fast on her behalf."
"2055","###\n Abdullah bin Sa'id Al-Ashaj narrated to us, saying: Abu Khalid narrated to us, saying: Al-A'mash narrated to us, from Al-Hakam, and Salamah bin Kuhail, and Muslim Al-Batin, from Sa'id bin Jubayr, and 'Ata', and Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said: A woman came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"My sister has passed away, and she owed two consecutive months of fasting.\" He ("
"2056","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Ma'bad, who said: Narrated to us by Salih bin Abdullah at-Tirmidhi, who said: Narrated to us by Athar, from Ash'ath, from Muhammad (who is Ibn Abi Layla), from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever dies while he still owes fasting for a month, let him feed a poor person for each day on his behalf.\" Abu Bakr (the narrator/compiler) said"
"2057","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Ziyad al-Dhabbi al-Wasiti at Aylah, who said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, and Sharik ibn Abdullah informed us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever dies while he has outstanding fasts of Ramadan that he failed to make up before his death must feed a poor person half a sa' of wheat for each missed day.\""
"2058","###\n Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abdah, and narrated to us by Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Al-Sabbah Al-Za'farani; they both said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, and narrated to us by Abu Mu'awiyah; they said: Narrated to us by Hisham ibn Urwah. Also, narrated to us by Harun ibn Ishaq, who said: Narrated to us by Abdah, from Hisham, from his father, from As"
"2059","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us Ibn Abi Hazim, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Sahl (who is Ibn Sa'd).\n\nAnd narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan.\n\nAnd narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Waki', on the authority of Sufyan, on the authority of Abu Hazim, on the authority of Sahl ibn Sa'd (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The people will remain upon goodness as long as they hasten to break the fast.\"\n\n---"
"2060","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Amr, and narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, narrated to us 'Ali ibn Muhammad. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Ahmas, narrated to us al-Muharibi, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, and from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The religion will continue to prevail as long as people hasten to break the fast, for the Jews and the Christians delay it.\"\n\n---"
"2061","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Ṣafwān al-Thaqafī, narrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, narrated to us Sufyān, from Abū Ḥāzim, from Sahl ibn Saʿd (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “My nation will remain upon my Sunnah as long as they do not wait for the stars to break their fast.”\n\nHe (RA) said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) was fasting, he would command a man to stand on something elevated, and when he said, “The sun has set,” he would break his fast.\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said: This is how Ibn Abī Ṣafwān narrated it to us. He hesitated to include the last statement as being from Sahl ibn Saʿd (RA), thinking it might be from the statement of al-Thawrī or from the saying of Abū Ḥāzim, so it was inserted into the hadith."
"2062","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli, narrated to us al-Walid, on the authority of al-Awza‘i, narrated to me Qurrah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Haywa’il that he heard al-Zuhri narrating.\n\nAnd narrated to us ‘Amr ibn ‘Ali, narrated to us Abu ‘Asim, narrated to us al-Awza‘i, narrated to us Qurrah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Ibn Shihab—he is al-Zuhri—on the authority of Abu Salamah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah, on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\nAllah, the Most Blessed and Exalted, said:\n“The most beloved of My servants to Me are those who hasten to break their fast.”"
"2063","**\nNarrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti, narrated to us Shu'ayb ibn Ishaq, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, and also narrated to us Musa ibn Sahl al-Ramli, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz, narrated to us al-Qasim ibn Ghusn, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) would not pray Maghrib until he had broken his fast, even if it was only a sip of water.\n\nMusa ibn Sahl said: His origin was Kufan, meaning al-Qasim ibn Ghusn. He was narrated from by Waki' and Sulayman ibn Hayyan."
"2064","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Fudail, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Malik. And also narrated to us by Muhammad bin Al-A'la Al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us by Yazid, meaning Ibn Zurai', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan bin Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abdur-Rahman bin Abi Layla. Both of them (Al-Malik"
"2065","**\nNarrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban, narrated to us Miskin ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman al-Tamimi, narrated to me Yahya ibn Ayyub, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:\n*\"When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was fasting, he would not pray (i.e., perform Maghrib) until we brought him fresh dates (rutab) and water. He would eat and drink if fresh dates were available. But in winter, he would not pray until we brought him dried dates (tamr) and water.\"*\n\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Muhriz, from Husayn ibn ‘Ali al-Ju‘fi, from Zai’dah, from Humayd al-Tawil with this chain."
"2066","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Umar ibn ‘Ali ibn Muqaddam and Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq (RA) – they said: Narrated to us Sa‘id ibn ‘Amir – from Shu‘bah – from ‘Abd al-‘Aziz ibn Suhayb – from Anas ibn Malik (RA) – who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said,\n“Whoever finds dates should break his fast with them, and whoever does not find them should break his fast with water, for it is purifying.”\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, “This narration has been transmitted only by this chain from Sa‘id ibn ‘Amir from Shu‘bah.”\n\n---"
"2067","**\nNarrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, and likewise narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammad (meaning Ibn Zayd), both from 'Asim, and narrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us 'Asim, from Hafsah bint Sirin, from al-Rabaab, from her uncle Salman ibn 'Amir al-Dhabbi (RA), who said:\n\nI heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say:\n*\"Charity given to a poor person is charity, but charity given to a relative is twofold: it is charity and a means of maintaining family ties.\"*\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) also said:\n*\"When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dates, for they bring blessings. If he does not find dates, then with water, for it is purifying.\"*\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) also said:\n*\"Sacrifice the 'aqeeqah for a boy, remove from him harm, and shed blood on his behalf.\"*\n\nThis narration is from the narration of 'Abd al-Jabbar. The other two narrators (Ahmad ibn 'Abdah and Hammad ibn Zayd) said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n*\"When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dates. If he does not find dates, let him break it with water, for it is purifying.\"*\n\nThey did not mention the narration about charity or the 'aqeeqah."
"2068","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Abu al-Zinad, on the authority of al-A'raj, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"None of you should observe continuous fasting (wisal).\"\nThey asked, \"O Messenger of Allah, but you yourself observe continuous fasting!\"\nHe replied, \"I am not like any one of you. My Lord feeds me and gives me to drink.\""
"2069","###\n Narrated to us by Abdullah bin Muhammad az-Zuhri, who said: Narrated to us by Abu Sa'id (meaning the freed slave of Banu Hashim), on the authority of Shu'bah, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Beware of Al-Wisal (continuing the fast without breaking it).\" They said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), you yourself practice Al-Wisal.\" He replied, \""
"2070","###\n Narrated to us 'Amr bin 'Ali, who said: Narrated to us Khalid bin Al-Harith, who said: Narrated to us Humaid. And narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to me Ibn Abi 'Adiyy, from Humaid, from Thabit, from Anas (RA) who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to observe continuous fasting (Wisal) during the month of Ramadan, and some of the Muslims also did so. When this reached the Prophet ("
"2071","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Fudail, who said: Narrated to us by Umarah bin Al-Qa'qa', on the authority of Ibn Abi Nu'aim, who said: I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) mentioning that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Beware of Al-Wisal (continuous fasting without breaking the fast at night).\" He said this three times. They said, \"O Messenger of Allah, you practice Al-W"
"2072","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani‘, narrated to us ‘Ubaydah — meaning ibn Humayd — on the authority of al-A‘mash, on the authority of Abu Salih, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would perform *wisal* (continuous fasting) until the last part of the night. Some of his Companions did the same, so he forbade them from it.\nThey said, “O Messenger of Allah, you yourself do this!”\nHe replied, “I am not like any of you. My Lord feeds and gives me drink. I spend the night with my Lord, who provides for me.”"
"2073","Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, reporting that Ibn Wahb informed his group, and that Amr ibn Malik al-Shara'bi narrated to him, from Ibn al-Had, from Abdullah ibn Khabbab, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). This narration is identical to the hadith of Ibn Umar on the topic of wilal (consecutive fasting without breaking the fast at sunset between consecutive days). The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"So which of you will fast consecutively from one suhur to the next suhur?\""
"2074","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bakr (RA): The narration of Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah (RA) regarding the question he asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about Islam, including fasting in Ramadan.\n\nHe said: \"Is there any other fast besides Ramadan that is obligatory upon me?\"\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) replied: \"No, except what you volunteer to fast.\"\n\n---"
"2075","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us Al-Muhallab bin Abi Habiba, on the authority of Al-Hasan, on the authority of Abu Bakra (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Let none of you say, 'I fasted the entire month of Ramadan' or 'I stood in prayer the entire month of Ramadan.'\" Allah knows best. Did he dislike self-praise for his Umm"
"2076","###\n Yusuf bin Musa and Muhammad bin Isa narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, who is Ibn Umair, from Muhammad bin al-Muntashir, from Humaid bin Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who raised it to the Prophet (ﷺ). Muhammad bin Isa added: \"To the Prophet (ﷺ).\" He said: \"He was asked: 'Which prayer is most virtuous after the obligatory prayers, and which fasting is most virtuous after the month"
"2077","Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq al-Khawlani narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, that Abdullah ibn Abi Qays narrated to him, that he heard Aisha (RA) say: [the wording of this narration is truncated in the source, with the full text reported via the following parallel chain:] And Abdullah ibn Hashim narrated to us; Abd al-Rahman narrated to him, from Mu'awiyah, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qays, that he heard Aisha (RA) say: \"The most beloved month to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for fasting was Sha'ban, after which he would connect its fasting to Ramadan.\""
"2078","Muhammad bin Aziz al-Ayli narrated to us, stating that Salama narrated to them, on the authority of Uqail, who said: Yahya bin Abi Kathir narrated to me, Abu Salamah bin Abdur-Rahman narrated to me, and Aisha (RA) narrated to me, saying: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not fast in any month of the year more than he fasted in the month of Sha'ban; he used to fast the entire month.\""
"2079","Narrated to us Al-Sana'ani Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la, who narrated to us Khalid, who narrated to us Hisham, on the authority of Yahya, who mentioned Abu Salamah, that Aishah (RA) narrated to him. And narrated to us Abu Musa, who narrated to us Abu Amir, who narrated to us Hisham ibn Sanbar, on the authority of Yahya, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Aishah (RA) with the same [content]. And [the narrator] added: The Prophet (ﷺ) would say, \"Take from religious deeds what you are able to bear, for indeed Allah does not grow weary of your deeds until you grow weary. And the most beloved prayer to him was the one he remained consistent in, even if it was small in amount. And when he offered a prayer, he would complete it and adhere to it steadfastly.\""
"2080","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, from Hisham, from his father, from 'Aishah (RA), who said:\n\"In the pre-Islamic era (Jahiliyyah), the Quraysh would fast on the day of 'Ashura'. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast on that day as well. When he migrated to Madinah, he continued to fast on that day and ordered the people to fast. Then, when Ramadan was revealed, Ramadan became the obligatory fast, and the fast of 'Ashura' was left as a voluntary act. So whoever wished would fast it, and whoever wished would leave it.\""
"2081","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Khushram, who said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us. And narrated to us by Salam bin Junadah, who said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us. And narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Jarir and Abu Mu'awiyah, both of them, narrated to us from Al-A'mash, from Umayrah, from Abdur-Rahman bin Yazid, who said: Al-Ash'ath bin Qays entered upon"
"2082","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, who narrated to us from Yahya, who narrated to us from Ubaydullah, who informed me from Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said: \"Ashura was a day that the people of the pre-Islamic era (Jahiliyyah) used to fast. When Ramadan was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about it, so he said: 'It is one of the days of Allah; whoever wishes may fast it,"
"2083","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Dawud, narrated to us Shayban ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman al-Nahwi, on the authority of Ash‘ath ibn Abi al-Sha‘tha’, on the authority of Ja‘far ibn Abi Thawr, on the authority of Jabir ibn Samurah (RA), who said:\n\n\"We used to fast the day of ‘Ashura’ before the obligation of Ramadan was revealed, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would urge us to fast it and encourage us to do so. When Ramadan was made obligatory, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) neither urged us to fast it nor encouraged us to do so, yet we still used to fast it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: The report of Jabir ibn Samurah (RA) is supported by the report of ‘Ammar ibn Yasir (RA), and it contains evidence that they used to fast the day of ‘Ashura’ even after the obligation of Ramadan was revealed, as is narrated in the reports of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) and ‘A’ishah (RA). Hence, whoever wishes may fast it, and whoever wishes may leave it.\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: Musaddad—one of our companions—asked me about the meaning of the report of ‘Ammar ibn Yasir (RA). I replied to him, explaining:\n\n\"When the Prophet (ﷺ) commands his Ummah to do something once, it does not necessarily mean that the command remains obligatory every year or at every subsequent time. Whatever he commanded at a particular time, his Ummah should act upon it if it was a command of obligation; then the obligation remains upon them forever until a later time when it is conveyed that the obligation has been lifted from them. But if it was a command of recommendation, guidance, or virtue, then that act remains a virtue forever unless he forbids them from it at a later time. His silence at a later time after having commanded it initially does not negate an obligation if it was initially a command of obligation, nor does his silence at a later time after having commanded a virtuous act invalidate the virtue of that act. For when he commands something once, that command suffices for all time unless he commands its opposite. Silence does not annul a command.\"\n\nThis is the meaning with which I answered the questioner regarding this issue. Perhaps I have expanded in the explanation here beyond what I had answered him at that time."
"2084","**\nNarrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub, narrated to us Hashim, informed us Abu Bishr, on the authority of Sa'id ibn Jubayr, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"When the Prophet (ﷺ) arrived in Madinah, he found the Jews fasting on the day of 'Ashura'. He asked them about it, and they replied, 'This is the day when Allah manifested Musa (AS) and the Children of Israel over Fir'awn (Pharaoh), and we fast it in honor of him.'\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, 'We have more right to Musa (AS) than you,' and he commanded the fasting of that day.\"\n\nNarrated to us Bishr ibn Mu'adh, narrated to us Hashim ibn Bashir, on the authority of Abu Bishr, with a similar narration.\nHe (Ibn 'Abbas) said: \"So the Prophet (ﷺ) fasted it and commanded its fasting.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \"Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj asked me about this.\""
"2085","###\n Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, saying: Uthman bin Umar narrated to us, saying: Yunus bin Yazid narrated to us, from Az-Zuhri, from Humaid bin Abdur-Rahman, from Mu'awiyah (RA), who said: He delivered a sermon in Medina on the day he arrived on the Day of Ashura, and he said: \"Where are your scholars, O people of Medina? I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'This is the Day of Ashura"
"2086","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, who said: Narrated to us by Ubaydullah, who is the son of Abu Yazid, and I verified it from him: Ibn Abbas (RA) was asked about fasting on the Day of Ashura. He said, \"I do not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fasted any day seeking its superiority over other days except the Day of Ashura, and this is the month of Ramadan.\""
"2087","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, who said: Narrated to us Ghaylan—he is Ibn Jarir—who said: Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, on the authority of Abu Qatadah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Fasting on the Day of 'Ashura', I anticipate that Allah will expiate the sins of the year preceding it. And fasting on the Day of 'Arafah, I anticipate that Allah will expiate the sins of the year preceding it and the year following it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: The Prophet (ﷺ) has informed us that fasting on the Day of 'Arafah expiates the sins of the year preceding it and the year following it. This indicates that righteous deeds may precede actions, such that a righteous deed performed in advance may expiate the sins of the subsequent year."
"2088","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh ibn 'Afra' (RA): She said, \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a message to the villages of the Ansar surrounding Madinah saying: 'Whoever woke up fasting should complete his fast, and whoever woke up not fasting should complete the rest of his day.' Thereafter, we used to fast on this day, and we would make our young children fast as well. We would take them to the mosque and make for them a toy"
"2089","**\nNarrated to us Abu'l-Mutarrif ibn Abi'l-Wazir who said: Ghilaynah bint Ummaynah, the freed slave of Allah and she was the daughter of Ruzaynah, reported:\n\nShe said: I said to my mother: Did you hear the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say anything regarding 'Ashura'?\n\nShe replied: \"He would magnify its status and summon the suckling children of his nursemaids and the suckling children of Fatimah (RA), then he would spit into their mouths, and he would command their mothers not to give them milk until the night.\""
"2090","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir — and he is among the trustworthy narrators of hadith — and narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Maslamah ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us ‘Alīlah bint al-Kumayt al-‘Atakiyyah, who said: I heard my mother Ummaynah — with similar wording — and she added: “Thus Allah was sufficient for them.”\nHe (Maslamah) said: And her mother was a servant of the Prophet (ﷺ), and she was called: Ruzaynah."
"2091","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Hashim, Ziyad ibn Ayub; he narrated to us from Hushaym; he informed us from Husayn; on the authority of Al-Sha'bi; from Muhammad ibn Sayfi Al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to us on the day of 'Ashura' and asked, \"Have you fasted today?\" Some of them said, \"Yes,\" while others said, \"No.\" He (ﷺ) said, \""
"2092","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Yazid bin Abi Ubayd, who said: Narrated to us Salamah bin Al-Akwa' (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to a man from the tribe of Aslam: \"Call out to your people, or to the people, on the day of Ashura: 'Whoever has eaten should fast for the remainder of the day, and whoever has not eaten should fast"
"2093","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, who narrated to us from Yahya, who narrated to us from Yazid bin Abi Ubayd, who narrated to us from Salamah, who is the son of Al-Akwa' (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to a man from the tribe of Aslam: \"Call out to your people, or to the people, on the day of 'Ashura': 'Whoever has eaten, let him fast for the remainder of the day, and"
"2094","###\n Abu Musa narrated to us, stating: Al-Dahhak bin Makhlad, known as Abu Asim, informed us; he said: Umar bin Muhammad informed us; he said: Salim narrated to us, from Ibn Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Today is the Day of Ashura. Whoever wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast.\" [This narration is supported by the reports of Aisha (RA) and Mu'awiyah (RA) on this same chapter.]"
"2095","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Musaddad, who said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us from Ibn Abi Layla, from Dawud bin Ali, from his father, from his grandfather Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Fast on the day of Ashura, and differ from the Jews; fast a day before it or a day after it.\""
"2096","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya bin Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us Mu'awiya bin 'Amr, who said: Narrated to us Al-Hakam bin Al-A'raj, who said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas (RA) while he was in the Sacred Mosque (Al-Masjid Al-Haram), reclining on his cloak. I asked him about fasting on the Day of 'Ashura'. He said, \"Count the days, and when the morning"
"2097","Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Waki' narrated to us, from Hajib ibn Umar, from Al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj, with a similar narration, stating that the Prophet (ﷺ) was resting his head on his cloak while sitting in the water of Zamzam."
"2098","Narrated to us 'Abdah ibn 'Abdullah, who said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us, who said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hajib ibn 'Umar, from Al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) regarding the Day of 'Ashura', he said: \"It is the ninth day.\" I said: \"Did Muhammad (ﷺ) fast on that day as well?\""
"2099","Abu Bakr said: The narration of Abu Qatadah from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Fasting on the Day of Arafah expiates the sins of the past year and the coming year.\" I dictated this in the chapter concerning the fast of Ashura."
"2100","Ja'far bin Muhammad al-Tha'labi narrated to us, saying: Waki' narrated to us, on the authority of Musa bin Ali, from his father, from 'Uqba bin 'Amir (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The Day of 'Arafah, the Day of Sacrifice (Eid al-Adha), and the Days of Tashriq are our festival for the people of Islam, and they are days of eating and drinking.\"\nAbu '"
"2101","Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Abu Dihyah Hawshab bin Uqayl al-Jarmi narrated to us, Al-Abdi narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting on the Day of Arafah while one is at Arafah.\""
"2102","Narrated to us by Bishr bin Mu'adh Al-'Aqadi, who said: Narrated to us by Hammad, meaning Ibn Zayd, who said: Narrated to us by Ayoub, on the authority of Ikrimah, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas, from his mother Umm Al-Fadl (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) broke his fast at 'Arafah. Milk was brought to him, and he drank it.\""
"2103","###\n Muhammad bin al-Ala' bin Kurayb narrated to us, saying: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Al-A'mash. And also, Bundar narrated to us, saying: Abdur-Rahman narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Al-Aswad, from Aisha (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not fast during the ten [days of Dhul-Hijjah].\" And in the"
"2104","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Uthman bin Umar, who said: Narrated to us Yunus, on the authority of Az-Zuhri, on the authority of Urwah, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would leave performing an act even though he loved to do it, out of fear that it might be taken as a Sunnah by the people and thus become obligatory upon them. And he (ﷺ) loved whatever of"
"2105","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Aban, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, who said: Narrated to us Husayn, on the authority of Mujahid, on the authority of Abdullah bin Amr (RA), who said: \"I was a man who was very zealous in worship. My father married me off, and then he visited me. He asked my wife, 'How do you find your husband?' She replied, 'He is an excellent man; he does not sleep at night, nor does he break his"
"2106","###\n Narrated to me by 'Abd al-Warith ibn 'Abd al-Samad, who dictated from the original text: Narrated to me by my father: Narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, from Abu 'Iyad, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr (RA), who said: \"I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and asked him about fasting. He said, 'Fast one day of every month, and you will have the"
"2107","Abu Bakr narrated to us, saying: In the report of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr (RA), he said: \"Fast the fast of Dawud (David), for it is the most just (al-a'dal) of fasting in the sight of Allah.\" And in the report of Habib ibn Abi Thabit, on the authority of Al-Abbas, on the authority of Abdullah ibn Amr (RA)..."
"2108","It is narrated on the authority of the Prophet Muhammad (ﷺ) that he said: \"The best of fasting is the fasting of David (AS).\" [The author of the collection notes:] \"I have compiled the chains of narration for these related reports in my book al-Kabir.\""
"2109","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Tasneem, and he said: narrated to us Muhammad – meaning Ibn Bakr, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi’, he said: narrated to us ‘Abd al-Razzaq, they both said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard ‘Aṭā’ claim that Abu al-‘Abbās the poet informed him that he heard ‘Abdullāh ibn ‘Amr ibn al-‘Āṣ (RA) say:\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) was informed that I would recite the Qur’an in a continuous manner and pray the night prayer. He (ﷺ) either sent for me or met me and said:\n\n*\"Have I not been informed that you fast without breaking and pray the night prayer? Do not do that, for your eyes have a right, your soul has a right, and your family has a right. So fast and break your fast, pray and sleep, and fast every tenth day one day, and you will have the reward of nine days.\"*\n\nHe (RA) said: \"But I find myself able to do more, O Messenger of Allah!\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Then fast the fast of Dāwūd (AS).\"*\n\nHe (RA) said: \"And how did Dāwūd (AS) fast, O Messenger of Allah?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"He would fast one day and break the next, and he would not flee when he met the enemy.\"*\n\nHe (RA) said: \"Who can bear this, O Prophet of Allah?\"\n\n‘Aṭā’ (RA) said: So I do not know how he mentioned fasting perpetually, then the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"No one who fasts perpetually has fasted.\"*\n\nThis is the narration of al-Bursānī.\n\nIn the narration of ‘Abd al-Razzaq (RA), he said: \"I fast continuously,\" and he said: \"So he either sent for me,\" and he said: \"I find myself able to do more than that.\"\n\n---"
"2110","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Abu Al-Walid, who said: Narrated to us by Ikrimah ibn Ammar, who said: It was narrated to me by Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who said: It was narrated to me by Abu Salamah ibn Abdur-Rahman, who said: It was narrated to us by Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Al-As, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent for me and"
"2111","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdah: On the authority of Hammad (i.e., ibn Zayd), narrated to us Ghaylān ibn Jarīr, narrated to us ‘Abdullāh ibn Ma‘bad al-Zimmānī, on the authority of Abū Qatādah, who said:\n‘Umar (RA) said to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), “What do you say about a man who fasts for two days and breaks his fast for one day?”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Can anyone endure that?”\nHe said, “What do you say about a man who fasts for one day and breaks his fast for one day?”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “That is the fast of Dāwūd (AS).”\nHe said, “What do you say about a man who fasts for one day and breaks his fast for two days?”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “I wish I were given the strength to do that.”"
"2112","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, who said: Narrated to us by Khalid, meaning Ibn Abdullah, from Suhayl, who is the son of Abu Salih, from an-Nu'man ibn Abi Ayyash al-Ansari, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"No servant fasts a day in the cause of Allah, but Allah will, on account of that day, distance his face from the Fire"
"2113","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Minhal, narrated to us Hammad, on the authority of Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, on the authority of al-Nu'man ibn Abi 'Ayyash, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"There is no servant who fasts a day in the way of Allah, seeking thereby the Face of Allah, except that Allah will distance him from the Fire by seventy autumns.\"*\n\n---"
"2114","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us al-'Aziz—meaning Ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi—on the authority of Safwan ibn Sulayman and Sa'd ibn Sa'id, on the authority of 'Umar ibn Thabit, on the authority of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever fasts Ramadan and then follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is as if he has fasted the entire year.\"\n\n---"
"2115","**\nNarrated to us Saʿīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam and al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr ibn al-Muʿārak al-Miṣriyyān, who said:\n\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān, who said:\n\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah, from Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Dhimārī, from Abū Asmāʾ al-Raḥabī, from Thawbān (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Fasting the month of Ramaḍān is equivalent to ten months, and fasting six days of Shawwāl is equivalent to two months. Thus, that is the fasting of the entire year.\"\n\nHe meant: Ramaḍān along with six days after it."
"2116","**\nNarrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn al-Shahid, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Yaman, from Sufyan, from 'Asim, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi', from Sawa' al-Khuza'i, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) would fast on Mondays and Thursdays.\""
"2117","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Abu Musa (RA), they said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah. And narrated to us Bundar as well, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far. And narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Sa'id, from Qatadah. And narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Waki', from Mahdi ibn Maymun, all of them from Ghaylan ibn Jarir, from 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani—meaning Abu Qatadah al-Ansari (RA)—who said:\n\nWhile we were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), 'Umar (RA) came to him and asked, \"O Prophet of Allah, what about fasting on Monday?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) replied, \"It is the day on which I was born, and the day on which I was sent revelation, and the day on which I shall die.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Qatadah.\n\nIn the narration of Waki': A man asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and 'Umar (RA) was not mentioned. And he (ﷺ) said, \"On it I was born, and on it revelation was sent to me.\""
"2118","And the hadith of Shu'bah (RA) is as follows: He narrated to us that he heard Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani (RA) report: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about his fasting, so he became angry. He was then asked about fasting on Mondays and Thursdays, so he said: \"That is the day, meaning Monday, on which I was born, and on which I was commissioned as a Prophet\", or he said: \"On which revelation was sent down to me.\"\nCommentary: This narration establishes the elevated virtue of Monday, which is the basis for the ruling that fasting on this day is mustahabb (recommended) in Islamic jurisprudence. The hadith links Monday to three pivotal, blessed events in the life of the Prophet (ﷺ): his birth, the start of his prophetic mission, and the descent of the first divine revelation to him, with mainstream Islamic tradition also confirming his blessed passing from this world occurred on the same day, further cementing the day’s significance.\n---"
"2119","Sa'id bin Abi Yazid al-Warraq al-Firyabi narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Yusuf narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr bin Ayyash narrated to me, from Umar bin Muhammad, who said: Sharhabil bin Sa'd narrated to me, from Usamah, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast on Mondays and Thursdays.\" And he would say: \"Indeed, on these two days, the deeds are presented (to Allah).\""
"2120","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, that Malik ibn Anas informed him, on the authority of Muslim ibn Abi Maryam, on the authority of Abu Salih al-Samman, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, said:\n\n\"Deeds of the people are presented twice every Friday: on Monday and on Thursday. Then every believing servant is granted forgiveness, except a servant between whom and his brother there is rancor.\" He said: \"Leave them, or defer them, until they reconcile.\"\n\n**COMMENTARY:**\nAbu Bakr said: \"This report in the Muwatta' of Malik is *mawquf* (stopped at the Companion) and not *marfu'* (attributed to the Prophet ﷺ). However, in the Muwatta' of Ibn Wahb, it is *marfu'* and *sahih*.\""
"2121","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abd al-Wārith ibn ‘Abd al-Ṣamad al-‘Anbarī, who said: My father narrated to me, who said: Shu‘bah narrated to us, from Ziyād ibn Fayyāḍ, from Abū ‘Iyāḍ, from ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Amr (RA), who said: I came to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) and asked him about fasting. He said: “Fast one day of the month, and you will have the reward of fasting the entire month.”\n\n---"
"2122","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Hujr, narrated to us Isma‘il ibn Ja‘far, narrated to us Muhammad—he is Ibn Abi Harmalah—on the authority of ‘Aṭā’ ibn Yasār, on the authority of Abū Dharr (RA), who said:\n“My close friend (ﷺ) enjoined three things upon me, and I shall never abandon them, if Allah wills:\nHe enjoined upon me the prayer of al-ḍuḥā, the witr before sleep, and fasting three days of every month.”\n\n---"
"2123","**\nNarrated to us Bishr ibn Hilal al-Sawwaf, who said: Narrated to us 'Abd al-Warith — meaning Ibn Sa'id al-'Anbari — on the authority of Abu al-Tayyah, on the authority of Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\"My close friend, Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ), enjoined three things upon me: fasting three days of every month, praying Witr before sleeping, and praying two rak'ahs of Duha.\""
"2124","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, he said: my father and Shu'ayb both informed us, they said: al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, that Mutarrif ibn 'Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah narrated to him, that 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As al-Thaqafi called for milk to give him to drink, so Mutarrif said: \"I am fasting.\" So he (i.e., 'Uthman) said: \"I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: 'Fasting is a shield from the Fire, like the shield of one of you in battle.'\""
"2125","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, he said: My father and Shu'ayb both narrated to us, they said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, that Mutarrif ibn 'Amir ibn Sa'sa'ah from Banu 'Amir reported to him that 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As al-Thaqafi called for milk to give him to drink. So Mutarrif said, \"I am fasting.\" Thereupon he said, \"I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: 'Fasting is a shield from the Fire, just as one of you shields himself from battle.' And I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Fasting is good; fasting for three days of the month.'\""
"2126","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: Narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, who said: Narrated to us Ghaylan ibn Jarir, who said: Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, on the authority of Abu Qatadah.\n\nAnd narrated to us Bundar, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of Ghaylan ibn Jarir, who heard 'Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, on the authority of Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Fasting for three days of every month is fasting for the entire year.\"\n\nThis is the wording of Shu'bah's hadith.\n\nIn the hadith of Hammad ibn Zayd, it is stated: \"Fasting for three days of every month, and Ramadan to Ramadan, that is fasting for the entire year.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: The reports of Abu Hurayrah and 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr in this meaning have been compiled in the *al-Kabir* (the larger collection). He also said: In the report of Abu Salamah, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr: \"For every good deed is multiplied tenfold; thus, that is fasting for the entire year.\"\n\nSimilarly, in the report of Abu 'Uthman, on the authority of Abu Dharr, he said: And the confirmation of this is in the Book of Allah, the Exalted: \"Whoever comes with a good deed, for him there shall be ten like it.\" (Quran 6:160)"
"2127","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn Abd al-A'la, who narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, the freedman of the clan of Talha, on the authority of Musa ibn Talha, on the authority of Ibn al-Hutaykiyyah, who reported: Umar (RA) said, \"Who is present among us on the Day of Qahah?\" Abu Dharr (RA) replied, \"I am. I bear witness that I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) being brought a hare, and on another occasion, a Bedouin brought a hare. The man who brought it said, 'I saw it as if it was bleeding.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ate from it, then said to the people, 'Eat.' A man said, 'I am fasting.' He (ﷺ) said, 'What is the nature of your fast?' The man informed him. He (ﷺ) then said, 'Then what about you with regard to the white days?' The man asked, 'What are those?' He (ﷺ) replied, 'Fasting three days every month: the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth [of the lunar month].'\"\n\nNarrated to us Abd al-Jabbar, who narrated to us Sufyan, who narrated to me Umar ibn Uthman ibn Mawahib, on the authority of Musa ibn Talha, on the authority of Ibn al-Hutaykiyyah, on the authority of Abu Dharr (RA) with the same wording.\n\nAbu Bakr (the compiler of this hadith collection) said: \"I have presented this chapter in full in my book *Al-Kabir*, and I have clarified that Musa ibn Talha heard from Abu Dharr (RA) the narration about fasting, but not the narration about the hare. And Ibn al-Hutaykiyyah narrated both narrations in full.\"\n---"
"2128","Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us by Abdur-Rahman, who narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Sulaiman Al-A'mash, from Yahya bin Sam, from Musa bin Talhah, who said: I heard Abu Dharr (RA) at Ar-Rabadhah saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: \"When you fast from the month, fast on the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth.\""
"2129","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Abu Dawud, who said: Narrated to us Shiban bin Abdur-Rahman an-Nahwi, on the authority of Asim, on the authority of Zirr, on the authority of Abdullah (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) used to fast three days at the beginning of every month, and among his fasting was the day of Friday. Abu Bakr (the commentator) said: This narration may be understood as the report of Abu U"
"2130","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-A'la al-Sana'ani. He said: Narrated to us Khalid — and he is Ibn al-Harith — who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from Yazid — and he is al-Rishk —, from Mu'adhah, who said:\nI asked 'Aa'ishah (RA): \"Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to fast three days every month?\"\nShe said: \"Yes.\"\nI asked: \"From which days?\"\nShe said: \"He (ﷺ) did not mind from which days he fasted.\""
"2131","**\nNarrated to us al-ʿAbbās ibn Yazīd al-Baḥrānī, who dictated it in Baghdād: narrated to us Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah: narrated to us Yazīd ibn Kaysān: on the authority of Abū Ḥāzim: on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “Among you, who has fasted today?”\nAbū Bakr (RA) said, “I have.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Among you, who has fed a needy person today?”\nAbū Bakr (RA) said, “I have.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Among you, who has followed a funeral today?”\nAbū Bakr (RA) said, “I have.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Among you, who has visited a sick person today?”\nAbū Bakr (RA) said, “I have.”\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) then said, “No man has ever combined these four qualities except that he will enter Paradise.”\n\nAbū Bakr (RA) commented, “This report is of the same type as that which I have clarified in my book al-Īmān. If the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ), ‘Whoever says, “There is no god but Allāh,” enters Paradise,’ were to imply that all faith consists solely in the utterance of these words, then this report would imply that all faith consists in fasting a day, feeding a needy person, attending a funeral, and visiting the sick. However, these are virtues of these actions, not—as those who neither understand nor practice knowledge claim—the very essence of faith itself.”"
"2132","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Salim bin Nuh, who said: Narrated to us Al-Jurayri, on the authority of Abdullah bin Shaqeeq, who said: I asked Aisha (RA): \"Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray the Duha prayer?\" She replied: \"No, except when he returned from a journey.\" Then I asked her: \"Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fast a complete month?\" She said: \"No,"
"2133","Narrated to us al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi and Bahr ibn Nasr, who said: \"Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who narrated to us Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi, that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to him, on the authority of Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman (RA), that he asked Aishah (RA) about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). She replied: 'He (ﷺ) used to fast until we would say: He will never break his fast, and he would break his fast until we would say: He will never fast. And he used to fast the month of Sha'ban, or most of Sha'ban.'\""
"2134","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il, meaning the son of Ja'far. And also narrated to us by Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Khalid, meaning the son of Al-Harith. They both said: Narrated to us by Humaid, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) was asked about the fasting of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: \"He would fast for part of the month until we would think that he would not"
"2135","**\nNarrated to me Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, and he said: And Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah (RA), that she said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would fast until we knew he was fasting, and he would break his fast until we said: 'He is not fasting.' And most of his fasting was in Sha'ban.\""
"2136","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bakr: As for the report of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq Abu Shaybah, then Ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, saying: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Nu'man ibn Sa'd, from 'Ali (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Indeed, in Paradise there are chambers whose outer parts can be seen from their inner parts and their inner parts can be seen from their outer parts.\"\n\nA Bedouin stood up and asked, \"O Messenger of Allah, for whom are they?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) replied, \"They are for the one who speaks good words, feeds others, fasts continuously, and stands in prayer for Allah (SWT) at night while people are asleep.\"\n\n---"
"2137","**\nNarrated to us al-Ḥasan ibn Mahdī: He said, \"Abd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Maʿmar informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Ibn Muʿāniq—or Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī—who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said,\n\n'Verily, in Paradise there is a chamber whose exterior is visible from its interior, and whose interior is visible from its exterior. Allah has prepared it for those who feed others, speak gently, persist in fasting, and pray at night while people are asleep.'\""
"2138","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, from Habib ibn Zayd, from a mawlah (freed slave) called Layla, from his grandmother Umm 'Imarah bint Ka'b (i.e., the grandmother of Habib ibn Zayd), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon her while she was fasting. She presented food to him, so he said, \"Come, eat.\" She replied, \"Indeed, I am fasting.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"When a fasting person has food presented to him, the angels supplicate for forgiveness for him.\""
"2139","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram: 'Isa (meaning Ibn Yunus) informed us, on the authority of Shu'bah, from Habib (or Habib al-Ansari—'Ali was uncertain), who said: I heard our female servant, called Layla, from her grandmother Umm 'Ummarah bint Ka'b, with similar wording. And he added: *\"until they finish or complete their eating.\"*\n\nShu'bah expressed doubt regarding the wording.\n\n'Ali said: Waki' said: Habib..."
"2140","**\nNarrated to us Ali ibn Hujr who said: It was conveyed to us by Shariq, on the authority of Habib ibn Zayd, from Layla, from her mistress, from the Prophet (ﷺ):\n\n> \"When a fasting person has someone eat in his presence the *mafātīr* (flatbreads), the angels supplicate for forgiveness for the fasting person until the evening.\""
"2141","**\nNarrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad and Abu Qilabah ‘Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad; they said: Narrated to us Rawḥ; he said: Narrated to us Shu‘bah, from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from ‘Ā’ishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ‘Ā’ishah, Mother of the Believers (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to like our food. One day he came and said, “Do you have any of that food?”\nI said, “No.”\nHe said, “Then I am fasting.”"
"2142","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \"We have indeed mentioned the reports of the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the fasting of 'Ashura' and his command to fast on that day. As for one who did not intend to fast from the night before, it falls under the chapters of the allowance for fasting 'Ashura'.\""
"2143","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā who said: ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from his maternal aunt ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah, Mother of the Believers (RA), who said:\n\nOne day, the Prophet (ﷺ) entered and asked, “Do you have anything (to eat)?” We said, “No.” He (ﷺ) then said, “In that case, I will fast.”\n\nShe (RA) said: Then another day he (ﷺ) came and we said, “O Messenger of Allah, ḥays has been gifted to us, so we saved some for you.” He (ﷺ) said, “Bring it near, for indeed I have begun fasting.” So he ate.\n\nThis is the narration of Wakīʿ."
"2144","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, from Ja'far bin 'Awn. And narrated to us Yusuf bin Musa, narrated to us Ja'far bin 'Awn Al-'Umari, narrated to us Abu Umays, from 'Awn bin Abi Juhaifah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) paired in brotherhood between Salman and Abu Ad-Darda'. Salman came to visit Abu Ad-Darda', and he found Umm Ad-Darda' in a disheveled state. He"
"2145","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar, who narrated to us Yahya, who narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Numayr ibn Arīb al-‘Absī, from Malik ibn Mas‘ūd (RA), who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The cold booty is fasting in winter.\""
"2146","Narrated to us by Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith, who said: My father narrated to me, who said: Hisham narrated to us, who said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Abu al-Ala', from Ibn Abbas, who said: Righteous men testified before me, among them Umar, and the most beloved of them to me is Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no prayer after the Fajr prayer until the sun rises"
"2147","Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari and Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Quta'i, who both reported: \"Narrated to us Abd al-A'la, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Hakam ibn Hakim ibn Abbad ibn Hunayf, on the authority of Mas'ud ibn al-Hakam, on the authority of his mother, that she narrated to him, saying: 'It is as if I am looking at Ali (RA) riding the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the valley of the Ansar, and he is saying to the people: O people, verily the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"These are not days of fasting; these are days of eating and drinking.\"'"
"2148","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', he said: Narrated to us Abdur-Razzaq, he said: Narrated to us Ma'mar, from Asim bin Sulaiman, from Al-Mutallib, who said: A Bedouin was invited to a meal, and that was after the Day of Nahr (Eid al-Adha). The Bedouin said, \"Indeed, I am fasting.\" He (the host) said, \"I heard Abdullah bin Umar say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ("
"2149","Narrated to me Ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam, who reported that his father and Shuʿayb narrated to them, and they said: \"Narrated to us al-Layth, on the authority of Yazīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Hād, on the authority of Abū Murrah, the freed slave of ʿUqayl, that he and ʿAbd Allāh entered upon ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀs (RA), and that was the day after Eid al-Adha, or two days after Eid al-Adha. ʿAmr brought them food, so ʿAbd Allāh said: 'Indeed I am fasting.' ʿAmr said to him: 'Break your fast, for these are the days which the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) used to command people to break their fast on, and forbid fasting on.' So ʿAbd Allāh broke his fast, ate, and I ate with him.\"\nCommentary: This narration establishes the definitive Islamic ruling that voluntary fasting is prohibited on the three days of Tashriq (the 11th, 12th, and 13th of Dhu al-Hijjah), the days immediately following Eid al-Adha. These days are designated for celebration, gratitude to Allah, and commemoration of Hajj rites, so fasting on them is explicitly forbidden as it contradicts the intended purpose of these days and the established practice of the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"2150","Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, and also by Abu Dawud; they both said: Narrated to us by Shu'bah, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Mutarrif, on the authority of his father, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"He who fasts the entire year has neither fasted nor broken his fast.\""
"2151","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ad-Dawraqi who said: Narrated to us Ibn 'Ulayyah who said: Narrated to us al-Jurayri from Abu al-'Ala' ibn ash-Shikhkhir.\n\nAnd narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr who said: Narrated to us Isma'il — meaning Ibn 'Ulayyah — from Sa'id ibn Iyas al-Jurayri from Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ash-Shikhkhir from Mutarrif from 'Imran ibn Husayn who said:\n\nIt was said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), \"Verily, so-and-so does not break his fast during the day throughout the year.\" He said, \"May he never fast nor break his fast.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: The prohibition regarding prayer is well-known from Qatadah from Abu al-'Aliyah. As for fasting, the narration from Qatadah from Abu al-'Aliyah is rare (gharib)."
"2152","**\nNarrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, they said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, from Abu al-'Abbas, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Has it not been conveyed to me that you stand (in prayer) at night and fast during the day?\"\n\nI said, \"Indeed, I do.\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"Do not do that. For if you do that, your eyes will become fatigued, your soul will become weary, and indeed your soul has a right over you, your family has a right over you, and your eyes have a right over you. So sleep, stand (in prayer), fast, and break your fast.\"\n\nThis is the narration of 'Abd al-Jabbar. Al-Makhzumi did not include the phrase \"Do not do that.\""
"2153","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb al-Hamdani: narrated to us 'Abdah, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami who said:\n\"I used to fast continuously during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I fast continuously without breaking my fast. Should I fast while traveling?' He (ﷺ) said: 'If you wish, fast; and if you wish, break your fast.'\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"I have examined the various chains of this narration in other places.\""
"2154","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Abu Musa, they said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adi, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Abu Tamimah, from al-Ash'ari — meaning Abu Musa — from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"Whoever fasts perpetually, Hellfire will be constricted for him like this,\" and he (the narrator) made a gesture with his hands indicating ninety times.\n\n---"
"2155","**\nNarrated to us Musa and Muhammad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn Bazī‘, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abī ‘Adiyy, from Sa‘īd, from Qatādah, from Abū Tamīmah al-Hujaymī, from Abū Mūsā al-Ash‘arī (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Whoever fasts perpetually, the gates of Hell will be constricted for him to the extent of this” — and he (the Prophet (ﷺ)) formed ninety with his fingers.\n\nIbn Bazī‘ said in the version of the one who fasts perpetually: “And he formed the ninety.”\n\nI heard Abū Mūsā say: “The name of Abū Tamīmah is Ṭarīf ibn Mujālid. He heard it from Maslamah ibn al-Ṣalt al-Shaybānī, from Jahḍam al-Hujaymī.”\n\nAbū Bakr said: “None transmitted this report from Qatādah except Ibn Abī ‘Adiyy from Sa‘īd.”\n\nAbū Bakr said: “I asked al-Muzanī about the meaning of this ḥadīth, so he said: ‘It is likely that its meaning is that Hell is constricted from him, so he does not enter Hell. And it is unlikely that its meaning is other than this, for whoever increases in deeds and obedience to Allāh, Allāh increases for him in rank, honour, and nearness.’ This is the meaning of al-Muzanī’s reply.”"
"2156","**\nNarrated to us Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed me, on the authority of ʿĀmir ibn Jashīb, that he heard Zurʿah ibn Thawb say: I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar about fasting every day, so he said, “We used to count those among us as the foremost (in good deeds).”\n\nHe said: And I asked him about fasting one day and breaking the fast the next day?\nHe said, “He did not leave that for any fasting person to fast.”\n\nHe said: And I asked him about fasting three days of every month?\nHe said, “He fasted that all year and broke his fast.”"
"2157","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala' and Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi (RA), both said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who informed me Yahya ibn Ja'dah, that he heard 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr al-Qari' (RA) saying while he was circumambulating the House (the Ka'bah):\n\n\"By the Lord of the Ka'bah, I did not forbid fasting on the Day of Friday. Indeed, Muhammad (ﷺ) forbade it.\"\n\nSa'id said: \"From Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr al-Qari', and he did not say: 'while he was circumambulating the House.'\""
"2158","**\nNarrated to us Abu Sa‘īd ‘Abdullāh ibn Sa‘īd al-Ashajj (RA), who said: Narrated to us Abu Numayr (RA), on the authority of al-A‘mash (RA), from Abu Salih (RA), from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “Do not fast on Friday alone, unless you fast the day before it or the day after it.”"
"2159","**\nNarrated to us al-Bukhari: From 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath: From his father."
"2160","Muslim narrated to us from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, from Abu Mu'awiyah, from Al-A'mash, on the authority of [the narrator chain continuing to the Prophet (ﷺ)]. This narration serves as a clarification and explanation of the hadith that prohibits fasting on Friday alone. It provides evidence that the prohibition applies specifically when one fasts on Friday in isolation, without fasting the day before or the day after."
"2161","###\n Abdullah bin Hashim narrated to us, saying: Abdur-Rahman narrated to us, on the authority of Mu'awiyah, on the authority of Abu Bishr, on the authority of 'Amir bin Ludayn al-Ash'ari, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Indeed, the day of Friday is a day of Eid (festival). So, do not make your day of Eid a day of fasting, unless you"
"2162","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Adiyy and Abd al-A'la, from Sa'id. And narrated to us Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us Khalid, meaning Ibn al-Harith, who said: Narrated to us Sa'id. And narrated to us Harun bin Ishaq, who said: Narrated to us Abda, from Sa'id, from Qatada, from Sa'id bin al-Mus"
"2163","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Ma'mar al-Qaysi, who said: Narrated to us Abu 'Asim, who said: Narrated to us Thawr bin Yazid, on the authority of Khalid bin Ma'dan, on the authority of Abdullah bin Busr, on the authority of his sister, who was As-Samma', she said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Do not fast on Saturday except for what has been made obligatory upon you. And if one of you finds nothing but a grapevine twig"
"2164","###\n Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban, who narrated to us Abdullah ibn Salih, who narrated to me Mu'awiya (who is the son of Salih), from Abdullah ibn Busr, from his father, from his paternal aunt, Al-Samma' (the sister of Busr), that she used to say: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade fasting on Saturday, and he said: 'If one of you does not find anything except a green twig, let"
"2165","Abu Bakr (RA) said: \"The narration from the Prophet (ﷺ) forbidding the fast of Friday unless one fasts the day before or the day after it is a clear indication that fasting on Saturday is permissible if one fasts on Friday before it or on Sunday after it.\""
"2166","**\nNarrated to us Abdah ibn ‘Abdillah al-Khuzā‘ī, who said: Narrated to us Zayd—meaning ibn al-Hubāb—who said: Narrated to us Mu‘āwiyah, from Abū Bishr, from ‘Āmir al-Ash‘arī—who is Ibn Ludayn—that he heard Abū Hurayrah (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say,\n\n“Friday is a day of celebration (Eid). Do not single out Friday for fasting, unless one fasts the day before it or the day after it.”\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Shaybah) said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) has indeed granted a concession regarding fasting on Saturday if a person fasts Friday and then follows it with fasting on Sunday.\n\n---"
"2167","###\n Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Marwazi narrated to us, saying: Salamah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak informed us, saying: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali narrated to us from his father, that Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas, informed him that Ibn Abbas and some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him to Umm Salamah (RA) to ask her on which days the Messenger of Allah ("
"2168","Narrated to us Abu Ammar al-Husayn ibn Harith, who narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who reported that the following narration reached him from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): \"A woman must not fast a day outside the month of Ramadan when her husband is present except with his permission.\"\nAbu Bakr (RA) then said: \"The words of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), 'outside the month of Ramadan,' fall under the same category as the principle we uphold: if a command is issued for a specific reason, then as long as that reason persists and the command remains applicable, the command stays in force. Since the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) permitted a woman to fast the month of Ramadan without her husband's permission, as fasting Ramadan is an obligation for her, then every fast that is an obligation of the same type is permissible for her to observe without her husband's permission. There is a separate treatise dedicated to this matter, in which I have clarified the commands that are issued for a specific reason, and the prohibitions that are issued for a specific reason.\""
"2169","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', who narrated to us Abu Asim, from Al-Awza'i, from Marthad or Abu Marthad (Abu Asim expressed uncertainty regarding the name), from his father, who said: We met Abu Dharr (RA) while he was at the middle Jamrah (the central of the three stone pillars in Mina, part of the Hajj pilgrimage rituals). I asked him about Laylat al-Qadr (the Night of Decree). He said: No one asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about it more than I did. I said: O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), was Laylat al-Qadr revealed to the Prophets before me by divine revelation to them, then did it cease? He (ﷺ) said: \"Rather, it continues until the Day of Resurrection.\" So I said: O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), which of the nights is it? He (ﷺ) said: \"If I had been granted permission, I would have informed you of it. But seek it in the last seven [nights], and do not ask me about it after this.\" He (the narrator) said: Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) turned to the people and began addressing them, so I said: O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), which of the last seven is it? He (ﷺ) became angry with me with an anger such as He had never been angry with me before nor after, equal to it. Then He (ﷺ) said: \"Did I not forbid you from asking me about it? If I had been granted permission, I would have informed you of it, indeed I would have told you, but I fear it is in the last of those seven [nights].\""
"2170","###\n Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Rahman, meaning Ibn Mahdi, who said: Narrated to us Ikrima bin Ammar, from Simak Al-Hanafi, who said: Malik bin Marthad narrated to me from his father, who said: I asked Abu Dharr (RA). I said, \"You asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about Laylatul-Qadr (the Night of Decree).\" He (Abu Dharr) said"
"2171","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-Sana'ani, who narrated to us al-Mu'tamir bin Sulayman, who narrated to me Amarah bin Ghaziyyah, who said: I heard Muhammad bin Ibrahim narrating, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed i'tikaf (seclusion in the mosque for worship) for the first ten days of Ramadan, then performed i'tikaf for the middle ten days in a Turkish tent, on whose threshold was a piece of straw matting. He (Amarah) said: So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took the matting in his hand, moved it to a side of the tent, then stuck his head out, spoke to the people, and they drew close to him. He (ﷺ) then said: \"Indeed, I performed i'tikaf for the first ten days seeking Laylat al-Qadr (the Night of Decree), then I performed i'tikaf for the middle ten days, then I came out and was told that it is in the last ten nights. So whoever among you wishes to perform i'tikaf, let him do so.\" So the people performed i'tikaf along with him. He (ﷺ) said: \"And I was shown that it is an odd-numbered night, and I will prostrate the next morning in mud and water.\" Then he (ﷺ) came out on the morning of the 21st night, after he had stood (in prayer) until dawn. Rain fell, so the mosque was covered (with water), and I saw mud and water. When he (ﷺ) finished the Fajr (dawn) prayer, he came out, and his forehead and nose were marked with water and mud, and indeed it was the 21st night, which is among the last ten nights. This is a noble (sharif) hadith."
"2172","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us 'Asim ibn Kulayb al-Jarmi, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\n'Umar (RA) used to call me along with the Companions of Muhammad (ﷺ) and would say to me, \"Do not speak until they speak.\" He said: So he called them and asked them about Laylat al-Qadr. He said: \"What do you think about the statement of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): 'Seek it in the last ten nights'? Which night do you think it is?\"\n\nSome of them said, \"The night of one,\" some said, \"The night of three,\" and another said, \"The night of five.\" I remained silent.\n\nHe said: \"Why do you not speak?\"\n\nI said: \"If you permit me, O Leader of the Believers, I will speak.\"\n\nHe said: \"I did not send for you except for you to speak.\"\n\nI said: \"Shall I inform you of my opinion?\"\n\nHe said: \"That is why we ask you.\"\n\nI said: \"The seventh.\"\n\nI saw that Allah, the Exalted, mentioned seven heavens, seven earths, created man from seven, and the vegetation of the earth is seven.\n\nHe said: \"This is something you have informed me of that I already know. But what about something I do not know? What is your statement: 'The vegetation of the earth is seven'?\"\n\nI said: \"Indeed, Allah, the Exalted, says: 'Then We split the earth with openings, and We caused to grow [therein] grain, and grapes, and herbs, and olives, and palm trees, and gardens dense [with trees], and fruit, and grass.' (Surah 'Abasa 80:26-31). The 'abb' (grass) is the vegetation of the earth that the animals eat, which humans do not eat.\"\n\nHe said: 'Umar (RA) said: \"Are you unable to say what this young boy—whose head has not yet fully developed—has said? By Allah, I do not see the statement except as you have said.\"\n\nHe said: \"I had ordered you not to speak until they speak, and now I order you to speak along with them.\"\n\n---"
"2173","###\n Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Idris, on the authority of Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: \"Umar (RA) would ask me questions alongside the senior companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he would say to me, 'Do not speak until they have spoken.' He then asked them about the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr). He said, 'Indeed, you know"
"2174","**\nNarrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Ibn Idrīs, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Malik, on the authority of Sa‘īd ibn Jubayr, on the authority of Ibn ‘Abbās (RA), a similar narration, except that he said:\n*\"The father [i.e., the grandfather in the previous narration] is of what the earth produces that people do not eat, but livestock eats.\"*"
"2175","**\nNarrated to us Mu'ammal ibn Hasham, narrated to us Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah, on the authority of 'Umaynah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of his father, who said: I mentioned the Night of Decree (*Laylat al-Qadr*) in the presence of Abu Bakrah, whereupon he said: I do not seek it except in the last ten nights, after having heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Indeed, I heard him (ﷺ) say: \"Seek it in the last ten nights, on the ninth remaining, or the seventh remaining, or the fifth remaining, or the third remaining, or the last night.\"\n\nHe would not pray during the first twenty nights with the same effort as he did in the rest of the year. But when the last ten nights entered, he would exert himself (in worship)."
"2176","**\nNarrated to us Ishaq ibn Shahin Abu Bishr al-Wasiti (RA), who said: Narrated to us Khalid, on the authority of al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Sa'id (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed i'tikaf during the middle ten nights of Ramadan seeking Laylat al-Qadr before it became clear to him. Then he ordered the structure (of the place of i'tikaf) to be dismantled, and it was. Then it became clear to him that it was in the last ten nights, so he ordered it to be rebuilt. Then he came out to us and said:\n'It has been made clear to me that Laylat al-Qadr is in these nights. I had come out to inform you of it, but two men quarreled, so I forgot it. Seek it in the ninth, seventh, and fifth (nights).'\n\nI (Abu Sa'id) said: 'O Abu Sa'id, you know the numbers better than us. Which are the ninth, seventh, and fifth nights?'\nHe (Abu Sa'id) said: 'Yes, and we are more entitled to that. If it is the night of the twenty-first, then the following night is the ninth. Then leave out a night, then the following night is the seventh. Then leave out a night, then the following night is the fifth.'\nAbu Sa'id, the one you call the twenty-fourth, twenty-sixth, and twenty-second (nights).\""
"2177","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti: Narrated to us Khalid, on the authority of al-Jurayri, from Abu al-'Ala', from Mutarrif, that he heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) say:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said the like of it and added the third time."
"2178","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Umar ibn Yunus, narrated to us 'Ikrimah — and he is the son of 'Ammar — narrated to me Simak Abu Zumayl, narrated to me 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, narrated to me 'Umar (RA), who said:\n\nWhen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) separated himself from his wives, I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, you were in seclusion for twenty-nine nights.\" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Indeed, the month can be twenty-nine days.\""
"2179","Narrated to us Yusuf bin Musa, who narrated to us Jarir, from Al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who reported: We mentioned the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr) in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"How many days of the month have passed?\" We answered, \"Twenty-two days have passed, and eight remain.\" He said, \"No, rather seven remain.\" They said, \"No, rather eight remain.\" He said, \"No, rather seven remain.\" They said, \"No, rather eight remain.\" He said, \"No, rather seven remain; the month is twenty-nine days.\" Then he gestured with his hand, counting up to twenty-nine, and then said, \"Seek it on this night.\"\n\nThe report of Abdullah bin Unais from this chapter states: \"Seek it on this night\", and that refers to the night of the twenty-third. The report of Abu Sa'id (RA) states: I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) on the morning of the twenty-first, and indeed his forehead and the tip of his nose were covered in water and mud of this type. This is because the Prophet (ﷺ) had informed them that he had seen himself prostrating on the morning of the Night of Decree in water and mud. Thus that night (the twenty-first) was an odd-numbered night among the days that had already passed of the month. It is probable that in that year, Ramadan consisted of twenty-nine days, so that night was the ninth remaining night of the month, and the twenty-first among the days that had already elapsed of it."
"2180","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA): Narrated to us Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, on the authority of al-Zuhrī, on the authority of Abū Salamah, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Unays (RA) who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, *\"Seek it during this night,\"* meaning the night of the twenty-third. This was because he had already passed the days of the month, and it was the seventh night from what remained."
"2181","###\n Narrated by Abu Sa'id (RA): I saw the Prophet (ﷺ) on the morning of the twenty-first [of Ramadan], and his forehead and the tip of his nose were covered with water and mud of this kind. This was because the Prophet (ﷺ) had informed them that he had seen in a dream that he would prostrate on the morning of Laylat al-Qadr in water and mud. The twenty-first night was the odd night of the days that had passed of the month. It is likely that Ramadan in that year was twenty"
"2182","###\n Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abda, who narrated to us Al-Warith, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: \"The people used to see dreams (regarding Laylat al-Qadr) and would relate them to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'I see that your dreams have converged upon the last seven nights. So whoever seeks it, let him seek it in the last seven nights.'\"\n\nAbu Bakr (the commentator"
"2183","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from 'Uqbah bin Hurayth, who said: I heard Ibn 'Umar (RA) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Seek it (Laylat al-Qadr) in the last ten nights. If one of you is weak or unable (to worship throughout the last ten nights), then let him not be overcome during the remaining seven nights"
"2184","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, \"The narration of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) I dictated in a place other than this.\""
"2185","**\nNarrated to us Mu'ammal ibn Hisham, narrated to us Isma'il — meaning Ibn 'Ulayyah — on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb, on the authority of his brother — a man from the tribe of 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb — who said:\n\nWe sat with 'Abdullah ibn Unays in a gathering of the tribe of Juhaynah during this month. We said: \"O Abu Yahya, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) speak about this blessed night?\" He replied: \"Yes. We sat with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at the end of this month, and a man asked him: 'When should we seek this blessed night?' He (ﷺ) said: 'Seek it on the twenty-third night,' three times.\"\n\nA man from the people said: \"Then that would be the first of the last eight [nights].\"\n\nAbu Bakr [ibn Abi Shaybah, one of the narrators] said: \"This man whose name Ibn 'Ulayyah did not mention is 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb.\"\n\n---"
"2186","###\n Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, saying: My father and Shu'ayb informed us, saying: Al-Layth informed us, from Zayd ibn Abi Habib, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Mu'adh ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khubayb, from 'Abdullah ibn Unays, the companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He was asked about Lay"
"2187","###\n Abu Musa and Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, both saying: Abdur-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, saying: Jabir ibn Yazid ibn Rifa'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abu Sulayman, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: \"If it were not for the foolish ones among you, I would have placed my hands over my ears and proclaimed: 'Indeed, Laylat al-Qadr is the twenty-seventh night!' This is the report"
"2188","**\nNarrated to us Ishaq ibn Mansur: Narrated to us Al-Nadr: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from 'Abdah — and he is Ibn Abi Lubabah — who said: I heard Zirr ibn Hubaysh say, on the authority of Ubayy (RA), who said:\n*\"Indeed, I know which night is Laylat al-Qadr. It is the night which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered us to seek — it is the night of the twenty-seventh.\"*"
"2189","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Al-Husayn bin Ibrahim bin Al-Hasan, who said: Narrated to us by Ali bin Asim, on the authority of Al-Jurayri, on the authority of Abdullah bin Buraydah, on the authority of Mu'awiyah bin Abi Sufyan (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Seek Laylat al-Qadr in the last night.\" In the narration of Abu Bakrah (RA), it is stated: \"Or in the"
"2190","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Ubaydullah al-Ziyadi and Muhammad ibn Musa al-Harashi (RA), both said: Narrated to us al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, on the authority of Abu al-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"I was shown the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr) but then I was made to forget it. It is in the last ten nights, specifically on an odd night. It is a night that is neither hot nor cold. The moon on that night is bright and clear, as if it has a light that illuminates the stars.\"\n\nThe narration of al-Ziyadi includes the additional phrase: \"As if in it there is a moon that exposes its stars.\"\n\nBoth narrators also said: \"No devil comes out on that night until the dawn breaks.\""
"2191","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala, who narrated to us Sufyan [ibn Uyaynah], on the authority of Adah ibn Abi Lubabah and Asim, on the authority of Zir, who said: I said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA): O Abu al-Mundhir.\n\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Adah ibn Abi Lubabah that he heard Zir say: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA), and said: Indeed your brother Ibn Mas'ud (RA) says: \"Whoever stands in prayer for the entire year will encounter Laylat al-Qadr.\" [Ubayy (RA)] replied: May Allah have mercy on him, he certainly intended that people would not grow complacent, and he certainly knew that it falls in the month of Ramadan, that it is in the last ten nights, and that it is the 27th night. We said: O Abu al-Mundhir, by what sign is this known? He said: By the sign, or the verse that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) informed us of: \"That the sun will rise on that day without rays.\"\n\n[Note: In the additional similar narrations transmitted by al-Dawraqi, he omitted the phrase \"he certainly intended that they would not grow complacent.\"] Al-Dawraqi further narrated at the end of his report: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Asim, from Zir, a similar narration. And al-Dawraqi narrated to us, Sufyan, from Abu Khalid, from Zir, a similar narration."
"2192","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, narrated to me Abū ʿĀmir, narrated to me Zamʿa, from Salamah (he is Ibn Wahbān), from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr):\n\n*\"It is a night that is neither hot nor cold. When the sun rises on its day, it is red and weak.\"*"
"2193","Ahmad bin Abdah narrated to us; Hammad bin Zayd informed us; on the authority of Asim bin Abi al-Najud; on the authority of Zirr bin Hubaysh (RA), who said: \"I said to Ubayy bin Ka'b (RA), 'Inform me about the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr), for indeed our companion, meaning Ibn Mas'ud (RA), was asked about it, and he said: 'Whoever stands in prayer throughout the entire year will encounter it.'\"\n[Ubayy bin Ka'b (RA)] said: \"May Allah have mercy on Abu Abd al-Rahman (i.e., Ibn Mas'ud). Indeed, he knew that it falls within Ramadan, but he disliked that people would become complacent [by relying on the knowledge that it occurs in Ramadan without striving to worship and seek it], or he preferred that they not become complacent. By Allah, it is in Ramadan, on the 27th night, without exception.\"\n[Zirr (RA)] said: \"I said, 'O Abu al-Mundhir (the kunya of Ubayy bin Ka'b), how did you come to know this?' He replied: 'By the sign that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) informed us of.' I said to him, 'What is that sign?' He said: 'The sun rises on the morning of that night without rays, like a shallow dish, until it has risen high above the horizon.'\""
"2194","**\nNarrated to us ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, from Abū Dāwūd, who narrated to us ʿImrān al-Qaṭṭān, from Qatādah, from Abū Maymūnah, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “The Night of Decree is the night of the twenty-seventh or twenty-ninth [of Ramaḍān], and indeed the angels on that night are more numerous on earth than the pebbles.”\n\n---"
"2195","Narrated to us ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, who narrated to us ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd al-Ḥanafī, who narrated to us Farqad ibn al-Ḥajjāj, who said: I heard ʿUqbah ibn Abī al-Ḥasanah al-Yamānī, who said: I heard Abū Hurairah (RA), who reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever prays the ʿIshāʾ prayer in congregation during Ramadan has attained the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr).\""
"2196","Abu Bakr said, regarding the narration of Abu Salamah from Abu Sa'id: \"Indeed, I was shown the Night of Qadr, but then I was made to forget it.\""
"2197","**\nNarrated to me Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, saying: Yunus informed me, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"I was shown the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr), but then I was awakened by my family, so I forgot it. Thus, seek it in the last ten nights.\""
"2198","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Hujr, narrated to us Isma‘il ibn Ja‘far, narrated to us Humaid, on the authority of Anas who said: ‘Ubādah ibn al-Sāmit informed me that the Prophet (ﷺ) went out intending to inform the people about the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr). Then two men from among the Muslims exchanged heated words. So he (ﷺ) said, “I went out to inform you about the Night of Decree, but so-and-so and so-and-so exchanged heated words, so it was raised. It may be that this is better for you. So seek it on the twenty-ninth, the twenty-seventh, and the twenty-fifth.”\n\nAbū Bakr (the sub-narrator) said: “It was raised,” meaning: my knowledge of that night was taken away."
"2199","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I memorized it from al-Zuhri.\n\nAnd narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi and 'Amr ibn 'Ali, they both said: narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) in a narration, he said:\n\n\"The one who fasts Ramadan with faith and seeking reward, his past sins will be forgiven.\""
"2200","**\nNarrated to us Mu'ammal ibn Hisham al-Yashkuri: Narrated to us Isma'il, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, on the authority of Ibn 'Abdullah ibn Unays, on the authority of his father, who said:\n\"I said: 'O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I reside in the countryside and, by the praise of Allah, I pray there. Instruct me regarding a night in which I should come to this mosque to pray in it.'\nHe (ﷺ) said: 'Come down on the night of the twenty-third.'\n\nHe (the narrator) said: I asked Ibn 'Abdullah: 'How did your father act?'\nHe said: 'He would enter after the prayer of 'Asr, and would not leave until he had prayed the prayer of Fajr. Then he would leave, and his mount—meaning at the door of the mosque—would be brought to him, and he would go to his family.'\""
"2201","Ahmad bin Al-Miqdam Al-Ijli narrated to us, Nuh bin Qays Al-Khuza'i narrated to us, Nasr bin Ali narrated to us, from An-Nadr bin Shaiban, from Abu Salamah bin Abdur-Rahman, who said: I said to Abu Salamah, \"Will you not narrate to us a hadith that you heard from your father, which your father heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?\" He replied, \"Yes. When Ramadan approached, the Messenger of Allah ("
"2202","Narrated to us ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, who narrated to us ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar, who narrated to us Mālik ibn Anas, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Salamah (RA): That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to enjoin the observance of the night prayer (qiyām) during Ramadan, without ever commanding it as a binding, obligatory duty. He (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever stands in prayer during Ramadan with faith and seeking reward from Allah, all his previous sins will be forgiven for him.\"\nCommentary: This narration clarifies that the congregational night prayer of Ramadan (Taraweeh) is a recommended (mustahabb) act of worship, not a mandatory (fard) obligation. The Prophet (ﷺ) encouraged the Muslim community to perform it to attain divine reward, but did not legislate it as a binding requirement, as evidenced by his never commanding it with a definitive obligatory decree.\n---"
"2203","It was narrated to us by 'Amr bin 'Ali, who said: It was narrated to us by 'Abdur-Rahman bin Mahdi, who said: It was narrated to us by Malik bin Anas, from Az-Zuhri, from Humaid bin 'Abdur-Rahman, from Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever stands (in prayer) during Ramadan out of faith and in the hope of reward, his previous sins will be forgiven.\""
"2204","**\nNarrated to us Abdah ibn ‘Abdillah al-Khuzā‘ī, who said: Narrated to us Zayd ibn al-Hubāb, from Mu‘āwiyah, who said:\nNu‘aym ibn Ziyād Abū Ṭalḥah al-Anmārī narrated to me, who said:\nI heard al-Nu‘mān ibn Bashīr (RA) on the pulpit of Ḥimṣ saying:\n“We stood in prayer with Allāh’s Messenger (ﷺ) in Ramaḍān on the night of the twenty-third until a third of the night had passed, then we stood in prayer with him on the night of the twenty-fifth until half the night had passed, then we stood in prayer with him on the night of the twenty-seventh until we thought we would not attain al-falāḥ.”\nAnd we used to call it al-saḥūr (the pre-dawn meal).\nBut you (people) say, “The night of the twenty-seventh,” whereas we say, “The seventh of the twenty-seven.”\nSo who is correct: us or you?"
"2205","**\nNarrated to us Abdah ibn Abdullah, narrated to us Zayd, narrated to us Mu'awiyah, narrated to me Abu az-Zahiriyyah, on the authority of Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, on the authority of Abu Dharr (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood to lead us in prayer during Ramadan on the night of the twenty-third until a third of the first part of the night had passed, then he said, *\"I do not think that what you seek is except behind you.\"*\nThen he stood on the night of the twenty-fifth until half of the night had passed, then he said, *\"I do not think that what you seek is except behind you.\"*\nThen we stood on the night of the twenty-seventh until dawn.\n\nAbu Bakr (a narrator in the chain) said:\nThis wording *\"except behind you\"* is, in my view, from the category of opposites, and he intended *\"before you\"*, because what has passed is behind a person, and what he is about to face is before him. The Prophet (ﷺ) only meant: *\"I do not think that what you seek\"*—meaning the Night of Decree—*\"is except in what you are about to face, not in what has passed of the month.\"*\nThis is like the saying of Allah, the Exalted:\n*\"And behind them was a king who seized every ship by force.\"*\n(Surat al-Kahf, 18:79)\nHe intended: *\"And before them was a king.\"*"
"2206","###\n Narrated to us Abu Qudamah Ubaydullah bin Sa'id, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Fudayl, on the authority of Dawud bin Abi Hind, on the authority of Al-Walid bin Abdur-Rahman, on the authority of Jubayr bin Nufayr Al-Hadhrami, on the authority of Abu Dharr (RA), who said: \"We fasted with the Prophet (ﷺ) during Ramadan, but he did not lead us in prayer (Qiyam"
"2207","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Umar, narrated to us Yunus, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of 'Urwah, on the authority of 'A'ishah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque. Some men prayed with his prayer. The next morning, some people began talking about it. When the third night came, more people gathered in the mosque, so he went out and prayed, and they prayed with him. When the fourth night came, the mosque could not accommodate the people, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not come out to them. Some of them began calling out: \"Prayer!\" but he did not come out. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stayed hidden until he came out for the dawn prayer. When he had finished the dawn prayer, he turned to face them, sat down, praised Allah, and extolled Him, then said:\n\n\"As for what follows: Indeed, your affair did not remain hidden from me, but I feared that the night prayer would be made obligatory for you, and you would be unable to do it.\"\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to encourage them to perform the night prayers in Ramadan without commanding it as an obligation, saying:\n\n\"Whoever prays during the night of Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven.\"\n\nWhen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, this practice continued during the caliphate of Abu Bakr (RA) and the early part of 'Umar's (RA) caliphate. Then 'Umar (RA) gathered them behind Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA) and led them in prayer. This was the first time people gathered to perform the night prayers in Ramadan."
"2208","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Rabi' bin Sulayman Al-Muradi, who said: Narrated to us by Abdullah bin Wahb, who said: He informed us by Muslim bin Khalid, from Al-Ala' bin Abdur-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went out, and he saw people in Ramadan praying in a corner of the mosque. He asked, \"What are these people?\" It was said to him, \"These are people"
"2209","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) narrated on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA) who said: It had reached the Prophet (ﷺ) that Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA) was leading a group of people in prayer while none of them had memorized the Qur'an. So the Prophet (ﷺ) approved of their action and said, \"They have done well, or what an excellent thing they have done.\""
"2210","###\n It was narrated to us on the authority of Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Abu Dharr (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever prays with the Imam until he concludes (the prayer), it is recorded for him as if he stood in prayer for the entire night.\" And in another narration, it is mentioned: \"He led us in prayer during the third (night of Ramadan). He gathered his family and his wives, and he stood in prayer until we feared that we would miss"
"2211","**\nNarrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Tamīmī (RA) who said: It was conveyed to us from Mālik (RA), from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī (RA), from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān (RA), from Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Whoever stands in prayer with the Imām until he departs, it shall be recorded for him the standing of a full night.”\n\nThis indicates that the reciter and the unlettered person—if they stand with the Imām until the completion of the prayer—it shall be recorded for them the standing of a full night. And the recording of the standing of an entire night is better than the recording of standing for only part of the night.\n\n---"
"2212","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Sa'id at-Tustari, who informed us that Al-Hakam bin Nafi' reported from Shu'ayb, meaning the son of Abu Hamzah, from Abdullah bin Abu Husain, who said: Isa bin Talhah narrated to me from Amr bin Murrah al-Juhani, who said: A man from the tribe of Quda'ah came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah! What do you say if I testify that there is"
"2213","Narrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub, who narrated to us Sufyan, from Ibn Abi Labid. And narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala, who narrated to us Sufyan, who narrated to us Abdullah ibn Abi Labid, who heard Abu Salamah, who said: I asked Aisha (RA), so I said, \"O my mother, please inform me about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\" She responded, \"The night prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the month of Ramadan and at other times consisted of thirteen rak'ahs.\"\nThis is the hadith reported by Abd al-Jabbar. And Abu Hashim said: I went to Aisha (RA) and asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the month of Ramadan, so she said, \"His prayer was thirteen rak'ahs, of which two were the rak'ahs of the Fajr (dawn) prayer.\"\n---"
"2214","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri and Muhammad ibn al-Walid, who said: \"Narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Abu Ya'fur al-Abdi, on the authority of Muslim ibn Subayh, on the authority of Masruq, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he entered the last ten nights of the month of Ramadan, would gird his loins, spend the night in worship, and wake up his family.'\" And Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Zuhri said: \"We heard Aisha (RA) state this narration directly.\""
"2215","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Ma'bad, who said: Narrated to us by Mu'alla bin Mansur, who said: Narrated to us by Abd al-Wahid, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Hasan bin Ubaydullah, who said: Narrated to us by Ibrahim, on the authority of Al-Aswad, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) would exert himself in worship during the last ten nights of Ramadan in a manner that he did"
"2216","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who narrated to me Sulayman (who is Ibn Bilal), who narrated to me 'Amr (who is Ibn Abi 'Amr), from Al-Muttalib ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered Ramadan, he would tighten his waist-wrapper ("
"2217","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Waleed, who said: Narrated to us Ya'la bin Ubayd, who said: Narrated to us Yahya bin Sa'id, on the authority of 'Amrah, from 'Aisha (RA), who said: \"When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) intended to perform I'tikaf, he would pray the Fajr prayer, then enter the place where he intended to perform I'tikaf. If he intended to perform I'tikaf during the last ten nights of Ramadan,"
"2218","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (RA) said: \"In the report transmitted by Umara bin Ghaziyyah, there is the narration of Abu Sa'id (RA): The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) observed iʿtikāf in a Turkish tent, and I have transmitted this hadith in another chapter [of this collection].\""
"2219","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd al-A'la al-Sana'ani, who narrated to us al-Mu'tamir, who narrated to me Umara ibn Ghaziyya al-Ansari (RA). He said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrating, on the authority of Abu Salamah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who reported: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) observed i'tikaf (seclusion in the mosque for worship) during the first ten days of Ramadan, then he observed i'tikaf during the middle ten days in a Turkish tent, on the entrance of which a piece of matting was placed.\" [Umara ibn Ghaziyya added:] He (Muhammad ibn Ibrahim) mentioned the full, detailed narration of this hadith, and I have already dictated it previously."
"2220","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bishr; he narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id and Abd al-Wahhab (who is Ibn Abd al-Thaqafi), both of whom said: \"Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who reported: 'We observed iʿtikāf (religious seclusion in the mosque) with the Prophet (ﷺ) during the middle ten days of Ramaḍān. When the morning of the 20th [of Ramaḍān] arrived and we returned [from our iʿtikāf], he slept, and he was shown the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr), but then it was erased from his memory. When the afternoon came, he sat upon the minbar and delivered a sermon to the people.' He (the Prophet ﷺ) then mentioned this ruling and said: 'Whoever was observing iʿtikāf with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) should return to his place of iʿtikāf.'\"\n---"
"2221","**\nNarrated to us Abu al-Fadl Fadalah ibn al-Fadl: Narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, on the authority of Abu Husayn, on the authority of Abu Salih, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform i'tikaf every Ramadan for the last ten nights. But in the year in which he passed away, he performed i'tikaf for twenty days.\""
"2222","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabee' ibn Sulaymaan, narrated to us Ibn Wahb, narrated to me Hanzalah ibn Abi Sufyaan, that he heard Saalim ibn 'Abdullaah ibn 'Umar say: I heard my father say: The companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) had passed the middle seven (nights) of Ramadhaan, so the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever among you intends (to observe i'tikaaf), then let him intend it in the last seven (nights).\""
"2223","Muhammad bin Al-Husayn bin Tasnim narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Bakr Al-Bursani narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, saying: Az-Zuhri informed me, mentioning the narration of Urwah and Ibn Al-Musayyib. Urwah narrated from Aisha (RA), and Sa'id narrated from Abu Hurairah (RA): \"The Prophet (ﷺ) used to observe I'tikaf (spiritual retreat) during the last ten nights of Ramadan until"
"2224","Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who narrated to us Abdullah ibn Wahb, who informed me that 'Amr ibn Al-Harith said: \"Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me from 'Amrah, who said: 'Aisha (RA) narrated to me that the Prophet (ﷺ) intended to observe I'tikaf (seclusion in the mosque for worship). Aisha (RA) asked his permission to observe I'tikaf with him. When Zaynab ("
"2225","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Warith ibn 'Abd al-Samad al-'Anbari, who said: Narrated to us my father, who said: Narrated to us Hammad, on the authority of Thabit, on the authority of Abu Rafi', on the authority of Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to observe i'tikaf during the last ten nights of Ramadan. However, one year he did not observe it. So in the following year, he observed i'tikaf for twenty nights."
"2226","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adiyy, he said: Anba'a to us Humaid, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) used to perform i'tikaf during the last ten nights of Ramadan. Once he traveled in a year, so he did not perform i'tikaf. In the following year, he performed i'tikaf for twenty nights.\""
"2227","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adiyy, he said: It was conveyed to us by Humaid, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) would perform i'tikaf during the last ten nights of Ramadan. One year, he did not perform it, so in the following year, he performed i'tikaf for twenty nights.\""
"2228","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: Narrated to us Hammad—meaning Ibn Zayd—who said: Narrated to us Ayyub, from Nafi', who said:\nIt was mentioned in the presence of Ibn 'Umar (RA) that the 'Umrah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was from al-Ji'ranah. So he said, \"He did not perform 'Umrah from there.\"\nHe said: And 'Umar (RA) had vowed in the pre-Islamic period to perform i'tikaf (seclusion) for one night. So he asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about it, and he (ﷺ) commanded him to fulfill it. So he entered the mosque that night and mentioned the hadith.\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: I have already explained in the Book of Jihad the time of the Prophet's (ﷺ) return to Makkah after the conquest of Hunayn. And indeed, this i'tikaf of 'Umar (RA) was on that night after the return of the Prophet (ﷺ) and his giving him from the spoils of Hunayn."
"2229","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, on the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), that 'Umar (RA) had made a vow in the pre-Islamic period to perform i'tikaf for one night. So he asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about it, and he commanded him to fulfill his vow.\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) had given him a slave girl from the spoils of Hunayn. While 'Umar (RA) was performing i'tikaf in the mosque, people entered, saying \"Allahu Akbar.\" He asked, \"What is this?\" They replied, \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has sent the spoils of Hunayn.\" So he sent out that slave girl.\n\nSome narrators in the narration of Nafi' said, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), on the authority of 'Umar (RA): \"I vowed to perform i'tikaf for one day.\" If this wording is confirmed, then it is of the same type as I know that the Arabs may say \"a day\" intending its night, and \"a night\" intending its day. The evidence for this is established in the Book of Allah, the Exalted and Majestic."
"2230","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed him, that Yunus informed him, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Urwah bin Az-Zubayr and Amrah, that Aisha (RA) said: \"When the Prophet (ﷺ) was in I'tikaf (seclusion) in the mosque and entered his house for a need, he would not inquire about the sick except while passing by.\" Aisha (RA) added: \"The Messenger of Allah ("
"2231","**\nNarrated to me Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that Ibn Wahb informed them, saying: Yunus, Malik, and al-Layth informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah and 'Amrah, with a similar hadith to that of Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, identical except that he said: *\"to me, his head.\"*"
"2232","Narrated to us Abu Musa, who narrated to me Muhammad ibn Ja'far, on whose authority Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Aishah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ): [It is reported that] the Prophet (ﷺ) was observing i'tikaf (seclusion for worship) in the mosque. Aishah (RA), who was in a state of menstruation (haidh) at that time, would come to him, and he would extend his head out of the i'tikaf area for her to comb his hair.\nCommentary: This hadith clarifies the rulings related to i'tikaf and interaction with a menstruating woman. It demonstrates that a menstruating woman is permitted to sit outside the mosque and engage in permissible physical contact with a person observing i'tikaf, as the Prophet (ﷺ) himself allowed his wife Aishah (RA), who was menstruating, to comb his hair while he was in i'tikaf, indicating no prohibition in such interaction."
"2233","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Abd ar-Razzaq, who said: He informed us Ma'mar, from az-Zuhri, from Ali bin Husayn, from Safiyyah bint Huyayy (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was observing I'tikaf (seclusion in the mosque for worship). I came to visit him at night, and I spoke with him. Then I stood up to leave and return. He (the Prophet ﷺ)"
"2234","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, Abu al-Yaman narrated to us, Shu'ayb informed us from az-Zuhri, who informed me from Abu al-Husayn, that Safiyyah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed him: \"She came to visit the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was observing I'tikaf in the mosque during the last ten nights of Ramadan. She conversed with him for a while, then stood up to return. The Prophet (ﷺ) also stood"
"2235","Narrated to us Al-Fadl bin Abi Talib, who narrated to us Al-Mu'alla bin Abdur-Rahman Al-Wasiti, who narrated to us Abdur-Hamid bin Ja'far, from Ubaidullah bin Abi Ja'far, from Abu Ma'mar, from Aisha (RA), who said: \"I would keep the company of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the night while he was in I'tikaf.\" In another narration, she said: \"I would stay awake keeping"
"2236","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Nu'aym bin Hammad, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Aziz, meaning Ibn Muhammad, from Isa bin Umar bin Musa, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"When he (ﷺ) performed I'tikaf, his bed was laid down, or his cot was placed behind the Pillar of Repentance.\" Abu Bakr (the narrator/compiler) said: The Pillar of Rep"
"2237","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Nasr, narrated to us Malik ibn Su'ayr, narrated to us Ibn Abi Layla, on the authority of Sadaqah (who is Ibn Yasar), on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:\n\"A hut was built for the Prophet (ﷺ) from palm fronds during Ramadan, in which he would perform i'tikaf. When it was one night, he brought out his head and heard them reciting. So he said:\n'Verily, when the one praying prays, he is whispering to his Lord, so let one of you know what he is whispering to his Lord. Some of you are raising your voices over others.'\"\nHe intended to rebuke them for raising their voices over one another."
"2238","###\n Narrated to us by Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Jurayj, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA); and also narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"We performed I'tikaf (spiritual retreat) with the Messenger of Allah"
"2239","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) asked the Prophet (ﷺ), saying: \"Indeed, I made a vow in the pre-Islamic period to observe I'tikaf for one night.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Fulfill your vow.\""
"2240","In the narration of Aisha (RA): Aisha sought permission from the Prophet (ﷺ) to observe I'tikaf with him, so he granted her permission. Then she sought permission on behalf of Hafsa (RA) [to do the same]. I have dictated this hadith in its entirety."
"2241","###\n Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sabbah narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Shafi'i, who said: \"As for one who makes a vow to perform I'tikaf (spiritual retreat) while standing, without speaking to anyone, without eating, and without lying down on a bed, with the intention of drawing near to Allah without it being an oath (Yamin), then he may sit, speak, eat, and lie down without any expiation (Kaffarah). One must only fulfill a vow in that which"
"2242","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) narrated in a report from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) saw Abu Isra'il standing in the sun. He asked, \"What is he doing standing in the sun?\" They replied, \"He has vowed to fast, not to sit, and not to seek shade.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Order him to sit, seek shade, and fast.\"\n\nThus, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded him to fulfill the fast, which is an act of obedience, and to abandon standing in the sun, as standing in the sun is not an act of obedience. Even though standing in the sun is not a sin in itself, if it involves self-torment, then it becomes a sin.\n\nI have already discussed this type in detail in the chapter on vows in my book *Kitab al-Nudhur*."
"2243","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Abdullah bin Wahb reported, on the authority of Malik, from Yazid bin Abdullah bin al-Had, from Muhammad bin Ibrahim bin al-Harith, from Abu Salamah bin Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to observe I'tikaf (spiritual retreat) during the middle ten days of Ramadan. One year, he observed I'tikaf until the night of"
"2244","It was narrated to us by Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: It was narrated to us by Ibn 'Uyaynah, who said: It was narrated to us by Abu Hayyan. And it was narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Musa, who said: It was narrated to us by Jarir, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi. And it was narrated to us by Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi, who said: It was narr"
"2245","###\n It was narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abdah, who said: Hammad (meaning Ibn Zayd) informed us, from Abu Jamrah, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: I heard him say: The delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). They said: \"O Messenger of Allah, this is the tribe of Rabi'ah, and the disbelievers of Mudar have come between us and you, so we cannot reach you except during the Sacred Month. So command us with something"
"2246","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who said: 'Abbad — meaning ibn 'Abbad al-Muhallabi — informed us; he said: Abu Jamrah al-Duba'i informed us, on the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\nThe delegation of 'Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said something similar [to the following], and he (ﷺ) said:\n\"Faith is in Allah.\"\nThen he (ﷺ) explained it to them, saying:\n\"[True] faith consists of testifying that there is no god worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\"\nThen he (ﷺ) mentioned the hadith at length.\n\n---"
"2247","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar and Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, who both said: Narrated to us by Amr bin 'Asim Al-Kilabi, who narrated from Imran (who is Ibn Dahir, Abu Al-Awwam Al-Qattan), who narrated from Ma'mar bin Rashid, from Az-Zuhri, from Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away, the Arabs apostatized. Then 'Umar bin Al-K"
"2248","Muhammad bin Aban narrated to us, from Abu Nu'aim, who narrated that Abu al-Anbas Sa'id bin Kathir said: My father narrated to me, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but Allah, establish the prayer, and pay the Zakat. Then, their blood and wealth become forbidden to me, and their reckoning is with Allah.\""
"2249","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Musa, Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna; he said: Narrated to us by Mu'adh bin Hisham; he said: My father narrated to me, from Yahya bin Abi Kathir; he said: 'Amir Al-'Uqaili narrated to me that his father informed him that he heard Abu Huraira (RA) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The first group to be shown to me entering Paradise and the first group to be shown to me entering the"
"2250","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Sahl Ar-Ramli, who narrated from Yahya bin Isa, who narrated from Al-A'mash, who narrated from Abdullah bin Murrah, who narrated from Masruq, who said: Abdullah (bin Mas'ud) (RA) said: \"The one who consumes riba (usury), the one who pays it, the two witnesses to it (if they are aware of it), the woman who tattoos and the one who gets herself tattooed, the one who withholds charity (La"
"2251","Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn al-Shahid and Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Taghlabi narrated to us, both saying: We were told by Waki'. Ishaq reported: Al-A'mash narrated to us, and Ja'far reported: On the authority of al-A'mash, from Ma'ruf ibn Suwayd, from Abu Dharr (RA). Ishaq said: [Abu Dharr (RA)] reported: I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was sitting in the shade of the Kaaba. When he saw me, he said: \"They are the greatest losers, by the Lord of the Kaaba!\" [Abu Dharr (RA)] said: So I sat down, and I had not even fully settled before I stood up [to inquire], and said: Who are they, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you? He (ﷺ) said: \"They are the most numerous, except for those who give charity from their wealth like this\" – [the Prophet (ﷺ) gestured with his hands] four times. They are very few. And there is no owner of camels, cattle, or sheep who fails to discharge their zakat, except that on the Day of Judgment, [their livestock] will come to him in the largest and fattest state they ever were, goring him with their horns and trampling him with their hooves. Every time their rear passes the spot where he is, their front returns to him, until judgment is rendered between all people.\nThis is the full narration as reported by Ishaq. Ja'far reported from Abu Dharr (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no owner of camels...\" and he mentioned from that point to the end the same content as the above narration, but he did not include the preceding part of the hadith.\n---"
"2252","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdah, who said: ‘Abd al-‘Azīz ibn Muḥammad al-Darāwardī informed us, who said: Suhayl narrated to us, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“No servant who does not pay the zakāh on his wealth will be spared: he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his wealth, and heated iron plates will be heated in the Fire of Jahannam. These will be branded upon his sides, forehead, and back until Allāh judges between His servants on a Day whose duration is a thousand years as you count them. Then he will see his path—either to Paradise or to the Fire.”\n\n“No servant who does not pay the zakāh on his camels will be spared: he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his camels in their fullest, most robust state. He will be laid flat upon a level, barren plain, and the camels will trample him. Whenever the last of them passes, the first returns to trample him again, until Allāh judges between His servants on a Day whose duration is a thousand years as you count them. Then he will see his path—either to Paradise or to the Fire.”\n\n“No servant who does not pay the zakāh on his sheep will be spared: he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with his sheep in their fullest, most robust state. He will be laid flat upon a level, barren plain, and the sheep will trample him with their hooves and butt him with their horns—there will be no crooked-horned or hornless sheep among them—until Allāh judges between His servants on a Day whose duration is a thousand years as you count them. Then he will see his path—either to Paradise or to the Fire.”\n\nThey asked, “O Messenger of Allāh, what about horses?”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection. Horses are for three kinds of people: for one man it is reward, for another it is a shield, and upon another it is a burden.”\n\nThen he (ﷺ) mentioned the hadith at length.\n\nThey asked, “And what about donkeys, O Messenger of Allāh?”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Allāh has not revealed to me anything regarding them except this comprehensive, singular verse:\n‘So whoever does an atom’s weight of good will see it,\nand whoever does an atom’s weight of evil will see it.’\n[Sūrah al-Zalzalah (99):7–8]”\n\n**Glossary:**\n- **‘Aqṣā’** (عَقْصَاء): A sheep with a broken horn.\n- **Jalḥā’** ("
"2253","Narrated to us Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Yazid ibn Zuray', narrated to us Rawḥ ibn al-Qāsim, narrated to us Suhayl ibn Abi Ṣāliḥ, with this chain of narration, and he mentioned the hadith in its lengthy form. He said in each of them: *\"On a Day whose measure is fifty thousand years of what you count.\"* And he also said: *\"Then he will be dragged across it.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2254","Narrated to us Ar-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān and ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm. Ar-Rabīʿ said: Narrated to us Shuʿayb, who said: Narrated to us Al-Layth. And ʿĪsā said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, on the authority of Al-Layth ibn Saʿd, on the authority of Ibn ʿAjlān, on the authority of Al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn Ḥakīm, on the authority of Abū Ṣāliḥ, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"One of you will have his treasure on the Day of Resurrection as a venomous snake with a bald head and two black spots, following its owner. The owner will seek refuge from it, but it will continue to pursue him until it devours his finger.\"\n\nAr-Rabīʿ did not mention the phrase *\"while he flees from it.\"*\n\nHe also said: *\"Your treasure...\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2255","Narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu‘ādh: narrated to us Yazīd ibn Zuray‘: narrated to us Sa‘īd ibn Abī ‘Arūbah: from Qatādah: from Sālim ibn Abī al-Ja‘d al-Ghaṭafānī: from Ma‘dān ibn Abī Ṭalḥah: from Thawbān (RA) who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “Whoever leaves behind a treasure, it will be made into a bald serpent on the Day of Resurrection. It will have two black spots and will chase him. It will say, ‘Woe to you! Who are you?’ He will say, ‘I am your treasure that you left behind after you.’ Then it will keep chasing him until it seizes him and bites his hand, then it will devour the rest of his body.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2256","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Abbar ibn Al-Ala', he said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, on the authority of Jamil, on the authority of Abu Wa'il, on the authority of Abdullah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"There is no man who does not pay his Zakah, except that on the Day of Resurrection, a serpent will be made for him, which will be placed around his neck.\" Then the Prophet (ﷺ) recited to us its confirmation from the"
"2257","###\n Ahmad bin Sinan al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us. [In another chain]: Al-Hasan bin Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Yahya bin Abbad narrated to us. [In a third chain]: Nasr bin Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Asad (meaning Asad bin Musa) narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-'Aziz bin al-Majishun narrated to us, from 'Abdullah bin Dinar, from"
"2258","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi: On the authority of Ibn Wahb, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n*\"When you have paid the zakah on your wealth, you have removed its evil from you.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2259","Narrated to us Abu'l-Ash'ath, narrated to us Mu'tamir, and narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us Isma'il.\n\nAnd narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, informed us Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid.\n\nAnd narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim and Yahya ibn Hakim, they both said: narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Habib—he is Ibn Nudbah—narrated to us Isma'il.\n\nAnd narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Muhammad az-Za'faraniyy, narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd, narrated to us Isma'il, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:\n\n\"I pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to establish the prayer, to give the zakah, and to give sincere advice to every Muslim.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2260","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin 'Isa, who said: Narrated to us Salamah (meaning Ibn al-Fadl), who said: Muhammad bin Ishaq (who is Ibn Yasar, the freed slave of Makhrama) narrated to me, and also narrated to me Muhammad bin Muslim bin 'Ubaidullah bin Shihab az-Zuhri, from Abu Bakr bin 'Abdur-Rahman bin al-Harith bin Hisham al-Makhzumi, from Umm Salamah bint Abi Umayyah"
"2261","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar (Bundar), Muhammad bin Yahya, Abu Musa Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, and Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abdullah Al-Ansari, who said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated to me that when Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq (RA) was appointed as the Caliph, he wrote a letter to him when he sent him to Bahrain. This is the letter he wrote"
"2262","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr as-Sa'di, who narrated from Ayoub bin Jabir, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Asim bin Damrah, on the authority of Ali (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakat due on camels fewer than five. When they reach fifteen, three sheep are due, up to twenty.\" He then mentioned the rest of the hadith in full. Then, regarding when the number increases,"
"2263","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan.\nAnd narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, who informed us Muhammad ibn Dinar.\nAnd narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammam ibn Zayd, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id and 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar.\n\nAnd narrated to us Abu Musa, from 'Abd al-Rahman—he is Ibn Mahdi—narrated to us Sufyan, Malik, and Shu'bah, all of them from 'Amr ibn Yahya, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"There is no charity due on less than five camels, nor on less than five wasqs.\"*\n\nThe meanings of their hadiths are the same. This is the hadith of Muhammad ibn Bashshar. And in the hadith of 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA): *\"There is nothing due on less than forty sheep.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2264","Abu Bakr narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Ma'arur bin Suwayd, who said: Abu Dharr (RA) narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"There is no owner of camels, cattle, or sheep who does not pay the Zakat due on them, but I have already granted him respite until the Day of Judgment.\""
"2265","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘lā al-San‘ānī, narrated to us al-Mu‘tamir who said: I heard this narration from Hishām ibn Hassān, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Sīrīn, on the authority of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (RA) who said:\n\n\"The charity (zakāh) for sheep is due when they are grazing (in pastures) if they reach forty up to one hundred and twenty sheep.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2266","###\n It was narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Yahya narrated to us, who said: Bahz narrated to us, who said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather. In another narration, it was narrated to us by Al-Hasan bin Muhammad bin Al-Sabbah, who said: Yazid bin Harun narrated to us, who informed us that Bahz bin Hakim narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ("
"2267","Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub who said: Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Sadaqah who said: Narrated to us Sufyan—he is the son of Husayn—on the authority of Az-Zuhri, on the authority of Salim, on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ) wrote regarding the charity (of camels and sheep), but he did not dispatch it to his workers until he passed away. He mentioned the hadith at length, and regarding sheep, he said: \"For every forty sheep that graze, one sheep is due, up to one hundred and twenty.\"\n\nHe then mentioned the rest of the hadith.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2268","Narrated to us Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, who said: Narrated to us Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Mu'adh.\nAnd it was also narrated to us by Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Rahman bin Maghra', who said: Narrated to us Al-A'mash, from Shaqiq bin Salamah and Ibrahim, from Masruq, from Mu'adh bin Jabal.\nAnd it was also narr"
"2269","Narrated to us by Abdur-Rahman bin Bishr bin Al-Hakam, who said: Narrated to us by Abdur-Razzaq, who said: We were informed by Ma'mar, from Abdullah bin Abu Bakr bin Muhammad bin Amr bin Hazm, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (ﷺ) wrote him a letter containing the following: \"In the case of cattle (Baqar), for thirty cows, there is a Tabi' (a one-year-old male calf), and for forty"
"2270","###\n Narrated to us Ali ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Jarr at al-Fustat, from my father. And also narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tamim al-Misri, from Amr ibn Khalid, from Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah and another man whom he named, from Ali ibn Abi Talib (RA). Zuhayr said: \"I heard it from the Prophet (ﷺ"
"2271","Narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, that Khalid ibn Yazid narrated to him, that Abu az-Zubayr narrated to him, that he heard Jabir ibn ‘Abdullah (RA) say:\n\n\"There is no zakah due on the one who stirs the earth (i.e., turns over the soil for cultivation).\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2272","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Umar bin Tamim al-Misri, who said: Narrated to us Yahya bin Bakir, who said: Narrated to me Al-Layth, who said: Narrated to me Hisham bin Sa'd, from Abbas bin Abdullah bin Mabad bin Abbas, from Asim bin Umar bin Qatadah al-Ansari, from Qays bin Sa'd bin Ubadah al-Ansari, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed me as a collector (of Zakat). My"
"2273","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, Abu Musa, Muhammad bin Yahya, and Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abdullah, who said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated to me that when Abu Bakr (RA) was appointed as the Caliph, he wrote a letter to him when he dispatched him to Bahrain. The letter stated: \"In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory"
"2274","Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, he said: Narrated to us by Abu 'Asim, he said: Narrated to us by Sufyan. In another narration, it is reported that Abu Musa narrated to us, saying: The Duhhak bin Makhlad narrated to me from Sufyan, from 'Asim bin Kulayb, from his father, from Wa'il bin Hujr, that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent someone to a man, and that man sent him a young camel that was weak"
"2275","Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, Abdullah bin Ishaq al-Jawhari, and this is the narration of Bundar, who said: Narrated to us by Abu 'Asim, who narrated from Zakariya bin Ishaq, who narrated to me from Yahya bin Abdullah bin Sayfi, who narrated to me from Abu Ma'bad, the freed slave of Abdullah bin Abbas, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Mu'adh bin Jabal to Yemen and"
"2276","Abu Bakr said: In the narration of Wail ibn Hujr, he sent a she-camel from among his finest camels, and he said: \"O Allah, bless him and his camels.\""
"2277","Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm narrated to me, from Yaḥy"
"2278","###\n Ibn Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, on the authority of Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Umayrah ibn Amr ibn Hazm said: \"Time passed and affected those it affected until the governorship of Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan came about. He appointed Marwan ibn al-Hakam as the governor of Medina. Marwan then sent me as a collector of Zakat over the tribes of Bani Bali', Bani U"
"2279","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, Abu Musa, Yusuf bin Musa, and Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abdullah, who said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated to me that when Abu Bakr Al-Siddiq (RA) was appointed as Caliph, he wrote the following decree: \"In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory charity (Zakat) that the Messenger"
"2280","###\n Narrated to us by Abu al-Khattab, Ziyad bin Yahya al-Hassani, who said: Narrated to us by Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Ishaq, from Amr bin Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) in the Year of the Conquest (of Makkah) saying: \"O people! Any alliance (Hilf) that existed in the pre-Islamic period of"
"2281","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, Abu Musa, Muhammad bin Yahya, and Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abdullah, who said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated to me that Abu Bakr (RA) wrote to him: \"In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory Zakat which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has prescribed upon the Muslims, which Allah has"
"2282","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar (Bundar), who said: Narrated to me Al-Ala' bin Al-Fadl bin Abd al-Malik bin Abi Sawiyyah, who said: Narrated to me Ubaydullah bin Ikkrash, from his father Ikkrash bin Dhuyayb, who said: The Banu Murrah bin Ubayd sent me with the Zakat (charity) of their wealth to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). When I arrived in Madinah, I found"
"2283","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, narrated to us by Yahya, Muhammad bin Ja'far, and Abdur-Rahman bin Mahdi, they said: Narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Hisham bin Yazid, who said: I heard Anas bin Malik (RA) say: When my mother gave birth, I took the newborn infant to the Prophet (ﷺ) so that he could perform the Tahneek (chewing a date and rubbing it on the infant's palate). I found the Prophet ("
"2284","Narrated to us Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi, narrated to us Abu Usamah, on the authority of Sufyan al-Thawri, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of 'Asim (who is the son of Damrah), on the authority of 'Ali (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n*\"I have excused you from the zakah on horses and slaves. So pay the zakah on wealth: for every forty dirhams, [pay] one dirham.\"*\n\nHe (Sufyan) said: And 'Ali (RA) said: *\"For every forty dinars, [pay] one dinar, and for every twenty dinars, half a dinar.\"*\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2285","** Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Ayyub ibn Musa—initially from Makhul, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from 'Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who elevated it (marfu'):\n\n*\"There is no zakah due from a Muslim on his slave or his horse.\"*"
"2286","Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, who said: Narrated to us Sulayman ibn Yasar, on the authority of 'Irak ibn Malik, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (RA) raising the hadith to the Prophet (ﷺ) say,\n\n\"There is no charity due on the Muslim's horse or slave.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2287","Then, the last of them narrated to us, and then the last of them narrated to us: Yazid bin Yazid bin Jabir said, \"I heard Irak bin Malik say, 'I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) say,' though Yazid did not raise the chain to the Prophet (ﷺ): 'There is no Zakat (charity) due upon a Muslim regarding his horse or his slave.'\""
"2288","###\n Muhammad bin Suhail bin As'kar narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, saying: Nafi' bin Yazid informed us, saying: Ja'far bin Rabi'ah narrated to me, on the authority of 'Irak bin Malik, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakat (charity) due from a Muslim upon his slave, nor upon his horse, except for the Zakat of"
"2289","Ahmad bin Abdur-Rahman bin Wahb narrated to us, saying: My uncle narrated to me; Makrama narrated to me, from his father, from Irbak bin Malik, who said: I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) narrating from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"There is no Zakat (charity) due on a slave, except for the Zakat of Fitr (Sadaqat al-Fitr).\""
"2290","Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib (RA), who said:\nA group of people from the people of ash-Sham came to Umar (RA) and said, \"We have acquired wealth: horses and slaves, and we would like there to be zakah and purification for us in them.\"\nSo he said, \"My two predecessors did not do it, so I will not do it.\"\nThen he consulted the companions of Muhammad (ﷺ), among them Ali (RA), and Ali said, \"It is good if it is not a jizyah that is taken from them as a fixed stipend.\"\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, \"The Sunnah of the Prophet (ﷺ) is that there is no zakah on four camels except that their owner wishes, and his statement regarding sheep: If a man's flock is deficient by one sheep from forty, then there is no zakah on it except that their owner wishes. And for silver, it is a quarter of a tenth. If it is not but ninety and one hundred, then there is no zakah on it except that their owner wishes.\"\nThis is an indication that if the owner of wealth gives zakah from his wealth, even if the zakah is not obligatory on his wealth, it is permissible for the Imam to take it if the giver's soul is pleased with it.\nAnd likewise, al-Faruq (Umar, RA) when he informed the people that the Prophet (ﷺ) and as-Siddiq before him did not take zakah on horses and slaves, their souls were pleased to give voluntary zakah from the horses and slaves, so it was permissible for al-Faruq to take the zakah from them, just as the Mustafā (ﷺ) permitted taking zakah from what is less than five camels, less than forty sheep, and less than two hundred dirhams of silver.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2291","**\nNarrated to us Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani, narrated to us Yazid ibn Zurai', narrated to us Ibn al-Qasim, narrated to us Suhayl ibn Abu Salih, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\n**\"No servant possesses wealth for which he does not pay the zakah, but on the Day of Resurrection his wealth will be made into sheets of fire, which will be heated in the Fire of Hell and branded with them on his sides and his back, until Allah has passed judgment between His servants on a Day whose length is fifty thousand years of what you count. Then he will see his path, whether it leads to Paradise or to Hell.\"**\n\nHe (RA) then mentioned the hadith at length in the story of camels and sheep.\n\nIt was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what about horses?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n\n**\"The goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection. Horses are for three people: for one man it is a reward, for another it is a shield, and upon another it is a burden.**\n\n**As for the one for whom it is a reward, it is the man who acquires it for the sake of Allah and prepares it for that purpose, spending nothing from its grazing but that a reward is recorded for him. If he lets it graze in a meadow or two, and his owner grazes it there, a reward is recorded for him for what it conceals in its bellies. If it walks up a hill or two, a reward is recorded for him for every step it takes. If he lets it drink from a river, a reward is recorded for him for every drop that is hidden in its belly from that river, until he even mentioned a reward for its dung and urine.**\n\n**As for the one for whom it is a shield, it is the man who acquires it for the sake of dignity, adornment, and protection, without withholding the right of its backs and bellies in ease or hardship.**\n\n**As for the one upon whom it is a burden, it is the man who acquires it for the sake of pride, extravagance, and showing off.\"**\n\nThey said: \"What about donkeys, O Messenger of Allah?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n\n**\"Allah has not sent down to me anything regarding them except this comprehensive and unique verse:**\n\n**'So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.' (Surah al-Zalzalah 99:7-8)\"**"
"2292","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Husayn ibn Al-Hasan, who informed us by Yazid ibn Zura'ah (Abu Mu'awiyah), who narrated from Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Amr ibn Budayn, who said: I heard Abu Dharr (RA) saying: A flock of sheep from the zakah animals gathered near the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He (ﷺ) said: \"Go out with them, O Abu Dharr.\" So I"
"2293","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Abdullah, on the authority of Amr ibn Yahya, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"There is no charity due on less than five *awaq* of silver.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2294","###\n Narrated to us by Imran bin Musa al-Qazzaz, who said: Narrated to us by Hammad (meaning Ibn Zayd), who said: Narrated to us by Yahya bin Sa'id, from 'Amr bin Yahya, from his father, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakat due on less than five Uqiyahs of silver, nor on less than five camels, nor on less than five Awsuq"
"2295","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, he said: Narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, that 'Amr ibn Yahya ibn 'Umarah ibn Abi Husayn al-Mazini informed him, on the authority of his father, that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) say:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"There is no charity due on less than five wasqs, nor on less than five awqiyah. And an awqiyah is two hundred dirhams.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2296","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, Abu Musa, Muhammad bin Yahya, and Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abdullah, who said: My father narrated to me, from Thumamah, who said: Anas bin Malik (RA) narrated to me that when Abu Bakr As-Siddiq (RA) was appointed as Caliph, he wrote the following: \"In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory charity (Zakat) that the Messenger of"
"2297","Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'di, narrated to us Ayyub ibn Jabir, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Give one-fourth of the tithe (one dirham from every forty dirhams). There is nothing due on less than two hundred dirhams. When it reaches two hundred dirhams, then five dirhams are due on it, and for anything more than that, the same ratio applies.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2298","Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī, who said:\nNarrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said:\nAnd ‘Iyāḍ ibn ‘Abd Allāh al-Fihrī informed me,\non the authority of Abū’l-Zubayr,\non the authority of Jābir ibn ‘Abd Allāh,\non the authority of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).\n\nHe also said:\nAnd narrated to me ‘Abd Allāh ibn ‘Umar,\nYaḥyā ibn ‘Abd Allāh ibn Sālim,\n Mālik ibn Anas,\nand Sufyān al-Thawrī,\non the authority of ‘Amr ibn Yaḥyā al-Māzinī,\non the authority of his father,\non the authority of Abū Sa‘īd al-Khudrī,\non the authority of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ),\nmeaning by it the like of the hadith of Abū Sa‘īd:\n“There is no charity due on silver less than five awāq.”\n\nThe hadith is complete.\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2299","###\n Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me that 'Iyad ibn Abdullah narrated to him from Abu az-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). Yunus said: \"He meant that there is no Zakat due on less than five Uqiyahs of silver (Waraq), no Zakat due on less than five female camels (Dhawd), and no Zakat due"
"2300","Abu Bakr said: \"The narration of Abu Sa'id states that there is no Zakat on anything less than five Awsuq.\""
"2301","Narrated to us Abu al-Khattab Ziyad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Yazid ibn Zuray', narrated to us Rawḥ ibn al-Qāsim, narrated to us ʿAmr ibn Yaḥya ibn ʿImārah, on the authority of his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (RA), from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said:\n\n> \"No zakāh is due on wheat or dates until they reach five awsuq. No zakāh is due on silver until it reaches five awāq. No zakāh is due on camels until they reach five.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2302","Narrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī and Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām, who said: Narrated to us Bashsh (i.e., Ibn al-Mufaḍḍal), who said: Narrated to us ʿUmārah ibn Ghaziyyah, on the authority of Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmārah, who said: I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) say:\n\n*\"There is no charity due on less than five wasqs, nor on less than five awāq, nor on less than five dhawd (camels).\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2303","###\n Narrated to us by Isa ibn Ibrahim, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, who said: Malik informed me that Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sa'sa'ah told him that his father informed him that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakah due on less than five Uqiyyahs (of silver), and there is no Zakah due on less than five camels (from the herd"
"2304","Narrated to us by Bashr bin Adam, who narrated to us from Mansur bin Zayd al-Mawsili, who narrated to us from Muhammad bin Muslim (meaning al-Ta'ifi), who narrated from 'Amr bin Dinar, who narrated from Jabir bin 'Abdullah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakat due upon a Muslim man regarding his vineyard or his crops if they amount to less than five Awsuq.\""
"2305","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us ‘Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Muhammad ibn Ishaq.\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad, who narrated to us al-Haytham ibn Jamil, who informed us Muhammad ibn Muslim.\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad, who narrated to us Dawud ibn ‘Amr ibn Zuhayr, who narrated to us Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta’ifi.\n\nAlso narrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi, who narrated to us Sa‘id ibn Abi Maryam, who informed us Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta’ifi.\n\nThey all related the hadith in the same manner as the hadith of Mansur ibn Zayd, except that Dawud ibn ‘Amr said: “From Jabir, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, Abu Bakr said: ‘This report was not heard by ‘Amr ibn Dinar from Jabir.’”"
"2306","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who narrated to us Abd ar-Razzaq, who narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me that Amr bin Dinar said: \"I heard him [Amr bin Dinar] narrating from Jabir bin Abdullah, from more than one person, from Jabir bin Abdullah, who said: 'There is no Zakat due on less than five Awsuq of grain, and there is no Zakat due on less than five Awsuq of dates.'\" Abu Bakr [the compiler"
"2307","###\n I heard Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb saying: \"I found in my book, written in my own handwriting, with my notation and my hearing, from my uncle, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: 'For that which is watered by the sky (rain), the tithe (one-tenth) is due; and for that which is watered by the water-lifting device (such as a well or"
"2308","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Sa'id bin Abi Maryam, who informed us: Ibn Wahb, on the authority of Yunus bin Yazid, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of Salim bin Abdullah, on the authority of his father (Abdullah bin Umar), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"As for that which is watered by the rain, springs, or is 'Athari' (rain-fed), the tithe (one-t"
"2309","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la with an unusual report. He said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me 'Amr ibn al-Harith, who said: Abu'z-Zubayr narrated to me.\n\nAlso, narrated to us 'Isa ibn Ibrahim: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: 'Amr said: And Abu'z-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"For what is watered by rivers and clouds, a tenth is due; and for what is watered by the bucket, half of a tenth.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr said: Yunus once told us that Abu'z-Zubayr had narrated to him, and 'Isa did not say: *\"and clouds.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2310","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa’id al-Ashaj, narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Ubayd al-Tanafisi who said: I heard Idris al-Awdiyy mention, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak al-Mukhrimi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Ubayd, from Idris al-Awdiyy, from ‘Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu’l-Bakhtari, from Abu Sa’id, who elevated it to the Prophet (ﷺ):\n\n“There is no zakah on less than five wasqs.”\n\nAnd a wasq is sixty sealed measures (sawa’).\n\nAbu Bakr [ibn Abi Shaybah] said: He means by “sealed measures” a sa’ (a known measure of volume), and there is no difference among the scholars that a wasq is sixty sa’s. I have already explained the measure of a sa’ in my book on Oaths and Vows in the chapter on the expiation of an oath.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2311","###\n Muhammad bin Isa narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak narrated to us, on the authority of Muhammad bin Hafsa, on the authority of Az-Zuhri, on the authority of Abu Umama bin Sahl bin Hunayf (RA), who said: \"There were people who would give in charity the worst of their fruits. Then Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, revealed the verse: 'And do not aim at the bad of it when you spend, while you would not take it except with closed eyes' (Sur"
"2312","Yunus bin Abd al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Abdullah bin Wahb informed us, saying: Abd al-Jalil bin Humayd al-Yahsubi narrated to me, saying: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, saying: Abu Umamah bin Sahl bin Hunayf narrated to me regarding this verse in which Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, said: \"And do not aim at the bad of it when you spend\" (Surah Al-Baqarah, 2:67). He said:"
"2313","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Sa'id ibn Sulayman, narrated to us 'Abbad—meaning Abu al-'Awwam—on the authority of Sufyan ibn Husayn, on the authority of al-Zuhri, on the authority of Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, on the authority of his father (Sahl ibn Hunayf), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded [the people] to give charity. A man from this palm grove came with some *kabāyis*. Sufyan said: *He means fresh dates.*\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever brings this?\"\n\nNo one would bring anything except that it would be attributed to the one who brought it.\n\nThen this verse was revealed:\n**\"And do not aim at the bad of it to spend.\"** (Qur'an 2:267)\n\nHe (ﷺ) also forbade the *ju'rūr* and the *lubn al-hubayq* from being taken as charity.\n\nAl-Zuhri said: *Two types of fruit from the fruits of Madinah.*\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2314","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Affan ibn Muslim, narrated to us Wuhayb, narrated to us Amr ibn Yahya, on the authority of al-Abbas ibn Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, on the authority of Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi (RA), who said:\n\"We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the year of Tabuk until we reached Wadi al-Qura. There was a woman in her garden, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to his companions: 'Estimate (the yield).' The people estimated, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) estimated ten wasqs. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said to the woman: 'Count what comes out of it until I return to you, if Allah wills.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then proceeded to Tabuk, and then returned, and we returned with him until we reached Wadi al-Qura. He said to the woman: 'How much has your garden yielded?' She said: 'Ten wasqs,' which was the Messenger of Allah's (ﷺ) estimate.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2315","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Razzaq, who said: He informed us Ibn Jurayj, who said: On the authority of Ibn Shihab, who said: On the authority of Urwah, who said: On the authority of Aisha (RA), who said, while she was mentioning the affair of Khaybar: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would send Ibn Rawahah to estimate the dates when the first fruits ripened, before they were eaten. Then he would"
"2316","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who said: Narrated to us Al-Shafi'i, who said: Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Nafi', from Muhammad ibn Salih At-Tammam, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn Al-Musayyib, from Attab ibn Usayd, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said regarding the Zakah of vineyards: \"Estimate the grapes just as dates are estimated, then pay its Zakah in the form"
"2317","Abu Bakr said: This narration was transmitted by 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq. He informed me that Az-Zuhri narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded 'Attab ibn Usayd to estimate the yield of grapes just as one estimates the yield of palm trees, and then its Zakah should be paid in the form of raisins, just as the Zakah of dates is paid in the form of dried dates. Abu Bakr added: This"
"2318","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abdullah ibn az-Zubayr al-Humaydi, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Raja’, on the authority of ‘Abbad ibn Ishaq. And narrated to us Muhammad, narrated to us al-‘Aziz ibn as-Sari, narrated to us Bishr ibn Mansur, on the authority of ‘Abd ar-Rahman ibn Ishaq, on the authority of az-Zuhri, on the authority of Sa‘id ibn al-Musayyib, on the authority of ‘Attab ibn Usayd, with this report without his statement: “This is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding date-palms and grapes.”\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: “‘Abbad” is his *laqab* (surname), and his name is Abd ar-Rahman.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2319","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya and Muhammad, on the authority of Shu'bah, who said: I heard Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrate from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mas'ud ibn Dinari, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah, who said: \"A man came to us while we were in the market, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, 'When you estimate (the yield of date palms), take (the zakat) and leave a third. If you do not take or leave a third'—Shu'bah was uncertain about the third—'then leave a quarter.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2320","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us; Wahb bin Jarir narrated to us; from Shu'bah; from Khubayb bin Abdur-Rahman; from Abdur-Rahman bin Mas'ud bin Niyar; from Sahl bin Abi Hathmah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When you estimate (the yield of the date palms), leave one-third (of the estimated amount) aside. If you do not leave one-third, then leave one-quarter.\""
"2321","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Ali ibn Manjuf, narrated to us Ruwh, narrated to us 'Awf, from Khilas, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"There is no owner of camels who does not pay their due from their milk and their ease, but he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with them in their fullest form. He will be made to lie down on a flat, barren plain, and they will kick him with their hooves and trample him with their feet. Whenever the last of them finishes, the first of them will return until the creatures are judged. Then he will see his path.\n\nAnd there is no owner of sheep who does not pay their due from their milk and their ease, but he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with them in their fullest and greatest form. He will be made to lie down on a flat, barren plain, and they will gore him with their horns and trample him with their hooves. Whenever the last of them finishes, the first of them will return until the creatures are judged. Then he will see his path.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \"I do not know if it is raised (to the Prophet ﷺ) or not.\""
"2322","**\nNarrated to us 'Ubaidah ibn 'Abdullah al-Khuza'i, who said: Narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Abu 'Umar al-Ghudani, that a man from Banu 'Amir passed by him. It was said, \"This man is among the wealthiest of people.\" Abu Hurayrah called him and asked him about that. He said, \"Yes, I have a hundred donkeys or a hundred leather containers, and I have such-and-such number of sheep.\"\n\nAbu Hurayrah said, \"Beware of overburdening camels and beware of exhausting sheep. Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n'There is no man who possesses camels and does not pay their due in their ease and hardship, their difficulty and their ease, but that they will be brought forth on the Day of Resurrection as a barren flat plain. They will come to him in their most emaciated and severe state—Shu'bah was uncertain whether he said \"most fat\" or \"most enormous\"—and they will trample him with their hooves. Every time their last one passes him, their first one will return to him on a Day whose duration is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the people, and he will see his path.'\n\nAnd there is no slave who possesses sheep and does not pay their due in their ease and hardship, their difficulty and their ease—he said this twice—except that they will be brought forth on the Day of Resurrection as a barren flat plain in their most emaciated and severe state—Shu'bah was uncertain whether he said \"most fat\" or \"most enormous\"—and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns. Every time their last one passes him, their first one will return to him on a Day whose duration is fifty thousand years, until Allah judges between the people, and he will see his path.'\n\nAnd there is no man who possesses cattle and does not pay their due in their ease and hardship, their difficulty and their ease—he said this twice—except that they will be brought forth on the Day of Resurrection as a barren flat plain in their most emaciated and severe state—Shu'bah was uncertain whether he said \"most fat\" or \"most enormous\"—and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns. Every time their last one passes him, their first one will return to him on a Day whose duration is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the people, and he will see his path.'\n\nThe man from Banu 'Amir asked, 'What is the due of camels, O Abu Hurayrah?'\n\nHe said, 'You give the noble one (i.e., the best camel) as charity, you grant the powerful one (i.e., the strongest camel) as a gift, you make the weak one (i.e., the one unfit for work) lean (i.e., leave it to graze), you let the stud camel mate (i.e., do not overwork it), and you provide water for the milk camels.'\n\nAbu Bakr said: No one narrated this hadith other than Yazid ibn Harun from Shu'bah.\"\n\n---"
"2323","###\n It was narrated to us by Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: It was narrated to us by Nu'aim bin Hammad, who said: It was narrated to us by Abdul Aziz (who is Ibn Muhammad Ad-Darawardi), from Rabi'ah (who is Ibn Abu Abdur-Rahman), from Al-Harith bin Bilal, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took the Zakah from the mines of the tribe. And he granted the entire valley of 'Aqiq to Bil"
"2324","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abdah, from al-Mughirah—he is Ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith. And narrated to us Murrah, who said: Narrated to us Mughirah ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman, who said: My father ‘Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, from ‘Amr ibn Shu‘ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Banu Shababah, a branch of the tribe of Fahm, used to pay the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from their honey a tithe—one skin out of every ten skins. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to reserve for them two valleys.\n\nWhen ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) became caliph, he appointed Sufyan ibn ‘Abd Allah al-Thaqafi over them. They refused to pay anything to him, saying, “This was something we used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).”\n\nSo Sufyan wrote to ‘Umar about this. ‘Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) wrote back to them:\n\n> “Honey is but a gift of rain that Allah drives as provision to whomsoever He wills. If they pay you what they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then reserve their two valleys for them. Otherwise, leave the people and the valleys between them.”\n\nSo they paid to him what they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he reserved their two valleys for them."
"2325","**\nNarrated to us Ar-Rabee', narrated to us Ibn Wahb, narrated to me Usaamah, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that Bani Shabaabah—a branch of Fuhm—mentioned something similar to the hadith of Al-Mugheerah ibn 'Abd ar-Rahmaan, to the same effect.\n\nAbu Bakr (i.e., Al-Bukhaaree) said: \"If this report is established, then it contains evidence that Bani Shabaabah used to pay only one-tenth of their honey as a voluntary contribution, not because the tithe was obligatory upon them for honey. Rather, they did so voluntarily as a means of protecting their two valleys.\n\nDo you not hear their argument with Sufyaan ibn 'Abdillaah and the letter of 'Umar ibn al-Khattaab to Sufyaan? For if they paid what they used to give to the Messenger of Allaah (ﷺ)—so that he would protect their two valleys for them—otherwise, he would leave the two valleys open to the people.\n\nIt is inconceivable that an owner of wealth would refrain from paying the obligatory charity on his wealth if his grazing land were not protected for him. It is not permissible for the leader to reserve a grazing land for some of the livestock owners so that they may pay the charity on their wealth, unless he also protects that land for them.\n\nAl-Faarooq (i.e., 'Umar ibn al-Khattaab, may Allaah have mercy on him) knew that this report—that Bani Shabaabah used to pay one-tenth of their honey to the Prophet (ﷺ) and that the Prophet (ﷺ) used to protect their two valleys for them—was established. So he ordered his agent, Sufyaan ibn 'Abdillaah, to protect their two valleys for them if they paid from their honey the same amount they used to give to the Prophet (ﷺ). Otherwise, he would leave the two valleys open to the people.\n\nIf 'Umar (may Allaah have mercy on him) had considered that the Prophet (ﷺ) took the one-tenth from their honey in the sense of obligation, as the obligation of charity on wealth upon which zakaah is due, he would not have accepted their refusal to pay the zakaah. Rather, he would have fought them if they refused to pay what was obligatory upon them of charity, for Abu Bakr (may Allaah have mercy on him) had followed the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) in fighting those who refused to pay the charity, as evidenced by Abu Bakr's oath that he would fight whoever refused to pay even a single 'iqaal (a small amount of wealth) that they used to give to the Prophet (ﷺ). And 'Umar (may Allaah have mercy on him) agreed with him on fighting them.\n\nThus, if the Prophet's (ﷺ) taking of the one-tenth from the honey of Bani Shabaabah at the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattaab had been in the sense of obligation, then the ruling in their case would have been the same as the ruling against those who refused to pay the charity to Abu Bakr at the time of the Prophet's (ﷺ) death.\"\n\nAnd Allaah knows best."
"2326","###\n Narrated to us 'Amr bin 'Ali, who said: Narrated to us Abu 'Asim, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me: Ibn Shihab told me, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurairah (RA). And also from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id bin Al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurairah (RA).\n\nAnd narrated to us 'Abdullah bin Ishaq Al-Jawhari, who said: Narr"
"2327","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed us that 'Amr bin al-Harith and Hisham bin Sa'd narrated to me from 'Amr bin Shu'ayb, from his father, from 'Abdullah bin 'Amr bin al-'As (RA), that a man from the tribe of Muzainah came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and asked: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is your ruling regarding a dead"
"2328","Narrated to us Yunus ibn Musa who said: Jarir narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr who said: I heard a man from Muzaynah asking the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about the obligation of one-fifth (khums) in what is found in ruined pre-Islamic dwellings.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2329","###\n Ahmad bin Hafiz bin Abdullah narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me, who narrated to me Ibrahim, from Musa bin Uqba, from Abu al-Zinad, from Abd al-Rahman bin Hurmuz al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered a charity (Sadaqah). Some of those who were critical said: 'Abu Jamil, Khalid bin al-Walid, and al-Abbas bin Abd al"
"2330","###\n Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Al-Sabbah, who narrated to us Shababah, who narrated to us Warqa', from Abu Al-Zinad, from Al-A'raj, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Umar ibn Al-Khattab as a collector of Zakat.\"\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Ali ibn Ayyash Al-Himsi, who narrated"
"2331","Muhammad ibn Yahya and Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mughirah al-Misri narrated to us, both saying: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, who said: Isma'il ibn Zakariya al-Asadi narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Dinar. (Note: Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman did not include the phrase \"before it becomes due\" [qabla an tahilla] in his narration)."
"2332","**\nNarrated to us 'Ali ibn al-Azhar ibn 'Abd Rabbih ibn al-Jarud ibn Mirdas ibn Hurmuzān, the freed slave of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA): Narrated to us Maslamah ibn Khalid, on the authority of Zayd ibn Aslam, on the authority of 'Ata ibn Yasar, on the authority of Abu Rafi', the freed slave of the Prophet (ﷺ):\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) borrowed a young camel from a man, saying, \"When the charity (Sadaqah) comes, we will repay you.\" When the charity arrived, the Prophet (ﷺ) said to Abu Rafi' (RA), \"Give the man his young camel.\"\n\nAbu Rafi' (RA) looked and found nothing but a camel older than the one borrowed or a younger one. He informed the Prophet (ﷺ) of this, so he (ﷺ) said, \"Give it to him, for the best of people are those who repay their debts most perfectly.\""
"2333","###\n Ali bin Al-Mundhir narrated to us, stating: Ibn Fudail narrated to us, stating: Muhammad bin Ishaq narrated to us. In another narration, Muhammad bin Yahya Al-Azdi narrated to us, stating: Yazid bin Harun informed us, stating: Muhammad bin Ishaq informed us, from Yazid bin Abi Habib, from Abdur-Rahman bin Shimasah, from Uqbah bin Amir Al-Juhani (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ("
"2334","Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us; Ahmad bin Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us; Muhammad bin Ishaq narrated to us, from Asim bin Umar bin Qatadah, from Mahmud bin Labid, from Rafi' bin Khadij (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The collector of Zakat who acts with justice is like one who fights in the cause of Allah until he returns to his home.\""
"2335","###\n Narrated to us by Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, on the authority of Umar ibn al-Harith and Layth ibn Sa'd, who said: Narrated to us by Yazid ibn Abi Habib, on the authority of Sinan ibn Sa'd al-Kindi, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"There is no faith for one who has no trustworthiness (amanah),"
"2336","###\n Narrated to us by Zakariya ibn Yahya ibn Aban al-Misri, who said: Narrated to us by Amr ibn Khalid and Ali ibn Ma'bad, both of whom said: Narrated to us by Abdullah ibn Amr al-Jazari, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaisah, from Al-Qasim ibn Awf al-Bakri, from Ali ibn Husayn, who said: Umm Salamah (RA) narrated to us, saying: The Messenger"
"2337","Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: On the authority of Ibn Jurayj: From a man from the family of Abū Rāfi‘: He informed him: From al-Faḍl ibn ‘Ubayd Allāh: From Abū Rāfi‘ who said:\nWhen the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) prayed the ‘Aṣr prayer, he would go to the Banū ‘Abd al-Ashhal and converse with them until the time of Maghrib approached.\n\nAbū Rāfi‘ said:\nWhile the Prophet (ﷺ) was hurrying to Maghrib, we passed by al-Baqī‘. He (ﷺ) said, “Uff! Uff!” to something. This greatly troubled me, so I stepped back, thinking that he was addressing me. He (ﷺ) then said, “What is the matter with you? Walk on.” I said, “Have you committed an act?” He (ﷺ) asked, “What is the matter with you?” I said, “You said ‘Uff’ to me.” He (ﷺ) replied, “No, rather this person—indeed I had sent him as a collector over so-and-so tribe, but he embezzled a striped garment, so he has been clothed in a similar one from the Fire.”\n\nAbū Bakr said:\n“Embezzlement” refers to taking from the spoils of war in the manner of theft.\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2338","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, narrated to us Isma'il, narrated to us Qays, on the authority of Qays ibn 'Umayrah al-Kindi who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever among you works for us and conceals from us even a needle or anything larger than that, it will be a theft that he will bring on the Day of Resurrection.\"\n\nA man from the Ansar, who was black (as if I am looking at him), stood up and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, take my work from me.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) asked, \"Why?\"\n\nHe replied, \"I heard you say such and such.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"I say this: Whoever we appoint for a task, let him come with whatever he has, whether little or much. Whatever is given to him, let him take it, and whatever is withheld from him, let him refrain.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2339","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us al-Zuhri, who informed me 'Urwah, on the authority of Abu Humaid al-Sa'idi that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed a man from the Azd tribe, called Ibn al-Lutbiyah, to collect the sadaqah (charity). When he returned, he said, \"This is for you, and this has been given to me as a gift.\" Thereupon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up, delivered a khutbah (sermon), praised Allah and extolled Him, then said:\n\n\"What is the matter with a worker whom we send out, and he comes and says, 'This is for you, and this has been given to me as a gift'? Why doesn't he sit in his father's and mother's house to see whether he will be given gifts or not? By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, none of you will bring anything [unlawfully taken] but that it will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with it branded on his neck—if it is a camel, it will bray; if it is a cow, it will low; if it is a goat, it will bleat.\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) then raised his hands until we could see the whiteness of his armpits, and said:\n\n\"O Allah, have I conveyed [the message]?\" three times.\n\n---"
"2340","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Alaa' ibn Kuraib, narrated to us Abu Usaamah, from Hishaam, from his father, from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi (RA) who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed a man from the Azd tribe over the charity of Bani Sulaym, called Ibn al-Lutbiyah. When he came, he presented the account, saying, \"This is for you, and this is a gift presented to me.\" Thereupon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Why didn't you stay in your father's and mother's house until your gift came to you if you were truthful?\" Then he (ﷺ) delivered a sermon to us, praising Allah and extolling Him, then said:\n\"Amma ba'd (to proceed). I appoint a man from among you to a position of authority over some of the affairs of Allah which He has entrusted to me. One of you comes and says, 'This is for you and this is a gift presented to me.' Why didn't he stay in his father's and mother's house until his gift came to him if he were truthful? By Allah, none of you takes anything from it unlawfully but he will meet Allah carrying it on the Day of Resurrection. I will certainly recognize one of you meeting Allah carrying a snorting camel, or a mooing cow, or a bleating sheep.\" Then he (ﷺ) raised his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was visible, then said:\n\"O Allah, have I conveyed?\""
"2341","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar, narrated to us by Abd al-Wahhab, narrated to us by Dawud. Also narrated to us by Muhammad bin Bashshar (Bundar), narrated to us by Ibn Abi Adiyy, from Dawud. Also narrated to us by Abu Musa, narrated to us by Abd al-Wahhab, narrated to us by Dawud. Also narrated to us by Abu Musa, narrated to us by Ibn Abi Adiyy and Abd al-A'la,"
"2342","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Ibraheem al-Ghafiqi: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: Narrated to me Yoosuf ibn Yahya al-Ma‘ini — and (in another narration) Yoosuf ibn ‘Adiyy — on the authority of Yoones, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn al-Haarith ibn Nawfal al-Haashimi that ‘Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabee‘ah ibn al-Haarith ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib narrated to him from his father Rabee‘ah ibn al-Haarith and al-‘Abbaas ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib, who both said:\n\nWe said to ‘Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabee‘ah and al-Fadl ibn al-‘Abbaas: “Go to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and say to him: ‘O Messenger of Allah! We have reached the age of marriage, and we would love to get married. You, O Messenger of Allah, are the most dutiful and the most generous of people. However, our parents do not have anything to give as dowry on our behalf. So appoint us, O Messenger of Allah, as collectors of the charity (sadaqah), so that we may deliver to you just as other collectors deliver, and we may take from it what is lawful for us as workers.’”\n\nSo ‘Ali ibn Abi Taalib (RA) came to us while we were in this state and said to us: “By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) will never appoint any one of you as a collector of charity.”\n\nRabee‘ah ibn al-Haarith said to him: “This is out of envy from you. You have attained good from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), yet we are not envious of you for that.”\n\nThen he cast aside his cloak, lay down upon it, and said: “I am Abu al-Hasan of this people. By Allah, I will not leave this place until your two sons return to you with a response better than what you sent them with to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).”\n\n‘Abdullah ibn al-Haarith said: So I and al-Fadl set out until the time for the Zuhr prayer came, and we prayed with the people. Then I and al-Fadl hurried to the door of the Messenger of Allah’s (ﷺ) chamber, where he was at that time with Zaynab bint Jahsh. We stood at the door until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came. He took hold of my ear and the ear of al-Fadl and said: “Bring out what you are concealing.”\n\nThen he entered and gave us permission to enter, so we entered. We exchanged some words briefly, then I or al-Fadl — ‘Abdullah ibn al-Haarith was unsure — spoke to him about what our parents had instructed us to ask for. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) remained silent for a while, raising his gaze towards the ceiling of the house for so long that we thought he would not give us any reply. Then we saw Zaynab gesturing to us from behind the curtain with her hand, indicating that we should not be hasty, and that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was occupied with our matter.\n\nThen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) lowered his head and said to us: “Verily, this charity is the filth of the people, and it is not lawful for Muhammad (ﷺ) nor for the family of Muhammad (ﷺ). Call Nawfal ibn al-Haarith to me.”\n\nSo Nawfal ibn al-Haarith was called, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: “O Nawfal, marry ‘Abd al-Muttalib.”\n\nSo he married me to her. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Call Mahmiyyah ibn Jaz’ who is a man from Banu Zubeyd, whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had appointed over the fifths (of the spoils of war).”\n\nSo the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Mahmiyyah: “Marry al-Fadl.”\n\nSo Mahmiyyah ibn Jaz’ married him. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: “Stand up and pay their dowries from the fifth (of the spoils of war) such and such an amount” — and ‘Abdullah ibn al-Haarith did not specify the amount.\n\nAbu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahmaan said to us: “al-Hoor” means “the response.”\n\n---"
"2343","**\nNarrated to me Muhammad ibn ‘Aziz al-Ayli, who said: Ibn Salamah informed me, who narrated to them from ‘Uqayl, who said: Ibn Shihab said, and ‘Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal al-Hashimi informed me of a similar narration, saying: “There is nothing with our two parents that can testify on our behalf.”\n\nHe added: So we returned, and ‘Ali was in his place. He said: “Inform me of what you have brought.” They said: “We found the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) the most generous of people and the most kind to them.”\n\nHe said: “Did he appoint you to any position regarding this charity?”\n\nThey said: “No, but he did something better than that for us: he married us off and paid our dowries for us.”\n\nHe said: “I am Abu al-Hasan. Did I not inform you both that he would not appoint you to any position regarding this charity?”\n\nAbu Bakr said: This wording “he married us off” is of the type that I say: the Arabs attribute the favor to the command just as they attribute it to the doer. The Prophet (ﷺ) only ordered the marriage of ‘Abd al-Muttalib and al-Fadl ibn ‘Abbas, and this was done by his command, so the marriage was attributed to the Prophet (ﷺ) since he was the one who ordered it, even if he himself did not conduct the marriage contract.\n\nAhmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, my uncle narrated to him the hadith in its lengthy form, and he said: “I am Abu al-Hasan al-Qawm.”\n\nAhmad said to us regarding his statement: “until your two sons return to you and bring back what you were sent with,” that al-hur means al-jawab (the answer).\n\n---"
"2344","###\n Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Yazid bin Zura'i narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Al-Hakam bin Utaibah, from Ibn Abi Rafi', from his father (who was a freed slave of the Prophet ﷺ), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man from the Banu Makhzum to collect charity (Sadaqah). He said to me: \"Accompany me.\" I said: \"No"
"2345","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Yahya ibn Hakim, who said: Narrated to us Abu Dawud, who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, who said: 'Amr ibn Murrah informed me, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (RA) say:\n\n*\"When a household would give the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) charity, he would pray for them.\"*\n\nSo Abu Awfa (RA) gave charity to him, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) supplicated:\n\n*\"O Allah, send Your prayers upon the family of Abu Awfa.\"*\n\n---"
"2346","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak al-Mukharrimi, narrated to us Waki', narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Ishaq al-Makki—he was trustworthy—and also narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Waki' from Zakariyya ibn Ishaq al-Makki from Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sayfiyy from Abu Ma'bad from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that the Prophet (ﷺ), when he sent Mu'adh (RA) as a governor to Yemen, said:\n\n\"You are going to a people who are People of the Book. Call them to bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. If they obey that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them five prayers in a day and night. If they obey that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them charity (Zakah) from their wealth, to be taken from their rich and returned to their poor. If they obey that, then beware of taking the best of their wealth, and beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Ja'far. Al-Mukharrimi said: \"Indeed, the Prophet (ﷺ) sent Mu'adh ibn Jabal (RA) to Yemen and said: 'Call them to bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah. If they respond to that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them...'\"\n\nAnd he said in all of it: \"If they respond to that, then inform them...\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2347","Narrated to us Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā al-Ṣanʿānī, narrated to us Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ, informed us Shuʿbah, on the authority of Yazīd ibn Abī Maryam, on the authority of Abū al-Ḥawrāʾ, who said:\n\nI asked al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī, \"What do you remember from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ)?\"\n\nHe replied, \"I remember that I took a date from the dates of charity and placed it in my mouth. The Prophet (ﷺ) then removed it from my mouth and said, 'Indeed, the family of Muḥammad does not make lawful for us charity.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2348","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar and Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us by Shu'bah, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Maryam narrating from Abu Al-Hawra', who said: I said to Al-Hasan (Al-Basri): \"What do you remember from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?\" He replied: \"I remember from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that I once took a date from the dates of"
"2349","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Ibn Abi ‘Adiyy, who said: Thabit ibn ‘Umarah informed us, who said: Ibn Shayban narrated to us, who said: I said to al-Hasan ibn ‘Ali: \"What do you remember from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?\"\n\nHe said: \"I remember that he took me into the room of charity with him. I took a date and put it into my mouth. He (ﷺ) said: 'Spit it out, for it is not lawful for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) nor for any one of the people of his household.'\"\n\n---"
"2350","Narrated to us Abu Bakr (RA) in the narration of Abu Rafi' (RA):\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) sent a man from the tribe of Makhzum to collect the *sadaqah* (alms). The man said, \"Accompany me.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) replied, «Indeed, I have sent the man from Makhzum only to take the obligatory *sadaqah*.»\nAbu Rafi' (RA) said: The Prophet's (ﷺ) statement to me, «Indeed, *sadaqah* is not lawful for us,» was in response to the *sadaqah* for which the answer was given.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2351","In the narration of Al-Hasan ibn Ali (RA), he said: \"I took a date from the dates of Sadaqah (charity). That date was from the 'Ushr (one-tenth) - or from half of the 'Ushr - which is the Sadaqah that is obligatory upon dates.\""
"2352","###\n In the narration of 'Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah and his journey with Al-Fadl ibn Abbas to the Prophet (ﷺ), wherein they asked him to appoint them as collectors over the sadaqah (charity), and the Prophet's (ﷺ) response informing them: \"Verily, this sadaqah is merely the filth of the people, and it is not lawful for Muhammad nor for the family of Muhammad.\" It is clarified that their request was specifically for appointment over the obligatory sadaqah"
"2353","Abu Bakr said: In the narration of Urwah, from Aisha (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"We do not inherit what we leave behind; it is charity (Sadaqah). The family of Muhammad (ﷺ) may only consume from this wealth.\" Thus, the Prophet (ﷺ) has informed that it is permissible for his family to consume from his charity, given that his charity was not from the obligatory charity (Zakat)."
"2354","###\n In the narration of Hudhayfah, Jabir ibn Abdullah, and Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Every act of kindness (ma'ruf) is charity (sadaqah).\" Therefore, if the Chosen One (ﷺ) had intended by his statement, \"Indeed, charity is not lawful for the family of Muhammad,\" to mean both voluntary charity and obligatory charity, then no act of kindness would be permissible to be done for any member of the family of the Prophet ("
"2355","###\n Al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, informing us that Al-Thaqafi, 'Abd al-Wahhab, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from 'Amr ibn Sa'id, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari, who said: Three men from the Banu Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas narrated to me, each of them reporting from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) visited Sa'd in M"
"2356","###\n Abu Bakr (RA) said: In the narration concerning Abd al-Muttalib ibn Rabi'ah, there is clear evidence that the family of Abd al-Muttalib are forbidden from receiving charity (Sadaqah), just as it is forbidden for other descendants of Hashim. This is as Abu Hayyan claimed, narrating on the authority of Yazid ibn Hayyan, who narrated on the authority of Zayd ibn Arqam (RA): \"The family of the Prophet (ﷺ) who were forbidden"
"2357","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir and Muhammad ibn Fudayl, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi — and he is Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Taymi al-Rabaab — on the authority of Yazid ibn Hayyan, who said:\nI set out with Husayn ibn Samurah and ‘Amr ibn Muslim, going to Zayd ibn Arqam. We sat with him, and Husayn said to him, “O Zayd, you have seen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), prayed behind him, heard his speech, and fought alongside him. By Allah, you have attained much good, Zayd. Relate to us a narration that you heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and witnessed with him.”\n\nZayd replied, “Yes, O nephew. Indeed, my memory is recent, my years have grown, and I have forgotten some of what I used to retain from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). So whatever I narrate to you, accept it; and whatever I do not narrate to you, do not burden me with it.”\n\nHe said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood one day among us at a place called Khumm and delivered a sermon. He praised Allah, extolled Him, exhorted and reminded. Then he said:\n“O people, I am only a human being, and it is soon that the messenger of my Lord will come to me and I will respond. I am leaving among you two weighty things: the first is the Book of Allah, in which is guidance and light. Whoever holds fast to it and takes it, he is upon guidance; and whoever abandons it and strays from it, he is upon misguidance. And my family — I remind you of Allah regarding my family, three times.”\n\nHusayn asked, “Who are his family, O Zayd? Are not his wives among his family?”\n\nZayd replied, “Yes, his wives are among his family. But his family are those for whom charity is forbidden.”\n\nHusayn asked, “Who are they?”\n\nZayd said, “The family of ‘Ali, the family of ‘Aqil, the family of Ja‘far, and the family of al-‘Abbas.”\n\nHusayn asked, “Are all of these forbidden from charity?”\n\nZayd said, “Yes.”\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2358","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me that Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi informed him from Sharik ibn Abi Namir that he heard Anas ibn Malik (RA).\n\nAlso narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Tamam al-Misri, who said: Al-Nasr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar and Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, both saying: Al-Layth narrated to us, who said: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to me from Sharik ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir al-Kinani that he heard Anas ibn Malik (RA) say:\n\n\"While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the mosque, a man entered riding a camel. He made it kneel in the mosque, then tied it, and then said: 'Which of you is Muhammad?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was reclining among us. We said to him: 'This white man reclining.' He said: 'O son of 'Abd al-Muttalib.'\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) replied: 'I have answered you.'\n\nThe man said: 'I am going to ask you, and I will ask you strictly, so do not take it ill from me.'\n\nHe (ﷺ) said: 'Ask whatever you wish.'\n\nHe said: 'I adjure you by your Lord and the Lord of those before you, has Allah sent you to all the people?'\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Allahumma, yes.'\n\nHe said: 'I adjure you by Allah, has Allah commanded you to pray the five prayers during the day and night?'\n\nHe (ﷺ) said: 'Allahumma, yes.'\n\nHe said: 'I adjure you by Allah, has Allah commanded you to take this charity from our rich and distribute it among our poor?'\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Allahumma, yes.'\n\nThe man said: 'I believe in what you have brought, and I am the messenger of those behind me from my people. I am Dhimam ibn Tha'labah, the brother of Sa'd ibn al-Hakam.'\n\nTheir wording is close to one another, and this is the narration of Ibn Wahb.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \"In this report, there is an indication that obligatory charity (zakat) is not permissible to give to non-Muslims, even if they are poor or needy, because the Prophet (ﷺ) knew that Allah had commanded him to take zakat from the rich Muslims and distribute it among their poor, not among the poor of others.\"\n\n---"
"2359","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar and Hafz bin 'Amr ar-Rabali, who said: Narrated to us 'Abdul-Wahhab, who narrated from Ayyub. [And in another chain]: Narrated to us 'Ali bin Hujr, who narrated to us Isma'il, meaning Ibn Ibraheem, from Ayyub, from Harun bin Riyab, from Kinanah bin Nu'aim, from Qabisah, who said: \"I came to the Prophet (ﷺ"
"2360","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi, who said: Bisr — meaning Ibn Bakr — informed us, who said: al-Awza'i said: Harun ibn Riyab narrated to me, who narrated to me Abu Bakr — that is Kinanah ibn Nu'aym — who said: I was sitting with Qabisah when some men from his people came to him asking about the marriage of their companion. He refused to give them anything, so I said: \"You are the leader of your people, so why did you not give them something?\" He replied: \"They asked me for something unjustly. If their companion had taken his own private part and bitten it until it withered, it would have been better for him than the request they made to me. Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Begging is not lawful except for three [types of people]: a man whose wealth has been destroyed by a calamity, so he asks until he obtains a means of livelihood, then he refrains from begging; a man who has taken on a liability for his people, so he asks until he fulfills his liability, then he refrains from begging; and a man for whom three of the wise men from his people swear by Allah that begging is lawful for him. Other than these, it is unlawful gain, and he does not consume anything but unlawful gain.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2361","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammad (meaning Ibn Zayd), narrated to us Harun ibn Riyab, narrated to us Kinanah ibn Nu'aym al-'Adawi, on the authority of Qabisah ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali who said:\n\nI took upon myself a debt (hamalah), so I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) asking him about it. He said, \"Stay here, O Qabisah, until the charity (sadaqah) comes to me, so that I may command that some be given to you.\"\n\nThen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Indeed, charity is not lawful except for one of three:\n1. A man who takes upon himself a debt (hamalah), so it becomes lawful for him to ask until he attains a means of livelihood, or he said: a sufficiency of livelihood.\n2. A man upon whom a calamity strikes that devastates his wealth, so it becomes lawful for him to ask until he attains a means of livelihood, or a sufficiency of livelihood.\n3. A man who is struck by poverty, so charity becomes lawful for him until he attains a means of livelihood, or a sufficiency of livelihood.\n\nAs for other than that, O Qabisah, it is unlawful (suht), and the one who takes it eats it unlawfully.\"\n\n---"
"2362","Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Sa‘id ibn Masruq al-Kindi, narrated to us Hafs—meaning ibn Ghiyath—on the authority of Ash‘ath, on the authority of ‘Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, on the authority of his father (Abū Juhayfah), who said:\n“A collector of the Prophet’s alms (sadaqah) came to us. He took the alms from our wealthy and distributed it among our poor. I was a young orphan, so he gave me a young camel from it.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2363","###\n Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us by Abu Mu'awiyah, who said: Narrated to us by Al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The poor person (Al-Miskin) is not the one who goes around (begging) and is turned away by a single mouthful or two mouthfuls, or by a date or two dates. Rather, the poor person"
"2364","**\nNarrated to us Al-Rabee' ibn Sulayman al-Muraadi, narrated to us Shu'bah, narrated to us al-Layth, on the authority of Bukayr, on the authority of Busr ibn Sa'eed, on the authority of Ibn al-Sa'idi al-Maaliki, who said:\n\n\"Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA) appointed me to collect the *sadaqah* (charity). When I finished and delivered it to him, he ordered that I be given a stipend (*ummalah*). I said, 'I only worked for the sake of Allah, and my reward is with Allah.' He said, 'Take what I have given you. Indeed, I worked during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he gave me a stipend.' I said something similar to your statement, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"If you are given something without asking for it, then eat it and give it in charity.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn al-Sa'idi al-Maaliki) said: I think he is Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh.\""
"2365","###\n Muhammad bin Uzaayr al-Ayli narrated to us, stating that Salamah bin Ruh narrated to them from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, who said: As-Sa'ib bin Yazid narrated to me, stating that Huwaytib bin Abd al-Uzza informed him, who stated that Abdullah bin Sa'd bin Abi Sarh informed him: He came to Umar bin al-Khattab (RA) during his caliphate. Umar (RA) said to him, \"Did I not tell you that you are"
"2366","###\n Yunus ibn al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, from his father ('Abdullah ibn 'Umar), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to give (a portion of the charity) to Ibn al-Khattab ('Umar). 'Umar would say, \"Give it to him, for he is more in need of it than I"
"2367","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Zuhayr, Abd al-Majid ibn Ibrahim al-Misri, who said: Narrated to us by Shu'ayb, meaning the son of Yahya al-Tujibi, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Layth, from Hisham, who is Ibn Sa'd, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father Aslam, who said:\n\nDuring the year of the \"Ramadats\" (a year of severe drought), when the lands were barren and"
"2368","###\n Muhammad bin Ma'mar bin Rabi' al-Qaysi narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Imran al-Bariqi, from 'Atiyyah, with my dissociation from his responsibility, from Abu Sa'id, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Charity is not lawful for a rich person except for five: the collector appointed over it, the one in debt, the one who buys it (to free a"
"2369","Narrated to us by Zayd bin Akhzam at-Ta'i, who said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from 'Abdul-Warith bin Sa'id, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from 'Abdullah bin Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever we appoint to a task and provide him with a sustenance (salary), then whatever he takes after that is considered embezzlement (Ghulul).\""
"2370","###\n Yahya bin Makhlad bin Al-Muftee narrated to us, saying: Mu'afa (who is Mu'afa bin Imran Al-Mawsili) narrated to us, on the authority of Al-Awza'i, who said: Harith bin Yazid narrated to us, on the authority of Abdur-Rahman bin Jubayr, who said: Al-Mustawrid bin Shaddad said: \"I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: 'Whoever is appointed as an administrator (worker) for"
"2371","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Abu Musa, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Adi, from Humayd, from Musa ibn Anas, from Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was never asked for anything in the name of Islam without giving it. He said: A man came to him and asked him, so he ordered that a large flock of sheep be given to him, grazing between two mountains, from the sheep of the Zakah. The man returned"
"2372","Narrated to us Al-Sana'ani, narrated to us Al-Mu'tamir who said: I heard from Humaid who said: Anas (RA) informed us that a man came to the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) and he ordered for him to be given something between two mountains. So he returned to his people and said: \"Embrace Islam, for indeed Muhammad (ﷺ) gives like a man who does not fear poverty.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2373","**\nNarrated to us Abu Hisham ar-Rifa’i, narrated to us Ibn Fudayl, narrated to us ‘Umarah—meaning Ibn al-Qa’qā’—on the authority of Abu Nu’aym, who is ‘Abd ar-Raḥmān ibn Abi Nu’aym, on the authority of Abu Sa’id al-Khudri (RA), who said:\n\n“Ali (RA) sent gold from Yemen to the Prophet (ﷺ) that had not been purified of its earth. He divided it among four men: al-Aqra’ ibn Ḥābis al-Ḥanẓali, ‘Uyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn al-Murādi, ‘Alqamah ibn ‘Ulāthah al-Ja’fari—or ‘Āmir ibn aṭ-Ṭufayl (he was uncertain)—and Zayd aṭ-Ṭā’i.\n\nSome of the Prophet’s Companions from the Anṣār and others among them found fault with this. When it reached him, he said:\n\n‘Do you not trust me, when I am the Trustworthy One to whom the inhabitants of the heaven entrust? Morning and evening, news from the heaven comes to me.’”\n\n---"
"2374","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar. And narrated to us Muhammad (Sahl ibn 'Asakir), narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Charity is not lawful except for five [categories of people]:\n1. The one who administers it (the collector),\n2. A man who buys it with his own wealth,\n3. A debtor,\n4. A warrior fighting in the cause of Allah,\n5. Or a poor person to whom charity is given, and he gives a gift from it to a rich person.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: \"I did not find in my book from Ibn 'Asakir the phrase: 'or a debtor.'\"\n\n---"
"2375","Narrated to us Abu Hashim Ziyad ibn Ayyub, al-Hasan ibn 'Isa al-Bistami, and Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la—they said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from Harun ibn Riyab, from Kinanah ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisah ibn Mukhariq (RA), who said:\n\nI undertook a liability (a guarantee for someone's debt), so I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) asking him about it. He said: \"We will pay it on your behalf and it will be taken from the camels of charity (Sadaqah).\"\n\nThen he (ﷺ) said: \"O Qabisah! Begging is unlawful except in three cases:\n1. A man who undertakes a liability, then it becomes permissible for him to beg until he pays it off, then he must stop.\n2. A man upon whom a calamity has befallen that has destroyed his wealth, then it becomes permissible for him to beg until he attains a means of livelihood or a way to live, then he must stop.\n3. A man upon whom a calamity and poverty have befallen such that he speaks (about his need) or three men of sound judgment from his people testify that it has become permissible for him to beg, until he attains a way to live or a means of livelihood, then he must stop. Other than these, begging is from unlawful earnings (Suhṭ).\"\n\nAl-Bistami added: \"And it (the liability) will be taken from the charity.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2376","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Isma'il bin Samurah al-Ahmisi, who narrated from Al-Muharibi, from Muhammad bin Ishaq, from Isa bin Maqil bin Abi Maqil al-Asadi (from Asad Khuzaimah), from Yusuf bin Abdullah bin Salam, from his grandmother Umm Maqil, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made preparations for Hajj and commanded the people to prepare themselves to go with him. She said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ"
"2377","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad az-Za'faraani, narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd at-Tanaafisi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibraaheem, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawbaan, from Abu Laas al-Khuzaa'ee who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted us upon camels from the camels of charity (sadaqah) that were weak for Hajj. So we said: \"O Messenger of Allah! We do not see that these camels will carry us.\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said: \"There is no camel except that upon its hump is a shaytaan (devil). So mention the Name of Allah upon it when you ride them, as He has commanded you. Then utilize them for yourselves, for indeed Allah is the One Who carries (the burden).\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2378","###\n Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani, Muhammad ibn Yahya, Ahmad ibn Sa'id al-Darimi, and Ahmad ibn al-Khalil, who said: Narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, who informed us from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ataa', from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Salamah ibn Sakhr al-Ansari (RA), who"
"2379","###\n Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, saying: Umar ibn Ali ibn Ata ibn Maqdam al-Muqaddami narrated to us, saying: Ash'ath ibn Sawwar narrated to us, from Awn ibn Abi Juhaifah, from his father, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a man as a collector (Sā'i) for charity (Sadaqah), and commanded him to take from the wealthy and distribute it among the poor. So, he"
"2380","###\n Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Ahmad bin Abd al-Malik bin Waqid al-Jurayri al-Harrani narrated to us, saying: Muhammad bin Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad bin Ishaq, from Abdullah bin Abi Najih, from Abd al-Rahman bin Abi Amrah, from Umarah bin Amr bin Hazm, from Ubayy bin Ka'b (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me to collect the Sadaqah"
"2381","In the narration of Abdullah bin Abi Awfa (RA), a man came to him bringing the Sadaqah (charity/Zakat) of his people. [The compiler notes]: \"And this chapter, along with the narration of Ik rash bin Dhuyayb, falls under this same topic.\""
"2382","**\nNarrated to us Abu Bishr al-Wasiti, narrated to us Khalid — meaning Ibn 'Abdullah —, on the authority of al-Shaybani, from 'Abdullah ibn Dhakwan, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a man from the people of Yemen to collect its zakah. He came with a large amount of wealth. When I sent someone to take it from him, he said, \"This is for me and this is for you.\" If he were asked, \"From where did you get this?\" he would say, \"It was gifted to me.\" But if he were truthful, would it not have been gifted to him while he was in his father's or mother's house?\n\nThen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"I will never send a man to carry out a task, then he steals anything from it, but he will bring it on the Day of Resurrection upon his neck: a camel that brays, or a cow that moos, or a sheep that bleats.\"\n\nThen he (ﷺ) said, \"O Allah, have I conveyed?\"\n\nIbn al-Zubayr said to Abu Humayd, \"Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?\"\n\nHe said, \"Yes.\"\n\n---"
"2383","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Aban and Yusuf ibn Musa ibn 'Isa al-Marwazi — they said: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan al-Dhabbi, who said: Narrated to us 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib and Abu Ja'far Musa ibn al-Sa'ib, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"Peace be upon you, O young man of the Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) replied, \"And upon you.\"\n\nThe man said, \"I am a man from the tribe of Bani Sa'd ibn Bakr, and I am the messenger and envoy of my people to you. I ask you, so make my request binding upon you, and I call upon you, so make my call binding upon you.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Take it easy, O brother of the sons of Sa'd.\"\n\nThe man asked, \"Who created you? And who created those before you? And who is the Creator of those after you?\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) replied, \"Allah.\"\n\nThe man said, \"Then I call upon you by that — did He send you?\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Yes.\"\n\nThe man continued, \"We have found in Your Book that You have sent messengers to us commanding us to take from the edges of our wealth and return it to our poor. So I call upon you by that — did He command you with that?\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Yes.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, \"The saying of Allah, the Exalted and Glorious:\n\n'If you disclose your charitable expenditures, they are good...' (Qur'an 2:271)\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2384","###\n Narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman bin Bashar bin al-Hakam, who said: Narrated to us by Malik, meaning Malik bin Su'ayr bin al-Khims, who said: Narrated to us by Sa'id bin Ubayd at-Ta'i, who said: Narrated to us by Bushayr bin Yasar, that a man from his people, named Ibn Abi Hathmah, informed him that a group of them went out to Khaybar and dispersed within it. One of them was found dead. Those who"
"2385","###\n Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani narrated to us, saying: Bishr (meaning Ibn al-Mufaddal) narrated to us, saying: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us. [In another narration:] Ali bin Khushram informed us, saying: 'Isa informed us. [In another narration:] Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, saying: Mu'adh bin Mu'adh narrated to us; both of them (Ibn 'Awn and Mu'adh) narrated from Ibn '"
"2386","###\n Ahmad ibn Abda narrated to us, informing us that Sufyan said: \"On the authority of Az-Zuhri, on the authority of Humayd ibn Abdur-Rahman, on the authority of his mother, Umm Kulthum bint Uqba.\" Sufyan added: \"She had prayed facing the Qibla with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on two occasions.\" She said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The best charity is that given to a relative who"
"2387","Narrated to us by Al-Abbas ibn al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, on the authority of Mansur, on the authority of Abu Hazim, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), who said that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Charity is not lawful for a rich person, nor for one who is strong and capable of earning.\""
"2388","Abu Bakr (RA) said: \"I have clarified this in the sequel to the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ): *‘Indeed, the family of Muhammad (ﷺ) are not lawful for us to take charity.’*\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2389","###\n Abu Bakr said: \"The narration of Salamah ibn Sakhr regarding his mention of the Prophet (ﷺ) is that they would come with wild game when they had no evening meal. The Prophet (ﷺ) sent him to the collector of the Zakat of the Banu Zurayq to collect their Zakat. There is no mention in this narration that the Prophet (ﷺ) asked anyone else. Furthermore, this narration indicates the permissibility of handing over the Zakat of a tribe to a single individual, rather than it"
"2390","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Qabisah, narrated to us Sufyan, from Musa ibn Abi 'Aishah, from 'Abdullah, from 'Ali (RA), who said:\nI said to al-'Abbas (RA): \"Ask the Prophet (ﷺ) to appoint you as the collector of the charity (Sadaqah).\"\nHe (ﷺ) said: \"I would not appoint you as the collector of the filth of the sins of the people.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2391","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Al-Nufayli, who said: Narrated to us Miskin Al-Hadda', who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Muhajir, on the authority of Rabi'ah bin Yazid, on the authority of Abu Kabshah As-Saluli, who said: Narrated to us Sahl bin Al-Hanzaliyyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Whoever asks for something while"
"2392","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la al-San‘ani, narrated to us al-Mu‘tamir, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Nafi‘, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA), who said:\n\"I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say when he prescribed the *sadaqah al-fitr*:\n'A *sa’* of dates or a *sa’* of barley.' And he would not accept anything except dates.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2393","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, narrated to us Ayyub, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prescribed the *sadaqah* of *Fitr* as one *sa’* of dates or one *sa’* of barley.\"\nAnd 'Abdullah (ibn 'Umar) would give on behalf of every member of his household—young and old, free and slave—one *sa’* of dates.\nOnce, he found it difficult to give dates, so he borrowed some barley.\nThen, in the time of Mu'awiyah, people began to give two *mudds* of wheat in exchange for one *sa’* of barley."
"2394","###\n Narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Tha'labi, who narrated to us Waki', from Sufyan, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu 'Ammar al-Hamdani, from Qays ibn Sa'd, who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to give the charity of Fitr (Sadaqat al-Fitr) before the obligation of Zakat on wealth was revealed. Then, when"
"2395","###\n Ahmad ibn Mani', Ziyad ibn Ayub, Mu'ammal ibn Hisam, and al-Hasan ibn al-Za'farani narrated to us. They said: Isma'il (i.e., Ibn 'Ulayyah) narrated to us. Al-Za'farani said: \"Ibn 'Ulayyah.\" Ahmad and Ziyad said: \"Ayyub informed us.\" Mu'ammal and al-Za'farani said: \"From Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn '"
"2396","###\n Muhammad bin Hakim narrated to us, stating that Uthman bin Umar narrated to us, stating that Usamah bin Zayd narrated to us, from Makhul, from Irak bin Malik, from Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no Zakat (charity) due from a Muslim upon his horse, nor upon his slave, nor upon his female slave, except for the Zakat of Fitr (Sadaqat al-Fitr).\" Abu Bakr (the"
"2397","###\n Narrated to us by Imran bin Musa Al-Qazzaz, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Warith, who said: Narrated to us by Ayyub, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) enjoined the Zakat of Fitr (Sadaqat al-Fitr) upon the free and the slave, the male and the female, amounting to one Sa' of dates or one Sa' of barley.\" He (the"
"2398","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Salamah, Muhammad bin al-Mughirah al-Makhzumi, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Fudaik, on the authority of al-Dahhak, who is Ibn Uthman, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Abdullah bin Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made obligatory the Zakat al-Fitr in Ramadan upon every soul from the Muslims, whether free or enslaved, male or female, young or old, amounting to"
"2399","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad az-Za'farani, narrated to us Abdullah ibn Nafīʿ az-Zubayri and Muhammad ibn Idrīs, they said: narrated to us Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) prescribed the Zakāh of al-Fiṭr in Ramaḍān upon the people—a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of barley—for every free person and slave, male and female, among the Muslims.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2400","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Anba'ana Ibn Wahb, that Malik informed him to the same effect, and he said: \"From Ramadan,\" and he said: \"Male and female.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2401","###\n Muhammad bin Aziz Al-Ayli narrated to us, saying: Salamah narrated to us. And it was also narrated to me by Uqail, from Hisham bin Urwah, from Urwah bin Az-Zubayr, from his mother, Asma bint Abi Bakr (RA), who informed him: \"They used to give the Zakat al-Fitr during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as a *Mudd* (a measure) which the people of Al-Madinah consumed, or"
"2402","Abu Bakr narrated to us, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Whatever I have commanded you with, do it as much as you are able, and whatever I have forbidden you from, avoid it as much as you are able.\""
"2403","**\nNarrated to us Bundār, (who said) narrated to us Yaḥyā. And narrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī, (who said) informed us ʿAbd al-Aʿlā. They both said: narrated to us ʿUbayd Allāh, who informed me Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prescribed the *ṣadaqah al-fiṭr* (obligatory charity for breaking the fast). Naṣr said: *Ṣadaqah al-Ramaḍān* upon the young and the old, the free and the slave — a *ṣāʿ* of dates or a *ṣāʿ* of barley.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, except that he said: \"from Nāfiʿ.\"\n\nAnd narrated to us al-Ṣanʿānī, (who said) narrated to us al-Muʿtamir, who said: I heard ʿUbayd Allāh narrate similar to the hadith of Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, and he added: \"and the male and the female.\""
"2404","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Aziz Al-Ayli, who said: Narrated to us Salamah, who said: Narrated to me Uqail, who said: Narrated to me Nafi', the freed slave of Abdullah bin Umar, from Abdullah bin Umar (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): \"He used to give the Zakat of Fitr as one Sa' of dates or one Sa' of barley.\" And Abdullah (bin Umar) said: \"The people then equated the value of the barley and dates to two M"
"2405","###\n Al-Hasan ibn Qaz'ah narrated to us, saying: Al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated to us, saying: Musa ibn 'Uqba narrated to us, saying: Nafi' informed me, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to give the Zakat al-Fitr as one Sa' of dates or one Sa' of barley. Ibn 'Umar (RA) said: The people equated this amount to two M"
"2406","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sufyan ibn Abi'z-Zarad al-Abuli, narrated to us 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa, who informed us Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:\n\"During the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), charity (Sadaqah) was not given except in the form of dates, raisins, and barley. Wheat was not given.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2407","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Yahya, who narrated to us from Dawud bin Qays, on the authority of Iyad, from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"We used to give out (as Zakat al-Fitr) one Sa' of dates, one Sa' of barley, or one Sa' of dried yogurt (Aqit) during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). This practice continued until the time of Mu'awiyah ("
"2408","###\n Narrated to us Ibn Hujr, who narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ja'far, who narrated to us Dawud (who is Dawud ibn Qays al-Farra'), on the authority of Iyad ibn Abdullah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"We used to give the Zakat al-Fitr during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as one Sa' of food, or one Sa' of dried yogurt (Aqit), or"
"2409","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Hasan bin Muhammad Az-Za'farani, who said: Narrated to us by Qabisah bin Uqbah, who said: He informed us by Sufyan, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) ordered that Zakat al-Fitr be given on behalf of every young and old, free or slave, amounting to one Sa' of barley or one Sa' of dates. Later, the people equated this with two"
"2410","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il al-Minqari, narrated to us Hammam, on the authority of Bakr al-Kufi (who is the son of Wa'il ibn Dawud), that al-Zuhri narrated to them, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah ibn al-Su'ayr, on the authority of his father, who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood as a speaker and ordered the *sadaqah al-fitr* (obligatory charity at the end of Ramadan) to be given as one *sa'* of dates or one *sa'* of barley for each person, for the young and the old, for the free and the slave."
"2411","###\n Narrated to us Al-Hasan bin Abdullah bin Mansur Al-Antaki, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Kathir, on the authority of Abdullah bin Shudhab, on the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) ordained Zakat al-Fitr upon the free and the slave, the male and the female, the young and the old among the Muslims: one Sa' of barley, or one Sa' of dates"
"2412","Narrated to us 'Amr bin 'Ali as-Sayrafi, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Khalid al-Hanafi, who said: Narrated to us Kathir bin 'Abdullah bin 'Amr bin 'Auf, who said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The Zakat (specifically Zakat al-Fitr) upon the Muslims is one Sa' of dates, or one Sa' of raisins, or one Sa"
"2413","Narrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Hammād ibn Mas‘adah, on the authority of Ibn ‘Ajlān, from ‘Iyāḍ, from Abū Sa‘īd al-Khudrī (RA) that Mu‘āwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān (RA) used to command them to give charity for Ramaḍān as half a ṣā‘ of wheat or one ṣā‘ of dates. So Abū Sa‘īd (RA) said, “We will not give except what we used to give during the time of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ): one ṣā‘ of dates, or one ṣā‘ of dried yogurt (aqit), or one ṣā‘ of raisins, or one ṣā‘ of barley.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2414","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', who narrated to us Sufyan, from Ibn 'Ajlan, who said: 'Iyad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh informed me that he heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA) say: \"We used to give out on the occasion of the Zakat al-Fitr one Sa' of dates, or one Sa' of barley, or one Sa' of raisins, or one Sa' of dried"
"2415","**\nNarrated to us Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, narrated to us ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, narrated to us Hishām, on the authority of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, on the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) commanded us to pay the zakāh of Ramaḍān: one ṣāʿ of food from every small and large, free and enslaved person. Whoever pays it in the form of *sult* (a type of grain), it will be accepted from him. I think he (the narrator) said: 'And whoever pays it in the form of *daqīq* (fine flour), it will be accepted from him. And whoever pays it in the form of *sawīq* (coarse flour), it will be accepted from him.'\""
"2416","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who narrated to us Al-Humaydi, who narrated to us Abdul-Aziz bin Abi Hazim, on the authority of Musa bin Uqba, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The Sadaqat al-Fitr is one Sa' of barley, or one Sa' of dates, or one Sa' of Sult.\""
"2417","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Abdul-Wahhab, who said: Narrated to us Ayyub, on the authority of Muhammad (Ibn Sirin), on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), that he used to say: \"The Sadaqat al-Fitr (Zakat al-Fitr) of Ramadan is one Sa' of food. Whoever brings wheat, it is accepted from him; whoever brings barley, it is accepted from him; whoever brings dates, it is accepted from him; whoever"
"2418","###\n Narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, who said: Narrated to us Waki', on the authority of Dawud ibn Qays al-Farra', on the authority of Iyad ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: \"We used to give the Sadaqat al-Fitr (Zakat al-Fitr) during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) as one Sa' of food, or one Sa' of dates"
"2419","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, narrated to us Ibn 'Ulayyah, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who narrated to me 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman ibn Hakim ibn Hizam, from 'Iyad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Sarh, who said:\nAbu Sa'id was mentioned in his presence the charity of Ramadan (i.e., Sadaqat al-Fitr), so he said:\n\"I do not give out except what I used to give out during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): a sa' of dates, or a sa' of wheat, or a sa' of barley, or a sa' of dried yogurt (aqit).\"\nA man from the people said to him: \"What if it were two mudds of wheat?\"\nHe said: \"No, that is the estimation of Mu'awiyah. I do not accept it, nor do I act upon it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said:\n\"The mention of wheat in the narration of Abu Sa'id is not preserved, and I do not know from whom the error came. His statement: 'A man from the people said to him: \"Or two mudds of wheat...\" until the end of the narration indicates that the mention of wheat at the beginning of the story is an error or a mistake. For if Abu Sa'id had informed them that they used to give out a sa' of wheat during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), the man's statement about two mudds of wheat would have no meaning.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2420","Narrated to us Abu 'Umar and Muslim ibn 'Amr ibn Muslim ibn Wahb al-Aslami al-Madini with an unusual report, who said:\nNarrated to me 'Abdullah ibn Nafi', on the authority of Kathir ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, on the authority of his father, on the authority of his grandfather, who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked about this verse:\n*\"Indeed, he has succeeded who purifies himself, and mentions the name of his Lord and prays.\"* [Surah Al-A'la 87:14-15]\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n*\"It was revealed concerning the charity of Fitr (Zakat al-Fitr).\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2421","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Salamah, Yahya bin Al-Mughirah Al-Makhzumi, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Abi Fudaik, on the authority of Al-Dahhak bin Uthman, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Abdullah bin Umar (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) commanded that the Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out to perform the Eid prayer.\" And indeed, Abdullah bin Umar (RA) used to pay it one or"
"2422","Narrated to us by Umar ibn Hafis al-Shaybani, who narrated to us from Abdul-Majid ibn Abdul-Aziz ibn Abi Rawad, who narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA) who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that the Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out to perform the prayer on the day of Eid al-Fitr.\""
"2423","Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman Al-Muradi and Bahrun ibn Nasr Al-Khawlan, they said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me that Ibn Abi Al-Zinad reported to him from Musa ibn 'Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that the Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out to the prayer place (Musalla).\""
"2424","###\n Narrated to us Hilal bin Bisr al-Basri with a rare narration; he said: Narrated to us Uthman bin al-Haytham, the muezzin of the Grand Mosque; he said: Narrated to us Awf, from Muhammad bin Sirin, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) informed me to guard the Zakat of Ramadan. Then, a person came to me in the dead of the night and began to scoop up food. I caught him and said"
"2425","Narrated to us Al-Husayn ibn Al-Hasan Al-Marwazi and ‘Utbah ibn ‘Abdillah: They both said: Narrated to us Ibn Al-Mubarak, who informed us: ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Umar narrated to us, from Sa‘id Al-Maqburi, from Abu Al-Hubab—he is Sa‘id ibn Yasar—from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“No Muslim servant gives charity from his lawful earnings—since Allah only accepts what is lawful—except Allah takes it with His right hand and nurtures it for him as one of you nurtures his foal or colt, until the charity becomes as large as the Mount Uhud.”\n\n‘Utbah said: “His foal.”\nAnd he did not preserve from ‘Utbah the phrase “as large as Uhud.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2426","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Rafi’ and ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr ibn al-Hakam who said: Anba’a to us ‘Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma’mar, on the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, “When a servant gives charity from his lawful earnings, Allah accepts it with His right hand and nurtures it as one of you nurtures his foal or young camel. Indeed, a man may give a single morsel in charity, and it grows in the hand of Allah until it becomes like a mountain. So give charity.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2427","**\nNarrated to us ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī, narrated to us ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, narrated to us Hishām, from al-Qāsim, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ).\n\nAnd narrated to us ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī, narrated to us ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad, narrated to us ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr.\n\nAnd narrated to us Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, narrated to us Wakīʿ, from ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr.\n\nAnd narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Quṭaʿī, narrated to us al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Minhāl, narrated to us Shuʿbah, from ʿAbbād ibn Manṣūr, from al-Qāsim, who said: Jaʿfar heard from Abū Hurayrah, and al-Quṭaʿī and ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī said from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said something similar to the hadith of ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Jaʿfar added in his narration: \"And the confirmation of that is in the Book of Allah: 'Allah destroys the ribā and increases the ṣadaqah.'\" (Qur'an 2:276)"
"2428","Narrated to us Al-Husayn ibn Al-Hasan and 'Utbah ibn 'Abdillah, they said: Narrated to us Ibn Al-Mubarak, who said: Narrated to us Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, that he heard Khaythamah narrating, from 'Adi ibn Hatim, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he mentioned the Fire, sought refuge from it, and turned his face away twice or thrice. Then he said:\n\n*\"Fear the Fire, even if it is with half a date. If you cannot find that, then with a good word.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2429","Narrated to us Bundār, narrated to us Abū Baḥr al-Bakrāwī, narrated to us Ismāʿīl, on the authority of Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī, on the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās (RA), from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said:\n*\"Guard yourselves against the Fire, even if it be by giving half a date in charity.\"*\n\nAbū Bakr said: He is Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim al-Makkī, and I absolve myself from any responsibility regarding him.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2430","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Abdullah bin Wahb, who informed us: Amr bin al-Harith. And also narrated to us Isa bin Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, from Amr bin al-Harith, from Yazid bin Abi Habib, from Sinan bin Sa'd al-Kindi, from Anas bin Malik (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Save yourselves from the Fire, even"
"2431","**\nNarrated to us Al-Husayn ibn Al-Hasan and 'Utbah ibn 'Abdullah, they said: Narrated to us Ibn Al-Mubarak, who narrated to us Harmalah ibn 'Imran, that he heard Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrate that Abu Al-Khayr narrated to him that he heard 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n*\"Every person will be in the shade of his charity until the people's disputes are settled, or he said: until judgment is passed between the people.\"*\n\nYazid (RA) said: Abu Al-Khayr would never miss a day in which he did not give charity, even if it was only a small piece of bread or an onion.\n\n---"
"2432","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us Yazid bin Zura'i, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Ishaq, who said: Yazid bin Abi Habib narrated to me, from Marthad bin Abdullah al-Muzani, who said: \"He (referring to a certain man) was the first among the people of Misr (Egypt) to go to the mosque in the evening. I never saw him enter the mosque except that he had charity"
"2433","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', who narrated to us Abu Al-Hasan An-Nadr bin Isma'il, from Abu Farwah, who said: I heard Sa'id bin Al-Musayyib, from Umar bin Al-Khattab (RA), who said: It was mentioned to me that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Indeed, deeds boast among themselves, and Al-Sadaqah (charity) says: 'I am the best of you.'\""
"2434","Narrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulaymaan al-Muraadiyy with a strange narration, (he said) narrated to us Asad, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khaazim—he is Abu Mu’aawiyah—from Muhammad ibn Ishaaq, from al-Zuhree, from ‘Ubaydullah ibn ‘Abdullah, from Maymoonah (RA), that she asked the Prophet (ﷺ) for a servant, so he gave her one, then she freed her. So he (ﷺ) said: “Indeed, if you had given her to your maternal uncles, it would have been greater in reward for you.”\n\nThis Muhammad ibn Khaazim is Abu Mu’aawiyah the blind.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2435","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, who said: Jarir informed us, on the authority of Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari.\n\nAnd narrated to us Bundar, who said: Muhammad narrated to us, who said: Shu'bah narrated to us, on the authority of Ibrahim al-Hajari, who said: I heard Abu'l-Ahwas, on the authority of 'Abdullah, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ), that he said:\n\n*\"There are three hands: the hand of Allah, the Most High, the hand of the giver which follows it, and the hand of the beggar which is below them until the Day of Resurrection. So refrain from begging as much as you are able.\"*\n\nYusuf said: *\"On the authority of Abu'l-Ahwas,\"* and he said: *\"which follows it,\"* and he said: *\"So refrain from begging as much as you are able.\"*\n\nThis is the wording of Bundar's hadith."
"2436","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah, narrated to us Hammād ibn Zayd, from 'Āṣim, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"The best charity is that which leaves you self-sufficient. The upper hand is better than the lower hand. Begin with those under your responsibility. Your wife will say to you, 'Spend on me or divorce me.' Your slave will say to you, 'Spend on me or sell me.' Your child will say to you, 'To whom will you leave us?'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2437","###\n Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar, who narrated from Sufyan, who narrated from Abu al-Zinad, from Al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"The example of the one who spends in charity and the miser is like that of two men wearing iron cloaks from their chests to their collarbones. When the one who gives in charity intends to spend, the cloak expands or becomes so ample that it covers his fingertips and erases any trace"
"2438","**\nNarrated to us ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr al-Saʿdī, narrated to us Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, who said: al-ʿAlāʾ narrated to us, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Charity does not decrease wealth, and Allāh does not increase a servant in forbearance except in honour, and no one humbles himself before Allāh except that Allāh raises him.”\n\nAlso narrated to us Bundār and Abū Mūsā. Bundār said: Muḥammad narrated to us. Abū Mūsā said: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me. Shuʿbah narrated to us, on the authority of al-ʿAlāʾ. Abū Mūsā said: I heard al-ʿAlāʾ relate this with the same chain, but they both added: “And no man pardons an injustice done to him except that Allāh increases him thereby in honour.”"
"2439","###\n Narrated to us by 'Isa bin Ibrahim, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, on the authority of Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, who said: It was narrated to me by Sa'id bin al-Musayyab that he heard Abu Huraira (RA) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The best charity is that which is given from surplus wealth, and begin with those whom you are responsible for.\" It was also reported to us by Muhammad bin 'Uzayr,"
"2440","###\n Narrated to us Al-Hasan bin Muhammad Al-Za'farani, who said: Narrated to us 'Ubaidah bin Humaid, who said: Abu Az-Zara' narrated to me, from Abu Al-Ahwas, from his father Malik bin Nadlah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There are three types of hands: The Hand of Allah, which is the highest; the Hand of the Giver, which is next to it; and the Hand of the Beggar, which is the"
"2441","Narrated to us Al-Dawraqī Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm: Narrated to us ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs, who said: I heard Ibn Isḥāq mentioning, and narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ: Narrated to us Yazīd—meaning Ibn Hārūn—who informed us Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah, from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (RA), who said:\n\nA man came to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) with an egg-sized piece of gold that he had obtained from some mines. Al-Dawraqī said: \"Like an egg of gold that he had obtained from some mines.\" They both said: The man said, \"O Messenger of Allāh, take this from me as charity. By Allāh, I do not possess anything else this morning.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) turned away from him.\n\nThen the man came to him from his right side and said the same thing, and the Prophet (ﷺ) turned away from him.\n\nThen he came to him from his left side and said the same thing, and the Prophet (ﷺ) turned away from him.\n\nThen he came to him a fourth time and said the same thing. The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Bring it here,\" in an angry tone. So he threw it at him with such force that had it struck him, it would have wounded him or broken his bones. Then the Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"One of you comes with all his wealth and gives it in charity, then begs from people. Charity should only come from surplus wealth.\"\n\nThis is the narration of Ibn Rāfiʿ. Al-Dawraqī added: \"Take your wealth back from us; we have no need for it.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2442","**\nNarrated to me Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, that 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to them, he said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me, from his father, that he said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when his repentance was accepted:\n\n\"O Messenger of Allah, I will dispose of all my wealth as charity for Allah and His Messenger.\"\n\nThereupon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: *\"Keep some of your wealth, for that is better for you.\"*\n\nYunus also informed us, 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us with the same chain."
"2443","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Safwan ibn ‘Isa, narrated to us Ibn ‘Ajlān, on the authority of Ibn ‘Ajlān, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, *\"A single dirham outpaces a hundred thousand dirhams.\"*\nThey asked, *\"O Messenger of Allāh, how can a single dirham outpace a hundred thousand dirhams?\"*\nHe (ﷺ) replied, *\"A man had two dirhams. He took one of them and gave it in charity. Another man had abundant wealth, so he took a hundred thousand dirhams from his holdings and gave it in charity.\"*\n\n---"
"2444","Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī (RA): Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, on the authority of al-Layth, that Abū al-Zubayr informed him.\n\nAnd narrated to us ‘Amr ibn ‘Alī: Narrated to us Abū al-Walīd: Narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa‘d, on the authority of Abū al-Zubayr, from Yaḥyā ibn Ja‘dah, from Abū Hurayrah (RA), that he said:\n“O Messenger of Allāh, which charity is best?”\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n“The effort of the one with little wealth, and begin with those under your care.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2445","###\n Ahmad bin Ma'ee narrated to us, saying: Ibn 'Ulayyah informed us, saying: Ayyub informed us, on the authority of Abu az-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"When one of you is in need (or poor), let him begin with himself. If there is a surplus, then (he should give) to his dependents. If there is still a surplus, then (he should give) to his relatives or those with whom he"
"2446","###\n Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Zayd ibn Akhzam at-Ta'i narrated to us, saying: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, and Israel narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Hubshi ibn Junadah as-Saluli, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever asks (for charity) while he has enough to suffice him, he is only eating embers.\" Zayd ibn Akhzam added in his narration: \"Whoever asks without being in need"
"2447","###\n Narrated to us Zakariyya bin Yahya bin Aban, who said: Narrated to us Abdullah bin Yusuf, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Ar-Rijal, on the authority of Umara bin Ghaziyya, on the authority of Abdur-Rahman bin Abi Sa'id Al-Khudri, on the authority of his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever asks (for charity) while he possesses the value of an Uqiyyah (of silver"
"2448","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from Dawud ibn Shabur, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Whoever asks (for something) while he has forty dirhams, then he is persistent in begging, and he is like one who scatters (i.e., throws away) the act of begging.\"\n\nHe meant: like one who scatters (i.e., throws away) the act of begging.\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2449","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb, who said: Abu Usama narrated to us, from Hisham bin Urwa, from Abdur-Rahman bin Al-Qasim, from his father, from Aisha (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered upon me, and food was brought which did not contain any meat. He said, \"Did I not see that you have a cooking pot?\" I said, \"Yes, that is meat which Barirah gave to me as charity"
"2450","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa’di, narrated to us Bashir ibn Maymun, narrated to us Mujahid ibn Jabr, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, **\"There is no charity more excellent than a charity given to a slave when he is under the possession of an evil master—provided the report is established.\"**"
"2451","Narrated to us ‘Īsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ghāfiqī,\nNarrated to us Ibn Wahb,\nOn the authority of al-Layth,\nThat Abū’l-Zubayr narrated to him.\n\nAnd narrated to us ‘Amr ibn ‘Alī,\nNarrated to us Abū’l-Walīd,\nNarrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa‘d,\nOn the authority of Abū’l-Zubayr,\nOn the authority of Yaḥyā ibn Ja‘dah,\nOn the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA) that he said:\n\n“O Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), which charity is best?”\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n“Expending from what one possesses when in straitened circumstances, and begin with those whom you maintain.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2452","###\n Narrated to us Ahmad bin Mani', who said: Narrated to us Ibn 'Uyaynah, who said: He informed us Ayyub, on the authority of Abu az-Zubayr, on the authority of Jabir, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"When one of you is in need, let him begin with himself. If there is a surplus, then (spend it) upon his dependents. If there is still a surplus, then (spend it) upon his relatives or those with whom he has"
"2453","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walid, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far, narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Abu Mas'ud (RA), who said:\n\"We used to carry (the burden of carrying the charity), and a man would come with a large charity, so it would be said, 'He is showing off.' And a man would come with half a *sa'* (of charity), so it would be said, 'Indeed, Allah is free from needing this.' Then this verse was revealed:\n*'Those who criticize the volunteering believers among the men and women concerning the charities they give, and those who find nothing to spend except their own hard-earned provision, so they ridicule them...'* [Quran 9:79]\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2454","**\nNarrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of 'Umarah—he is Ibn al-Qa'qa'—on the authority of Abu Zur'ah, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nA man came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, which charity is greatest?\"\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"That you give charity when you are healthy, stingy, fearing poverty, and hoping for longevity—*not* until the death-rattle reaches the throat, when you say, 'So-and-so shall have such-and-such, and so-and-so shall have such-and-such,' while it has already belonged to so-and-so.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: This wording \"while it has already belonged to so-and-so\" is of the same kind as saying, \"When the time has drawn near, it is permissible to say, 'The time has already come,' and the time has entered when it has drawn near, and it has already belonged to so-and-so,\" even if it has not yet entered. For the Prophet (ﷺ) intended by his words, \"while it has already belonged to so-and-so,\" that the descent of death upon a person has drawn near when the death-rattle reaches the throat, so the wealth passes to someone else—not that the wealth passes to someone else before the soul is seized. And of this kind is the saying of Abu Bakr (RA): \"Indeed, it is the day that is the heir.\"\n\n---"
"2455","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, narrated to us Bahz ibn Asad, narrated to us Hammam, narrated to us Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah, on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:\n\nWhen the verse was revealed: *\"You will never attain righteousness until you spend from what you love\"* (Quran 3:92), Abu Talhah (RA) came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was on the pulpit and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, I do not have any land more beloved to me than my land of Bayraha.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said: *\"Bayraha is good in its yield, or good in its return\"*—the narrator (Hammam) was uncertain about the wording.\n\nAbu Talhah (RA) said, \"Then I will dedicate it to Allah.\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said: *\"Then give it in charity to your relatives.\"*\n\nSo Abu Talhah (RA) divided it among his relatives as orchards—a sound narration.\n\nHumaid ibn Anas (RA) said: I have narrated this elsewhere."
"2456","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja‘far, narrated to us Shu‘bah, from Mansur, from Rib‘i ibn Hirash, from Zayd ibn Thabyan, who raised it to Abu Dharr, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"Three types of people Allah loves, and three types of people Allah hates.\n\nAs for those whom Allah loves:\n\n1. A man who comes to a people and asks them by Allah, not asking them by any kinship between them and him. So a man follows behind them and gives him secretly, a gift whose charity is known only to Allah and the one who gave it.\n2. A people who travel during their night until sleep becomes more beloved to them than anything else, so they stop and lay their heads down. Then a man stands up, flattering me and reciting My verses.\n3. A man who is in a military expedition, meets the enemy, and they are defeated. He then advances with his chest until he is killed or victory is granted to him.\n\nAs for the three whom Allah hates:\n\n1. An old man who commits adultery.\n2. A poor man who is arrogant.\n3. A rich man who is oppressive.\""
"2457","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Mukharrimi, narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, on the authority of al-A'mash, on the authority of Ibn Buraydah, on the authority of his father (Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"No man gives charity from his wealth until seventy devils are removed from it.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2458","###\n Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, narrated to us Khalid bin Al-Harith, narrated to us Humaid, who said: Anas (RA) said, \"The following verse was revealed: 'You will not attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love.' (Surah Al-Imran, 3:92).\" He also mentioned the verse: \"Who is it that would loan Allah a goodly loan?\" (Surah Al-Baqarah, 2:245). Abu Talha ("
"2459","Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Sahl ibn Yusuf, from Humaid, from Anas (RA), who said:\nWhen this verse was revealed—he mentioned something similar from the Prophet (ﷺ).\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2460","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, narrated to us Bahz, narrated to us Hammad, from Thabit, from Anas (RA), who said:\n\nWhen this verse was revealed: *\"Never will you attain the good [al-Birr] until you spend [in charity] from that which you love\"* (Qur'an 3:92), Abu Talhah (RA) said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), I see that our Lord is asking us for our wealth. I bear witness before you that I have made my land at Bayraha' for Allah.\"\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Make it for your relatives.\"\n\nSo he made it for Hassan ibn Thabit and Ubayy ibn Ka'b (RA).\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2461","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr as-Sa'di, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us by Amr bin Abi 'Amr, the freed slave of Al-Muttalib, from Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri, from Abu Hurairah (RA): That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) once finished the Fajr prayer, then came to the women in the mosque and stood over them, saying: \"O assembly of women!"
"2462","**\nAbu Bakr (RA) said in the narration of Iyad ibn Abdullah, from Abu Sa'id (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said to her:\n*\"Ibn Mas'ud (RA) has spoken the truth. Your husband and your child are more entitled to your voluntary charity than others.\"*\n\nThis narration indicates that the children of Ibn Mas'ud (RA) mentioned in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah (RA) — *\"and upon his children\"* — were the children of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (RA) from Zaynab.\n\n**Narrated to us** Yahya, **on the authority of** Abu Sa'id Muhammad ibn Yahya and Zakariya ibn Yahya ibn Aban (RA), both said:\n**We were informed by** Ibn Abi Maryam, **who informed us** Muhammad ibn Ja'far, **who informed me** Zayd — and he is the son of Aslam — **on the authority of** Iyad ibn Abdullah, **on the authority of** Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA).\n\n---"
"2463","###\n Abdullah bin Sa'id al-Ashaj narrated to us, saying: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, saying: Al-A'mash narrated to us, from Shaqiq, from Amr bin al-Harith, from Zaynab, the wife of Abdullah bin Mas'ud (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to give charity, saying: \"O women, give charity, even if it is from your jewelry.\" She said: I was supporting Abdullah and my daughters who were in my care."
"2464","###\n Narrated to us Ali bin Al-Mundhir, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Fudail, who said: Narrated to us Al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abu Ubaidah, from Amr bin Al-Harith bin Al-Mustaliq, from Zainab, the wife of Abdullah bin Mas'ud (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) came to us while we were in the mosque, and he said: \"O women, give charity, even if it is from your jewelry.\" Then he"
"2465","Narrated to us Musa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi: narrated to us Abu Usamah, from Husayn (who is al-Mu'allim), from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: A man gave a piece of land as charity to his son, but then it was returned to him as inheritance. He mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said to him, \"Your reward is due, and your property has returned to you.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2466","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn 'Asakir, narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA) who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered that from every orchard, a *qinw* (a small bunch of dates) be set aside for the Masjid before the dates were harvested.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2467","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, narrated to us 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, on the authority of Salih ibn Abi 'Arīb, on the authority of Kathīr ibn Murrah, on the authority of 'Awf ibn Mālik al-Ashja'ī, who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) entered the mosque, and there was a hanging basket, some of which was dried dates (hashaf). He (ﷺ) was carrying a staff, so he poked the basket with his staff and said:\n\"If its Owner (i.e., Allāh) had willed, He could have made this charity consist of something better than this. Indeed, the owner of this charity will eat the dried dates on the Day of Resurrection.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2468","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Mukhrimi: Narrated to us Waki' and 'Abd al-Rahman, they said: Narrated to us Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"For the beggar there is a right, even if he comes on a horse.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2469","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Sa‘id al-Darimi: narrated to us Suhayl ibn Bakkar: narrated to us Hammād ibn Salamah, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, from Wāsi‘ ibn Ḥabbān, from Jābir ibn ‘Abd Allāh (RA) who said:\n“The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) granted a concession in the case of ‘arāyā (barter sales of fresh dates for dried dates) for one wasaq, two wasqs, three wasqs, and four wasqs. He said: for every ten wasqs of good quality, a qinw (a basket) is to be set aside for the poor in the mosque.”\nI heard al-Dārimī say: qinw and qan‘ are one and the same.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2470","Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"When you pay the Zakah of your wealth, you have removed its evil from you.\""
"2471","**\nNarrated to us ‘Ali ibn Khashram, who said: ‘Abdullah ibn Wahb informed us, on the authority of ‘Amr ibn al-Harith, from Darraj Abu al-Samh, from Ibn Hujayrah al-Khawlani, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"If you have paid the zakah due on your wealth, then you have fulfilled what is upon you. And whoever gathers wealth unlawfully, then gives charity from it, he will have no reward for it, and the burden of his sin will remain upon him.\"\n\nNarrated to us ‘Isa ibn Ibrahim, who said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me, from Darraj Abu al-Samh, with the wording:\n\n\"If you have paid the zakah due on your wealth...\"\n\n---"
"2472","###\n Narrated to us by Abdullah bin Saeed Al-Ashaj, who said: Abu Khalid Al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Mansur bin Hassan. And it was also narrated to us by Harun bin Ishaq, who said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur bin Hayyan, from Ibn Bujayd, from his grandmother, who said: I said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), a beggar comes to me, and I have nothing to give him.\" He"
"2473","###\n Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who said: Narrated to us Shu'ayb, who said: Narrated to us Al-Layth, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bujayd, the brother of Ibn Harithah, who said: My grandmother, Umm Bujayd, narrated to me. She was among those who had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). She said: \"I said to"
"2474","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Musa Muhammad bin al-Muthanna, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Walid bin Muslim, who said: Narrated to me by Al-Awza'i. [In another chain]: Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Miskin al-Yamami, who said: Narrated to us by Bishr bin Bakr, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Awza'i, who said: Narrated to me by Abu Ja'far Muhammad bin Ali, who said: I heard from Sa"
"2475","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-'Ala' ibn Kurayb, narrated to us Ibn al-Mubarak, from al-Awza'i who said: I heard Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn al-Husayn mention Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"One who revokes his voluntary charity is like a dog that vomits, then returns to eat its vomit.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2476","Narrated to us by Umar bin Hafss, who said: Narrated to us by Abd al-Majid bin Abd al-Aziz bin Abi Rawwad, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Jurayj, from Musa bin Uqba, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that the Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out to perform the prayer on the day of Eid al-Fitr."
"2477","###\n Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, saying: Al-A'mash narrated to us, from Muslim, who is the son of Subayh, from 'Abdur-Rahman ibn Hilal al-'Absi, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed us and urged us to give charity. Some people were slow to respond, until anger could be seen on"
"2478","###\n Narrated to us by Abd al-Rahman bin Bishr bin al-Hakam, who said: Narrated to us by Abd al-Razzaq, who said: He informed us by Ma'mar, from Yahya bin Abi Kathir, from Zayd bin Salam, from Abdullah bin Zayd bin al-Azraq, from Uqbah bin Amir al-Juhani (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There are two types of jealousy (ghayrah): one of which"
"2479","###\n Narrated to us by Abu Hashim, Ziyad ibn Ayub, who narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Harun, who said: Narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: Allah, Mighty and Sublime, says: \"My servant asked me for a loan, but he did not lend to Me; and my servant reviled Me, yet he does not know what"
"2480","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, who informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Whoever spends a pair of his wealth in the way of Allah, the servants of Paradise will call him. For Paradise has doors. Whoever is from the people of Prayer will be called from the door of Prayer. Whoever is from the people of Charity will be called from the door of Charity. Whoever is from the people of Jihad will be called from the door of Jihad. And whoever is from the people of Fasting will be called from the door of al-Rayyan.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, \"O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, no one will be in need of any of these doors. Will anyone be called from all of them?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"Yes, and I hope that you will be among them.\""
"2481","###\n Sa'id bin Abdur-Rahman Al-Makhzumi narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, on the authority of Ibn Ajlan, on the authority of Iyad bin Abdullah bin Sa'd bin Abi Sarh, that Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (RA) mentioned: A man came on the day of Jumu'ah while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was delivering the sermon. The man was in a wretched state of dress. So, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
"2482","###\n Narrated to us 'Utbah ibn 'Abdullah, who said: We were informed by 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak, who said: We were informed by Haywah ibn Shurayh, who said: Al-Walid ibn Abi al-Walid, Abu 'Uthman, narrated to me that 'Uqbah ibn Muslim told him that Shufayy told him: I entered Madinah and saw a man around whom people had gathered. I asked, \"Who is this?\""
"2483","Narrated to us Abu Musa Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna, narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adiy, on the authority of Ibn 'Awn, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA) that:\n\n'Umar (RA) acquired land in Khaybar, so he came to the Prophet (ﷺ) to seek his counsel regarding it. He said, \"I have acquired land in Khaybar, and I have never acquired wealth more precious to me than it. What do you command me to do with it?\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"If you wish, you may retain its principal (i.e., the land itself) and give its charity (i.e., its usufruct).\"\n\nSo 'Umar (RA) gave it in charity on the condition that it would not be sold, nor given as a gift, nor inherited. He dedicated it to the poor, the relatives, the slaves seeking to free themselves, in the way of Allah, the wayfarer, and the weak.\n\nThere is no harm if the one in charge of it eats from it in a lawful manner or feeds a friend without hoarding wealth from it.\n\nIbn 'Awn said: So I narrated this to Muhammad (ibn Sirin), and he said: \"Not hoarding wealth.\" Ibn 'Awn said: And someone who read the document (of the hadith) said: \"Not accumulating wealth.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: And 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar al-'Umari narrated to us on the authority of Nafi', who narrated to them, that he heard Ibn 'Umar (RA) say:\n\n\"The first charity given in Islam was the charity of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA). And 'Umar (RA) said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): 'I have wealth, and I wish to give it in charity.' So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Retain its principal and make its fruit (i.e., its produce) lawful for others.'\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2484","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la al-Sana‘ani, narrated to us Bushr (meaning Ibn al-Mufaddal), narrated to us Ibn ‘Awn, and narrated to us al-Za‘farani, narrated to us Mu‘adh ibn Mu‘adh, from Ibn ‘Awn.\n\nAl-Za‘farani said: Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Yusuf, narrated to us Ibn ‘Awn.\n\nAlso, narrated to us al-Za‘farani, narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, narrated to us Ibn ‘Awn. They mentioned the hadith in its entirety.\n\nAl-Sana‘ani did not mention Ibn al-Sabil, and he said: “Not possessing wealth in it.”\n\nHe said: So Muhammad said: “Not acquiring it.”\n\nHe did not mention Ibn ‘Awn’s recitation of the Book."
"2485","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli, narrated to us Yazid ibn Zuray', narrated to us Ibn 'Awn, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said:\nUmar (RA) acquired some land in Khaybar. He came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and mentioned the matter in its entirety, saying:\n\"O Messenger of Allah, I have acquired land in Khaybar that is more valuable than any I have ever possessed. What do you command me to do with it?\"\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) replied:\n\"If you wish, you may retain the principal (i.e., the land itself) and give its usufruct in charity.\"\nSo Umar (RA) gave it in charity on the condition that it would not be sold, nor given as a gift, nor inherited. He allocated it for the poor, the needy, the emancipation of slaves, in the way of Allah, for travelers, and for the son of the way. There is no harm if the one in charge of it eats from it in a reasonable manner, or feeds a friend without hoarding wealth from it.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2486","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu Ghassan Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Kinani, who said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to me, from 'Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (RA) that 'Umar (RA) sought the counsel of the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding his charity, and he (ﷺ) said: \"Keep the principal intact and dedicate its produce (for charity).\"\n\n'Abd Allah (ibn 'Umar) said: So 'Umar (RA) dedicated it by keeping the principal intact and dedicating its produce to the needy, the deprived, the wayfarer, in the cause of Allah, for freeing slaves, and for the poor. He also used part of it for his own sustenance and to feed others, without making it resemble a trade transaction.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2487","###\n Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Abdullah ibn Idris said: I heard Husayn mentioning, on the authority of Umar ibn Jawan, from Al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, who mentioned a long hadith regarding the killing of Uthman (RA). He said: \"Then Ali, Az-Zubayr, Talhah, Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, and I were present when Uthman (RA) came and said: 'I call"
"2488","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Aziz Al-Ayli, who said: Salamah narrated to us, on the authority of Uqail, who said: Ibn Shihab said: \"Abdur-Rahman bin Hurmuz informed me that he heard Abu Hurairah (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, my heirs shall not distribute anything from what I leave behind; what I leave behind is charity (Sadaqah).' This charity was in the possession"
"2489","###\n Narrated to us Zayd bin Sinan, who narrated to us Husayn bin Al-Hasan Al-Ashqar, who narrated to us Zuhayr, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr bin Al-Harith, from Juwayriyah (RA), who said: \"By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not leave behind at the time of his death a dinar, nor a dirham, nor a male slave, nor a female slave, except for his mule, his weapons"
"2490","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn 'Atiyyah, narrated to us al-Walid ibn Muslim, narrated to us Marzuq ibn al-Hudhayl, who informed us al-Zuhri, who narrated to me Abu 'Abd Allah al-Agharr, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Among what follows the believer after his death from his deeds and good actions are:\n- Knowledge which he learned and then spread,\n- A righteous child whom he left behind,\n- A mosque which he built,\n- A house built for the son of the road (traveler),\n- A river which he dug,\n- Or charity which he extracted from his wealth during his health and life.\n\nAll of these will reach him after his death.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \""
"2491","Narrated to us Isma'il ibn Abi Isra'il al-Mula'i in Ramla, narrated to us 'Amr ibn 'Uthman and 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far, they said: Narrated to us 'Ubaydullah (who is the son of 'Amr), from Zayd (who is the son of Abi Unaysa), from Abu Ishaq, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, who said:\n\nWhen 'Uthman (RA) was besieged, he ascended to them from the top of his house, then he said, \"I remind you by Allah, do you know that the well of Rumah was such that no one could drink from it except by paying a price? So I purchased it with my own wealth and made it available for the rich, the poor, and the wayfarer?\"\n\nThey said, \"Yes.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2492","Narrated to us Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥalabī, narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Abī al-Ḥajjāj, narrated to us al-Jurayrī in its entirety, narrated to me al-Qushayrī, who said:\n\nI was present at the house on the day when ʿUthmān (RA) was struck and he came out to us. He said:\n\n\"O people! I adjure you by Allāh and Islām: Do you know that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) came to Madīnah and there was no sweet-tasting well in it except the well of Rūmah?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) then said:\n\n\"Who will purchase Rūmah so that he may place his bucket in it like the buckets of the Muslims, with better reward for him from it than Paradise?\"\n\nThey said: \"Yes, O Allāh!\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"So I purchased it from my own wealth, yet you prevent me from drinking from it until I drink from the water of the sea.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2493","Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqy: narrated to us al-Mu'tamir: narrated to me my father: narrated to us Abu Nadrah: on the authority of Abu Sa'id, the freed slave of Abu Usayd al-Ansari, who said:\nUthman ibn Affan (RA) ascended the pulpit and addressed the people, saying, \"I adjure you by Allah, do you know that I purchased the well of Rumah from my own wealth, from which sweet water is drawn, and I made its watering rights equal to the watering rights of a Muslim?\" They said, \"Yes.\" He then said, \"Then why do you prevent me from drinking from it until I break my fast with the water of the sea?\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2494","###\n Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr as-Sa'di, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il (meaning Ibn Ja'far), who said: Narrated to us by Al-Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"When a person dies, his deeds come to an end except for three: ongoing charity (Sadaqah Jariyah), knowledge from which benefit is derived, or a righteous child who prays for him.\""
"2495","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abbad al-Nasa'i in Baghdad, narrated to us Muhammad — meaning Ibn Yazid ibn Sinan al-Rahawi — who informed us Yazid — meaning his father — who narrated to us Zayd ibn Abi Unaysa, from Fuhayl ibn Sulayman, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qatada, from his father (Abi Qatada al-Ansari), who said:\n\nI heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n\"The best things that a person leaves behind after his death are three:\n1. A righteous child who supplicates for him and his supplication reaches him,\n2. A charity that continues to flow and its reward reaches him, or\n3. Knowledge that is acted upon after him.\"\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2496","**\nNarrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Abū Muʿāwiyah, on the authority of Shuʿbah, on the authority of Qatādah, on the authority of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, on the authority of Saʿd (RA), who said:\n\"I said: 'O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), indeed my mother has died. May I give charity on her behalf?'\nHe said: 'Yes.'\nI said: 'Which charity is best?'\nHe said: 'Providing water to drink.'\""
"2497","Narrated to us Abu ‘Ammar: Narrated to us Waki‘ ibn al-Jarrah, on the authority of Hisham, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Sa‘id ibn al-Musayyib, on the authority of Sa‘d ibn ‘Ubadah (RA), who said:\nI said, “O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), which charity is best?”\nHe (ﷺ) replied, “Providing water to drink.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2498","Narrated to us by Ali bin Hujr, who said: Narrated to us by Isma'il bin Ja'far, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Ala', from his father, from Abu Hurairah (RA), that a man said to the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Indeed, my father has passed away, leaving behind wealth, but he did not make a will. If I give charity on his behalf, will it expiate his sins?\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Yes.\""
"2499","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb, who said: Narrated to us Abu Usama. And narrated to us Yusuf bin Musa, who said: Narrated to us Jarir; both narrated from Hisham bin Urwa, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said: A man said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), my mother passed away suddenly, and I believe that if she had been able to speak, she would have made a will to give charity"
"2500","###\n Narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi, who said: Narrated to us Rawh ibn 'Ubada, who said: Narrated to us Malik ibn Anas, from Sa'id ibn 'Amr ibn Shurahbil ibn Sa'id ibn Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah went out with the Prophet (ﷺ) in one of his military expeditions. During his absence, his mother passed"
"2501","###\n Abdullah ibn Ishaq al-Jawhari narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ya'la ibn Hakim informed me, that Ikrima, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas, informed him, saying: Ibn Abbas informed us that Sa'd ibn Ubadah, the brother of Banu Sa'idah, said: \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), my mother has passed away while I was absent. Will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?\" He ("
"2502","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Qazzaz: narrated to us Abu 'Asim, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, from Ya'la, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA) that a man said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), \"Indeed my mother has passed away. Would it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?\" And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: O Messenger of Allah, indeed my mother has passed away, and he said: \"Indeed I have a date-palm orchard (mukhraf), meaning an orchard.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2503","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Al-Mubarak Al-Mukharimi, who said: Narrated to us Waki', on the authority of Al-A'mash, on the authority of Abu Ishaq, on the authority of Kudeir Ad-Dabbi, who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, guide me to a deed that will admit me into Paradise.\" He (ﷺ) said, \"Speak the truth and give generously.\" The man said, \"O"
"2504","###\n Narrated to us Abu Musa, Muhammad bin Al-Muthanna, who said: Narrated to us Husayn bin Al-Hasan, who said: Narrated to us Kahmas bin Al-Hasan, from Ibn Buraydah, from Yahya bin Ya'mar, who said: \"Humayd bin Abdur-Rahman and I set out as pilgrims and performers of Umrah. We said, 'Let us go to one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ).' So we met Abdullah bin Umar. He said"
"2505","Narrated to us Abu'l-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-'Ijli: Narrated to us Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal: Narrated to us 'Asim — and he is the son of Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab — who said: I heard my father narrating from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\"Indeed, Islam has been built upon five [pillars]: testifying that there is no god worthy of worship except Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakah, performing the Hajj of the House, and fasting the month of Ramadan.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2506","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Qaz'ah ibn 'Ubayd with an unusual report, narrated to us Sufyan ibn Habib, narrated to us Humaid al-Tawil, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, *\"Benefit from this House, for it has been destroyed twice, and it will be raised on the third time.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: His (ﷺ) statement *\"it will be raised on the third time\"* means after the third destruction, for raising what has already been destroyed is impossible, since if a house is destroyed, it no longer bears the name of a house unless there is some structure remaining.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2507","Narrated to us Abu Qudāmah and Abū Mūsā Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā (RA), both said: Narrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: Narrated to us Abān ibn Yazīd, from Qatādah.\n\nAnd (also) narrated to us Ibrāhīm ibn Bisṭām al-Zaʿfaranī, who said: Narrated to us Abū Dāwūd, who said: Narrated to us ʿImrān—he is al-Qaṭṭān—from Qatādah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī ʿUtbah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (RA), that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"This House will certainly be visited for Hajj, and ʿUmrah will certainly be performed after the emergence of Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj.\"*\n\nAbū Qudāmah (RA) added: *\"after the emergence of Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj.\"*\nAbū Mūsā (RA) said: *\"The House will certainly be visited for Hajj.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2508","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Ubaydullah bin Musa, who said: He informed us Ar-Rabi' bin Muslim, from Muhammad bin Ziyad, from Abu Huraira (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed the people and said, \"Indeed, Allah has made Hajj obligatory upon you.\" A man said, \"O Messenger of Allah, (is it) every year?\" The Prophet (ﷺ) remained silent until the man repeated his question three times."
"2509","Abu Bakr (RA) said: \"The narration of Abu Las al-Khuza'i, which I dictated, is found in the Book of Zakat.\""
"2510","Abu Bakr said: \"The narration of Umm Ma'qil, I have already dictated it in the Book of Charity (Sadaqat) as well.\""
"2511","Narrated to us by Ali ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi and Ibrahim ibn Munqidh ibn Abdullah al-Khawlan, who said: Narrated to us by Ibn Wahb, from Makhrama, from his father, who said: I heard Suhail ibn Abi Salih say: I heard my father say: I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The envoys (delegates) of Allah are three: the one who fights in His cause (the Gh"
"2512","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa‘id al-Ashajj, narrated to us Abu Khalid who said: And it was conveyed to us ‘Amr ibn Qays, on the authority of ‘Asim, from Shaqiq, from ‘Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n“Follow one Hajj with another ‘Umrah, for they remove poverty and sins just as the bellows removes the filth from iron, gold and silver. And there is no reward for an accepted Hajj except Paradise.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2513","Narrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbār ibn al-‘Alā’ who said: Sufyān narrated to me, he said: Sammayy informed me.\n\nAlso narrated to us al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Za‘farānī who said: Ibn ‘Uyaynah narrated to us, from Sammayy.\n\nAlso narrated to us ‘Alī ibn al-Mundhir who said: ‘Abd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, from ‘Ubayd Allāh, from Sammayy, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“The minor pilgrimage (ʿUmrah) to the next minor pilgrimage expiates for what is between them, and the accepted pilgrimage (Ḥajj al-Mabrūr) has no reward except Paradise.”\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2514","Narrated to us Al-Husayn ibn Hurayth Abu 'Ammar, narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn 'Iyad.\n\nAlso narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi and Yusuf ibn Musa, they said: narrated to us Jarir, all of them from Mansur, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\n\"Whoever performs Hajj and does not commit Rafath (i.e., sexual relations) nor commits Fusuq (i.e., sins), he will return (free from sins) as he was on the day his mother gave birth to him.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2515","Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Muslim, narrated to us Abu ‘Asim, who said: Khaywah ibn Shurayh informed us, who said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib informed me, on the authority of Abu Shimasah, who said:\nWe were present with ‘Amr ibn al-‘As while he was in the throes of death. He wept for a long time and then said:\nWhen Allah placed Islam in my heart, I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, “O Messenger of Allah, extend your right hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you.”\nHe (ﷺ) extended his hand, but I drew back my hand and said, “I wish to stipulate a condition.”\nHe (ﷺ) asked, “What do you wish to stipulate?”\nI replied, “That I be forgiven.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “Do you not know, O ‘Amr, that Islam demolishes all that came before it, that the Hijrah demolishes all that came before it, and that the Hajj demolishes all that came before it?”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2516","Narrated to us Ibrahim bin Sa'id al-Jawhari, he said: Narrated to us Abu Ahmad Husayn bin Muhammad, from Shurayk, from Mansur, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"O Allah, forgive the pilgrims and those for whom the pilgrim seeks forgiveness.\""
"2517","Narrated to us by Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us, that Yunus informed him from Ibn Shihab, who said: \"Abdur-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me from his father, who used to say: 'The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) rarely set out on a journey, whether for Jihad or otherwise, except on Thursday.'\""
"2518","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us, who said: Yunus ibn Yazid informed me, who said: Ibn Shihab said: 'Urwah said: 'A'ishah (RA) said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the house of Abu Bakr (RA) and sought permission to enter. Abu Bakr (RA) permitted him. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then said, \"Indeed, I have been permitted to leave (for migration).\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said, \"May my father be sacrificed for you, O Messenger of Allah!\"\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) replied, \"Yes.\"\n\n'A'ishah (RA) further said: So I prepared their provisions in haste and made a travel bag for them. Asma bint Abu Bakr (RA) cut a piece from her waistband and used it to tie the bag. Because of this, she came to be called *Dhat al-Nitaq* (the one with the waistband).\n\n---"
"2519","Narrated to us by Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us. Also, Salam narrated to us, and Waki' narrated to us. Also, Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashaj narrated to us, and Ibn Numayr narrated to us. Also, Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Kindi narrated to us, and Yahya (meaning Ibn Abi Zaidah) narrated to us; all of them from al-A'mash. Abu"
"2520","Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, who said: 'Isa informed us, from al-A'mash, similar to the hadith of Abu Za'idah.\n\nNarrated to us al-Ashajj, who said: Abu Khalid narrated to us, who said: al-A'mash narrated to us, and he mentioned a hadith similar to it.\n\n**COMMENTARY:**\nThis hadith is narrated through multiple chains, all of which trace back to al-A'mash (Sulayman ibn Mihran). The narration emphasizes the prohibition of a woman traveling without a *mahram* (unmarriageable male relative) or her husband. The repetition of the chain serves to strengthen the authenticity of the ruling.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2521","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Yahya bin Sa'id, who narrated to us from Ubaydullah bin Umar, who informed me from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade a woman from traveling for three days unless she is accompanied by a Mahram. Abu Bakr (the narrator/compiler) said: I have already recorded this wording in the reports in the book \"Al-Kabir.\" The narration of Ibn Umar is abbreviated and not fully detailed; it"
"2522","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us Sadaqah – meaning Ibn Khalid – on the authority of Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, on the authority of Qaz’ah ibn Yahya, on the authority of ‘Abdullah ibn ‘Amr ibn al-‘As (RA), on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said:\n\n“A woman must not travel for two days unless she is accompanied by her husband or a mahram.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2523","Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Muslim and Yahya ibn Hakim—both said: Narrated to us Bushr ibn ‘Umar, who said: Mālik narrated to us, from Sa‘īd ibn Abī Sa‘īd, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said:\n\n“It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allāh and the Last Day to travel a day and a night’s distance except with a mahram.”\n\nAbū Bakr (ibn Abī Khaythamah) said: “To my knowledge, none of Mālik’s companions transmitted this report with the wording ‘from his father’ except Bushr ibn ‘Umar. This hadith is in al-Muwatṭā’ from Sa‘īd, from Abū Hurayrah.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2524","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la and ‘Isa ibn Ibrahim. ‘Isa said: Narrated to us, and Yunus said: ‘Abd al-Razzaq informed us, saying: Mālik informed me, from Sa‘īd, from Abū Hurayrah, that Abū Bakr said in the report: It is *ṣaḥīḥ* from his father, from Abū Hurayrah. Al-Layth ibn Sa‘d, Ibn ‘Ajlān and Ibn Abī Dhī’b narrated it from Sa‘īd, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah. I have already produced it in my *al-Kabīr*."
"2525","**\nNarrated to us Bundar, narrated to us Abu Hisham al-Makhzumi, narrated to us Wuhayb, on the authority of Ibn Ajlan, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"A woman must not travel a distance of one night's journey except with a mahram.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: I have thoroughly examined these reports in my book al-Kabir.\n\n---"
"2526","Yusuf ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us, from Sufyan. And Abu al-Bishr al-Wasiti narrated to us, saying: Khalid narrated to us, from Suhayl, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"A woman must not travel the distance of a *barid* (a stage) except with a *Mahram* (a male guardian whom she cannot"
"2527","Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani and Ahmad bin al-Miqdam narrated to us, saying: We were narrated by Bishr, who is Ibn al-Mufaddal, who narrated from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"It is not permissible for a woman to travel for three days unless she is accompanied by a Mahram (a male guardian whom she cannot marry).\""
"2528","###\n Narrated to us Ahmad bin Abdur-Rahman bin Wahb, my uncle narrated to us, Amr bin Al-Harith informed me, from Bukair bin Abdullah Al-Ashajj, that Al-Hasan bin Abi Rafi' narrated to him from his father, Abi Rafi', who said: \"I was once with a military expedition. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me: 'Go and bring me Maymuna.' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I am with the expedition.'"
"2529","Narrated to us Abu `Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth: narrated to us Sufyan, from `Amr—he is Ibn Dinar—from Abu Ma`bad, on the authority of Ibn `Abbas (RA), who said:\nI heard the Prophet (ﷺ) delivering a sermon in which he said:\n*\"No man should be alone with a woman unless there is a *mahram* with her.\"*\nThen a man stood up and said:\n*\"O Messenger of Allah, I have enrolled myself for such-and-such expedition, but my wife has set out for Hajj.\"*\nHe (ﷺ) said:\n*\"Go forth and perform Hajj with your wife.\"*\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2530","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar, narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Amr, who said: I heard Abu Ma'bad say: I heard Ibn 'Abbas say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), while he was on the pulpit delivering a sermon, say: (and he mentioned a hadith similar to it) and he said: \"So go and perform Hajj with your wife.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2531","Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli: Narrated to us al-Walid — and he is Ibn Muslim —: Narrated to us Hanẓalah, that he heard al-Qāsim say:\n\nI was with Ibn 'Umar when a man came to him and said, \"I intend to travel.\"\n\nSo 'Abdullah said, \"Wait until I bid you farewell as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to bid us farewell:\n\n*'Bid Allah to take charge of your religion, your trusts, and the conclusions of your deeds.'\"*[1]\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2532","Narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy, narrated to us Sayyar ibn Hatim, on the authority of Ja'far ibn Sulayman, on the authority of Thabit, on the authority of Anas (RA), who said:\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, I intend to travel, so furnish me (with provision).\"\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"May Allah furnish you with taqwa (God-consciousness).\"\nThe man said, \"Give me more.\"\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"And may He forgive your sins.\"\nThe man said, \"Give me more, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you.\"\nHe (ﷺ) said, \"And may He make it easy for you wherever you may be.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2533","###\n Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abdah al-Dabbi, who said: Hammad (meaning Hammad ibn Zayd) informed us, from Asim (who is Asim ibn Sulayman al-Ahwal), from Abdullah ibn Sarjis, who said: \"When the Prophet (ﷺ) would travel, he would say: 'O Allah, You are the Companion in travel and the Successor over the family. O Allah, accompany us in our travel and succeed us over our family. O Allah, I seek refuge"
"2534","Narrated to us ‘Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa’di: narrated to us Isma’il ibn Ja’far: narrated to us Ja’far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stayed in Madinah for nine years, not performing Hajj. Then he permitted Hajj. It was said, “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is performing Hajj.” So many people came to Madinah, all of them hoping to follow the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).” Then he mentioned part of the Hadith and said: “Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out, meaning from the Masjid of Dhu’l-Hulayfah, and mounted his camel, and with him were many people, riders and pedestrians.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2535","###\n Isma'il bin Hafs bin Umar and Ibn Maymun narrated to us; they said: Yahya bin al-Yaman narrated to us, from Hamza al-Zayyat, from Humran bin A'yan, from Abu at-Tufayl, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (RA), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) performed Hajj, and his companions walked from Medina to Makkah. He (ﷺ) said: \"Tie your loins with your waist-cloths,\" and he walked"
"2536","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid, narrated to us Ja'far ibn Muhammad, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out during the Year of the Conquest (Fath). The people gathered around him, forming rows before him, and said, \"We present ourselves to receive the supplications of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\" They said, \"The journey has become arduous for us, and the hardship has been prolonged.\" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to them, \"Seek assistance.\" 'Abd al-Wahhab (RA) said, \"I believe he said: 'With walking briskly, for indeed it will shorten the distance for you and lighten the burden upon you.'\" So we did that, and we found it lightened the burden upon us and removed what we were experiencing of fatigue.\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2537","###\n It was narrated to us by Ishaq bin Mansur, who said: It was narrated to us by Rauh bin Ubadah, who said: It was informed to us by Ibn Jurayj, who said: It was informed to us by Ja'far bin Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir (RA), who said: Some people complained to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about the hardship of walking. So he called them and said: \"You should adopt the Naslan (a brisk, light walking pace).\" So we"
"2538","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Khalaf al-Asqalani, Ibrahim bin Marzuq, and my uncle Ismail bin Khuzaymah; they said: Narrated to us Wahb bin Jarir; he said: My father narrated to me, saying: I heard Yunus bin Yazid narrating from Az-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah bin Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"The best companions are four, the best expeditions are four hundred, and"
"2539","###\n The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"The best of companions with Allah is the one who is best to his companion, and the best of neighbors with Allah is the one who is best to his neighbor.\" This was narrated to us by Al-Hasan bin Al-Hasan, who informed us that Ibn Al-Mubarak informed us that Haywah bin Shurayh informed us that Sharhabil narrated to him from Abu Abdur-Rahman Al-Hubuli, from Abdullah bin Amr, from the Prophet (ﷺ)."
"2540","**\nNarrated to us Abu 'Ammar al-Husayn ibn Hurayth, narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Musa, from 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Ata' the freed slave of Abu Ahmad, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent out an expedition force. They were a group of men, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called them and asked, \"What portion of the Qur'an do you have with you?\" He then began to question them about it until he came to a young man among them, who was one of the youngest of the group. He asked, \"What do you have with you, O so-and-so?\" The young man replied, \"I have such-and-such, and Surah al-Baqarah.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Go, for you are their commander.\"\n\n---"
"2541","**\nNarrated to us ʿAmmār ibn Khālid al-Wāsiṭī, who said: Narrated to us al-Qāsim ibn Mālik al-Muzanī, on the authority of al-Aʿmash, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: ʿUmar (RA) said, “If there are three people traveling together, then let them appoint one of them as their leader. That is an amīr whom the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) appointed.”\n\n---"
"2542","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn As-Sabbaḥ Az-Zaʿfarānī, who said:\nNarrated to us Ḥajjāj ibn Muhammad, who said:\nIbn Jurayj said:\nAbu Az-Zubayr informed me, that ʿAlī Al-Azdī informed him, that Ibn ʿUmar (RA) taught him:\nWhen the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) would mount his camel to set out on a journey, he would say the takbīr three times, then say:\n\n*\"Subḥāna lladhī sakhkhara lanā hādhā wa mā kunnā lahū muqrīnīn, wa innā ilā rabbinā lamunqalibūn. Allāhumma innā nas’aluka fī safarinā hādhā al-birra wa at-taqwā, wa mina l-ʿamali mā tarḍā. Allāhumma hawwin ʿalaynā safaranā, waṭwi ʿannā buʿdah. Allāhumma anta aṣ-ṣāḥibu fī s-safar, wa l-khalīfatu fī l-ahl. Allāhumma innī aʿūdhu bika min waʿthāʾi s-safar, wa kāʾābati l-munqalab, wa sūʾi l-manẓari fī l-ahl wa l-māl.\"*\n\nThen, when he returned, he would say the same and add:\n*\"Āyibūna tāʾibūna ʿābidūna li-rabbinā ḥāmidūn.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2543","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn az-Za'farani, Ishaq ibn Wahb al-Wasiti, Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad, and Raja' ibn Muhammad al-'Udhri—they all said:\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd at-Tanafisi, who said:\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam ibn Thawban, from Abu Las al-Khuza'i (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted us upon camels from the camels of sadaqah (charity), light camels suitable for Hajj. So we said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we do not see that these camels are suitable for us to ride.'\nHe (ﷺ) said: 'There is no camel except that upon its hump is a shaytan (devil). So remember Allah when you ride them as He has commanded you. Then make use of them for yourselves, for indeed Allah is the One Who carries (the burden).'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2544","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us 'Asim—meaning ibn 'Ali—, narrated to us Layth—meaning ibn Sa'd—and Az-Za'farani also narrated to us Shababah, who informed us Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ibn Mu'adh ibn Anas, from his father, in the narration of Shababah, who was one of the Companions of the Prophet (ﷺ). In both narrations, the Prophet (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"Ride these mounts in a sound manner, and dismount from them in a sound manner, and do not take them as chairs while you are upon them or while you have dismounted from them.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2545","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Al-Nufayli, who said: Narrated to us Miskin Al-Hadda', who said: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Al-Muhajir, on the authority of Rabi'ah bin Yazid, on the authority of Abu Kabshah As-Saluli, who said: Sahl bin Hanzalah narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by a camel whose back had become so emaciated that it touched"
"2546","###\n Narrated to us 'Abdah ibn 'Abdullah al-Khuza'i, who informed us that Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated from Usamah, who said: Muhammad ibn Hamzah ibn 'Amr al-Aslami narrated to me from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Upon the back of every camel is a devil. So when you mount them, mention the Name of Allah, and do not be negligent in your needs.\" And narrated to us"
"2547","###\n Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, from his father, from Al-A'raj, from Abu Hurairah (RA), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Indeed, upon the summit of every camel is a devil; so wear them out by riding them, for indeed Allah is the One who carries (them).\"\n\nAbu Bakr (the commentator) said: In the narration of"
"2548","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah, on the authority of Zuhayr—meaning ibn Muhammad—who said: Salim narrated to us that he heard al-Hasan say: Narrated to us Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"When you travel during times of prosperity, allow the mounts to graze freely from their teeth, and do not pass by the resting places. But when you travel during times of drought, hasten your journey and travel by night, for the earth is folded up by night. When the ghouls (evil spirits) trouble you, hasten to prayer, and avoid resting or praying on the middle of the road, for it is the abode of snakes and beasts. Likewise, relieve yourselves on the road, for it is a place of filth.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2549","**\nNarrated to us Abu Hisham ar-Rifa’i, narrated to us Yahya ibn Yaman, narrated to us Hisham, on the authority of al-Hasan, on the authority of Jabir (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"When the land is lush and green, allow the riding animals to graze (and rest). Stay in the designated stopping places. But when the land is barren and dry, hasten your journey and travel by night, for the earth is folded (i.e., darkness covers it) during the night. Beware of the edges of the road, for they are the abode of snakes and beasts of prey. And when you see the ghaylan (i.e., mirages or heat waves), call out (the adhan).\"*\n\nAl-Hasan (RA) said: I heard Muhammad ibn Yahya say that ‘Ali ibn ‘Abdullah used to reject that al-Hasan had heard from Jabir (RA).\n\n---"
"2550","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi, narrated to us al-'Aziz — meaning Ibn Muhammad ad-Darawardi — from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\"If you travel in a land of fertility, then give the camels their due, and if you travel in a year of drought, then hasten with their young, and when you make camp, avoid the road, for it is the path of beasts and the refuge of vermin by night.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2551","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar ibn Rib'iyy al-Qaysiyy, narrated to us Muhammad—meaning Ibn Bakr al-Bursaniyy, who informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu az-Zubayr, that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA) say:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) forbade branding on the face and striking on the face.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: In the reports of Jabir (RA) regarding the story of the camel that the Prophet (ﷺ) purchased, he said:\n\"My camel became weak, so the Prophet (ﷺ) prodded it with a staff or struck it,\" indicating that striking animals on parts other than the face is permissible.\n\nI have recorded those reports in the chapter on sales (*Kitab al-Buyu'*).\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2552","**\nNarrated to us Nasr ibn Marzuq: Narrated to us Asad (meaning Ibn Musa): Narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah: From Qatadah: From ‘Ikrimah: From Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade drinking from the mouth of a waterskin and riding the *ajal* (an animal that eats filth), and the *mujaththamah*.\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: He meant and forbade the *mujaththamah*. And the *mujaththamah* is the animal that is tied up and then stoned until it is killed. I have written it in the chapter of foods or the chapter of jihad. And the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ) that he forbade killing any of the beasts by stoning.\n\n---"
"2553","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Suhayl, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) said, \"Indeed, the angels do not accompany a group of travelers in which there is a bell or in which there is a dog.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2554","Narrated to us Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Narrated to me Sulayman (i.e., Ibn Bilal), who said: Narrated to me Al-Ala' from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"The bell is the flute of Satan.\""
"2555","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Aslam, who said: Narrated to us by Qabisah bin Uqbah, who said: Narrated to us by Al-Layth, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas bin Malik (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"You should travel at night, for the earth is folded up at night.\" Also narrated to us by Humaid bin Ar-Rabi' Al-Khazzaz and Abu Bishr, who said"
"2556","Narrated to us Ahmad ibn 'Abdah ad-Dabbi, narrated to us 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad ad-Darawardi, on the authority of Suhayl, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Abu Hurayrah (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n*\"When you stop to rest for the night, avoid the road, for it is the path of beasts and the abode of vermin by night.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2557","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Suhayl, with similar wording, and he said: *\"If you make camp at night, then avoid the road, for indeed it is the refuge of vermin at night.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2558","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Abu an-Nu'man, who said: Narrated to us Hammad bin Salamah, from Humaid, from Bakr bin Abdullah bin Rabah, from Abu Qatadah (RA): \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he would rest (sleep) at night, would lie on his right side; and when he would rest just before dawn (Fajr), he would raise his arms up and place his head upon his palms.\""
"2559","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Reduce your journeys when the night has advanced, for indeed Allah sends forth in the latter part of the night whatever of His creation He wills.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2560","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, who narrated to us Jarir, from Fithr ibn Khalifah, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Hold back your livestock and your families from going out from sunset until the twilight disappears.\" Yusuf [ibn Musa] said: \"This refers to the early part of the night (fahwat al-‘isha').\" Abu Bakr (RA) said: \"This is a scribal error in my transmission. The correct meaning is fahwat al-‘isha' when the darkness has intensified, as this is how other transmitters besides Yusuf reported this hadith, referring to this period as fahwah.\"\n---"
"2561","**\nNarrated to us Salm ibn Junādah al-Qurashī: narrated to us Wakīʿ, on the authority of Usāmah ibn Zayd, on the authority of Saʿīd al-Maqburī, on the authority of Abū Hurayrah (RA), who said:\nA man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) wishing to travel, and said, “O Messenger of Allāh, give me some advice.”\nHe (ﷺ) said, “I advise you to have taqwā of Allāh and to say Allāhu Akbar on every ascent.”\nWhen the man had left, the Prophet (ﷺ) said, “O Allāh, contract the earth for him and make the journey easy for him.”\n\n---"
"2562","Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn al-Mundhir, narrated to us Ibn Fuḍayl, narrated to us Ḥaṣīn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, on the authority of Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, on the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (RA), who said:\n*\"When we would ascend a height, we would say Allāhu Akbar (Allah is the Greatest), and when we would descend, we would say Subḥān Allāh (Glory be to Allah).\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2563","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Marhum ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, narrated to us Abu Na'amah al-Sa'di, on the authority of Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, on the authority of Abu Musa al-Ash'ari (RA), who said:\n\"We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during a military expedition. When we approached the city of Medina and pronounced the takbir (Allah is the Greatest) aloud, raising their voices with it, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n'Verily, your Lord is not deaf, nor absent. He is between you and the heads of your riding beasts.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2564","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, narrated to us by Muhammad, narrated to us by Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Rabi'i bin Khirash, from Zayd bin Zabyan, who raised it to Abu Dharr (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"There are three whom Allah loves, and three whom Allah hates. As for those whom Allah loves: A group of people who travel during the night until, when sleep becomes dearer to one of them than what he would exchange it"
"2565","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who informed us that Ibn Wahb informed him, saying: Hafs bin Maysarah informed me, from Musa bin Uqba, from Ata' bin Abi Marwan, from his father, that Ka'b informed him that Suhayb, the companion of the Prophet (ﷺ), informed him, saying: The Prophet (ﷺ) never saw a town that he intended to enter without saying when he saw it: \"O Allah, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade"
"2566","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, who said: Our father and Shu'ayb informed us, both saying: Al-Layth informed us, on the authority of Yazid ibn Abi Habib, on the authority of al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, that Ya'qub ibn 'Abdullah informed him that he heard Busr ibn Sa'id saying: I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas saying: I heard Khawlah bint Thalabah al-Sulamiyyah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:\n\n\"Whoever alights at a place and then says: 'I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He has created,' nothing will harm him until he departs from that place.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2567","Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la:\nHe informed us Ibn Wahb:\nHe informed me ‘Amr ibn al-Harith,\nOn the authority of Yazid ibn Abi Habib and al-Harith ibn Ya‘qub,\nOn the authority of Ya‘qub ibn ‘Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj,\nWith this chain, in like manner.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2568","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Safwan al-Thaqafi, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Salam ibn Hashim, who said: Narrated to us Uthman ibn Sa'd al-Katib — and he was a man of dignity and intelligence — on the authority of Anas ibn Malik (RA), who said:\n\"The Prophet (ﷺ) would never descend upon a place except that he would bid it farewell with two rak'ahs.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2569","###\n Narrated to us by Abu al-Ash'ath Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam, who said: Narrated to us by Bishr, meaning ibn al-Mufaddal, who said: Narrated to us by Asim, who is the son of Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA), who said: I heard my father (RA) saying: Ibn Umar (RA) said: The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"If people knew what I know about"
"2570","**\nNarrated to us Bundar and Abdullah ibn Hashim, both saying:\nNarrated to us Yahya (i.e., Ibn Sa'id), from Ibn 'Ajlan, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"One person is a devil, two persons are two devils, and three persons are a riding party.\"\n\nBundar said: Narrated to us Ibn 'Ajlan."
"2571","###\n Narrated to us Yunus bin Abd al-A'la, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Narrated to me also—meaning Sulayman bin Bilal—from Suhayl bin Abi Salih. [And another chain]: Narrated to us Muhammad bin Yahya, who said: Narrated to us Abu Mus'ab Ahmad bin Abi Bakr al-Zuhri, who said: Narrated to us Abd al-Aziz bin Abi Hazim, from Abd Allah bin Amir. [And yet another chain"
"2572","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu al-Mughirah, narrated to us Safwan ibn Amr, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd al-Hadrami (RA), that he heard al-Zubayr ibn al-Walid narrating, from Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab (RA), who said:\n\nWhen the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would go out for a military expedition or travel, and night would overtake him, he would say:\n\n*\"O earth, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. I seek refuge in Allah from your evil, from the evil of what is in you, from the evil of what has been created in you, and from the evil of what crawls upon you. I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of every lion, black serpent, and scorpion, from the inhabitants of the land, and from the evil of a parent and what he has begotten.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2573","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala al-'Attar and Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi: They said, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\"I used to braid the garlands for the sacrificial camels of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with these two hands of mine.\"\n\n[Sahih]\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2574","Narrated to us Ya'qub ad-Dawraqi, narrated to us 'Uthman ibn 'Umar, who informed us Malik ibn Anas, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (RA) who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) placed the sacrificial garland around the neck of his sacrificial camels and marked them (by drawing blood from their humps).\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2575","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Yahya ibn Sa'id, on the authority of Shu'bah, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Abu al-Hasan al-A'raj, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA): \"The Prophet (ﷺ) prayed the Dhuhr prayer, and he ordered that his sacrificial camels be marked (Ish'ar) on the right side of their humps, that they be shod with two sandals (Qilad),"
"2576","Narrated to us Salamah ibn Junādah, who said: Narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Hishām al-Dastuwāʾī, from Qatādah, with this chain of narration similar to it, except that he said: \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) marked the sacrificial camel by making an incision in the right hump.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2577","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-`Ala' ibn Kurayb, who said: Narrated to us `Abd ar-Rahim, meaning ibn Sulayman. And narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, who said: Narrated to us Waki`, both of them from Hisham ibn `Urwah, from his father, who said: Najiyah al-Khuza'i, the keeper of the Prophet's (ﷺ) sacrificial camels, narrated to me that he asked the"
"2578","Narrated to us Bundār, who said: narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, who said: narrated to us Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Sinān ibn Salamah al-Hudhalī, from Ibn ʿAbbās (RA) that Dhūwayb Abū Qabīṣah al-Khuzāʿī narrated to him that the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) sent sacrificial camels with him and said:\n\n\"If any of them becomes too weak to continue, slaughter it, then dip its hoof in the blood of its chest, and neither you nor any of your companions should eat from it.\"\n\nAnd Bundār narrated to us, who said: narrated to us Ibn Abī ʿAdiyy, from Saʿīd, with this chain, and he added: \"and strike its side.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2579","###\n Narrated to us Ar-Rabi' bin Sulayman and Salih bin Ayub, who said: Narrated to us by Bishr bin Bakr, on the authority of Al-Awza'i, who said: Narrated to us by Abdullah bin 'Amir, who said: Narrated to us by Nafi', on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: \"Whoever offers a voluntary sacrifice (Hady) and it gets lost, then if he wishes, he"
"2580","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bazi' — narrated to us Ziyad, meaning ibn 'Abdullah al-Bakka'i — narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — he is Ibn Abi Layla — on the authority of 'Ata', on the authority of Abu al-Khalil, on the authority of Abu Qatadah, who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\"Whoever drives sacrificial animals voluntarily and they perish, then he should not eat from them. If he eats from them, then he must make compensation for them. Instead, he should slaughter them, then dip his sandal in their blood, then strike their sides. But if it is an obligatory sacrifice, then he may eat from it if he wishes, for it is inevitable that he must make up for it.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said:\n\"This hadith is mursal between Abu al-Khalil and Abu Qatadah; a man [was omitted].\"\n\n---"
"2581","Narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram who said: Narrated to us Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, on the authority of his father al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: I saw Aisha (RA) applying perfume with her own hands to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at the time of his entering the state of ihram, and again before he performed tawaf of the House.\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2582","Narrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar, narrated to us Sufyan, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, who heard 'A'ishah (RA) say while spreading out her hands: *\"I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with these two hands of mine for his ihram (when he intended to enter ihram) and for his halal (before he performed tawaf around the Ka'bah).\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: This wording, *\"when he entered ihram\"* (حِينَ أَحْرَمَ), is of the type that we say the Arabs use when they say *\"when you do such-and-such\"* meaning *\"when you intend to do it.\"* 'A'ishah (RA) only meant that she perfumed the Prophet (ﷺ) when he intended to enter ihram, not after he had already entered it. The proof for what I have stated is the report of Mansur ibn Zadhan, which I mentioned in the chapter that follows this, along with the reports that I have compiled in the *al-Kitab al-Kabir* (the larger book).\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2583","Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi, Ahmad ibn Mani', and Muhammad ibn Hisham — they said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who informed us Mansur (ibn Zadhan), on the authority of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, on the authority of al-Qasim, who said:\nA'ishah (RA) said: \"I applied perfume to the Prophet (ﷺ) before he entered ihram, and on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Nahr) before he performed Tawaf around the House, with a perfume that contained musk.\"\n\nIbn Hisham said, on the authority of Mansur: And Ahmad said, on the authority of A'ishah (RA), who said: \"I applied perfume to the Prophet (ﷺ).\"\n\nIn the report of Abu Nadrah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Indeed, the best of your perfumes is musk.\"\n\nThis is a clear indication against the claim of those who say it is impure.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2584","In the report of Abu Nadrah, on the authority of Abu Sa'id, from the Prophet (ﷺ), it is stated: \"The best of your perfumes is musk.\" This is clear evidence against the claim of those who assert that musk is impure. [Commentary] 2584 - Al-Azami said: Its chain of narration is Sahih (authentic)."
"2585","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of Mansur, on the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of al-Aswad, on the authority of Aisha (RA), who said:\n*\"It is as if I can still see the gleam of perfume on the head of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was in the state of ihram.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2586","Narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, on the authority of al-A'mash, on the authority of Ibrahim, on the authority of al-Aswad who said:\nA'ishah (RA) said, \"I saw the perfume on the parts of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was in a state of Ihram, and indeed he was responding to the call (of Talbiyah).\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2587","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad Az-Za'farani, narrated to us Rawḥ, narrated to us Shu'bah, narrated to us Al-Ḥakam, Ḥammād, Manṣūr, and Sulaymān—all of them from Ibrāhīm, from Al-Aswad, from ʿĀʾishah (RA), who said:\n\n*\"It is as if I can still see the gleam of perfume on the parting of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was in the state of ihrām.\"*\n\nSulaymān added: *\"In his hair.\"*\nManṣūr said: *\"In the roots of his hair.\"*\nAl-Ḥakam and Ḥammād said: *\"In the parting of his head.\"*\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2588","###\n Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id and Muhammad ibn Abi 'Adi, from Shu'bah, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muntasir, from his father, that he asked Ibn 'Umar regarding the use of perfume while in the state of Ihram. Ibn 'Umar said: \"I would prefer to anoint myself with tar rather than do that.\" He (the narrator) said: \"So I mentioned this to 'A'ishah (RA),"
"2589","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan.\nAnd narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah.\nAnd narrated to us Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father:\n\nThat the Prophet (ﷺ) designated for the people of Madinah *Dhu al-Hulayfah*, for the people of Syria *al-Juhfah*, and for the people of Najd *Qarn*.\n\n'Abd al-Jabbar said in his narration: He mentioned to me, but I did not hear him say, that he said: And for the people of Yemen *Yalamlam*.\n\nAl-Makhzumi said: And 'Abdullah said: It reached me that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"And the people of Yemen will enter the state of ihram from *Yalamlam*.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2590","###\n Narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abdah al-Dabbi, who said: Narrated to us by Hammad, meaning Ibn Zayd, on the authority of Amr, who is Ibn Dinar, on the authority of Tawus, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA): The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) designated Dhul-Hulayfah as the Miqat for the people of Madinah, Al-Juhfah for the people of Sham, Yalamlam for the people of Yemen, and Qarn"
"2591","Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub Al-Jazari: Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ja'far Ghundar: Narrated to us Ma'mar: On the authority of Ibn Tawus: On the authority of his father: On the authority of Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed the Miqat for the people of Madinah at Dhu'l-Hulayfah, for the people of Sham at Al-Juhfah, for the people of Najd at Qarn, and for the people of Yemen at Yalamlam. He (ﷺ) then said: 'These are for them and for whoever comes upon them from other than their people who intends to perform Hajj or 'Umrah. Then, whoever is beyond that, he should start from where he is until he reaches the Miqat of the people of Makkah.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2592","###\n Muhammad bin Ma'mar al-Qaysi narrated to us, saying: Muhammad, meaning Ibn Bakr, narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed us, saying: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir bin Abdullah (RA) asking about the place of Ihram (Mihlal), and he said: \"I think he is referring to the Prophet (ﷺ).\" He (the Prophet ﷺ) said: \"The place of Ihram for the people of Medina is Dhu al-Hulay"
"2593","Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Hujr al-Sa'di, narrated to us Isma'il — meaning Ibn Ja'far — on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, on the authority of Ibn 'Umar, on the authority of the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said:\n\"Command the people of Madinah to enter ihram from Dhu'l-Hulayfah, the people of ash-Sham from al-Juhfah, the people of Najd from Qarn.\"\n'Abdullah ibn 'Umar said: And I was informed that he (ﷺ) also said:\n\"And the people of Yemen should enter ihram from Yalamlam.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2594","###\n Narrated to us by Bundar, who narrated to us from Yahya bin Sa'id, who narrated to us from Ja'far (who is Ibn Muhammad), who narrated to me from my father, who said: We went to Jabir bin Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: Asma' bint Umays gave birth to Muhammad bin Abu Bakr. She sent a message to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) asking, \"What should I do?\" He replied, \"Take"
"2595","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qataniy, who said:\nNarrated to us Abdullah ibn Ya'qub al-Madini, on the authority of Ibn Abi al-Zinad, from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from his father:\nThat the Prophet (ﷺ) removed his clothes for his ihram and performed ghusl (ritual bath).\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2596","###\n Muhammad bin Al-Ala bin Kurayb narrated to us, saying: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: \"One does not enter the state of Ihram for Hajj except during the months of Hajj, for it is from the Sunnah of Hajj to enter the state of Ihram for Hajj during the months of Hajj.\" Additionally, Abu Kurayb narrated to us, saying: Abu Khalid narr"
"2597","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us Bushr (meaning Ibn al-Mufaddal), narrated to us 'Ubayd Allah, on the authority of Nafi', on the authority of 'Abdullah (RA) that a man said:\n\n\"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what garments should we wear when we enter ihram?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) said: \"Do not wear shirts, trousers, turbans, 'amā'im (a type of cloak), hats, or leather socks, except for one who does not have sandals, so he may wear them below the ankles. And do not wear any garment that has been touched by wars (a yellow dye) or saffron.\"\n\nAnd 'Abdullah (RA) used to say: \"And a woman should not cover her face, nor wear gloves.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2598","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Saʿīd al-Ashajj, narrated to us Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth, on the authority of ʿUbaydullah, on the authority of Nāfiʿ, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA) who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade the muḥrim (pilgrim in the state of ihrām) from wearing shirts, cloaks (qubtiyyah), or leather socks, unless he cannot find sandals. He also forbade him from wearing trousers or any garment that has been dyed with wars (a yellow dye) or saffron.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2599","Narrated to us ʿAlī ibn Khashram, who said: ʿĪsā (meaning ibn Yūnus) informed us, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed me, on the authority of Nāfiʿ, on the authority of Ibn ʿUmar (RA) that a man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said:\n\n\"O Prophet of Allah! What do you command us to wear of clothing during the state of ihrām?\"\n\nHe (ﷺ) replied:\n\n\"Do not wear shirts (*qumṣ*), nor turbans (*ʿamāʾim*), nor hooded cloaks (*barānis*), nor trousers (*sarāwīlāt*), nor leather socks (*khafāf*), except if a man does not have sandals, then let him wear leather socks (*khuffayn*) that cover below the ankles. And do not wear clothing that has been touched by saffron (*zaʿfaran*) or wars (*wars*).\n\nHe (ﷺ) also said:\n\n\"The woman in ihrām is not to wear a face-veil (*niqāb*), nor is she to wear gloves (*quffāzayn*).\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2600","Narrated to us Abu Dawud Sulayman ibn Tubah, who said: Abu Badr narrated to us. And narrated to us Ali ibn al-Husayn al-Dihrami, and this is his hadith: Shuja' ibn al-Walid Abu Badr narrated to us. Abu Dawud said: [he narrated to us], and al-Dihrami reported: From Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"A woman in the state of ihram shall not veil her face, nor shall she wear gloves.\" This is the exact wording of al-Dihrami's hadith."
"2601","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar (RA), that a man called out and said, \"O Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), what garments should the one in ihram avoid wearing?\" He (ﷺ) said: \"Do not wear trousers, shirts, hooded cloaks, turbans, or garments dyed with saffron or wars (a type of yellow dye). Let one of you enter ihram in an izar (waist wrapper) and rida' (upper garment), and sandals. If he does not find sandals, then let him wear leather socks and cut them until they reach the ankles.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2602","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Ala ibn Kurayb, both of them narrating from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aisha (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by Duba'ah bint al-Zubayr (RA) while she was unwell, and he said to her: \"Do you intend to perform Hajj?\" She replied: Yes. He said: \"Then perform Hajj, make a stipulation, and say: 'O Allah, my place of exiting ihram is wherever You detain me.'\" This is the exact wording of the hadith reported by Abd al-Jabbar [ibn al-Ala]."
"2603","Narrated to us Ali ibn Hujr, who narrated to us Ismail ibn Ja'far, who narrated to us Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Husayn ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, on the authority of his father, on the authority of Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA):\nThat the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) remained in Medina for nine years without performing Hajj, then he was granted permission to perform Hajj. It was announced: \"Indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is going to perform Hajj.\" So a great multitude of people arrived in Medina, all of them desiring to follow the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and act in accordance with his actions. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then set out until he reached the Masjid of Dhu al-Hulayfah, where he prayed, then he departed. A large number of people rode and walked alongside him, all of them desiring to follow the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until he emerged onto the open plain of al-Bayda'. He then entered the state of ihram, and we had intended only Hajj, with no knowledge of Umrah. I looked ahead of me, to my right, to my left, and behind me, and as far as the eye could see there were riders and pedestrians, all of them desiring to follow the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."
"2604","Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam al-‘Ijli narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham ibn ‘Urwah, from his father ‘Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from ‘A’ishah (RA), that she reported: \"We set out at the sighting of the crescent of Dhu al-Hijjah, so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever intends to enter ihram for Hajj, let him enter ihram for Hajj, and whoever intends to enter ihram for ‘Umrah, let him enter ihram for ‘Umrah.' Thus, among our group were those who entered ihram for Hajj, and among us were those who entered ihram for ‘Umrah.\""
"2605","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala and Ziyad ibn Yahya al-Hassani (may Allah have mercy on them both), they said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'Aishah (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj, and some people pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj, and some people pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj and 'Umrah together, and some people pronounced the talbiyah for 'Umrah.\"\nAbd al-Jabbar did not say: \"and some people pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj,\" and he added in his narration: \"And I was among those who pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj and 'Umrah together.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2606","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad—meaning ibn Ja'far—narrated to us Shu'bah, on the authority of al-Hakam, from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Dhakwan, the freed slave of 'Aishah, from 'Aishah (RA), who said:\n\nThe Prophet (ﷺ) arrived (in Makkah) four or five days before the end of Dhu al-Hijjah. He entered upon me while he was angry. I asked, \"Who has angered you?\" He replied, \"Have you not noticed? I commanded the people to do something, but they are hesitating.\"\n\nAl-Hakam said: \"They are hesitating. I think that if I could go back to my previous decision, I would not have brought the sacrificial animals with me until I had purchased them, then I would have exited the state of ihram just as they have done.\""
"2607","Narrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-Sadafi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who said: Malik informed him.\n\nAnd narrated to us al-Fadl ibn Ya'qub al-Jazari, who said: Narrated to us Muhammad (meaning Ibn Ja'far), who said: Narrated to us Malik ibn Anas, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\n\"We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered the state of ihram for 'umrah. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever has the sacrificial animal with him, let him enter the state of ihram for hajj along with 'umrah.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2608","Narrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad az-Za'farani, narrated to us 'Abdah — meaning ibn Humayd — narrated to me Mansur, and narrated to us Yusuf ibn Musa, narrated to us Jarir, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah (RA), who said:\n\n\"I have seen myself twisting the garlands for the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), then he would remain in a state of lawful (ihram) until the sacrificial animals were driven away.\"\n\nThis is the narration of az-Za'farani.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2609","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bishr, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan al-A'raj, from Ibn Abbas (RA), who reported: \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed the Zuhr prayer, then commanded that his sacrificial camel be marked on its right side, hung two sandals around its neck, and had blood drained from it. When he reached the open desert plain (al-Bayda') with the camel, he entered the state of ihram.\"\nNarrated to us Bundar as well, who narrated to us Muhammad [referring to Muhammad ibn Ja'far], who narrated to us Shu'bah, with this same chain of narration, conveying the same core meaning, and he reported: \"He prayed the Zuhr at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and he marked his sacrificial camel.\" He did not include the phrase \"and had blood drained from it.\"\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: This wording found in the report of Muhammad ibn Ja'far, \"and he marked his sacrificial camel\", is of the same category that I have clarified in numerous places in my works: the Arabs attribute an action to the one who commands it in the same way they attribute it to the one who performs the action. Therefore, his statement \"and he marked his sacrificial camel\" means that the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered that it be marked, as the report of Yahya al-Qattan states \"he commanded that his sacrificial camel be marked\", which is clear evidence that the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered the marking, not that he performed the action himself. It is also possible that he marked part of his sacrificial camel with his own hand, and commanded others to mark the rest of it. Thus, whoever states in a report \"he commanded that his sacrificial camel be marked\" is referring to part of it, and whoever says \"he marked his sacrificial camel\" is also referring to part of it, not the entire camel. As such, the reports are fully consistent, not contradictory, as the ignorant assume."
"2610","Muhammad bin Abdullah bin Abdul-Hakam narrated to us, stating that Ibn Abi Maryam informed them, saying: Sulayman bin Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya bin Sa'id, who said: I heard Al-Qasim bin Muhammad narrating from his father, from Abu Bakr (RA), that he went out as a pilgrim with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the Farewell Pilgrimage. With him was his wife, Asma' bint Umays bin Khath'am. When they reached Ash-Sh"
"2611","Yahya bin Hakim narrated to us, saying: Sufyan bin Uyaynah narrated to us, on the authority of Musa bin Uqba, on the authority of Salim, who said: I heard Ibn Umar (RA) say: \"This is the barren land (Al-Bayda') where you falsely attribute statements to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not pronounce the Talbiyah (Ihram) except at the door of the Mosque.\""
"2612","Narrated to us Ali ibn Sahl al-Ramli, who said: Narrated to us al-Walid — and he is Ibn Muslim — on the authority of Abu Amr al-Awza'i, on the authority of Ata', who said he was told by Jabir (RA):\n\nThat the Prophet (ﷺ) began his talbiyah (i.e., said, \"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk...\") from Dhu al-Hulayfah when his camel stood upright with him.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2613","Ali ibn Khashram narrated to us; Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim, who reported: Ibn Umar (RA) said, \"Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would, when he placed his foot in the stirrup and his riding camel had steadied for him, pronounce the Talbiyah.\""
"2614","###\n Narrated to us by Abd al-Warith ibn Abd al-Samad, who said: My father narrated to me, from his father, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar (RA) would, when he arrived at Dhul-Hulaifah, order his mount to be saddled. Then he would pray the Fajr prayer, then mount. When the mount had risen with him and he was seated firmly, he would face the Qiblah and pronounce the Talbiyah (Ihram). He said: Then he would"
"2615","Narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim as-Sawwaf, who said: Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami, who said: Narrated to us Wuhayb, who said: Narrated to me Musa ibn ‘Uqba, who said: Narrated to me Nafi‘ and Salim, that Ibn ‘Umar (RA) would, when he passed by Dhu’l-Hulayfa, spend the night there until he would spend the morning, and he would inform that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to do that.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2616","###\n Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, saying: Al-Khadir bin Muhammad bin Shuja' narrated to us, saying: Isma'il bin Ja'far informed us, from Musa bin Uqba, from Salim bin Abdullah, from his father (Abdullah bin Umar, RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) came while he was at his encampment in Dhu al-Hulayfah. It was said to him: \"Indeed, you are in a blessed valley.\" Musa said: \"And Salim would al"
"2617","Narrated to us Al-Rabee’ ibn Sulaymaan and Muhammad ibn Maskeen al-Yamaamee (may Allaah have mercy upon them), both said: Narrated to us Bushr ibn Bakr, who said: Al-Awzaa’ee informed us, who said: Yahyaa ibn Abee Katheer narrated to me, who said: ‘Ikrimah narrated to me, who said: Ibn ‘Abbaas narrated to me, who said: ‘Umar ibn al-Khattaab narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allaah (ﷺ) said:\n\n“Last night, an angel came to me from my Lord while he was at al-‘Aqeeq and said: ‘Pray in this blessed valley and say: ‘Umrah during Hajj.’”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2618","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us Khalid, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah, from Anas ibn Malik (RA) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n*\"Labbayka bi-Hajjin wa 'Umrah\"*\n(Here I am for Hajj and 'Umrah.)\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2619","Ali ibn Hajar narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, and Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, and Humayd al-Tawil all informed us, each stating: I heard Anas (RA) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: \"Labbayk for an Umrah and Hajj, Labbayk for an Umrah and Hajj,\" repeating this utterance multiple times."
"2620","Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, saying: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who said: \"We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) thinking that our intention was only for Hajj, until the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arrived in Makkah. He performed Tawaf of the Ka'bah seven times, then prayed two Rak'"
"2621","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn Mani’ and Mu’ammal ibn Hisham—both said: Narrated to us Isma’il—\nAhmad said: He informed us, and Mu’ammal said: On the authority of Ayyub, on the authority of Nafi’, on the authority of Ibn ‘Umar (RA) that the Talbiyah of the Prophet (ﷺ) was:\n\n> “Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharīka laka labbayk. Inna al-ḥamda wa al-ni’mata laka wa al-mulka, la sharīka lak.”\n\nMu’ammal added in his narration: And Ibn ‘Umar (RA) would also say:\n\n> “Labbayka labbayka labbayk, wa sa’dayk. Wa al-khayru fī yadayk, wa al-raghbā’u ilayk, wa al-‘amal.”"
"2622","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, narrated to us 'Ubaydullah, who informed me Nafi' on the authority of Ibn 'Umar (RA), who said: *\"I learned the Talbiyah directly from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).\"* Then he mentioned a narration similar to that of Mu'ammal."
"2623","Narrated to us Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Ashjaj, who narrated to us Waki' ibn al-Jarrah, who narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah. Salm ibn Junadah also narrated to us Waki', on the authority of Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Salamah, on the authority of Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, on the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-A'raj, on the authority of Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said in his talbiyah: \"Labbayk, O God of the Truth.\""
"2624","**\nNarrated to us Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us 'Abdullah ibn Wahb, who said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to me that 'Abdullah ibn al-Fadl informed him from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said:\n\"The Talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to include: *Labbayka ilaha l-haqq* (Here I am at Your service, O Allah, the Truth).\""
"2625","###\n Narrated to us by Al-Abbar ibn al-Ala' and Ahmad ibn Mani', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abdul Malik ibn Al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khalid ibn Al-Sa'ib, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: \"Jibril (Gabriel) came to me and said: 'Order your companions to raise their voices with the Talbiyah.'\" And Ahmad ibn"
"2626","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, who narrated to us Ja'far, who narrated to me his father, who reported: We went to Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA) and asked him about the Hajj pilgrimage of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: The Prophet (ﷺ) set out until his camel had come to rest on the open plain of Al-Baidah, at which point he pronounced the Talbiyah with the declaration of Tawhid: \"Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, labbayk la sharika lak labbayk, innal-hamda wan-ni'mata laka wal-mulku la sharika lak [Here I am at your service, O Allah, here I am at your service. Here I am at your service, You have no partner, here I am at your service. Indeed all praise and all bounty are Yours, and all sovereignty is Yours, You have no partner].\" He added: As for the people, they add phrases such as \"Dha al-Ma'araj\" [the Possessor of the Ascension] and similar expressions, and the Prophet (ﷺ) would hear them but would not say anything in objection."
"2627","**\nNarrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala and Ahmad ibn Mani', who said: Narrated to us Sufyan, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Khallad ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said:\n\n*\"Jibril (Gabriel) came to me and said: 'Command your companions to raise their voices with the Talbiyah.'\"*\n\nAhmad ibn Mani' added: *\"With the Talbiyah and the declaration of entering the state of Ihram.\"*"
"2628","**\nNarrated to us Salm ibn Junādah: Narrated to us Wakīʿ, from Sufyān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Labīd, from al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥanṭab, from Khālid ibn al-Sāʾib, from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī (RA) who said:\nThe Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said, “Jibrīl (AS) came to me and said, ‘O Muḥammad! Command your companions to raise their voices with the talbiyah, for it is one of the symbols of the Ḥajj.’”"
"2629","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Muhammad ibn az-Zubriqan, narrated to us Musa ibn 'Uqbah, narrated to me al-Muttalib ibn 'Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Khallad ibn as-Sa'ib, from Yazid ibn Khalid al-Juhani who said:\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"Jibril (AS) came to me and said: 'Make the Talbiyah known, for indeed it is one of the symbols of Hajj.'\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: This wording, \"for indeed it is one of the symbols of Hajj,\" is of the same type that I used to know the Arabs say, \"Indeed the best of actions is such-and-such,\" when they only meant \"of the best actions,\" and \"Indeed the best of actions is such-and-such,\" when they only meant \"of the best actions.\" And the Prophet (ﷺ) only meant by his statement, \"for indeed it is one of the symbols of Hajj,\" that it is from the symbols of Hajj.\n\n---"
"2630","Rabi'ah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, and he narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who informed me Usamah that Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Amr ibn Uthman ibn Affan and Abdullah ibn Abi Labid informed him, from Abd al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah, who said: I heard Abu Hurairah (RA) report that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"Jibril (AS) commanded me to raise my voice with the Ihlam (Talbiyah), for indeed it is from the rites (shi'ar) of Hajj.\" Abu Bakr (RA) said: \"I have documented the chains of narration of this report in my book Al-Kabir.\""
"2631","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi’, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Isma’il ibn Abi Fudayk, who informed us al-Dahhak ibn Uthman, on the authority of Ibn al-Munkadir, from ‘Abd al-Rahman ibn Yarbu’, from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked: “Which actions are most virtuous?” He replied: “Al-‘Ajj and al-Thajj.”\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: “Al-‘Ajj means raising the voice with the talbiyah, and al-Thajj means the slaughtering of the camels, and the blood flowing from the place of slaughter.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2632","Narrated to us 'Ali ibn Sa'id ibn Masruq al-Kindi, who said: Narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Za'idah, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu al-'Aliyah, who said: Narrated to us Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\nWe set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from Makkah to al-Madinah. When we reached Wadi al-Azraq, he (ﷺ) asked, \"Which valley is this?\" We replied, \"This is Wadi al-Azraq.\" He (ﷺ) said, \"It is as if I can see Musa (AS) with his long hair and complexion, placing his two fingers in his ears, reciting the Talbiyah loudly as he passes through this valley.\"\n\nThen we continued until we reached another place. Dawud (one of the narrators) said, \"I think it was Thanyat Musa.\" The Prophet (ﷺ) asked, \"Which pass is this?\" We replied, \"This is Thanyat Musa.\" He (ﷺ) said, \"It is as if I can see Yunus (AS) on a red camel, its halter loose, wearing a woolen cloak, reciting the Talbiyah at this pass.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2633","Narrated to us Abu Musa, narrated to us Ibn Abi 'Adiyy, from Dawud, from Abu 'Aliyah, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) between Makkah and Madinah. We passed through a valley, so he (ﷺ) asked, 'Which valley is this?' They said, 'This is Wadi al-Azraq (the Blue Valley).' He (ﷺ) said, 'It is as if I am looking at Musa (AS),' and he mentioned something about his complexion and hair that Dawud did not remember, 'placing his two fingers in his ears, reciting the Talbiyah and passing through this valley.'\"\n\nThen we traveled until we came to a mountain pass. He (ﷺ) asked, 'Which mountain pass is this?' They said, 'It is such and such.' He (ﷺ) said, 'It is as if I am looking at Yunus (AS) on a red camel, wearing a woolen cloak, with the rein of his camel made of rope, reciting the Talbiyah and passing through this valley.'\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2634","###\n Narrated to us Al-Hasan bin Muhammad Az-Za'farani, who said: Narrated to us Ubaydah, meaning the son of Humayd, who said: Narrated to me Umara bin Ghaziya, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl bin Sa'd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: \"There is no one who pronounces the Talbiyah (saying 'Labbayk Allahumma Labbayk') but that what is to his right and to his left of trees"
"2635","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu 'Amir, narrated to us Shu'bah. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bazī', narrated to us Ibn Abī 'Adī, from Shu'bah. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Walīd, narrated to us Ibn Abī 'Adī, from Shu'bah, from 'Uthmān ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mawhab, who said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Abī Qatādah narrating from his father (Abū Qatādah) that they were on a journey, and among them was one who had assumed the state of ihrām. He (Abū Qatādah) said: So Abū Qatādah mounted his horse, then he came upon a wild donkey and hunted it, and they ate from its meat. Then it seemed to them that this was not permissible, so they asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) about it. He said: \"Did you participate in it or point it out (to the hunter)?\" They said: No. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"Then eat it.\"\n\nIn the narration of Ibn Abī 'Adī, he said: \"Did you point it out or assist (the hunter)?\" And in the narration of Ibn Abī 'Adī from Shu'bah, it is similar, and he said: \"Did you point it out or hunt it or assist (the hunter)?\" They said: No. He (ﷺ) said: \"Then eat it.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2636","###\n Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqi narrated to us, saying: Yazid (meaning Ibn Harun) narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father (Abu Qatadah) (RA), who said: \"We hunted a wild donkey while we were with some people who were in the state of Ihram. They mentioned it to the Prophet (ﷺ),"
"2637","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us Ma'mar, on the authority of al-Zuhri. And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani, informed us Ibn Jurayj, informed me Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Utbah, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, from al-Sa'b ibn Jaththamah (RA), who said:\n\n\"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by me while I was in al-Abwa'—Ibn Ma'mar said: or in Waddan—and I presented him with a wild donkey. He returned it to me. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw the dislike on my face, he said, 'It is not that I am rejecting it from you, but we are in a state of ihram.'\"\n\nIn the narration of Ibn Jurayj, I asked Ibn Shihab, \"Was the donkey a carcass (i.e., already dead)?\" He said, \"I do not know.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: In the version of Ibn Jurayj, where he asked al-Zuhri and received his response, there is an indication that whoever claims in the narration of al-Sa'b that he presented the Prophet (ﷺ) with the flesh of a donkey or a leg of a donkey is mistaken. For al-Zuhri explicitly stated that he did not know whether the donkey was a carcass when it was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ). How then can it be narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) was presented with the flesh or a leg of a donkey, when it is unknown whether the donkey presented to the Prophet (ﷺ) was a carcass or not?\n\nI have recorded the various wordings of this narration in my book al-Kabir. Whoever claims in this narration that he said, 'I presented him with the flesh of a donkey,' or 'a leg of a donkey,' or 'a donkey,' has erred.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2638","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqī, (and) Yahyā narrated to us.\nI also read it to Bunādār, from Yahyā, from Ibn Jurayj who said:\nMuḥammad ibn al-Munkadir informed us, from Muʿādh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Taymī, from his father, who said:\nWe were with Ṭalḥah while we were in the state of ihrām. A bird was presented as a gift, and Ṭalḥah was asleep. Some of us ate (of it), and some of us refrained out of caution.\nWhen Ṭalḥah awoke, he approved of those who had eaten and said:\n“We ate it while we were with the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).”\n\nThis is the wording of the narration of al-Dawraqī.\nBunādār said: From Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir.\nAbū Bakr said:\nThe reports of Abū Qatādah and the Prophet’s (ﷺ) approval of the action of the one who ate the game hunted by Abū Qatādah, as well as his questioning them, “Do you have any of its meat with you?” and his eating from that meat, all fall under this chapter.\nThe report of ʿUmayr ibn Salamah al-Ḍumayrī also belongs to this chapter."
"2639","I recited this narration to Bundar, on the authority of Yahya, on the authority of Ibn Jurayj, who reported: Al-Hasan bin Muslim informed me, on the authority of Tawus, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who said: When Zayd bin Arqam arrived, Ibn Abbas (RA) said to him: \"Ask him to recall for us how he narrated to us about meat that was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in the state of ihram?\" So they asked him to recount it, and he said: \"Meat from hunted game was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in ihram, so he returned it and said: 'Indeed, we are in a state of ihram.'\"\n\nAbu Bakr (the narrator/editor of this collection) stated: Zuhayr narrated this report from Abu al-Zubayr, on the authority of Tawus, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), on the authority of al-Bara' bin 'Azib (RA), who said: \"Meat from hunted game was presented to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he said: 'Were it not that we are in a state of ihram, we would have accepted it.'\"\n\n[Ibrahim bin Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us, Al-Hasan bin Bishr bin Muslim narrated to us, from Zuhayr]. Abu Bakr said: The report of Tawus from Ibn Abbas clarifies that any narration attributed to Ibn Abbas stating that \"meat of a wild donkey was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ)\" refers to his report from al-Sa'ib bin Juthamah, as per the narration of the one who said: \"I presented a wild donkey to him, and he did not eat it.\" It is likely that confusion befell some narrators, so they assigned the report of Ibn Abbas from Zayd bin Arqam regarding hunted game meat to the story of al-Sa'ib bin Juthamah. The report of 'A'ishah (RA) that meat of a gazelle was presented to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was in ihram, and he did not eat it, is consistent with the reports of Zayd bin Arqam and al-Bara' bin 'Azib."
"2640","###\n Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us, saying: Muhammad, meaning Ibn Bakr, informed us, saying: Ibn Jurayj informed us. In another narration, Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me: Al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas, who said: Zayd ibn Arqam arrived in Makkah (though Ibn Ma'mar did not mention \"Makkah\"). Then Ibn"
"2641","Narrated to us Yunus ibn Abd al-A'la, who narrated to us Ibn Wahb, who narrated to me Ya'qub [meaning Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri] and Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Salim, that Amr, the freed slave of al-Muttalib, informed them both on the authority of al-Muttalib, and on the authority of Abd Allah ibn Hantab, on the authority of Jabir ibn Abd Allah (RA), on the authority of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), that he said: \"The meat of the land game is permissible for you while you are in the state of ihram, provided that you do not hunt it yourselves, nor is it driven towards you for hunting.\"\nNarrated to us Nasr ibn Marzuq, who narrated to us Asad [meaning Asad ibn Musa], who narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Yahya ibn Abd Allah [who is Ibn Salim], from Amr, the freed slave of al-Muttalib, with this same chain of narration, exactly matching the above, except that he said \"land game\" and did not say \"meat\"."
"2642","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, who narrated to us Abdur-Razzaq, who narrated to us Ma'mar, from Yahya Abu Kathir, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father Abu Qatadah al-Ansari (RA), who said: \"We set out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during the time of the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah. My companions entered the state of ihram, but I did not enter ihram. I sighted a wild donkey, so I charged at it and hunted it. I mentioned the matter to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I informed him that I had not entered ihram, and that I had only hunted it for him. So the Prophet (ﷺ) ordered his companions, and they ate from it, but he did not eat from it when I informed him that I had hunted it for him.\"\n\nAbu Bakr said: \"This additional wording – 'I only hunted it for you' and the statement 'but he did not eat from it when I informed him that I had hunted it for you' – I do not know of anyone who mentioned this wording in the report of Abu Qatadah except Ma'mar in this isnad. If this wording is authentic, it is most likely that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ate from the meat of that donkey before Abu Qatadah informed him that he had hunted it for his sake. Then when Abu Qatadah informed him that he had hunted it for his sake, he abstained from eating it after being informed of that, because it is established from him (ﷺ) that he ate from the meat of that donkey.\"\n---"
"2643","**\nNarrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Dawraqiyy, narrated to me Ibn Abi Hazim, on the authority of his father, on the authority of 'Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah, on the authority of Abu Qatadah (RA):\n\nAbu Qatadah (RA) said, \"I went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while they were in the state of ihram, and I was not in ihram. I saw a wild donkey, so I mounted my horse. I asked them to hand me the spear or the whip, but they refused to hand it to me. So I took it myself, then charged at it and slaughtered it. Then I brought it to them and caught up with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). They mentioned that to him, so he asked, 'Do you have any of its meat with you?' They said, 'Yes.' So they brought him a leg of it, and he ate from it.\"\n\nI extracted this narration in my book *al-Kabir* through the various chains of Abu Qatadah (RA), and this is the statement of those who said that the Prophet (ﷺ) ate from the meat of that donkey."
"2644","**\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Mukharrimi, narrated to us Ishaq ibn 'Isa, narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Qays, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas (RA), who said:\n\"O Zaid ibn Arqam! Did you know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was presented with ostrich eggs while he was in a state of ihram, and he rejected them?\"\nHe (Zaid ibn Arqam) said, \"Yes.\"\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said in the narration of Jabir (RA):\n\"The flesh of game is lawful for you while you are in ihram, as long as you did not hunt it or it was hunted for you.\"\n\nThis indicates that the eggs of game are permissible for the one in ihram if they were not taken for the sake of the muhrim (one in ihram), because the ruling for the egg of game cannot be more severe than the ruling for its flesh."
"2645","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from Ibn Abi 'Ammar.\n\nAnd narrated to us Abu Musa, and narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari, informed us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Ammar, who said:\nI met Jabir ibn 'Abdullah and asked him about the hyena: \"Can we eat it?\"\nHe said: \"Yes.\"\nI asked: \"Is it a game animal?\"\nHe said: \"Yes.\"\nI asked: \"Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?\"\nHe said: \"Yes.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2646","Narrated to us Salm ibn Junādah, narrated to us Wakīʿ, on the authority of Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim, on the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmayr, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī ʿAmmār, on the authority of Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (RA), who said:\n\"The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) made the expiation for a person in the state of ihrām who kills a hyena (ḍabuʿ) a male sheep from Najd, and he considered it part of the game (ṣayd).\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2647","Narrated to us Ya'qub al-Dawraqi and Muhammad ibn Hisham, both said: Narrated to us Hushaym, who said: Akhbarana Mansur (and he is Ibn Zadhan), on the authority of 'Ata', on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) decreed regarding the hyena (when an unclothed person kills it) with a ram.\"\n\nIbn Hisham said: \"On the authority of Mansur.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2648","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abi Musa al-Kharshi, who narrated to us Hassan ibn Ibrahim, who narrated to us Ibrahim al-Sa'igh, from Atta [ibn Abi Rabah], from Jabir ibn Abdullah (RA), who reported: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, \"The hyena is lawful game. If a Muhrim (one in the sacred state of ihram for Hajj or Umrah) hunts it, the expiation due for this is the sacrifice of an adult ram, and its meat is permissible to eat.\""
"2649","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashar, who narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, who narrated to us Malik, from Nafi', from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn Uthman, from Uthman ibn Affan (RA), from the Prophet (ﷺ). The Prophet (ﷺ) said: \"A person in the state of ihram (the sacred state of pilgrimage, referred to as a muhrim) is forbidden from contracting a marriage for himself, and it is also forbidden for a marriage to be contracted on his behalf.\" Abu Bakr stated: \"I have included this chapter in its entirety in my book titled al-Kabir.\""
"2650","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard Zayd ibn Aslam saying: Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Husayn narrated to me, on the authority of his father, who said: Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Ibn 'Abbas (RA) were disputing about the washing of the head of the one in ihram, while they were at al-'Arj. He (the narrator) said once: \"About the washing of the Prophet's (ﷺ) head.\" So they sent me to Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (RA) to ask him. I came to him at al-'Arj while he was bathing between the two horns of the well. I greeted him, and when he saw me, he wrapped his garment around his chest so tightly that it was as if I could see his chest. I said: \"Your nephew, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas (RA), has sent me to you to ask: How did you see the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) wash his head while he was in ihram?\"\n\nSo he (Abu Ayyub) ordered a bucket, and poured water, letting it flow over his head. Then he moved his hands forward and backward over his head and said: \"This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do it.\"\n\nSo I came to Ibn 'Abbas (RA) and informed him. Thereupon al-Miswar said to him: \"I will never dispute with you about anything after this.\""
"2651","**\nNarrated to us ‘Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-‘Ala’:\nNarrated to us Sufyan, who said: I heard ‘Amr — meaning Ibn Dinar — say: I heard ‘Ata’ say: I heard Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) say: “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had himself cupped while in ihram.”\n\nThen I heard ‘Amr say after that: Tawus informed me, saying: I heard Ibn ‘Abbas (RA) say: “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had himself cupped while in ihram.”\n\nSo I thought that he narrated it from both of them."
"2652","**\nNarrated to us Al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, narrated to us Affan ibn Muslim and Yahya ibn ‘Abbad, they said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, who informed us Faraqad, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ‘Umar (RA):\n\n*\"That the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) applied oil that was not scented while he was in the state of ihram.\"*\n\nAbu Bakr (RA) said: *\"I fear that Faraqad as-Sabakhi may have erred in narrating this report in marfu‘ form, for indeed Sufyan ath-Thawri narrated from Mansur, from Sa‘id ibn Jubayr, that he said: ‘Ibn ‘Umar (RA) would apply oil when he intended to enter ihram.’\"*"
"2653","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Rafi', narrated to us 'Abd al-Razzaq, informed us al-Thawri.\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \"The correct knowledge is that the narration of al-Duhn (oil) in the hadith of Sa'id ibn Jubayr is a mistake. It is actually from the action of Ibn 'Umar, not from the action of the Prophet (ﷺ).\"\n\n\"Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir is more preservative, more knowledgeable about hadith, and more precise than someone like Faraqad al-Sabakhi.\"\n\nThis is how it was narrated by Hajjaj ibn Minhal, from Hammad.\n\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Hajjaj ibn Minhal, narrated to us Waki' ibn al-Jarrah, from Hammad ibn Salamah, who said: \"At the time of entering ihram.\"\n\nAlso narrated to us Salam ibn Junadah, narrated to us Waki', and narrated to us al-Haytham ibn Jamil, from Hammad, who said: \"When he intends to enter ihram.\"\n\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us al-Haytham ibn Jamil.\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Shaybah) said: \"The wording mentioned by Waki' and the wording mentioned by al-Haytham ibn Jamil—if the oil is mixed with the finest perfume—it is permissible to use it when intending to enter ihram. This is because the Prophet (ﷺ) had perfumed himself with a perfume containing musk when he intended to enter ihram, and musk is the finest of perfumes, as the Chosen One (ﷺ) has informed us.\"\n\nI heard Muhammad ibn Yahya say: \"Unmixed oil, not perfumed.\"\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2654","Narrated to us by Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala', who said: Narrated to us by Sufyan, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nubayh ibn Wahb, from Aban ibn Uthman, that Uthman ibn Affan (RA) narrated from the Prophet (ﷺ): \"Indeed, if a man suffers from an eye ailment while he is in the state of Ihram, he may bind his eyes with aloe.\""
"2655","###\n Muhammad bin Yahya narrated to us, stating that Al-Hakam bin Musa narrated to us, stating that Yahya bin Hamza narrated to us. And Abu Hatim Muhammad bin Idris Ad-Darimi narrated to us, stating that Al-Haytham bin Kharija narrated to us, stating that Yahya bin Hamza narrated to us, on the authority of An-Nu'man bin Al-Mundhir, on the authority of Ata', Tawus, and Mujahid, on the authority of Ibn"
"2656","Narrated to us Yunus ibn ‘Abd al-A‘la: Narrated to us Ibn Wahb: He informed me Yunus, on the authority of Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn ‘Abdullah, from his father, who said:\nI heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say, “One who is in ihram may apply *talbīd* (a paste of clay and water).”\n\nNarrated to us Yunus: He informed us Ibn Wahb, who said:\nI asked Mālik, “Why does the person in ihram apply *talbīd* to his head?”\nHe replied, “With gum and *ghāsūl* (a type of clay).”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2657","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Sagani, who narrated to us Rawh ibn Ubada, who narrated to us Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, who narrated to us Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, who reported: Ibn Abbas (RA) said, \"The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) performed cupping (ḥijāma) on his head while he was in the state of iḥrām (ritual consecration for Hajj or Umrah).\" Abu Bakr (RA) then remarked: \"The narration of Ibn Buhaynah falls under this chapter.\""
"2658","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, narrated to us al-Mu'tamir, who said: I heard Humaid say that Anas (RA) was asked about a fasting person cupping (hijama). He said: \"We did not consider it disliked except due to the difficulty it causes.\" He did not attribute it (to the Prophet ﷺ). And he (Anas RA) said: \"The Prophet ﷺ indeed cupped while in ihram, and due to a pain he found in his head.\"\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
"2659","Narrated to us Muhammad bin Rafi', who said: Narrated to us Abdur-Razzaq, who said: He informed us Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from Anas (RA), who said: \"The Prophet (ﷺ) underwent cupping (hijama) while he was in the state of Ihram on the back of his foot, due to an ailment he was suffering from.\""
"2660","###\n Narrated to us by Muhammad bin Abd al-A'la al-San'ani, who said: Narrated to us by Khalid, meaning Ibn al-Harith. And also narrated to us by Bundar, who said: Narrated to me by Abd al-A'la. And also narrated to us by Ahmad bin al-Miqdam al-Ijli, who said: Narrated to us by Bishr, meaning Ibn al-Mufaddal. They all said: Narrated to us by Hisham, from Abu al-Zub"
"2661","###\n It was narrated that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) underwent cupping (Hijama) due to an ailment that afflicted him. This was narrated to us by Al-Ziyadi, who said: It was narrated to us by Al-Fadl bin Sulayman, from Ibn Khuthaym. Abu Bakr (the narrator/compiler) said: This ailment (Ar-Rukhsah) appears to be the same as the injury mentioned in the report of Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir (RA)."
"2662","Narrated to us Bundar, who narrated to us Abu Dawud, who narrated to us Shu'bah. And narrated to us Ali ibn Khashram, and narrated to us Isa, on the authority of Shu'bah. And narrated to us Yahya ibn Hakim, who narrated to us Ibn Abi Adiyy, on the authority of Shu'bah. Then narrated to us Bundar, who narrated to us Ibn Abi Adiyy, on the authority of Sa'id, on the authority of Qatadah, on the authority of Anas (RA), that the Prophet (ﷺ) came upon a man who was driving a sacrificial camel (badanah). He (ﷺ) said to him: \"Ride it.\" The man replied: \"It is a sacrificial camel (badanah).\" He (ﷺ) said: \"Ride it, woe to you (or: woe upon you).\" This is the wording of the hadith reported by Abu Dawud."
"2663","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Yahya, from Ibn Jurayj, and he (Muhammad ibn Bashshar) narrated to us once, narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, who informed me Abu’l-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn ‘Abd Allah (RA), who said:\n“I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) being asked about riding a camel that has been brought for sacrifice. He said: ‘Ride it until you find another mount.’”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
